/* DealCraft — Chapter 1 · Lesson 1.3 Private vs Public & the Gatekeeper (audited Oct 2026).
   Sources: Intro 2.4; Wan Ch 2 §E, Ch 3 [3.9], Ch 9, Ch 13 [13.47]–[13.62], Ch 14 [14.57]; Code (2026) Intro, Defs, r 14, r 15. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.f0v2 = (window.DC_BANK.f0v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"mx-q-0.3-01", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A buyer is acquiring a private company owned by three individuals. Which is TRUE of the regulatory frame?",
  options:[
    {t:"No Code: it is a private SPA under contract and company law", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. With a private target the buyer negotiates directly with a handful of identifiable owners, each of whom can protect their own interests at the table. The Code exists to protect dispersed public shareholders who cannot do that, so it does not apply. The parties are free to structure, condition and price the deal as they agree, constrained by contract law, company law (for example s 76 if a public company sits in the chain, s 160 on an asset sale) and any sector regulator. That freedom is why Chapter 2 deals can use earn-outs, selective pricing between sellers and heavily negotiated conditions that would be impossible or tightly limited in a Code-regulated offer."},
    {t:"The Take-Over Code applies because control is changing", correct:false,
     why:"A change of control is not, by itself, a Code trigger. The Code applies to acquisitions of control of listed companies, listed business trusts and REITs, and (so far as possible and appropriate) unlisted public companies and business trusts with more than 50 holders and net tangible assets of S$5m or more. A private company owned by three people is outside it."},
    {t:"A mandatory general offer is triggered at 30%", correct:false,
     why:"The 30% mandatory-offer trigger is a Code rule. Because the Code does not apply to this private company, buying 30%, 60% or 100% of it carries no mandatory-offer obligation. The buyer can buy from one seller at one price and not from the others."},
    {t:"SGX listing rules apply", correct:false,
     why:"SGX listing rules bind listed issuers. A private company is not listed, so they do not apply to it. They could become relevant only if the buyer itself is listed and the deal is large enough relative to the buyer to need its own shareholders' approval."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2 §E, Ch 3; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Private target → private SPA; no Code."
},

{ id:"mx-q-0.3-02", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why can't a buyer of a listed company simply 'negotiate with the owners' as in a private deal?",
  options:[
    {t:"Thousands of holders, so the Code, SGX rules and the CA apply", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. A listed company is owned by the market: thousands of holders buying and selling every day. The buyer cannot sign one contract with 'the owners', and individual small holders cannot negotiate protections for themselves. So the law supplies the protections a private SPA negotiation would otherwise provide. The Code, administered by the Securities Industry Council, requires equal treatment, sets the mandatory-offer thresholds, limits deal protections and paces the offer through a timetable. SGX rules add continuous disclosure and transaction classification. The Companies Act supplies the scheme (s 210) and squeeze-out (s 215) machinery. This is the world of Chapter 3."},
    {t:"Because listed companies have no owners", correct:false,
     why:"Listed companies have owners; they just have very many of them, and the list changes daily. It is that dispersion, not an absence of owners, that makes direct negotiation impossible and creates the need for protective rules."},
    {t:"Because the SPA form is prohibited by statute", correct:false,
     why:"No statute bans contracts in public deals. A bidder can and often does sign an agreement with a major shareholder to buy its stake, or an implementation agreement with the target. But that does not bind the thousands of other holders, so the Code framework governs how control of the company as a whole is acquired."},
    {t:"Because directors cannot sell shares", correct:false,
     why:"Directors face dealing restrictions around deals, which may suggest the problem lies there. But directors can deal in shares, subject to disclosure, insider-trading rules and Code dealing restrictions. The real reason public deals need a different framework is that ownership is dispersed among thousands of changing holders who cannot bargain individually."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 3; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Dispersed owners → the Code, SIC, SGX rules and CA replace private bargaining."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-01", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"TargetCo is an unlisted public company with 180 shareholders and net tangible assets of S$40m. A buyer plans to acquire 60% by private negotiation with the largest holders.",
  prompt:"Does the Take-Over Code have any role?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes, if it has over 50 holders and NTA of S$5m+", correct:true,
     why:"The Code's Introduction (and Wan [3.9]) extends it beyond listed companies. It is drafted with listed companies, listed business trusts and REITs in mind, but unlisted public companies and unlisted registered business trusts with more than 50 shareholders or unitholders and net tangible assets of S$5m or more must also observe the letter and spirit of the General Principles and Rules wherever possible and appropriate. TargetCo meets both tests. The buyer should treat the Code as engaged: buying 60% could trigger mandatory-offer obligations and equal-treatment concerns. Such companies may apply to the SIC to waive the Code's application, and the SIC considers factors such as the number of shareholders and the nature of the company."},
    {t:"No: the Code only ever applies to SGX-listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"This is the common assumption the question tests. The Code's reach expressly includes certain unlisted public companies and business trusts. Missing that could leave a buyer having triggered a mandatory offer without realising it."},
    {t:"No, because the deal is negotiated privately", correct:false,
     why:"How the shares are bought does not decide whether the Code applies. Private negotiation with large holders is a common way to cross 30% in a Code company. The status and size of the target, not the method of purchase, decide the Code's reach."},
    {t:"Only if the buyer is foreign", correct:false,
     why:"Foreign buyers often face extra regulation, which may suggest the Code depends on nationality. But the Code applies according to the target: listed companies, REITs and business trusts, and unlisted public companies with more than 50 holders and NTA of S$5m or more. Foreign ownership raises SIRA or sector rules, which are separate regimes."}
  ],
  cite:"Code Intro; Wan Ch 3 [3.9]", takeaway:"Unlisted public co with >50 holders and NTA ≥ S$5m → observe the Code."
},

{ id:"f0x-3-01", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An investor buys 31% of the units in an SGX-listed REIT from the REIT's former sponsor.",
  prompt:"Does the Take-Over Code apply?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: the Code covers REITs, so crossing 30% triggers an offer", correct:true,
     why:"The Code's Introduction states that it applies to corporations with a primary listing in Singapore, listed registered business trusts and REITs, and Wan [3.9] makes the same point. REIT units are not shares, and a REIT is a trust rather than a company, but the Code treats units as carrying voting rights for these purposes. So an investor that crosses 30% of the units is in the same position as one crossing 30% of a listed company's shares: it must make a mandatory general offer for the remaining units, in cash or with a cash alternative, at the highest price paid in the look-back period, unless the SIC grants a waiver. Chapter 5 builds on this."},
    {t:"No: the Code applies only to companies, and a REIT is a trust", correct:false,
     why:"The Code expressly covers REITs and registered business trusts. Treating a REIT as outside the Code because it is a trust would miss one of the most important points in Chapter 5."},
    {t:"No: buying from the sponsor is a private transaction", correct:false,
     why:"The method of acquisition does not matter. A private purchase from a single large holder is the classic way to cross 30% in a listed vehicle and trigger a mandatory offer."},
    {t:"Only if the REIT manager consents", correct:false,
     why:"The Code's application does not depend on the manager's consent. The manager has its own duties in an offer, but whether the investor must make an offer is decided by the Code thresholds."}
  ],
  cite:"Code Intro; Wan Ch 3 [3.9]", takeaway:"The Code covers REITs and business trusts too."
},

{ id:"f0-3-04", lesson:"0.3", type:"order", dia:"thresholdLadder",
  prompt:"Put these Code / Companies Act control thresholds in ascending order.",
  steps:[">25% (blocks a special resolution: negative control)","30% (effective control: mandatory offer)",">50% (simple majority control)","75% (passes a special resolution)","90% of offer shares (unlocks s 215 squeeze-out)"],
  why:"These are the numbers the Intro tells you to memorise, and each one unlocks a different power. Just over 25% blocks a special resolution, because a special resolution needs 75% of the votes cast (negative control). 30% is the Code's effective-control line, where Rule 14 forces a mandatory general offer. Over 50% gives simple control of ordinary resolutions and the board; the Code calls this 'statutory control'. 75% passes special resolutions such as constitutional amendments and capital reductions. 90% of the shares the offer relates to unlocks compulsory acquisition of the remaining minority under s 215 of the Companies Act. One more number belongs on the ladder: a holder already between 30% and 50% re-triggers a mandatory offer by acquiring more than 1% in any six months. Route choice often turns on which threshold a controller can realistically reach.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 9, Ch 14; Intro 2.4", takeaway:">25 block · 30 offer · >50 control · 75 special · 90 squeeze-out."
},

{ id:"d1-004", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq", dia:"thresholdLadder",
  prompt:"A shareholder wants a veto over major corporate actions (like constitutional changes) but not necessarily day-to-day control. What stake is the meaningful line?",
  options:[
    {t:"Just over 25% (blocks a special resolution)", correct:true,
     why:"Special resolutions are used for fundamental changes: amending the constitution, reducing capital, changing the company's name, approving certain schemes and conversions. They need at least 75% of the votes cast. A holder with more than 25% of all votes can therefore defeat any special resolution even if every other shareholder votes in favour. That is a blocking veto, or 'negative control'. It does not let the holder pass anything or appoint the board, which needs more than 50%. In practice, because the 75% is measured on votes cast, a smaller stake may block if turnout is low; just over 25% is the level that guarantees a block whatever the turnout."},
    {t:"Exactly 50%, for a deadlock on every vote", correct:false,
     why:"More than 50% gives positive control of ordinary resolutions, which is more than a veto. Exactly 50% gives deadlock on ordinary resolutions. For a veto over special resolutions, a holding above 25% is enough."},
    {t:"30%, the mandatory-offer threshold", correct:false,
     why:"30% is the Code's mandatory-offer trigger, not the veto threshold. A 30% holder can of course block special resolutions too, but the meaningful line for a blocking stake is just over 25%."},
    {t:"5%", correct:false,
     why:"5% is a well-known threshold, which may make it seem significant for control. But 5% makes a holder a substantial shareholder with disclosure obligations. It gives no veto over special resolutions, which need 75% of votes cast, except where turnout is unusually low."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 14; CA s 184; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Just over 25% = negative control (blocks special resolutions)."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-10", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Shareholder Z owns 20% of ListCo. Turnout at general meetings is typically about 60% of the issued shares, and Z always attends.",
  prompt:"Can Z block a special resolution on its own?",
  options:[
    {t:"Very likely: 20% of a 60% turnout is about a third of votes cast", correct:true,
     why:"A special resolution is passed by at least 75% of the votes cast by members entitled to vote (CA s 184), not 75% of all issued shares. So the real question is Z's share of the votes actually cast. If 60% of shares vote and Z is among them, Z's 20% is one third of the votes cast (20 ÷ 60), well above the 25% needed to block. The 'just over 25%' figure is the stake that guarantees a block whatever the turnout; with ordinary turnout, a smaller stake can be enough. This matters in practice: a bidder or controller planning a capital reduction or constitutional change must model turnout, and a minority holder may have more leverage than its headline percentage suggests."},
    {t:"No: a blocking stake always needs more than 25% of the issued shares", correct:false,
     why:"More than 25% of issued shares guarantees a block, but it is not always needed. Because the 75% is measured on votes cast, lower turnout magnifies the voting power of those who attend."},
    {t:"No: Z would need 30%", correct:false,
     why:"30% is the most memorable control number, which makes it easy to apply here. But it is the Code's mandatory-offer trigger. Blocking a special resolution depends on the votes actually cast: Z needs more than 25% of them, and with 60% turnout its 20% is about 33%."},
    {t:"Yes, but only if Z is a director", correct:false,
     why:"Directors have influence over company decisions, which may suggest they have extra voting power. But at a general meeting, votes come from shares. A director who is also a shareholder votes only those shares, so Z's position depends on its 20% and the turnout."}
  ],
  cite:"CA s 184; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Special resolutions are 75% of votes cast, so turnout changes who can block."
},

{ id:"f0-3-02", lesson:"0.3", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Under the Take-Over Code, crossing ____ of voting rights is treated as acquiring 'effective control' and triggers a mandatory general offer (Rule 14).",
  accept:["30%","30","30 percent","thirty percent","thirty%"], answer:"30%",
  why:"30% is the Code's effective-control line. Under Rule 14.1(a), a person who acquires shares that, together with those held by its concert parties, carry 30% or more of the voting rights must make a general offer to all other shareholders. The offer must be in cash or with a cash alternative, at not less than the highest price paid by the offeror or its concert parties during the offer period and the six months before it. It is rigid: Rule 14.2 allows only one condition, that acceptances take the offeror and its concert parties to more than 50% of the voting rights. Why 30%? In a listed company with dispersed holders and imperfect turnout, 30% is usually enough to control the board in practice, so the Code treats it as the point at which control changes hands and minorities deserve an exit.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 §C; Code r 14; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"30% = effective control → mandatory offer (Rule 14)."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-02", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Investor X holds 29% of ListCo. It buys a further 2% on-market in a single week.",
  prompt:"What is the consequence?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: crossing 30% triggers a Rule 14 mandatory offer", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 14.1(a) is triggered when a person acquires shares that take it, with its concert parties, to 30% or more of the voting rights. X moves from 29% to 31%, so the obligation arises on the purchase that crosses the line. X must announce and make a general offer to all other shareholders, in cash or with a cash alternative, at no less than the highest price it paid during the six months before the offer period (including the purchases this week). The creep rule is not the relevant limb here; it governs holders already in the 30% to 50% band. A 29% holder should treat every further purchase as potentially triggering."},
    {t:"Nothing, because X bought less than 1% in six months", correct:false,
     why:"X bought 2%, not less than 1%. More importantly, the 1% creep limit applies only to holders already between 30% and 50%. A holder below 30% triggers an offer by crossing 30% itself, regardless of the size of the purchase."},
    {t:"Nothing until X reaches 50%", correct:false,
     why:"Majority control is the intuitive meaning of control, which makes 50% sound like the trigger. But the Code treats 30% as effective control and requires a mandatory offer when it is crossed. Over 50%, which the Code calls statutory control, matters for other purposes such as the creep rule falling away."},
    {t:"X must seek court sanction", correct:false,
     why:"Courts sanction schemes, which may suggest they supervise all takeovers. But a mandatory offer is a Code obligation administered by the SIC. No court is involved in triggering or making it; X must announce and make the offer on the Code's terms."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 §C; Code r 14.1(a); Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Cross 30% on any purchase → mandatory offer."
},

{ id:"f0-3-03", lesson:"0.3", type:"fill",
  prompt:"A holder already sitting between 30% and 50% triggers a fresh mandatory offer by acquiring more than ____ in any rolling 6-month period ('creeping acquisitions').",
  accept:["1%","1","1 percent","one percent","one%"], answer:"1%",
  why:"Rule 14.1(b) applies to a person who, with its concert parties, holds between 30% and 50% of the voting rights. If that person or any concert party acquires additional shares carrying more than 1% of the voting rights in any period of six months, a mandatory offer is triggered. The rule stops a holder with effective control from quietly 'creeping' towards majority control without ever giving minorities an exit at a fair price. The six months is a rolling window, so several small purchases are added together. Above 50% the holder already has statutory control, so the creep rule falls away; below 30% the relevant trigger is crossing 30% itself.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 §C; Code r 14.1(b); Intro 2.4", takeaway:"30–50% holder: >1% in any 6 months → mandatory offer."
},

{ id:"d1-006", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The holder sits in the 30–50% band and is steadily buying on-market.",
  prompt:"A holder at 45% of a listed company acquires another 0.8% in three months, then a further 0.5% two months later. Has the creep rule been breached?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: 1.3% within six months breaches the 1% creep limit", correct:true,
     why:"The creep rule looks at acquisitions in 'any period of 6 months', so it adds purchases together within a rolling window rather than testing each one on its own. Both purchases fall within five months of each other, totalling 1.3%, which exceeds 1%. The second purchase is the one that crosses the line, and it triggers the obligation to make a general offer. A holder in the 30% to 50% band who wants to keep buying without making an offer has to track its rolling six-month total carefully, including purchases by concert parties. If it wants to consolidate quickly, it can instead make a voluntary offer, or seek a whitewash waiver from independent shareholders where available."},
    {t:"No: each individual purchase was under 1%, so no breach", correct:false,
     why:"Each purchase being small can feel like staying within the rule. But Rule 14.1(b) aggregates acquisitions over any period of six months. 0.8% plus 0.5% within five months is 1.3%, above the 1% limit, so the second purchase triggers a mandatory offer."},
    {t:"No: the creep rule only applies above 50%", correct:false,
     why:"This is backwards. Above 50% the holder already has statutory control and the creep rule falls away. The rule is aimed precisely at the 30% to 50% band, where this holder sits."},
    {t:"No: creep only matters for concert parties", correct:false,
     why:"The creep rule applies to a single holder as well as to a concert-party group. Concert parties are relevant because their holdings and purchases are aggregated, but a solo holder at 45% is caught on its own."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 §C; Code r 14.1(b); Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Creep is a rolling 6-month total, not a per-trade test."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-03", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Investor Y already holds 52% of ListCo. Over three months it buys another 5% on-market.",
  prompt:"Has Y triggered a mandatory offer?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the creep rule applies only between 30% and 50%", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 14.1(b) is limited to holders with not less than 30% but not more than 50% of the voting rights. Its purpose is to stop a holder with effective control from consolidating towards majority control without making an offer. Y is already above 50%, which the Code calls statutory control, so further purchases do not take Y across any new control line and do not trigger a fresh mandatory offer. Other rules still apply: Y must disclose its dealings, and if Y later makes an offer, its recent purchase prices will set the floor. Y should also watch the free float, because if public shareholders fall below 10% SGX may suspend trading."},
    {t:"Yes: any purchase over 1% in six months triggers an offer", correct:false,
     why:"The 1% creep rule is easy to remember without its band, which makes this answer tempting. But Rule 14.1(b) applies only to holders with 30% to 50%. Y is above 50%, already holding statutory control, so further purchases do not trigger a fresh mandatory offer."},
    {t:"Yes: crossing 55% is a separate trigger", correct:false,
     why:"A further threshold above 50% may seem logical. But the Code has no 55% trigger. The mandatory-offer triggers are crossing 30%, and acquiring more than 1% in six months while between 30% and 50%. Y's purchases hit neither."},
    {t:"Only if Y's purchases were made off-market", correct:false,
     why:"Whether purchases are on-market or off-market does not change the threshold analysis. It can matter for other rules, such as disclosure timing, but not for whether a mandatory offer is triggered."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 §C; Code r 14.1(b); Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Above 50%, the creep rule falls away."
},

{ id:"mx-q-0.3-08", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Crossing 30% of voting rights generally triggers a mandatory general offer. Which best describes that offer?",
  options:[
    {t:"Rigid: cash, highest look-back price, only a >50% condition", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. The mandatory offer protects minorities when control changes hands. It must go to all holders of the class, so a bidder cannot buy out only the large holders. It must be in cash or with a cash alternative, so minorities cannot be forced to take the bidder's paper. Its price must be at least the highest price paid by the offeror or any concert party during the offer period and the six months before it, so minorities get what the controller paid for its stake. And Rule 14.2 says it may be conditional on, and only on, acceptances taking the offeror and its concert parties above 50% of the voting rights. The bidder cannot add a MAC, a financing condition or a higher acceptance threshold."},
    {t:"A discretionary offer the bidder can load with conditions", correct:false,
     why:"That describes a voluntary offer, which can carry conditions so long as they are not subjective or within the offeror's control. A mandatory offer is rigid, because the bidder chose to cross 30% and the Code requires it to give minorities a clean exit."},
    {t:"An offer made only to the largest shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Buying from the largest holders is how many controlling stakes are built, which may make this sound normal. But once a mandatory offer is triggered, General Principle equality requires it to go to all holders of the class on the same terms. Minorities must get the same exit at the same price."},
    {t:"A share-for-share offer with no cash option", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer must be in cash or accompanied by a cash alternative at the required price. A bidder may offer shares as well, but it cannot force minorities to accept paper only."}
  ],
  cite:"Code r 14; Wan Ch 9", takeaway:"Mandatory offer: all holders · cash · highest price · only the >50% condition."
},

{ id:"f0x-3-02", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"BidCo agrees to buy a founder's 35% stake in ListCo. BidCo's draft offer terms say the resulting general offer will be conditional on 90% acceptances, no material adverse change, and approval by BidCo's own shareholders.",
  prompt:"How should BidCo's lawyer respond?",
  options:[
    {t:"Rule 14.2 allows only the >50% condition; move pre-conditions into the SPA", correct:true,
     why:"Buying 35% triggers a mandatory offer, and Rule 14.2(a) says that offer must be conditional upon, and only upon, acceptances taking BidCo and its concert parties above 50%. The 90% condition and the MAC are not allowed. Rule 14.2(b) goes further: BidCo must not make an acquisition that triggers an offer if the offer would or might depend on a resolution of its own shareholders or on any other conditions, consents or arrangements, including a foreign regulator's approval. The solution, recognised in Note 1 to Rule 14.2, is to make the share purchase agreement with the founder conditional on clear, objective and reasonable pre-conditions with a reasonable time limit. The 35% is acquired, and the offer obligation arises, only once they are met. The SIC should be consulted."},
    {t:"Keep all three conditions; a bidder can always protect itself with conditions", correct:false,
     why:"That is true of a voluntary offer within limits, but not of a mandatory offer. Rule 14.2 is deliberately rigid because the bidder chose to cross 30%. An offer document carrying these conditions would breach the Code."},
    {t:"Drop the conditions and rely on the MAC clause in the SPA to withdraw the general offer if needed", correct:false,
     why:"Once the 35% is acquired, the obligation to make the general offer exists, and a firm offer generally cannot be withdrawn. A MAC in the SPA with the founder may protect BidCo before it acquires the stake, but it cannot be used to escape the general offer once triggered."},
    {t:"Make the general offer conditional on 75% acceptances instead of 90%", correct:false,
     why:"Any acceptance threshold other than more than 50% is a condition Rule 14.2 does not permit without the SIC's consent. The problem is not the number chosen; it is that a mandatory offer cannot carry the bidder's preferred conditions at all."}
  ],
  cite:"Code r 14.2 & Note 1; Wan Ch 9", takeaway:"Mandatory offer: only the >50% condition, so put pre-conditions in the SPA."
},

{ id:"mx-q-0.3-10", lesson:"0.3", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: For a mandatory offer, the price look-back period for the highest price paid (six months) is LONGER than the three-month period used for a voluntary offer.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. Wan [7.42]–[7.43] notes that the mandatory-offer look-back is six months, whereas a voluntary offer must match the highest price paid in the three months before the offer period. The longer period reflects the mandatory offer's purpose: the bidder has taken effective control, and minorities should receive at least what the bidder paid to build its controlling stake over a longer stretch. In practice, a bidder that may cross 30% must plan its purchases with the six-month window in mind, because a single expensive block bought five months earlier sets the floor for the whole offer."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"It is true. The mandatory-offer look-back (six months) is longer than the voluntary-offer look-back (three months). The difference is deliberately protective of minorities in the mandatory case, where control has already changed hands."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [7.42]–[7.43]; Code rr 14, 17", takeaway:"Price floor: mandatory 6 months · voluntary 3 months."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-04", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Bidder bought ListCo shares at S$1.40 eight months ago, S$1.30 five months ago and S$1.22 last month. The offer period is about to begin.",
  prompt:"What is the minimum offer price if the offer is (a) a mandatory offer and (b) a voluntary offer?",
  options:[
    {t:"(a) S$1.30 (six-month look-back); (b) S$1.22 (three-month look-back)", correct:true,
     why:"A mandatory offer must be at not less than the highest price paid by the bidder or its concert parties during the offer period and the six months before it. The S$1.30 purchase (five months ago) is inside that window; the S$1.40 purchase (eight months ago) is outside it. So the floor is S$1.30. A voluntary offer must match the highest price paid in the three months before the offer period, which captures only the S$1.22 purchase last month, so the floor is S$1.22. In both cases purchases made during the offer period itself also count, and buying above the offer price during the offer would force the offer price up. The bidder's financial adviser needs this history before the offer is priced."},
    {t:"(a) S$1.40; (b) S$1.40", correct:false,
     why:"S$1.40 is the highest price the bidder ever paid, which makes it seem the floor. But the Code only looks back a set period: six months for a mandatory offer and three months for a voluntary one. The S$1.40 purchase was eight months ago, outside both windows."},
    {t:"(a) S$1.22; (b) S$1.30", correct:false,
     why:"It is easy to mix up which offer has the longer look-back. But the mandatory offer has six months, which reaches the S$1.30 purchase five months ago. The voluntary offer has three months, which captures only the S$1.22 purchase, so the floors are S$1.30 and S$1.22."},
    {t:"(a) the average of all three prices; (b) the market price on announcement", correct:false,
     why:"The Code uses the highest price paid in the look-back period, not an average and not the market price. The aim is that no shareholder gets less than the best price the bidder paid anyone in that period."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [7.42]–[7.43], Ch 9; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Floor = highest price paid in the look-back window."
},

{ id:"f0x-3-03", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"BidCo crosses 30% of ListCo by buying at S$1.95. Four months earlier, BidCo's parent company (its concert party) bought ListCo shares at S$2.10.",
  prompt:"What is the minimum price for BidCo's mandatory offer?",
  options:[
    {t:"S$2.10: the look-back includes concert parties' purchases", correct:true,
     why:"The mandatory offer must be at not less than the highest price paid by the offeror or any person acting in concert with it during the offer period and the six months before it. A parent and its subsidiary are presumed to act in concert under the Code's definition. The parent's S$2.10 purchase four months ago is within the window, so it sets the floor even though BidCo itself paid only S$1.95. This is the same logic as aggregating holdings for the thresholds: the Code treats the group as one acquirer, so a group cannot buy a block expensively through one entity and then make a cheaper offer through another."},
    {t:"S$1.95, the price BidCo paid for the triggering block", correct:false,
     why:"The triggering purchase seems the natural benchmark. But the Code's price floor uses the highest price paid by the offeror or any concert party in the look-back period. A parent and subsidiary are presumed to act in concert, so the parent's S$2.10 sets the floor."},
    {t:"The average of S$1.95 and S$2.10", correct:false,
     why:"An average may seem fairer to the bidder. But the Code uses the highest price paid, not an average, so that no minority holder receives less than the best price the bidder group paid anyone in the period. Here that is the parent's S$2.10."},
    {t:"Whatever the current market price is", correct:false,
     why:"Market price is a natural reference point for an offer. But the Code's test is the highest price paid by the offeror group in the look-back period. The market price may be lower than that, and using it would let the group pay minorities less than it paid others."}
  ],
  cite:"Code r 14.3 & Defs (acting in concert); Wan Ch 9", takeaway:"Concert parties' purchases count in the price look-back too."
},

{ id:"f0-3-05", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What are 'concert parties', and why do they matter?",
  options:[
    {t:"People cooperating for control, counted as one for the Code", correct:true,
     why:"The Code defines persons acting in concert as those who, under an agreement or understanding (formal or informal), cooperate to obtain or consolidate effective control of a company through acquiring voting rights. Their holdings and purchases are aggregated as if they were a single acquirer, for the 30% trigger, for the creep rule and for the price look-back. The Code also lists classes of persons presumed to act in concert unless the contrary is shown, such as a company and its parent and subsidiaries, a company and its directors, and an individual and his close relatives. The Intro notes this is what catches consortium buyers, families acting together and bidder-plus-financier groups, and it underlies the chain principle."},
    {t:"Advisers who must abstain from voting on any scheme", correct:false,
     why:"That confuses concert parties with abstention rules. Advisers can be presumed concert parties of their clients for some purposes, but the concept itself is about aggregating holdings of persons cooperating to obtain or consolidate control."},
    {t:"Shareholders who receive a special deal not offered to others", correct:false,
     why:"A benefit given to some shareholders but not others is a 'special deal', regulated under the equality-of-treatment principle (Wan Ch 11). It is a separate concept, although special deals often involve concert parties."},
    {t:"Any two shareholders who happen to vote the same way", correct:false,
     why:"Voting the same way is not enough. Concert-party status requires cooperation, by agreement or understanding, to obtain or consolidate control. Unconnected holders who independently reach the same view are not acting in concert."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 §D; Code Defs; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Concert parties are aggregated as one acquirer."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-05", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Fund A holds 18% and Fund B holds 10% of ListCo. They agree to vote together and plan a joint bid. Fund B then buys a further 3%.",
  prompt:"What follows under the Code?",
  options:[
    {t:"Their stakes aggregate to 31%, so a mandatory offer is triggered", correct:true,
     why:"Once the funds agree to vote together and plan a joint bid, they are cooperating to obtain control and are acting in concert. Their holdings are aggregated: 18% + 10% = 28%. Fund B's purchase of 3% takes the group to 31%, crossing the 30% line, so a mandatory offer is triggered even though neither fund on its own is close to 30%. The offer obligation normally falls on the person whose acquisition caused the crossing, but the Code can extend it to the principal members of the group. Had the funds wanted to avoid this, they needed to plan the bid as a voluntary offer before crossing, or keep their combined holding below 30%."},
    {t:"Nothing: each fund is below 30% on its own", correct:false,
     why:"Each fund being below 30% makes the threshold seem untouched. But once they agree to vote together and bid jointly, they are concert parties and their holdings are aggregated. 18% plus 10% plus Fund B's 3% is 31%, so the group has crossed 30%."},
    {t:"Only Fund B's 13% is relevant", correct:false,
     why:"Fund B made the purchase, so its holding may seem the one that matters. But the Code measures the concert group's combined holding. Fund B's purchase matters because it takes the group from 28% to 31%, triggering the mandatory offer."},
    {t:"They need a formal written agreement before they can be concert parties", correct:false,
     why:"A formal agreement can seem necessary to create legal consequences. But the Code's definition covers agreements and understandings, formal or informal. Agreeing to vote together and plan a joint bid is plainly cooperation to obtain control."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 §D; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Concert parties' stakes add up for the 30% test."
},

{ id:"f0x-3-04", amended:true, lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A father holds 22% of ListCo and his adult daughter holds 9%, which she bought years ago. The daughter buys another 0.5%. She says she invests independently and has never discussed ListCo with her father.",
  prompt:"What is the Code position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Relatives are presumed in concert: 31.5% crosses 30% unless rebutted", correct:true,
     why:"The Code's definition of acting in concert presumes that an individual and his close relatives act together, and close relatives include immediate family such as children. The father and daughter together held 31% before her purchase and 31.5% after it; on the presumption, the family is already above 30%, and her purchase is a further acquisition by the group. The presumption is rebuttable. Note 1 to the definition lists the factors the SIC considers: the pattern, volume, timing and price of the relatives' purchases, how they vote their shares, and whether the relative has independent financial means for the purchases. The daughter should consult the SIC before dealing, not after."},
    {t:"No issue: each holds less than 30% individually", correct:false,
     why:"Each family member's holding is below 30%, which makes this answer intuitive. But the Code presumes close relatives act in concert, so their holdings are aggregated unless the presumption is rebutted to the SIC's satisfaction. Together they hold more than 30%."},
    {t:"The daughter's statement that she invests independently is conclusive", correct:false,
     why:"Her account is relevant evidence, but the presumption is rebutted only if the SIC is satisfied, looking at objective factors such as trading and voting patterns and her financial means. A bare assertion is not enough."},
    {t:"Only spouses are presumed to act in concert", correct:false,
     why:"Spouses are the most obvious example of close relatives, which may suggest the category ends there. But the Code's definition covers immediate family, including parents, siblings, spouse and children, and the 2026 definition extends further. An adult daughter is a close relative."}
  ],
  cite:"Code Defs (acting in concert, close relatives) & Note 1; Wan Ch 9 §D", takeaway:"Family is presumed to act in concert; rebuttal is for the SIC."
},

{ id:"mx-q-0.3-12", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Bidder acquires HoldCo, an unlisted company whose main asset is a 60% stake in ListCo (a listed company).",
  prompt:"Which Code concept may force Bidder to make an offer for ListCo?",
  options:[
    {t:"The chain principle", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. By taking control of HoldCo, Bidder indirectly acquires control of HoldCo's 60% stake in ListCo, even though it never buys a ListCo share directly. The chain principle can require a mandatory offer for ListCo in these circumstances. Wan [9.37]–[9.38] explains that it is not automatic: an indirect acquisition generally triggers it only where the listed company is significant to the intermediate company, measured by its contribution to assets, market value, sales or earnings. Here ListCo is HoldCo's main asset, so the significance test is likely met. Bidder should consult the SIC early, and its financing must allow for a possible offer for ListCo's minorities."},
    {t:"The whitewash procedure", correct:false,
     why:"A whitewash is closely associated with mandatory offers, which may make it seem relevant. But it is a way for independent shareholders to waive an offer obligation once triggered. It does not create the obligation; the chain principle does."},
    {t:"s 18A", correct:false,
     why:"s 18A also concerns changes of ownership, which may cause the confusion. But it deals with employees moving in a going-concern business transfer. It has nothing to do with takeover thresholds or offers for listed companies."},
    {t:"Structural arbitrage between routes", correct:false,
     why:"Structural arbitrage is a Wan concept about control transactions, which may make it seem relevant. But it describes a controller choosing the route with the lowest approval bar. It does not trigger offers; the chain principle is what can require an offer for ListCo."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 [9.37]–[9.38]; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Upstream control can trigger a downstream offer (chain principle)."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-06", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Bidder acquires 100% of HoldCo, a large unlisted industrial group. One of HoldCo's many assets is a 40% stake in ListCo, which accounts for about 3% of HoldCo's assets, sales and earnings.",
  prompt:"Is Bidder likely to have to make an offer for ListCo under the chain principle?",
  options:[
    {t:"Probably not, unless ListCo is significant to the upstream company", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [9.38] explains that the mandatory-offer obligation does not generally apply to an indirect acquisition unless the downstream company is significant to the intermediate holding company, assessed by its contribution to assets, market capitalisation, sales or earnings. At about 3% on every measure, ListCo is a minor part of HoldCo, and Bidder is clearly buying HoldCo for the industrial group, not to get control of ListCo. Wan notes that Singapore, like the UK, has dropped the old 'purpose' test in favour of this more objective significance approach. Because the outcome depends on the SIC's view, Bidder should consult the SIC before completion."},
    {t:"Yes, always: any indirect acquisition of a 30%+ stake triggers an offer", correct:false,
     why:"Crossing 30% indirectly can look like any other acquisition of control. But the chain principle is not automatic. Wan [9.38] explains that it generally applies only where the listed company is significant to the holding company, by assets, market value, sales or earnings."},
    {t:"No, because the chain principle has been abolished", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore has dropped part of the old test, which may suggest the whole principle has gone. But it dropped the purpose test, not the principle. The significance test remains, so a minor listed stake usually does not trigger an offer, while a significant one may."},
    {t:"Only if Bidder intended to control ListCo", correct:false,
     why:"Intention can seem the natural test for whether an acquisition of control was 'really' aimed at ListCo. But Wan notes that Singapore, like the UK, has abandoned the purpose test to make the analysis objective. The question is whether ListCo is significant to HoldCo."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 [9.37]–[9.38]", takeaway:"Chain principle needs significance: a minor listed stake won't usually trigger it."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-07", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"At ListCo's rescue placement, an investor subscribes for new shares taking it from 0% to 35%. The board wants to avoid the investor having to make a general offer.",
  prompt:"What is the usual route?",
  options:[
    {t:"Seek an SIC waiver, subject to a whitewash vote by independent holders", correct:true,
     why:"Acquiring voting rights through a new issue counts towards the 30% threshold just as a purchase does, so the investor's subscription would normally trigger Rule 14. The SIC can waive the obligation where independent shareholders, voting at a general meeting with the investor and its concert parties excluded, approve a whitewash resolution after proper disclosure, including the dilution and the fact that they are giving up a general offer. This is common in rescue financings, because the investor will often only subscribe if it is not also forced to buy everyone else out. Note that 'whitewash' here is the Code's offer-waiver procedure, quite separate from the financial-assistance whitewash under s 76."},
    {t:"Run a s 76(9B) whitewash", correct:false,
     why:"That is the Companies Act procedure to approve financial assistance. The word is the same but the procedure and purpose are different. It does nothing about a Code mandatory-offer obligation."},
    {t:"Nothing is needed because shares are newly issued rather than bought", correct:false,
     why:"New shares come from the company rather than from another shareholder, which may suggest they fall outside the rule. But the Code looks at voting rights held, however obtained. Acquiring 35% through a subscription crosses 30% just as a purchase would."},
    {t:"Split the placement between the investor and a friendly party to stay below 30% each", correct:false,
     why:"Splitting the placement keeps each holding below 30%, which may seem a neat solution. But if the investor and the friendly party cooperate, they are concert parties and their holdings are aggregated. A deliberate split would be treated as an attempt to evade the Code."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9; Code r 14 & App 1; Intro glossary 'Whitewash'", takeaway:"New issue over 30% → SIC waiver + independent shareholders' whitewash."
},

{ id:"mx-q-0.3-03", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which route to acquire or take private a listed company is company-driven and works ONLY friendly (recommended)?",
  options:[
    {t:"A scheme of arrangement (s 210)", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. A scheme is the target company's own proposal to its members. The company applies to court to convene the class meetings, sends out the scheme document with the IFA's opinion, holds the meetings and then applies for court sanction. A bidder cannot do any of that without the board, so a scheme only works on a friendly, recommended basis, usually under an implementation agreement between bidder and target. In return, the bidder gets all-or-nothing certainty: if the votes and sanction are obtained, the scheme binds every shareholder, including those who voted against or did not vote, and the bidder gets 100%."},
    {t:"A takeover offer under the Code", correct:false,
     why:"A takeover offer is made by the bidder directly to shareholders. It can be recommended by the board, but it does not need the board's cooperation, which is why a hostile bid must be an offer."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer under Rule 14", correct:false,
     why:"Mandatory offers are a distinct route to control, which may make them seem company-driven. But a mandatory offer is forced on a bidder that crosses 30%, and the bidder makes it directly to shareholders. The company-driven, court-sanctioned route is the scheme of arrangement."},
    {t:"A selective capital reduction", correct:false,
     why:"A selective capital reduction is also driven by the company and can be used to eliminate a minority, but the classic company-driven, court-sanctioned route for a friendly take-private is the scheme of arrangement."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 13; CA s 210; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Scheme = company-driven, court-sanctioned, friendly only."
},

{ id:"f0x-3-05", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bidder wants to take ListCo private. The founding family (45%) supports the deal, but the board has firmly rejected the bidder's approach and refuses to engage.",
  prompt:"Which route is available to the bidder?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only a takeover offer, since a scheme needs the board to propose it", correct:true,
     why:"A scheme of arrangement is the company's own proposal: the board must apply to court to convene meetings and put the scheme to shareholders. With the board refusing, the bidder cannot use it. A takeover offer is the bidder's own offer to shareholders, so it works without the board's support, which is why the Intro says only an offer works hostile. Here, the family's 45% gives the offer a strong start. If the family accepts and the bidder reaches over 50%, it controls ListCo; to get to 100% it needs 90% of the offer shares for a s 215 squeeze-out. The board still has duties during the offer, including obtaining IFA advice and not frustrating the offer."},
    {t:"A scheme of arrangement, using the family's 45% to force the board to apply to court", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders cannot make the company propose a scheme simply by supporting one. The family could try to change the board through ordinary resolutions, but at 45% it does not have majority control, and in any case that is not a scheme route available to the bidder now."},
    {t:"A selective capital reduction proposed by the bidder", correct:false,
     why:"A capital reduction is a corporate action that the company's board proposes and shareholders approve by special resolution. A bidder cannot propose one for a company it does not control."},
    {t:"No route: a hostile take-private is prohibited in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"Hostile bids attract regulatory scrutiny, which may suggest they are banned. But they are permitted. The Code regulates how offers are made and limits the board's ability to frustrate them. With the family's support, an offer directly to shareholders is open to the bidder."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.4; Wan Ch 2 §E, Ch 13", takeaway:"Hostile board → offer is the only route."
},

{ id:"mx-q-0.3-05", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The voting test for a scheme of arrangement (CA s 210) is…",
  options:[
    {t:"Majority in number + 75% in value, plus court sanction", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. Under s 210(3AB), each class meeting must approve the scheme by a majority in number of the members present and voting (in person or by proxy), who together represent at least three-quarters in value of the shares voted. Both limbs must be met: a headcount test and a value test. Then the court must sanction the scheme, checking that the classes were correctly constituted, the statutory requirements met and the scheme is one an intelligent and honest member of the class could reasonably approve. Getting the classes wrong is fatal, because a vote by the wrong class does not give the court jurisdiction. Wan notes the court may alter the headcount test under s 210(3AB), but the power is expected to be used sparingly."},
    {t:"More than 50% of the shares voted, plus court sanction", correct:false,
     why:"Simple majorities decide most shareholder votes, which makes this sound familiar. But a scheme needs much more: a majority in number of those present and voting, who together hold at least 75% in value of the shares voted, in each class, plus court sanction."},
    {t:"90% of the offer shares, as for a squeeze-out", correct:false,
     why:"90% is a familiar takeover number, which may make it seem the scheme threshold. But 90% of the offer shares is the s 215 squeeze-out threshold for offers. A scheme is approved by the dual test of majority in number and 75% in value, then court sanction."},
    {t:"30% of voting rights", correct:false,
     why:"30% is the best-known control threshold, which may cause the confusion. But it is the Code's mandatory-offer trigger. It has nothing to do with the votes needed to approve a scheme under s 210."}
  ],
  cite:"CA s 210(3AB); Wan Ch 13", takeaway:"Scheme: majority in number + 75% in value of each class voting + court sanction."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-08", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"At a scheme meeting of a single class, 100 shareholders are present and voting. 70 vote in favour, holding 72% of the shares voted; 30 vote against, holding 28%.",
  prompt:"Is the scheme approved?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: headcount passes, but 72% fails the value test", correct:true,
     why:"A scheme needs both limbs of the dual test in each class. The headcount limb is a majority in number of those present and voting: 70 out of 100 is comfortably met. The value limb requires those voting in favour to hold at least 75% of the value of shares voted: 72% falls short. So the resolution fails and the court has nothing to sanction. Notice how close it is: a single holder with 3% of the votes cast switching sides would change the result. This is why bidders seek irrevocable undertakings from key holders before a scheme meeting, and why dissenting minorities with just over 25% of the shares likely to vote can block a scheme."},
    {t:"Yes: a majority in number voted in favour", correct:false,
     why:"A clear majority of holders in favour can feel like approval. But the scheme test has two limbs, and both must be met. The headcount passes with 70 of 100, but those in favour hold only 72% of the shares voted, short of the 75% value limb."},
    {t:"Yes: over 50% by value is enough", correct:false,
     why:"A majority by value would pass an ordinary resolution, which makes this sound sufficient. But a scheme needs at least 75% in value of the shares voted in the class. 72% falls short, so the scheme fails at the meeting."},
    {t:"It depends on the 90% squeeze-out threshold", correct:false,
     why:"90% is the threshold for squeeze-out, which may make it seem relevant to any take-private. But it belongs to the offer route under s 215. A scheme is decided by its own dual test at the class meeting, followed by court sanction."}
  ],
  cite:"CA s 210(3AB); Wan Ch 13; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Both limbs must pass: headcount and 75% value."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-09", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A scheme is supported by 30 institutions holding 92% of the shares voted, but opposed by 70 small holders with 8%. Just before the meeting, one opponent split its holding among 40 friends so they could vote.",
  prompt:"What is the correct analysis?",
  options:[
    {t:"Value passes, headcount fails; s 210(3AB) may let the court adjust it", correct:true,
     why:"The dual test is exposed to share-splitting because the headcount counts holders, not shares: 70 against outnumber 30 in favour, even though the 30 hold 92%. Wan [13.47]–[13.62] notes that s 210(3AB)(a) lets the court order otherwise and alter the headcount requirement, though the power is expected to be used sparingly because the headcount protects small holders. Wan discusses Re PCCW in Hong Kong, where share-splitting to influence a scheme vote was condemned, and a Singapore case in which the court agreed with the chair's decision to disallow votes cast after a share-splitting exercise because the recipients were not voting for the benefit of the class. The 40 manufactured votes are at real risk of being disregarded."},
    {t:"The scheme passes because 92% by value is all that matters", correct:false,
     why:"92% by value is overwhelming support, which makes this answer intuitive. But by default both limbs of the test apply, and the headcount fails. Value alone is enough only if the court exercises its power under s 210(3AB) to alter the headcount requirement."},
    {t:"The scheme fails and nothing can be done", correct:false,
     why:"A failed headcount can look final. But s 210(3AB) lets the court order otherwise and alter the headcount requirement, though sparingly. And Wan [13.47]–[13.62] notes courts have disregarded votes manufactured by share-splitting, which could change the count here."},
    {t:"The opponents' votes are automatically void because they hold less than 1% each", correct:false,
     why:"Small holdings may seem too minor to count. But there is no size floor for voting at a scheme meeting. Small holders count in the headcount, which is why that limb exists, and why the problem here is the manufactured votes, not their size."}
  ],
  cite:"CA s 210(3AB); Wan Ch 13 [13.47]–[13.62]; Intro 2.17 (Ex 3)", takeaway:"Headcount can be split-gamed; the court can alter it and disallow manufactured votes."
},

{ id:"f0-3-01", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq", dia:"schemeVsOffer",
  prompt:"Why does shareholder apathy help a bidder in a SCHEME but hurt a bidder in a takeover OFFER?",
  options:[
    {t:"A scheme counts only votes cast; an offer counts every holder", correct:true,
     why:"In a scheme the question is whether those present and voting clear a majority in number and 75% in value, so shareholders who stay at home simply do not count. Wan [14.57] puts it this way: any opposition to a scheme requires a greater turnout from dissenters. In an offer, the 90% needed for a s 215 squeeze-out is measured against all the shares the offer relates to, so every share that is not tendered counts against the bidder, whether its holder opposes the deal or simply ignored the post. Wan concludes that passivity operates in favour of the controller in a scheme and in favour of dissentients in compulsory acquisition. A target with a large, inactive retail register points strongly towards a scheme."},
    {t:"Schemes require 100% acceptance while offers require only 50%", correct:false,
     why:"A scheme does not need 100% acceptance. It needs the dual test of those voting, plus court sanction, and then binds 100%. An offer needs over 50% to gain control and 90% to squeeze out. The option misstates both."},
    {t:"Apathy is irrelevant in both because the court decides the outcome", correct:false,
     why:"The court's role in schemes can suggest it decides the outcome. But the court sanctions a scheme only after the vote, and does not override it, and it has no role in counting offer acceptances. Apathy matters a great deal, and it cuts in opposite directions in the two routes."},
    {t:"Offers ignore non-acceptances entirely", correct:false,
     why:"Offers count acceptances, which may suggest silence is ignored. But the s 215 threshold is 90% of all the shares the offer relates to. Every share not tendered counts against the bidder, so non-acceptances are exactly what prevents squeeze-out."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2 §E, Ch 13, Ch 14 [14.57]; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Apathy: helps a scheme, hurts an offer."
},

{ id:"f0x-3-06", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A controller holds 60% of ListCo and wants the remaining 40m minority shares. It expects about half the minority to vote or accept, and three-quarters of those who engage to support it. The controller's own shares will not count in the minority's decision.",
  prompt:"On these assumptions, which route is more likely to deliver 100%?",
  options:[
    {t:"A scheme: 75% of votes cast is reachable; 90% of all minority shares is not", correct:true,
     why:"Run the numbers. In a scheme the value test is measured on shares present and voting in the class: if 20m minority shares vote and 15m are in favour, that is 75% and the value limb is met (the headcount must also pass). In an offer, the s 215 squeeze-out needs 90% of the shares the offer relates to: 36m of the 40m. With only 15m acceptances the bidder falls far short and is left with a stranded minority. Wan [14.57] draws exactly this contrast: the scheme's 75% of those voting is 'far less onerous' than 90% of all offeree shares, so passivity favours the controller in a scheme. The flip side is that a controller-led scheme carries fairness scrutiny: correct classes, abstention by interested parties, IFA advice and the court's review."},
    {t:"An offer, because 90% is a lower bar than 75%", correct:false,
     why:"90% is a higher number, and it is measured against all offeree shares rather than only those voting. On these assumptions an offer would fail to reach squeeze-out by a wide margin."},
    {t:"Either route gives the same result because the same shareholders support it", correct:false,
     why:"The same shareholders supporting the deal can seem to guarantee the same result. But the denominators differ: a scheme counts shares voting, while s 215 counts all offer shares. With half the minority not engaging, 75% support among voters clears a scheme but falls far short of 90% of all offer shares."},
    {t:"Neither: a controller cannot take a company private", correct:false,
     why:"Taking a company private removes minorities, which may sound impossible without their consent. But the law provides several routes: an offer with squeeze-out, a scheme, a selective capital reduction or an amalgamation. Each has its own approval bar and protections."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 14 [14.57]; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Scheme: 75% of votes cast. Offer: 90% of all offer shares. Apathy decides."
},

{ id:"f0x-3-07", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bidder's voluntary offer for ListCo closes with the bidder holding 82% of the shares. It had hoped to reach 90% of the offer shares.",
  prompt:"What is the bidder's position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Control, but no s 215 squeeze-out: a stranded minority", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro notes that an offer 'can end with partial control and a stranded minority', unlike a scheme, which is all-or-nothing. At 82% the bidder controls the board and can pass special resolutions on its own, but without 90% of the offer shares it has no s 215 right to buy out the remaining 18%. Those minorities stay, with their rights to fair treatment, to information, and to bring oppression claims. ListCo's public float has also shrunk; under the SGX listing rules a float below 10% can lead to suspension of trading. The bidder's options include living with the minority, making a further offer, or using another route on the minority menu (a scheme, a selective capital reduction), each with its own approval bar and fairness safeguards."},
    {t:"It can squeeze out the minority because it holds more than 75%", correct:false,
     why:"75% lets the bidder pass special resolutions, but squeeze-out under s 215 needs 90% of the shares the offer relates to. Holding 82% overall does not confer a compulsory-acquisition right."},
    {t:"The offer automatically lapses because 90% was not reached", correct:false,
     why:"A voluntary offer lapses only if its conditions are not met. The Code requires only that acceptances take the bidder above 50%; a bidder can set a higher acceptance condition, but then it can lapse at that level. Nothing in the facts says the offer was conditional on 90%."},
    {t:"The minorities are deemed to have accepted", correct:false,
     why:"Schemes bind all holders once approved, which may suggest offers work the same way. But there is no deemed acceptance in an offer. Every holder who does not accept keeps its shares, which is why the bidder at 82% is left with a stranded minority."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.4; Wan Ch 14", takeaway:"Offer short of 90% → control but a stranded minority."
},

{ id:"f0x-3-08", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two weeks before a scheme meeting for ListCo, a rival announces a possible higher bid. The first bidder wants to raise its price quickly to stay ahead.",
  prompt:"How does the choice of route affect the first bidder's ability to respond?",
  options:[
    {t:"Revising a scheme is harder: court timetable and documents must change", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro's comparison table notes that price revision is harder in a scheme: the court has fixed the meeting on the basis of a scheme document, so raising the price may mean issuing supplementary documents, possibly adjourning the meeting and returning to court, all of which takes time while the rival moves. In an offer, a revised offer is 'just an announcement' (subject to the Code timetable and rules on revisions), and the bidder can respond quickly. This is one of the trade-offs in route choice: a scheme gives all-or-nothing certainty and benefits from apathy, but at the cost of needing the board, court time and flexibility. In a contested situation, the flexibility of an offer can be decisive."},
    {t:"A scheme is easier to revise because the court can approve a new price on the spot", correct:false,
     why:"The court does not set or approve the price at short notice. A higher price in a scheme means revised documents and shareholder information, which run through a court-supervised process."},
    {t:"Neither route allows any price revision once announced", correct:false,
     why:"Both allow revisions. The Code regulates the timing and terms of offer revisions, and schemes can be amended with the necessary documents and timing. The difference is speed and complexity."},
    {t:"The route makes no difference; only the rival's price matters", correct:false,
     why:"The route matters for how quickly and cheaply the first bidder can respond. That is why the Intro lists price revision as a point of difference between schemes and offers."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.4 (scheme vs offer table); Wan Ch 2 §E, Ch 13", takeaway:"Contested deal: offers are quicker to bump than schemes."
},

{ id:"ap0-3-11", lesson:"0.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bidder has the support of ListCo's board and wants certainty of getting 100%. ListCo has 8,000 shareholders, many of them small retail holders who rarely vote. The bidder is paying cash and expects no rival.",
  prompt:"Which route fits best, and why?",
  options:[
    {t:"A scheme: board support, all-or-nothing, apathy helps", correct:true,
     why:"Every factor points to a scheme. It needs the board, and the board is supportive. It delivers all-or-nothing certainty: if approved and sanctioned, it binds every holder and the bidder gets 100% in a single stage. The large inactive retail base is the decisive point. In a scheme, holders who do not vote are left out of the count, so the bidder needs only a majority in number and 75% in value of those voting. In an offer, every untendered retail share counts against the 90% needed for squeeze-out. The costs of a scheme (court time, harder to bump the price) matter little where no rival is expected. The bidder must still get the classes right and supply an IFA opinion."},
    {t:"A takeover offer, because offers are always quicker", correct:false,
     why:"An offer may be quicker to revise, but here speed of revision is not needed. With thousands of inactive holders, an offer risks stalling below 90% and leaving a stranded minority, which is the opposite of the certainty the bidder wants."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer, triggered deliberately by buying 30%", correct:false,
     why:"Deliberately triggering a mandatory offer gives the bidder a rigid, cash, all-holders offer with only a 50% condition. It does nothing to solve the apathy problem in reaching 90% and adds inflexibility."},
    {t:"A partial offer for 60%", correct:false,
     why:"A partial offer can be a flexible way to gain control, which may make it seem useful here. But it is designed to acquire less than 100% and needs SIC consent. It cannot deliver the 100% the bidder wants."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.4; Wan Ch 2 §E, Ch 13–14", takeaway:"Friendly + want 100% + apathetic register → scheme."
},

{ id:"mx-q-0.3-06", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under a takeover offer, what proportion of the OFFER shares must a bidder reach to compulsorily acquire (squeeze out) the remaining minority under CA s 215?",
  options:[
    {t:"90% of the offer shares", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. Under s 215, once the bidder has acquired not less than 90% of the shares the offer relates to (excluding shares already held by the bidder, its related corporations and their nominees at the date of the offer), it can give notice to compulsorily acquire the shares of the remaining holders on the same terms. The exclusion matters: shares the bidder already owned do not count towards the 90%, so a bidder starting at 40% needs 90% of the other 60%, not just 90% overall. Dissenting holders can apply to court, but the burden is on them to show the terms are unfair. This is why apathy hurts an offer: every untendered share eats into the 90%."},
    {t:"75% of the issued shares", correct:false,
     why:"75% is an important threshold in schemes and special resolutions, which may cause confusion. But it is not the s 215 squeeze-out level. A bidder needs 90% of the shares the offer relates to before it can compulsorily acquire the rest."},
    {t:"50% plus one share", correct:false,
     why:"Majority control gives the bidder the board, which may feel like enough. But it gives no right to buy out the remaining holders. s 215 requires acceptances for 90% of the offer shares before compulsory acquisition is available."},
    {t:"30% of voting rights", correct:false,
     why:"30% is the best-known Code threshold, which may make it seem relevant. But it is the mandatory-offer trigger, an obligation on the bidder, not a right against the minority. Squeeze-out requires 90% of the offer shares."}
  ],
  cite:"CA s 215; Wan Ch 14", takeaway:"Squeeze-out: 90% of the shares the offer relates to."
},

{ id:"f0-3-06", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A listed company is the BUYER. It makes an acquisition so large relative to its own size that the target effectively reverses into the listed shell. What is this called, and what follows?",
  options:[
    {t:"A reverse takeover (RTO), like a new listing", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro's 'one more public-deal concept' is SGX transaction classification. Even when the listed company is the buyer, the listing rules ask whether the acquisition is big enough relative to the buyer to need its own shareholders' approval. Wan [2.25] summarises the tiers: a major transaction, where a relative figure exceeds 20%, needs shareholder approval; where the relative figure reaches 100%, or there is a change of control of the issuer, the deal is a very substantial acquisition or reverse takeover. In an RTO the target's owners effectively take over the listed shell, so SGX treats it almost like a new listing, with shareholder approval and admission requirements. Chapter 4 covers RTOs in detail."},
    {t:"A mandatory general offer under Rule 14", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14 concerns someone acquiring 30% or more of a Code company's voting rights. It can arise in an RTO if the vendors receive enough new shares to cross 30%, but it is not the name for an oversized acquisition by a listed buyer."},
    {t:"A scheme of arrangement", correct:false,
     why:"Schemes are a common way to effect large combinations, which may make the term seem to fit. But a scheme is a court-sanctioned mechanism for an acquisition or restructuring. The label for a listed buyer's acquisition so large it effectively reverses into the buyer is a reverse takeover under the SGX rules."},
    {t:"A partial offer", correct:false,
     why:"A partial offer is an offer for less than 100% of a target's shares under the Code. It has nothing to do with a listed buyer's acquisition being large relative to itself."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2 [2.25], Ch 6 §D; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Listed buyer + huge target → RTO, treated like a new listing."
},

{ id:"f0-3-07", lesson:"0.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The Intro warns that a controller will pick the route with the lowest approval bar ('structural arbitrage'). What is the lawyer's job in response?",
  options:[
    {t:"Check that the route's own protections were honoured in full", correct:true,
     why:"Structural arbitrage is legitimate. A controller is entitled to choose the route that best suits it, whether a scheme, an offer and squeeze-out, a selective capital reduction or an amalgamation. But each route carries its own safeguards, and the lawyer's job is to check they are genuinely observed. In a scheme that means correct share classes and the court's fairness review. Where the controller is on both sides, it means an independent board committee, an IFA opinion and abstention by the interested controller at the vote. In a capital reduction it means creditor protection and solvency. The Intro calls the recurring trap a controller using a low-bar route while skimping on the scrutiny the minority is owed. Advising on the route and testing its safeguards are both part of the job."},
    {t:"Advise the controller to choose whichever route is cheapest regardless of fairness", correct:false,
     why:"That ignores the safeguards each route carries and invites challenge from minorities, the SIC or the court. A route that is cheap on paper becomes expensive if a court refuses sanction or minorities bring proceedings."},
    {t:"Insist on a mandatory offer in every case", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer arises only when someone crosses 30% or creeps. It is not a default route for every control transaction, and forcing it would ignore schemes, capital reductions and amalgamations."},
    {t:"Refuse to act because route selection is unethical", correct:false,
     why:"Structural arbitrage can sound like exploiting loopholes, which may suggest it is improper. But choosing an efficient route is ordinary, legitimate practice. The lawyer's duty is to make sure the chosen route's safeguards, such as correct classes, abstention and fairness review, are honoured."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2 §E, Ch 14; Intro 2.4, 2.15", takeaway:"Low approval bar ≠ low fairness: test the safeguards."
}

]);

;
