/* DealCraft — Chapter 1 · Lesson 1.8 Warranties, Indemnities & MAC (audited Oct 2026).
   Sources: Intro 2.10–2.11; Wan Ch 5 §C–D; Code r 15.1 notes, App (merger control). */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.f0v2 = (window.DC_BANK.f0v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"f0-8-01", lesson:"0.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the key difference between a WARRANTY and an INDEMNITY?",
  options:[
    {t:"Warranty: a statement giving damages; indemnity: cover for a named risk", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro (2.10) draws the line clearly. A warranty is the seller's statement of fact about the target: 'the accounts are accurate', 'there is no undisclosed litigation', 'the company owns its IP'. If it is untrue and the buyer suffers loss, the buyer has a damages claim, but it must prove breach and loss, and the ordinary contract rules apply (causation, remoteness, mitigation), along with the SPA's caps, baskets and time limits. An indemnity is a promise to reimburse a specific, identified exposure dollar for dollar: 'if the clean-up at the Jurong site costs anything, the seller pays it'. The buyer need not prove a breach or run the damages rules; it shows the loss fell within the indemnity. That is why known risks found in diligence go to indemnities, and unknown risks to warranties."},
    {t:"They are two words for the same thing", correct:false,
     why:"They are different tools. A warranty gives a breach-based damages claim with all the contract-law hurdles; an indemnity gives direct cover for a defined risk. Confusing them leads to the wrong protection for the risk."},
    {t:"A warranty covers only tax; an indemnity covers everything else", correct:false,
     why:"Neither is limited by subject matter. Warranties cover many facts about the target, including tax. Indemnities cover specific identified risks, which may be tax (a tax deed is a tax indemnity) or something else."},
    {t:"An indemnity requires proving breach, causation and remoteness; a warranty does not", correct:false,
     why:"Indemnities can feel like the more demanding tool because they give stronger protection. But the hurdles run the other way. A warranty claimant must prove breach, causation, remoteness and mitigation, subject to caps and baskets. An indemnity is designed to avoid that: the buyer shows the loss falls within the indemnified risk and recovers it pound-for-pound."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10", takeaway:"Warranty = statement of fact → damages. Indemnity = pound-for-pound cover for a named risk."
},

{ id:"f0x-8-01", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The seller's counsel objects to a litigation warranty: 'Why do you need it? We will tell you about every claim anyway.'",
  prompt:"What is the best explanation of why the buyer still wants the warranty?",
  options:[
    {t:"Warranties allocate unknown risk and force the seller to disclose", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro (2.10) says warranties 'do double duty: they allocate risk and they flush out information, because to avoid breaching them the seller must disclose the true position'. A promise to 'tell you about every claim' is informal and carries no remedy if the seller forgets or chooses not to. A warranty that there is no litigation, combined with the disclosure letter, gives the seller a strong reason to search its records and disclose every claim, and if something undisclosed later emerges, the buyer has a damages claim. The warranty asks the question, the disclosure letter gives the honest answer, and the residual undisclosed risk stays with the seller. That is the mechanism the seller's counsel's offer leaves out."},
    {t:"The warranty is pointless if the seller will disclose everything, so the buyer should drop it", correct:false,
     why:"That misses the point of warranties. Without the warranty, the seller has no contractual reason to disclose fully and no liability if it does not. The warranty is what makes disclosure reliable."},
    {t:"The warranty lets the buyer claim even for claims the seller discloses", correct:false,
     why:"A warranty sounds like a guarantee against the risk, so it may seem to cover disclosed claims too. But properly disclosed matters are carved out of the warranty they qualify. If the buyer wants protection for a lawsuit it already knows about, it needs a specific indemnity, a retention or a price adjustment."},
    {t:"The warranty means the buyer does not need to do diligence", correct:false,
     why:"Warranties shift risk to the seller, which may make diligence seem less necessary. But a warranty claim gives damages after the event, subject to caps, time limits and proof. Diligence finds problems before the price is fixed, so the buyer can price them, make them conditions or take indemnities."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10", takeaway:"Warranties allocate risk and flush out disclosure."
},

{ id:"f0-8-02", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario", dia:"fourTools",
  scenario:"DD reveals a pending lawsuit against the target of uncertain size.",
  prompt:"The Intro shows the SAME risk handled four ways. Which set correctly names all four?",
  options:[
    {t:"A warranty, a specific indemnity, a condition, or a price adjustment", correct:true,
     why:"This is the Intro's signature exercise (2.10): one risk, four tools. As a warranty, 'there is no litigation other than as disclosed' mainly catches undisclosed claims; once this suit is disclosed, the warranty no longer covers it. As a specific indemnity, the seller covers all losses from this lawsuit pound-for-pound, whatever the outcome. As a condition, if the suit threatens the business itself, completion can be made conditional on its settlement or dismissal. As a price tool, the expected exposure can be taken off the price or held in escrow pending the outcome. The Intro's point is that the right tool depends on how certain, how large and how fixable the risk is, and that naming the tool, rather than merely spotting 'there's a lawsuit', is the advice."},
    {t:"As a warranty, a restrictive covenant, a rollover and a poison pill", correct:false,
     why:"Each of these is a real deal term, which is what makes the list plausible. But a restrictive covenant stops the seller competing, a rollover concerns management equity, and a poison pill is a US takeover defence. None of them allocates the risk of a pending lawsuit, which the Intro (2.10) handles by warranty, indemnity, condition or price."},
    {t:"As a scheme, an offer, an amalgamation and a capital reduction", correct:false,
     why:"These are all ways of structuring a transaction, so they can sound like 'ways of handling' a deal. But they are routes for acquiring or restructuring a company. Once a route is chosen, the risk of a specific lawsuit is allocated within the deal by warranty, indemnity, condition or price."},
    {t:"As stamp duty, GST, withholding tax and a tax deed", correct:false,
     why:"A tax deed is a specific indemnity, so this list contains one genuine tool. But the others are tax overlays, and the list as a whole is about tax, not risk allocation for litigation. The Intro's four-way framework for any risk is warranty, specific indemnity, condition and price or retention."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10", takeaway:"Same risk, four tools: warranty · indemnity · condition · price."
},

{ id:"d1-013", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario", dia:"fourTools",
  scenario:"DD reveals a specific, quantified environmental clean-up obligation at one site of about S$2m that is virtually certain to be incurred.",
  prompt:"Which tool fits this KNOWN, quantifiable exposure best?",
  options:[
    {t:"A specific indemnity for the clean-up cost", correct:true,
     why:"When a liability is specific, identified and quantifiable, the precise tool is a specific indemnity. It is the Intro's own example (2.10): 'if the clean-up at the Jurong site costs anything, the seller pays it, dollar for dollar'. A warranty would not work, because the seller will disclose the clean-up obligation and so carve it out. Because the cost is close to certain and roughly S$2m, the parties may instead simply reduce the price by that amount, or combine an indemnity with an escrow so the money is available when the clean-up happens. The indemnity should sit outside the general warranty cap, basket and short time limits, and should say who controls the clean-up and how costs are verified."},
    {t:"A general warranty that there are no environmental liabilities", correct:false,
     why:"An environmental warranty sounds like the obvious protection for an environmental problem. But the seller will disclose the known clean-up obligation against it, which carves it out. The buyer would be left with a near-certain S$2m cost and no claim, which is why known exposures go to specific indemnities."},
    {t:"Nothing: environmental risk is uninsurable", correct:false,
     why:"Insurability is beside the point. The deal tool for a known exposure is an indemnity, an escrow or a price cut. Doing nothing leaves a near-certain cost with the buyer."},
    {t:"A restrictive covenant", correct:false,
     why:"Restrictive covenants appear in most SPAs, so they can seem like general protection. But a non-compete or non-solicit protects the goodwill from competition by the seller. It has nothing to do with paying for a clean-up obligation the target already owes."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.6, 2.10", takeaway:"Known, quantified exposure → specific indemnity (or price cut / escrow)."
},

{ id:"f0-8-03", lesson:"0.8", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each limitation-on-liability term to what it does.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Cap", r:"Maximum total warranty liability"},
    {l:"Basket / threshold", r:"Claims only 'tip in' once aggregate losses exceed a floor"},
    {l:"De minimis", r:"Screens out trivial individual claims"},
    {l:"Time limit", r:"Window within which claims must be brought"}
  ],
  why:"Warranties are not unlimited; the limitations package (clause 7 of the Intro's SPA skeleton) contains the seller's exposure. A cap sets the maximum total liability, often a percentage of the price for general warranties, and up to the full price for title or other fundamental warranties. A basket or threshold means no claims can be brought until aggregate losses pass a floor; a 'tipping' basket then lets the whole amount be claimed, while a 'deductible' basket allows only the excess. A de minimis screens out individual claims below a set size, which then do not count towards the basket either. Time limits require claims to be notified within a window, commonly longer for tax and title than for general commercial warranties. Each is a point on the negotiation axis.",
  cite:"Intro 2.10", takeaway:"Cap = ceiling · basket = aggregate floor · de minimis = per-claim floor · time limit = window."
},

{ id:"ap0-8-01", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"SPA: price S$50m; cap 30% of price; de minimis S$50k per claim; 'tipping' basket of S$500k. After completion the buyer has three warranty claims: S$40k, S$300k and S$400k.",
  prompt:"How much can the buyer recover?",
  options:[
    {t:"S$700k: the S$40k claim is below de minimis", correct:true,
     why:"Apply the limitations in order. First the de minimis: individual claims below S$50k are screened out entirely, so the S$40k claim is ignored and does not count towards the basket. Second the basket: the remaining claims total S$300k + S$400k = S$700k, which exceeds the S$500k floor. Because it is a tipping basket, once the floor is passed the whole S$700k is recoverable, not just the S$200k excess. Third the cap: 30% of S$50m is S$15m, far above S$700k, so it does not bite. The buyer recovers S$700k, assuming each claim is proved and brought within the time limit. With a deductible basket, the answer would have been S$200k."},
    {t:"S$740k", correct:false,
     why:"Adding all three claims is the natural first step, which gives S$740k. But the de minimis screens out any individual claim below S$50k, so the S$40k claim drops out entirely. Claims below the de minimis are usually also excluded when testing whether the basket has been passed."},
    {t:"S$200k", correct:false,
     why:"S$200k is the excess over the S$500k basket, which is the right answer if the basket is a deductible. But the facts say it is a tipping basket: once aggregate claims pass the threshold, the whole amount is recoverable from the first dollar, so the buyer recovers S$700k."},
    {t:"Nothing: the claims are under the S$15m cap", correct:false,
     why:"Being under the cap can be misread as meaning nothing is payable. But the cap is a ceiling on total liability, not a floor. Claims below it are recoverable in full, subject to the de minimis and basket, and the cap only bites if claims exceed S$15m."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10 [practice]", takeaway:"Apply de minimis → basket (tipping or deductible) → cap."
},

{ id:"f0x-8-02", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The SPA has a general warranty cap of 20% of the S$60m price and an 18-month claim period, but a 'fundamental warranties' cap of 100% and a 5-year tax claim period. Two years after completion, the buyer discovers (1) the seller never owned 10% of the shares it sold, and (2) a commercial contract warranty was untrue.",
  prompt:"What is the likely position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Title claim likely in time; contract claim out of time", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro (2.10) notes that caps are often a percentage of the price for general warranties but the full price for title or fundamental warranties, and that time limits are commonly longer for tax and title than for general commercial warranties. That split is exactly this SPA. A seller that did not own 10% of the shares it sold has breached the most basic promise in the deal: that it owns what it is selling. That is a fundamental warranty, capped at 100% of the price and usually given a long claim period. The commercial contract warranty is a general warranty with an 18-month window; at two years, the buyer is out of time, however good the claim. The lesson for buyers is to diarise claim deadlines and notify early."},
    {t:"Both claims are capped at S$12m and both are in time", correct:false,
     why:"A single cap and claim period for all warranties would make the analysis simpler, which is why this answer is tempting. But this SPA, like many, separates general warranties (20% cap, 18 months) from fundamental warranties such as title (100% cap, longer period). The commercial claim is also out of time at two years."},
    {t:"Both claims are barred because two years have passed", correct:false,
     why:"Two years is past the 18-month general period, so it may seem every claim is out of time. But different warranties can have different periods. Title is a fundamental warranty, commonly given a much longer period, so the title claim may still be in time."},
    {t:"Neither matters because warranties expire at completion", correct:false,
     why:"Completion can feel like the end of the seller's exposure. But warranties are given at signing, often repeated at completion, and claims can be brought afterwards within the periods the SPA sets. They are designed to survive completion; otherwise they would protect the buyer against almost nothing."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10", takeaway:"Title/fundamental: higher cap, longer period. General: lower cap, shorter window."
},

{ id:"f0-8-04", lesson:"0.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"'Anti-sandbagging' vs 'pro-sandbagging': what is the question, and which is seller-friendly?",
  options:[
    {t:"Whether known breaches can be claimed; 'anti' is seller-friendly", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro (2.10) calls sandbagging a recurring flashpoint, sharpest in auctions. The question is whether a buyer that knew at signing that a warranty was untrue can complete and then sue on it. A pro-sandbagging clause says yes: the buyer bargained for the warranty and can rely on it whatever it knew. That is buyer-friendly. An anti-sandbagging clause says no: the buyer cannot claim for a breach it knew about, because it should have negotiated a price cut or an indemnity instead. That is seller-friendly. Many SPAs say nothing, or say knowledge only bars a claim where the matter was fairly disclosed. The practical lesson for the buyer is the same under any clause: deal with known problems specifically, not by relying on general warranties."},
    {t:"Whether the buyer can walk away under a MAC clause; anti-sandbagging is seller-friendly", correct:false,
     why:"Both sandbagging and MAC clauses deal with bad news the buyer learns about, which invites confusion. But a MAC concerns walking away before completion after a serious adverse change. Sandbagging concerns whether a buyer that knew of a warranty breach at signing can still claim for it after completion."},
    {t:"Whether the seller can shop the deal to rivals; pro-sandbagging is seller-friendly", correct:false,
     why:"Both topics involve one side gaining an advantage at the other's expense. But shopping the deal is governed by no-shop and exclusivity clauses. Sandbagging is about warranty claims on breaches the buyer already knew about, and pro-sandbagging favours the buyer, not the seller."},
    {t:"Whether stamp duty applies to a cancellation scheme; both are buyer-friendly", correct:false,
     why:"Technical-sounding terms can be confused with tax concepts. But sandbagging has nothing to do with stamp duty or schemes. It is the question whether a buyer's prior knowledge of a breach bars its warranty claim: anti-sandbagging (seller-friendly) says yes, pro-sandbagging (buyer-friendly) says no."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10, glossary 'Anti-sandbagging'", takeaway:"Anti-sandbagging (seller) = known breach, no claim. Pro (buyer) = claim anyway."
},

{ id:"ap0-8-02", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"DD revealed that the target had under-declared GST last year. The buyer signed anyway, relying on the general tax warranty. The SPA contains an anti-sandbagging clause.",
  prompt:"What is the buyer's position, and what should it have done?",
  options:[
    {t:"Probably no claim for a known breach; it needed an indemnity", correct:true,
     why:"An anti-sandbagging clause, which is seller-friendly, bars claims for breaches the buyer knew about at signing. The buyer's own diligence found the GST under-declaration, so it knew. Even without the clause, the seller would probably have disclosed the issue against the tax warranty, which would have carved it out. The buyer made the classic mistake of leaving a known problem to a general warranty. Known issues found in diligence belong in specific instruments: a specific indemnity (here, within a tax deed) so the seller pays any GST, penalties and interest pound-for-pound, a retention to secure it, or simply a reduction in price. Under the Intro's triage, a known, quantifiable liability is an indemnity point."},
    {t:"It can claim in full, because warranties always survive knowledge", correct:false,
     why:"Warranties are often said to give protection whatever the buyer knew, which is the pro-sandbagging view. But this SPA contains an anti-sandbagging clause, which bars claims for breaches the buyer knew of at signing. The buyer's own diligence found the GST issue, so the clause applies."},
    {t:"It can rescind the SPA", correct:false,
     why:"Rescission undoes a contract, which can sound like a strong remedy for a problem. But it is available for misrepresentation, where a party was misled into contracting. A buyer that discovered the issue in diligence and signed anyway was not misled, so rescission is not available."},
    {t:"The MAC clause covers it", correct:false,
     why:"The MAC clause is the buyer's general exit, which may make it seem relevant. But it addresses serious adverse changes between signing and completion. A known historic GST under-declaration is neither new nor a change in the gap, and the buyer has already completed."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10", takeaway:"Known DD finding → indemnity or price, never just the general warranty."
},

{ id:"f0x-8-03", lesson:"0.8", type:"match",
  prompt:"On the Intro's 'negotiation axis', match each position to the party it favours.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Longer warranty claim periods and higher caps", r:"Buyer"},
    {l:"A MAC with carve-outs for market-wide and industry-wide events", r:"Seller"},
    {l:"A 'hell-or-high-water' efforts obligation on the buyer", r:"Seller"},
    {l:"Pro-sandbagging", r:"Buyer"}
  ],
  why:"The Intro (2.10) describes the negotiation axis that runs through almost every deal. The buyer wants more and broader warranties, wider indemnities, tighter conditions, a stronger MAC, higher caps and longer claim periods. The seller wants fewer, capped, time-limited warranties, narrow indemnities, certainty of completion and a clean exit. Longer periods and higher caps favour the buyer. A MAC narrowed by market-wide and industry-wide carve-outs favours the seller, because only target-specific harm lets the buyer walk. Hell-or-high-water is the most buyer-unfriendly efforts standard, so it favours the seller: the buyer must accept any remedy a regulator demands. Pro-sandbagging lets the buyer claim even for breaches it knew about, so it favours the buyer. Knowing where each term sits tells you what to push for.",
  cite:"Intro 2.10–2.11", takeaway:"Buyer wants more cover; seller wants certainty and a clean exit."
},

{ id:"f0-8-05", lesson:"0.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which pairing correctly distinguishes MANDATORY from PROTECTIVE conditions?",
  options:[
    {t:"Mandatory: imposed by law; protective: for the buyer, waivable", correct:true,
     why:"Wan Ch 5 §C–D and the Intro (2.11) distinguish the two. Mandatory conditions are things the law or a regulator requires before the deal can lawfully complete: merger clearance where filed, a sector regulator's approval such as MAS, an FDI approval under SIRA. The parties cannot agree to skip them, because the requirement does not come from the contract. Protective conditions are negotiated by the buyer for its own benefit: no MAC, warranties true at completion, a key customer's consent, financing available. Because they exist to protect the buyer, the buyer can usually choose to waive them and complete anyway. The distinction matters in practice: when a deal stalls, the first question is which conditions can be waived and by whom."},
    {t:"Mandatory = waivable by the buyer; protective = required by law", correct:false,
     why:"The two labels are easy to swap. But mandatory conditions are imposed by law or a regulator, such as MAS approval of a controller, and cannot be waived by the parties. Protective conditions are negotiated by the buyer for its own benefit, so the buyer can usually waive them."},
    {t:"Both types can always be waived by either party", correct:false,
     why:"Flexibility seems commercially sensible, which makes this option attractive. But mandatory conditions do not come from the contract, so the parties have no power to waive them. Protective conditions can usually be waived only by the party they are designed to protect."},
    {t:"Neither type can ever be waived", correct:false,
     why:"Conditions are gates, which may suggest they can never be bypassed. But protective conditions exist precisely so that the buyer can decide whether to rely on them or proceed anyway. Only mandatory conditions, imposed by law or a regulator, are beyond the parties' power to waive."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 5 §C–D; Intro 2.11", takeaway:"Mandatory (law/regulator): no waiver. Protective (buyer's): buyer can waive."
},

{ id:"ap0-8-04", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Completion is conditional on (i) MAS approval of the buyer as controller and (ii) the target's warranties being true at completion. One warranty has become slightly untrue, and MAS has not yet approved. The buyer wants to close now.",
  prompt:"What can it do?",
  options:[
    {t:"Waive the warranty condition only", correct:true,
     why:"Protective conditions are negotiated for the buyer and are usually waivable by it. The warranty-accuracy condition protects the buyer, so the buyer can decide that a slight inaccuracy does not matter and waive it, usually while reserving any damages claim. Mandatory conditions are different. MAS approval of a new controller is required by statute; becoming a controller of a regulated institution without it would be unlawful, whatever the SPA says. So the buyer must wait for MAS. This is why regulatory approvals typically set the clock for completion, as the Intro's method (2.16, step 5) notes, and why the long-stop date must allow for them."},
    {t:"It can waive both conditions", correct:false,
     why:"The buyer controls its own conditions, so it may seem it can waive anything. But MAS approval of a new controller is a statutory requirement, not a protection the buyer negotiated. Completing without it would be unlawful, whatever the SPA says, so the buyer must wait."},
    {t:"It can waive neither condition", correct:false,
     why:"Both conditions appear in the same clause, which may suggest they are equally binding. But the warranty condition exists for the buyer's protection, so it can usually waive it, often reserving a damages claim. Only the MAS approval is beyond its power to waive."},
    {t:"The seller alone decides both", correct:false,
     why:"Sellers sometimes control parts of the completion process, which may cause this confusion. But protective conditions are generally for the buyer, as the party they protect, to waive. Regulatory approval is for the regulator, and neither party can dispense with it."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 5 §C–D; Intro 2.11", takeaway:"Waive your own protections; never a regulator's approval."
},

{ id:"f0x-8-04", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bidder wants to make a voluntary offer for ListCo, a direct competitor. It does not want to be bound to a firm offer until it knows whether the CCCS will clear the combination.",
  prompt:"Which structure does the Code allow?",
  options:[
    {t:"A pre-conditional offer, subject to an objective CCCS pre-condition", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro (2.11) explains that pre-conditions sit earlier than ordinary conditions: they must be satisfied before the deal is signed or the offer is made, and are used when a clearance must come first. The Code allows a pre-conditional voluntary offer, and its appendix on merger control specifically contemplates a pre-condition that the CCCS issues a favourable decision. The Notes on Rule 15 set the safeguards: the pre-conditions must be clearly stated in the announcement, objective and reasonable, with a reasonable period for fulfilment failing which the offer lapses; and the bidder cannot rely on a pre-condition to let the offer lapse unless it has made reasonable efforts to fulfil it. The same idea applies to mandatory offers through a pre-conditional share purchase agreement."},
    {t:"Announce a firm offer and simply withdraw it if CCCS objects", correct:false,
     why:"Withdrawing an announced offer may seem a simple escape. But under the Code a firm offer generally cannot be withdrawn at will. The bidder needs an objective, properly framed condition or pre-condition, announced in advance, such as a favourable CCCS decision as a pre-condition."},
    {t:"Make the offer conditional on the bidder's own view that the deal still makes sense", correct:false,
     why:"Bidders understandably want flexibility to drop a deal that no longer makes sense. But Rule 15.1 forbids voluntary offer conditions that depend on the offeror's subjective judgement or are within its control. Conditions must be objective, so that shareholders know what the offer depends on."},
    {t:"Nothing can be done: the Code forbids regulatory conditions", correct:false,
     why:"The Code's strictness about conditions may suggest it bars regulatory ones. But it expressly accommodates regulatory clearances, including merger control, through offer conditions and pre-conditional offers. The constraint is that conditions must be objective and not within the bidder's control."}
  ],
  cite:"Code r 15.1 & Notes, App (merger control); Intro 2.11; Wan Ch 5", takeaway:"Need clearance before committing → pre-conditional offer."
},

{ id:"f0-8-06", lesson:"0.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A deal needs approvals that take time and might not come. What is a 'hell-or-high-water' standard?",
  options:[
    {t:"The buyer must accept whatever remedy a regulator demands", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro (2.11) lists three tools for allocating regulatory risk: an efforts standard, a long-stop date and a reverse break fee. The efforts standard answers how hard the buyer must try to get clearance. At the buyer-friendly end is 'reasonable endeavours', under which the buyer need not take steps against its own commercial interests. At the other end is 'hell-or-high-water': the buyer must do whatever it takes, including accepting any remedy, divestment or behavioural commitment the regulator demands. A seller with a deal that raises competition concerns will push hard for it, because it shifts almost all regulatory risk to the buyer. Buyers try to cap it, for example by excluding divestments above a certain size."},
    {t:"A clause letting the buyer walk if the market falls", correct:false,
     why:"Hell-or-high-water sounds like a phrase about adverse events, which is why this option is tempting. But walking away on adverse change is the role of a MAC, and most MACs exclude market-wide falls. Hell-or-high-water is an efforts standard: how far the buyer must go to obtain regulatory clearance."},
    {t:"A fee the seller pays if it accepts a rival bid", correct:false,
     why:"Fees and regulatory risk are both part of the Intro's gap-risk toolkit, which can blur them. But a break fee is paid by the seller or target if it accepts a rival bid or the deal fails for defined reasons. Hell-or-high-water governs the buyer's obligation to accept remedies to get clearance."},
    {t:"A promise that financing is committed and near-unconditional", correct:false,
     why:"Certain funds and hell-or-high-water both make completion more certain for the seller, which invites confusion. But certain funds concerns committed financing. Hell-or-high-water concerns regulatory approvals: the buyer must accept any remedy or divestment a regulator demands."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 5; Intro 2.11", takeaway:"Hell-or-high-water: buyer accepts any remedy needed for clearance."
},

{ id:"ap0-8-05", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"CCCS signals it will clear a merger only if the buyer divests three overlapping sites. The buyer would rather walk away.",
  prompt:"How does the efforts standard in the SPA change the answer?",
  options:[
    {t:"Hell-or-high-water obliges it; reasonable endeavours likely not", correct:true,
     why:"The efforts clause is precisely what decides this. Under hell-or-high-water, the most buyer-unfriendly standard, the buyer has promised to accept any remedy the regulator demands, so it must divest the three sites and complete; refusing would be a breach, and the seller could claim damages or a reverse break fee. Under reasonable endeavours, the buyer generally does not have to accept remedies that materially damage the deal it bargained for, so it may be able to let the deal lapse at the long-stop date, possibly paying a reverse break fee if the SPA provides one. Many SPAs sit between the two, for example obliging the buyer to accept divestments below a defined revenue threshold. Chapter 8 covers remedies in detail."},
    {t:"The efforts standard is irrelevant once CCCS has spoken", correct:false,
     why:"The CCCS decision may feel final, leaving nothing for the contract to do. But the CCCS decides only what remedy it will accept to clear the merger. Whether the buyer is contractually obliged to accept that remedy, or can let the deal lapse, depends on the efforts clause."},
    {t:"Under either standard the buyer can walk away freely", correct:false,
     why:"Buyers often assume they keep a right to walk if conditions are not met. But hell-or-high-water removes that freedom: the buyer has promised to accept any remedy needed for clearance. Only under a weaker standard such as reasonable endeavours is the buyer likely to be able to refuse the divestment."},
    {t:"The seller must divest its own sites instead", correct:false,
     why:"Divesting the seller's sites might seem a way to solve the overlap. But remedies concern the merged business, and the efforts clause governs the buyer's obligation. Nothing in the SPA obliges the seller to divest, and the seller's sites are what the buyer is acquiring."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.11; Chapter 8", takeaway:"The efforts clause decides whether the buyer must swallow a remedy."
},

{ id:"d1-015", lesson:"0.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why are MAC (material adverse change) clauses so rarely invoked successfully in practice?",
  options:[
    {t:"Narrow definitions and market-wide carve-outs", correct:true,
     why:"A MAC lets a buyer walk or decline to complete if something seriously bad happens to the target in the gap. The Intro (2.11) explains why it is hard to use. Sellers resist broad MACs and push for carve-outs: changes caused by general economic or market conditions, or by events affecting the whole industry, do not count, so only target-specific harm qualifies. Courts and tribunals have also generally required the change to be material in a significant, lasting sense, not a short-term dip. The Intro adds that MACs are nonetheless important: their presence shapes behaviour and gives the buyer leverage to renegotiate if things deteriorate. Buyers who expect a specific risk to materialise should use a specific condition instead."},
    {t:"MAC clauses are prohibited by the Companies Act", correct:false,
     why:"MAC clauses are rarely used successfully, which can suggest they are not allowed. But they are lawful and common. They are hard to invoke because sellers negotiate narrow definitions with carve-outs for market and industry events, and because courts require a significant, lasting change."},
    {t:"Buyers can invoke them freely, so they are used constantly", correct:false,
     why:"Buyers do negotiate MACs as an exit, which might suggest frequent use. But successful invocation is rare. The Intro (2.11) notes that MACs matter mainly as leverage to renegotiate, because carve-outs and the materiality threshold make walking away difficult."},
    {t:"Because a MAC automatically increases the price", correct:false,
     why:"Some deal clauses adjust price, which may cause the confusion. But a MAC is a walk-away right or a condition. It does not change the price automatically, although a buyer may use the threat of invoking it to renegotiate the price."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10–2.11", takeaway:"MACs: hard to invoke, but they create leverage."
},

{ id:"ap0-8-03", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Between signing and completion, a new regulation hits every operator in the target's industry, cutting sector profits by 15%. The SPA MAC clause excludes changes 'affecting the industry generally'.",
  prompt:"Can the buyer walk away?",
  options:[
    {t:"Unlikely: industry-wide change is carved out", correct:true,
     why:"Sellers negotiate MAC carve-outs for market-wide and industry-wide events precisely for this situation. A regulation that hits every operator is the textbook example of a change 'affecting the industry generally', so it falls outside the MAC. The buyer could argue only if the clause carves back changes that affect the target disproportionately compared with its peers, and if the target has been hit much harder than the industry. Even then, a 15% sector-wide fall may not be material in the durable sense MAC clauses require. The buyer's leverage is limited to commercial renegotiation. This shows why the Intro (2.11) says MACs are rarely invoked successfully, and why buyers worried about a specific regulatory change should negotiate a specific condition."},
    {t:"Yes: any 15% fall in profit is a MAC", correct:false,
     why:"A 15% fall sounds material, which makes this answer tempting. But MACs are interpreted strictly and require a significant, lasting change, and this SPA carves out changes affecting the industry generally. A regulation hitting every operator is the classic carve-out case."},
    {t:"Yes, via the bring-down of warranties", correct:false,
     why:"A bring-down re-tests warranties at completion, so it may seem a way round the MAC carve-out. But it only helps if a particular warranty becomes untrue. A new industry-wide regulation does not usually make target-specific warranties untrue, and any 'no material adverse change' warranty would raise the same carve-out argument."},
    {t:"Only if the SIC consents", correct:false,
     why:"The SIC regulates walk-away rights in public offers, which may explain this option. But this is a private SPA, so the Code does not apply. Whether the buyer can walk depends on the contract's MAC definition and its carve-outs."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10–2.11", takeaway:"Industry-wide hits fall in the MAC carve-out."
},

{ id:"f0x-8-05", lesson:"0.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A voluntary cash offer for ListCo is conditional on no material adverse change. Two weeks in, the stock market falls 12% and ListCo's share price falls with it. ListCo's business is otherwise unchanged. The bidder wants to invoke the MAC condition and let the offer lapse.",
  prompt:"What does the Code say?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only for circumstances of material significance; consult the SIC", correct:true,
     why:"Public offers add a layer to the MAC analysis. The Notes on Rule 15.1 say an offeror should not invoke any condition, other than the acceptance condition, so as to cause the offer to lapse unless the circumstances giving rise to the right are of material significance to the offeror in the context of the offer, and information about them was not available from public records or known to the offeror before the offer. A general market fall that leaves ListCo's business unchanged is not a target-specific change of material significance; it is the kind of risk a bidder takes when it announces an offer. The SIC, not the bidder alone, polices this. The point mirrors the Intro's observation that MACs are rarely successfully invoked, with the Code making it harder still."},
    {t:"The bidder can invoke the MAC condition freely because it negotiated it", correct:false,
     why:"Negotiated conditions are normally enforceable as written, which makes this answer feel right. But in a Code offer, the Notes on Rule 15.1 say a condition (other than acceptance) may not be invoked to lapse the offer unless the circumstances are of material significance to the offeror in the context of the offer. A general market fall rarely qualifies."},
    {t:"The bidder must reduce the offer price to reflect the market fall", correct:false,
     why:"Adjusting the price to reflect new market conditions may seem fair. But once a firm offer is announced, the bidder cannot reduce its price. Its choices are to proceed or, only if a condition is properly invoked with the SIC's agreement, to let the offer lapse."},
    {t:"The Code prohibits any MAC condition in an offer", correct:false,
     why:"Because invoking a MAC condition is so restricted, it may seem the condition is banned. But MAC conditions are permitted in voluntary offers if objective. The Code limits when they can be relied on to lapse the offer, through the material-significance test, not whether they can be included."}
  ],
  cite:"Code r 15.1 Notes; Intro 2.11; Wan Ch 5", takeaway:"Code offers: conditions lapse an offer only on material significance; market falls rarely qualify."
}

]);

