{ id:"f0-11-02", lesson:"0.11", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Post-2014, what is the SCOPE of s 76: which companies does it catch?",
  options:[
    {t:"Public companies and those with a public holding company", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.61] explains that s 76 used to apply to both public and private companies, but the Companies (Amendment) Act 2014 limited it to (i) a public company and (ii) a company whose holding company or ultimate holding company is a public company. So a private company is entirely outside the rule as long as no public company sits above it. That is why the Intro (2.15) says the first question in any LBO hypo is: is there a public company anywhere in the acquisition chain? Note that 'public' includes unlisted public companies, not just listed ones, and that the test looks at the chain at the time the assistance is given, which is what makes post-take-private structuring possible."},
    {t:"Every company incorporated in Singapore, without exception", correct:false,
     why:"This was close to the law before 2014, when s 76 applied to private as well as public companies, so older materials may suggest it. But Wan [15.61] explains that the Companies (Amendment) Act 2014 limited the rule to public companies and companies whose holding or ultimate holding company is public. A private company with no public parent is now outside it."},
    {t:"Only companies listed on SGX", correct:false,
     why:"Listed companies are the most obvious public companies, so this narrower reading is tempting. But s 76 catches any public company, listed or not, and any company whose holding or ultimate holding company is public. A private subsidiary of a listed group, or of an unlisted public company, is within scope."},
    {t:"Only foreign-incorporated companies", correct:false,
     why:"Cross-border LBOs often involve foreign parents, which may cause the confusion. But s 76 is a Singapore Companies Act rule about Singapore companies giving assistance. The question is whether the company is public or has a public holding or ultimate holding company, not where anyone is incorporated."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F [15.61]; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"s 76 scope: public company, or one with a public (ultimate) parent."
},

{ id:"d1-016", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario", dia:"s76test",
  scenario:"BidCo will borrow to buy 100% of TargetCo's shares. TargetCo is a private company with NO public company anywhere in its group, before or after the deal, and lenders want security over TargetCo's assets.",
  prompt:"Does s 76 bite?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: a private company with no public parent is outside s 76", correct:true,
     why:"The first question in any LBO financial-assistance analysis is whether a public company sits anywhere in the chain, because since 2014 s 76 applies only to public companies and companies whose holding or ultimate holding company is public. TargetCo is private, and neither its current group nor BidCo's group contains a public company, so the prohibition does not apply. TargetCo can give security for BidCo's acquisition debt without a whitewash. That does not make the directors' decision free of all constraints: they must still act in TargetCo's interests and consider its solvency and its creditors, and the security must be properly authorised. But the specific s 76 machinery of exceptions and whitewashes is not needed."},
    {t:"Yes: s 76 always applies whenever a target secures acquisition debt", correct:false,
     why:"Security over a target's assets for acquisition debt is the classic form of financial assistance, so it is natural to assume s 76 always bites. But since 2014 s 76 only applies where the company is public or has a public holding or ultimate holding company. With no public company in the chain, TargetCo is outside the rule."},
    {t:"Yes, but only because the buyer is a shell (BidCo)", correct:false,
     why:"Shell buyers are typical of leveraged deals, so it may seem the shell creates the problem. But the identity of the buyer does not trigger s 76. The rule turns on whether the company giving assistance is public or sits under a public company, and here none is in the chain."},
    {t:"It cannot be determined without knowing the interest rate", correct:false,
     why:"Debt terms affect whether the lending is commercially sensible, which may make the interest rate seem relevant. But s 76's scope is a structural question about whether a public company is in the chain. The rate has nothing to do with whether the prohibition applies."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F [15.61]; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"No public company in the chain → s 76 doesn't apply."
},

{ id:"ap0-11-01", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Project Sprouts: Little Sprouts is private. BrightPath is private but is a subsidiary of an SGX-listed group. After completion, the lenders want Little Sprouts to guarantee BrightPath's acquisition loan (a debt push-down).",
  prompt:"Is s 76 engaged?",
  options:[
    {t:"Likely yes: its ultimate holding company will be public", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro's Sprouts analysis (2.17) says s 76 matters 'only if there's a public company in BrightPath's chain and debt is pushed down'. Both conditions are met. Once BrightPath owns Little Sprouts, Little Sprouts' ultimate holding company is the SGX-listed parent, which brings it within s 76's scope. A guarantee of the acquisition loan is classic financial assistance: it is a contingent liability that can deplete Little Sprouts' assets (PP v Lew Syn Pau), and it is given in connection with the acquisition of its shares, even though it comes after completion. The parties need an exception (such as a s 76(9BA) board resolution) or a whitewash (with unanimous shareholder approval under s 76(9B) being easy once BrightPath is the sole shareholder), sequenced properly and made a condition of the financing."},
    {t:"No: Little Sprouts is a private company, so s 76 never applies", correct:false,
     why:"Little Sprouts is private, so the post-2014 relaxation seems to take it out of s 76. But scope depends on the whole chain at the time assistance is given. Once BrightPath owns it, Little Sprouts' ultimate holding company is the SGX-listed group, which brings it within s 76."},
    {t:"No: the guarantee is given after completion, not before", correct:false,
     why:"Assistance is often thought of as something given before the share purchase, so a post-completion guarantee may seem safe. But s 76 catches assistance given before, at the same time as, or after the acquisition if it is in connection with it. A guarantee of the acquisition loan pushed down after completion is the textbook example."},
    {t:"Yes, but only if the listed group is foreign", correct:false,
     why:"Foreign groups sometimes fall outside local rules, which makes this sound plausible. But the s 76 test is whether the holding or ultimate holding company is a public company. It does not matter whether the listed parent is listed in Singapore or abroad."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F; Intro 2.13, 2.15, 2.17 (Ex 1)", takeaway:"Public parent in the buyer's chain + push-down → s 76 engaged."
},

{ id:"f0-11-01", lesson:"0.11", type:"order", dia:"s76test",
  prompt:"Wan frames financial assistance (s 76) as three questions. Put them in the order the Intro gives.",
  steps:["Is there an acquisition of shares?","Is the assistance 'financial': does it deplete or contingently deplete the company's assets?","Is it given 'for the purpose of' or 'in connection with' the acquisition?"],
  why:"Wan [15.62] sets out the three questions, which structure every s 76 analysis once scope is established. First, is there an acquisition or proposed acquisition of shares in the company or its holding company? In an LBO, always yes ([15.63]). Second, is the assistance 'financial'? PP v Lew Syn Pau held that assistance is financial if it actually or contingently depletes the company's assets, and a guarantee or security is a contingent liability, so it qualifies; the Court of Appeal agreed in Wu Yang ([15.64]–[15.65]). Third, is it given 'for the purpose of' or 'in connection with' the acquisition? s 76(3) treats it as for that purpose if that is the substantial purpose among several, and s 76(4) treats it as in connection with the acquisition if the company was aware it would assist the acquirer ([15.66]). Wu Yang, applying Brady v Brady, holds that commercial benefit is the reason, not the purpose.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F [15.62]–[15.67]; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Share acquisition? → financial (depletes)? → for/in connection with?"
},

{ id:"r0-11-01", lesson:"0.11", type:"match", dia:"s76test",
  prompt:"Match each act by a target company to whether it is a typical form of prohibited 'financial assistance' under s 76.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Target guarantees the buyer's acquisition loan", r:"Yes: classic financial assistance"},
    {l:"Target grants security over its assets for that loan", r:"Yes: classic financial assistance"},
    {l:"Target lends the buyer money to pay for the shares", r:"Yes: classic financial assistance"},
    {l:"Target pays a lawful dividend after completion", r:"No: within the s 76(8)(a) exception"}
  ],
  why:"s 76(2) lists the forms financial assistance typically takes, and PP v Lew Syn Pau identified the common thread: each actually or contingently depletes the company's assets. 'The loan that is not repaid, the guarantee that is called upon, the security that is resorted to can all lead to a depletion' (quoted at Wan [15.64]). So a guarantee, security or a loan to the buyer for the share purchase are the paradigm cases, where the company is within scope. A dividend is different. s 76(8)(a) excepts a distribution of assets by way of dividends lawfully made, and since 2014 the old requirement that the dividend be in good faith and in the ordinary course has been removed (Wan [15.73]). That makes a post-completion dividend, used by BidCo to repay acquisition debt, a recognised route around s 76, provided it is lawfully declared out of profits.",
  cite:"Companies Act s 76(2), (8)(a); Wan Ch 15 [15.64], [15.72]–[15.73]", takeaway:"Guarantee, security, loan = assistance; lawful dividend = excepted."
},

{ id:"f0x-11-01", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A public target's directors honestly intend its security to support its own refinancing. They know, however, that the same facility will also fund a buyer's purchase of the target's shares, and they sign anyway.",
  prompt:"How do s 76(3) and (4) frame the purpose question?",
  options:[
    {t:"s 76(3): substantial purpose; s 76(4): awareness it assists the buyer", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.66] explains that both expressions are defined in the Act. s 76(3) covers assistance given for several purposes where the substantial one is to help the acquisition. s 76(4) covers assistance where the company was aware that it would assist the acquirer in acquiring the shares. Read literally and disjunctively, awareness alone could be enough, and here the directors knew. But Wan notes that in Wu Yang v Zhejiang Jinyi the High Court gave the provisions a purposive reading, defining the purpose and connection tests by reference to each other, to keep s 76 within its intended mischief. And after Wu Yang v Mao Yong Hui, honest motives and commercial benefit are only the 'reason', not the 'purpose'. The safe course where s 76 applies is an exception or a whitewash."},
    {t:"It cannot be financial assistance because the directors' honest intention was refinancing", correct:false,
     why:"Courts care about honesty, and directors acting in good faith usually get some protection, so this sounds fair. But s 76 asks about the substantial purpose of the assistance and the company's awareness that it would assist the acquirer. Wu Yang also separates the directors' reasons from the transaction's purpose, so honest motives do not decide it."},
    {t:"Only the buyer's purpose matters, not the company's", correct:false,
     why:"The buyer is the one acquiring shares, so its purpose may seem decisive. But ss 76(3) and (4) focus on the company giving the assistance: whether helping the acquisition was a substantial purpose, and whether the company was aware the assistance would help the acquirer."},
    {t:"s 76 applies only if the security is given before the shares are acquired", correct:false,
     why:"Wan [15.62] notes the acquisition can be before or at the same time as the assistance, and assistance after the acquisition can also be caught (for example to discharge acquisition debt)."}
  ],
  cite:"Companies Act s 76(3)–(4); Wan Ch 15 [15.66]–[15.67]", takeaway:"Purpose = substantial purpose; connection = awareness; courts read them together."
},

{ id:"r0-11-02", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"TargetCo's directors say that guaranteeing BidCo's acquisition loan is fine because the new owner will bring TargetCo cheaper supplies and new customers, a real commercial benefit.",
  prompt:"Following Wu Yang v Mao Yong Hui (applying Brady v Brady), how will a Singapore court treat that argument?",
  options:[
    {t:"It fails: benefit is the reason, but the purpose is the acquisition", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.67] traces two approaches. In Intraco v Multi-Pak the Court of Appeal held that if the transaction was commercially beneficial to the company, s 76 was not breached. But in the later Wu Yang Construction Group v Mao Yong Hui, the Court of Appeal did not accept that view. Relying on Brady v Brady, it distinguished the reason for a transaction from its purpose: even if the directors' motive was to benefit the company commercially, that is only the reason, and if the purpose is to help the acquirer buy the shares, the transaction is financial assistance. Wan [15.68] criticises the result for catching innocuous transactions and for the artificiality of the distinction, but it is the law. TargetCo, if within scope, needs an exception such as s 76(9BA) or a whitewash, not a commercial-benefit argument."},
    {t:"It succeeds: under Intraco v Multi-Pak, commercial benefit to the target means s 76 is not breached", correct:false,
     why:"Intraco v Multi-Pak is a Court of Appeal decision that did treat commercial benefit as an answer, so relying on it is understandable. But Wan [15.67] explains that the Court of Appeal in Wu Yang did not accept that approach, and relied on Brady v Brady to distinguish reason from purpose. The later authority governs."},
    {t:"It succeeds because s 76 contains a general 'larger purpose' exception", correct:false,
     why:"Older UK law had a 'principal or larger purpose' exception, so a similar defence may seem available. But Singapore's s 76 has no general exception of that kind. The recognised ways through are the specific exceptions in s 76(8)–(9BA), such as lawful dividends or the 'no material prejudice' route, and the whitewashes."},
    {t:"It is irrelevant, because a guarantee never depletes the company's assets", correct:false,
     why:"The opposite: PP v Lew Syn Pau treats a guarantee as a contingent depletion of assets, so it is financial assistance. The live question is purpose, which Wu Yang answers against the directors."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.64]–[15.68]; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Wu Yang: commercial benefit is the reason, not the purpose."
},

{ id:"ap0-11-04", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A public target agrees to pay for the bidder's due-diligence reports on the target.",
  prompt:"Could that be financial assistance?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: paying a buyer's DD costs can be assistance", correct:true,
     why:"Financial assistance is not limited to loans, guarantees and security. Wan (footnote to [15.65]) refers to Chaston v SWP Group, where the English Court of Appeal held that a target's payment of fees for a due-diligence report commissioned for the buyer was prohibited financial assistance. Run the three questions: there is an acquisition of shares; paying the bidder's costs actually depletes the target's assets; and the payment is made to help the bidder acquire the shares. A public target is squarely within s 76. Wan also flags that other assurances by the target, such as warranties and indemnities, and break fees, can raise similar questions. Bidders should pay their own diligence costs, or the parties should rely on an exception."},
    {t:"No: only loans and guarantees count", correct:false,
     why:"Loans, guarantees and security are the forms most often discussed, so it is easy to think s 76 stops there. But the test from PP v Lew Syn Pau is whether assistance depletes the company's assets, actually or contingently. Paying the bidder's costs depletes assets directly, as Chaston v SWP Group shows."},
    {t:"No: DD fees are always ordinary business expenses", correct:false,
     why:"Companies pay advisers routinely, so diligence fees can look like normal expenses. But these are the bidder's costs of acquiring the target's shares. When the target pays them, it is using its own money to help someone buy its shares, which is exactly what s 76 targets."},
    {t:"Only if the fees exceed 1% of the target's value", correct:false,
     why:"The 1% figure is familiar from break fees, which is why a threshold may come to mind. But that is Code Rule 13. s 76 has no de minimis: any assistance within its scope needs an exception or a whitewash, however small."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.65] fn (Chaston v SWP Group)", takeaway:"Paying the bidder's acquisition costs can be financial assistance."
},

{ id:"f0x-11-02", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In a recommended offer for a listed target, the implementation agreement requires the target to pay the bidder a break fee of 1% of the offer value if the target board recommends a rival bid.",
  prompt:"Why does the Intro say a break fee 'itself raises a s 76 question'?",
  options:[
    {t:"Paying the bidder can deplete assets, so s 76 must be satisfied", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro (2.15) and glossary note that a break fee in public deals is capped at 1% of target value and 'itself raises a s 76 question'. The target is a public company, so s 76 applies. If the fee is paid, the target's assets are depleted, and the fee is promised in connection with the bidder's proposed acquisition of the target's shares. Wan cross-refers to Chapter 8 ([8.63]–[8.72]) for the detailed analysis. The usual answer is that a modest fee, agreed by an independent board to secure an offer that benefits shareholders, falls within an exception such as s 76(9BA), with a board resolution stating the reasons and lodged with the Registrar. The Code cap and the s 76 analysis are separate hurdles: complying with one does not satisfy the other."},
    {t:"It doesn't: break fees are outside s 76 because they are paid only if the deal fails", correct:false,
     why:"The fee is promised as part of the bidder's acquisition process, and paying it depletes the target. That it is contingent does not take it outside s 76; contingent depletion is enough (Lew Syn Pau)."},
    {t:"Because the Code prohibits all break fees", correct:false,
     why:"Break fees are regulated by the Code, which may suggest they are banned. But Rule 13 permits them, normally up to 1% of the target's value at the offer price, with written confirmations and disclosure. The s 76 question is a separate Companies Act issue that remains even when the Code is complied with."},
    {t:"Because a break fee triggers a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"Break fees and mandatory offers are both public-deal topics, which invites confusion. But Rule 14 is triggered by acquiring voting rights at or above 30%, or by creeping. A break fee is a cash payment by the target and involves no acquisition of voting rights."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.15, glossary 'Break fee'; Wan Ch 8 [8.63]–[8.72], Ch 15", takeaway:"Break fee: Code ≤1% cap and a separate s 76 analysis."
},

{ id:"f0-11-03", lesson:"0.11", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which whitewash route is the practical choice once the target is a private company with few shareholders?",
  options:[
    {t:"The s 76(9B) shareholder route: unanimous, uncapped", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.79] describes the shareholder-approved route introduced in 2005: in addition to board approval and a declaration of solvency by the directors, the assistance must receive the unanimous approval of the shareholders after notice. Unlike the director-approved route, there is no maximum amount. Wan notes it is suited to assistance given once the target has been taken private and has only a few shareholders, which makes unanimity practical. After a buyout, BidCo is often the sole shareholder, so unanimity is a formality. The director-approved route under s 76(9A) is capped at 10% of paid-up capital and reserves, too small for a buyout ([15.78]), and the traditional route involves a court and public notice ([15.76]–[15.77])."},
    {t:"The director-approved route under s 76(9A), because it is uncapped", correct:false,
     why:"Director-approved routes are quicker, which makes 9A attractive. But Wan [15.78] notes s 76(9A) is capped at 10% of paid-up capital and reserves, too small for most buyouts. The uncapped route is s 76(9B), which needs unanimous shareholder approval plus the directors' solvency declaration."},
    {t:"The traditional route (special resolution + court + newspaper notice), because it is fastest", correct:false,
     why:"The traditional route involves the court, which can look authoritative and therefore safe. But Wan [15.76]–[15.77] describes it as cumbersome: a special resolution, court approval and newspaper notice, with delay, cost, publicity and the risk of objections. With few shareholders, the (9B) route is far simpler."},
    {t:"No whitewash is ever needed for a private company", correct:false,
     why:"Since 2014 many private companies are outside s 76, which may suggest none needs a whitewash. But a private company is still caught if its holding or ultimate holding company is public. In that case it needs an exception or a whitewash before giving assistance."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.76]–[15.79]; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Few holders, big assistance → s 76(9B): unanimous, solvency, no cap."
},

{ id:"r0-11-04", lesson:"0.11", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each s 76 route to its defining requirement.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Traditional whitewash (ss 76(10)–(14))", r:"Special resolution + court approval + newspaper notice; no directors' solvency declaration"},
    {l:"Director-approved (s 76(9A))", r:"Board approval + solvency declaration; capped at 10% of capital and reserves"},
    {l:"Shareholder-approved (s 76(9B))", r:"Solvency declaration + unanimous shareholder approval; no cap"},
    {l:"'No material prejudice' exception (s 76(9BA))", r:"Board resolution with reasons, lodged with the Registrar"}
  ],
  why:"Wan [15.74]–[15.79] sets out the routes. The traditional procedure requires a special resolution after proper notice (and a special resolution of the holding company where the company is a subsidiary of a listed company or its ultimate holding company is Singapore-incorporated), then court approval and public notice in a daily newspaper. It does not require directors to declare solvency, but it is slow, costly and public. The two 2005 routes rest on a solvency declaration by all the directors: the director-approved route needs the board to decide the assistance is in the company's best interests and on fair terms, with notice to shareholders, but is capped at 10% of paid-up capital and reserves; the shareholder-approved route adds unanimous shareholder approval and has no cap. The 2014 'no material prejudice' exception in s 76(9BA) needs a board resolution that the company should give the assistance on fair and reasonable terms, stating its reasons, lodged with the Registrar.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.74]–[15.79]; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Traditional (court) · 9A (10% cap) · 9B (unanimous, no cap) · 9BA (no material prejudice)."
},

{ id:"ap0-11-05", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A private subsidiary of a listed group must give security worth 40% of its capital and reserves for a buyer's loan. One minority shareholder refuses to approve. The board is satisfied the assistance is fair and won't harm the company or its creditors.",
  prompt:"Which route is realistic?",
  options:[
    {t:"The s 76(9BA) 'no material prejudice' route", correct:true,
     why:"Eliminate the routes that fail. s 76(9B) needs unanimous shareholder approval, and one shareholder refuses. s 76(9A) is capped at 10% of paid-up capital and reserves, and this assistance is 40%. That leaves the 2014 exception in s 76(9BA), described at Wan [15.74]: assistance is permitted if it does not materially prejudice the interests of the company or its shareholders, or the company's ability to pay its creditors, and the board resolves that the company should give it and that its terms are fair and reasonable, stating the reasons and lodging the resolution with the Registrar. The board here is satisfied on the substance, so 9BA fits, provided its conclusion is genuine and documented. Wan calls this exception 'eminently capable' of supporting a genuine LBO. The traditional whitewash, through a special resolution and the court, remains a slower fallback."},
    {t:"s 76(9A), because it is director-approved", correct:false,
     why:"The board is satisfied, so a director-approved route seems natural. But s 76(9A) is capped at 10% of paid-up capital and reserves. Assistance worth 40% is far above that limit, so (9A) cannot be used."},
    {t:"s 76(9B), overriding the refusing shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"(9B) has no cap, so it looks ideal for large assistance. But it requires the unanimous approval of the shareholders. One refusing shareholder defeats it, and there is no mechanism for overriding a holdout."},
    {t:"No route exists", correct:false,
     why:"With (9A) capped and (9B) blocked, it may seem nothing is left. But s 76(9BA) allows assistance that does not materially prejudice the company, its shareholders or its creditors, by board resolution with reasons lodged with the Registrar. The traditional court-based whitewash also remains available."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.74]–[15.79]; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Big assistance + a holdout → s 76(9BA) (or the court route)."
},

{ id:"f0x-11-03", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"After a take-private, the target (still a public company, and now a subsidiary of private BidCo) has S$80m of distributable profits. The lenders want those profits used to reduce BidCo's acquisition debt.",
  prompt:"Which route does Wan highlight as the cleanest way to do this?",
  options:[
    {t:"A lawful dividend from the target to BidCo to repay the debt", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.72]–[15.73] discusses exactly this structure. A cash-rich target declares a large dividend after the acquisition, and the bidder uses it to repay acquisition borrowings. The s 76(8)(a) exception covers a distribution of assets by way of dividends lawfully made. Before 2014 the exception required the dividend to be paid in good faith and in the ordinary course of commercial dealing, which a one-off, deal-driven dividend might not satisfy. The 2014 amendments removed those requirements, so 'all that matters currently is whether the dividend was lawfully declared'. Wan suggests it is now arguable that the target can even undertake to distribute profits once the LBO completes. The dividend must still be paid only out of profits and comply with the Companies Act and the constitution."},
    {t:"An upstream loan from the target to BidCo, which is never financial assistance", correct:false,
     why:"Intra-group loans are common, so an upstream loan may seem harmless. But where s 76 applies, a target lending money to repay the debt used to buy its shares is classic financial assistance, depleting its assets. It needs an exception or a whitewash, unlike a lawful dividend under s 76(8)(a)."},
    {t:"A guarantee by the target, which the scheme exception always covers", correct:false,
     why:"The scheme exception applies to acquisitions under a s 210 scheme, and Wan cautions against adopting a scheme only to avoid s 76. A guarantee here would still need analysis."},
    {t:"Nothing can be done until the target is liquidated", correct:false,
     why:"Returning value to a parent can seem to require a formal process such as liquidation. But Wan [15.72]–[15.73] explains that a lawfully declared dividend is excepted under s 76(8)(a). Since 2014 the dividend only needs to be lawfully made, so liquidation is unnecessary."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.72]–[15.73]; Companies Act s 76(8)(a); Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Lawful dividend → s 76(8)(a): the cleanest debt pay-down route."
},

{ id:"ap0-11-02", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A sponsor wants to take a listed company private and have the target grant security for the acquisition debt. It can structure the deal as a takeover offer or as a s 210 scheme.",
  prompt:"How does s 76 affect the route choice?",
  options:[
    {t:"A scheme can help: s 76(8)(d) excepts s 210 schemes", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.71] identifies the scheme exception in s 76(8)(d): if the acquisition is structured as a scheme of arrangement rather than a takeover offer, the target can give guarantees, security or other support for the bidder's financing within the exception. That can be a real advantage in a leveraged take-private. But Wan adds an important caution: it has been argued that unless there is a sufficient connection between the scheme and the assistance, the court may not sanction the scheme, so it may be 'somewhat adventurous' to adopt the scheme route only to overcome s 76. An offer gets no equivalent exception, and the listed target is squarely within s 76; the alternatives are to wait until it is private with no public parent, use a dividend, or use 9BA or a whitewash."},
    {t:"An offer helps, because offers are outside s 76", correct:false,
     why:"Offers and schemes are often treated as interchangeable routes, so an offer may seem equally safe. But only acquisitions under a s 210 scheme benefit from the s 76(8)(d) exception. A listed target in an offer is squarely within s 76, so security for the bidder's debt needs another route."},
    {t:"The route makes no difference", correct:false,
     why:"Route choice is usually about votes and timing, so it may seem irrelevant to financing. But s 76(8)(d) excepts assistance given in an acquisition under a s 210 scheme, which can be a real advantage. Wan cautions only against choosing a scheme solely to avoid s 76."},
    {t:"Both routes need the target to be private first", correct:false,
     why:"Taking a target private is one way out of s 76, so it may seem a precondition for any assistance. But the scheme exception applies to a public target. Going private first and then giving assistance is an alternative approach, not a requirement for the scheme route."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.71]; Companies Act s 76(8)(d); Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Scheme exception exists, but don't pick a scheme only to dodge s 76."
},

{ id:"ap0-11-03", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"After a successful take-private, TargetCo is delisted and converted to a private company. Its new parent BidCo is private, and so is BidCo's fund owner. The lenders now want TargetCo to guarantee the acquisition debt.",
  prompt:"What is the best advice?",
  options:[
    {t:"Arguably outside s 76 now, but whitewash anyway to be safe", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.61] notes that the 2014 amendments open up a structuring possibility: the guarantee or security can be provided lawfully after the target has been converted into a private company, so long as its holding and ultimate holding companies are not public, an approach accepted in the UK in Paros v Worldlink. Wan [15.73] adds that s 76 as revised arguably permits assistance to be given after conversion even though the target was public when acquired. TargetCo now has no public company above it. But Wan says that 'in case of any doubt, it would be prudent to rely upon the whitewash provisions'. With BidCo as sole shareholder, a s 76(9B) whitewash (unanimous approval, solvency declaration, no cap) is straightforward, so the belt-and-braces approach costs little."},
    {t:"It can never give assistance, because it was public when acquired", correct:false,
     why:"Since the target was public when acquired, it may seem tainted forever. But s 76 looks at the company's position when the assistance is given. Wan [15.61] and [15.73] consider post-conversion assistance arguably permissible once there is no public company above it, citing Paros v Worldlink."},
    {t:"It must first relist", correct:false,
     why:"Relisting would restore market access, but it would put TargetCo back within s 76 as a public company. The advantage of the take-private is that the company is now outside the rule, so relisting would create the problem rather than solve it."},
    {t:"It needs SIC consent", correct:false,
     why:"The SIC is involved in many take-privates, so its consent may seem needed. But s 76 is a Companies Act provision. The SIC administers the Code and has no role in authorising financial assistance; the routes are the Act's exceptions and whitewashes."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.61], [15.73]", takeaway:"Take private first, then assist; whitewash anyway for certainty."
},

{ id:"f0x-11-04", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A buyer's lenders require the target (a subsidiary of a listed company) to grant security at completion. The parties plan to run a s 76(9B) whitewash 'sometime after closing'.",
  prompt:"What is the problem, and the fix?",
  options:[
    {t:"The security predates the whitewash; complete it first as a CP", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro (2.15) gives the practical move: 'sequence the whitewash/exception correctly against signing and completion, and make it a CP'. A whitewash or exception authorises assistance; it does not retrospectively cure assistance already given. If the target grants security at completion and the whitewash comes later, the security was given in breach of s 76 when given. The consequences are serious: the target may avoid the security under s 76A(2), so the lenders' package is at risk, and officers face potential criminal exposure. Note also that s 76(9B) needs unanimous approval, which the current shareholders (the listed seller group) must give if the security is granted at completion. The fix is to plan the steps on a completion timeline and make the whitewash a condition of drawdown and completion."},
    {t:"No problem: a whitewash can always be done later and backdated", correct:false,
     why:"Paperwork is often tidied up after completion, so a later whitewash may seem fine. But the procedures authorise assistance before it is given. Assistance given first is unlawful when given and voidable under s 76A(2). Backdating would be improper and would not change when the assistance actually happened."},
    {t:"The only issue is that the lenders might charge more", correct:false,
     why:"Lenders do price risk, so a higher margin may seem the main consequence. But the real problem is legal: unlawful security may be avoided by the company under s 76A(2), leaving lenders unsecured, and officers face criminal exposure. That threatens the financing itself, not just its cost."},
    {t:"Whitewashes are only needed for public companies, so none is needed", correct:false,
     why:"The target is a private subsidiary, so it may seem outside s 76 after 2014. But its holding company is listed, which brings it within scope. It needs an exception or a whitewash, completed before the security is given and made a condition of completion."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.15; Wan Ch 15 [15.69]–[15.70]", takeaway:"Whitewash first, assist second; make it a CP."
},

{ id:"d4-004", lesson:"0.11", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the consequence of giving unlawful financial assistance in breach of s 76?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company may avoid it (s 76A(2)); officers face sanctions", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.69]–[15.70] sets out the consequences. Under s 76A(2), the company can avoid any contract entered into in contravention of s 76, and related contracts or transactions, although how far that reaches is unclear. In an LBO this hits the lenders hardest: they may be unable to enforce the target's guarantee or security, which is usually central to their security package. Wan considers it unlikely that the avoidance would extend to the takeover itself. Breach can also lead to criminal sanctions for officers found guilty of an offence (s 76(5)); PP v Lew Syn Pau is an example of a prosecution, though unsuccessful. That combination, an unenforceable security package and personal criminal exposure, is why lawyers insist on exceptions or whitewashes being in place first."},
    {t:"The assistance is automatically validated at completion", correct:false,
     why:"Completion feels like the moment everything is regularised, which is why this sounds right. But completion does not cure a breach of s 76. The exception or whitewash must be in place before the assistance is given; otherwise the transaction remains voidable and the breach remains."},
    {t:"Only a small administrative fine applies", correct:false,
     why:"Many regulatory breaches carry modest fines, so this may seem proportionate. But Wan [15.69]–[15.70] sets out more serious consequences: the company may avoid the contract or transaction under s 76A(2), and officers in default face criminal sanction."},
    {t:"Nothing, if the buyer acted in good faith", correct:false,
     why:"Good faith often protects a buyer in other contexts, such as s 160(3) for asset sales. But s 76 contains no good-faith defence for the parties to the assistance. A buyer or lender acting in good faith still faces avoidance of the security if the rule was breached."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.69]–[15.70]; Companies Act ss 76(5), 76A(2)", takeaway:"Breach → contract voidable by the company + criminal exposure for officers."
},

{ id:"ap0-11-06", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An LBO completed a year ago. The target's guarantee of the acquisition loan turns out to have breached s 76. A minority shareholder argues the whole takeover must now be undone.",
  prompt:"What is the likely consequence?",
  options:[
    {t:"The guarantee is voidable; the takeover itself likely stands", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.69]–[15.70] explains that s 76A(2) lets the company avoid the contract that gave the assistance, so the lenders may be unable to enforce their guarantee or security. The statute also lets the company avoid related contracts or transactions, but Wan notes the extent of that is unclear and that it is unlikely to extend to the validity of the takeover itself. So the minority shareholder's argument is unlikely to succeed. The practical fallout is a financing problem, with lenders exposed and likely to seek new security after a proper whitewash, and a personal one for officers, who may face criminal liability. The episode illustrates why sequencing matters."},
    {t:"The takeover is automatically void and the shares revert", correct:false,
     why:"If the financing was unlawful, it may seem the whole deal falls with it. But Wan [15.70] considers it unlikely that avoidance under s 76A(2) extends to the takeover itself. The main risk is to the lenders' guarantee or security, which the company may avoid."},
    {t:"Nothing happens once completion has occurred", correct:false,
     why:"Completed transactions are usually hard to reopen, so this may seem safe. But the company's right to avoid the unlawful assistance and the officers' criminal exposure both survive completion. A breach discovered later can still unravel the security package."},
    {t:"The SIC must order a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"Takeover consequences often run through the SIC, which may explain this option. But s 76 is a Companies Act matter, and its breach is not a Code trigger. Rule 14 depends on acquiring voting rights, not on how the deal was financed."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.69]–[15.70]; CA s 76A(2)", takeaway:"s 76 breach: lenders' security at risk; takeover itself likely stands."
},

{ id:"f0x-11-05", lesson:"0.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A sponsor wants to buy the operating business of an SGX-listed company using debt secured on the business's assets. It is unsure whether to buy the shares of the operating subsidiary or the business and assets directly.",
  prompt:"Which structure avoids s 76 altogether, according to Wan?",
  options:[
    {t:"Buying the business and assets, not shares", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.80] concludes that 'if an LBO transaction is structured as an acquisition of assets or a business division of the target, it is not impeded by the rule against financial assistance, as that rule is limited to the acquisition of shares'. The Intro (2.3, 2.15) lists the asset route as one of the escape routes. If the sponsor's new vehicle buys the business and assets, there is no acquisition of shares for anyone to assist, and the lenders can take security over the assets the buyer now owns. The trade-off is the asset-deal friction from Lesson 1.2: novations, consents, licences, s 18A and possibly a TSA. Buying the subsidiary's shares would put the subsidiary within s 76 (its parent is public), needing an exception or whitewash."},
    {t:"Buying the subsidiary's shares, because subsidiaries are always outside s 76", correct:false,
     why:"Subsidiaries are often private companies, so they may seem outside s 76. But a subsidiary of a public company falls within its scope. Buying its shares with debt it secures is classic financial assistance and would need an exception or a whitewash."},
    {t:"Neither: s 76 applies to every leveraged acquisition", correct:false,
     why:"Leveraged deals and financial assistance are closely linked, so the rule may seem universal. But Wan [15.80] explains s 76 is limited to the acquisition of shares, and to companies within its scope. Buying a business and assets involves no share acquisition, so the rule does not apply."},
    {t:"Either, as long as the deal completes quickly", correct:false,
     why:"Speed is often valuable in deals, but it has no bearing on s 76. What matters is structure: whether shares are being acquired and whether the company giving assistance is within scope. An asset purchase avoids the rule; a share purchase of a public subsidiary does not."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 [15.80]; Intro 2.3, 2.15", takeaway:"Buy assets, not shares, and s 76 falls away."
}

