/* DealCraft — Chapter 1 · Lesson 1.14 Valuation & Deal Economics (audited Oct 2026).
   Sources: Intro 2.1–2.2; Gaughan Ch 4, Ch 15 (Valuation). Gaughan is US/finance: vocabulary and economics, never Singapore law. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.f0v2 = (window.DC_BANK.f0v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"f0x-14-01", lesson:"0.14", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A client's CFO asks the deal lawyer: 'Valuation isn't your job, so why do you keep asking about the EV-to-equity bridge and how debt is defined?'",
  prompt:"What is the best answer?",
  options:[
    {t:"The documents turn value into price, so the inputs matter", correct:true,
     why:"The Intro (2.1) takes 'what it's worth' off the lawyer's list: valuation is for the client and its financial advisers. But valuation concepts run through the legal work. A headline enterprise value becomes an equity price only after deducting debt and adding cash, so the SPA's definitions of 'Indebtedness' and 'Cash', and whether items such as unfunded pension liabilities count as debt-like, move real money between the parties (Gaughan Ch 15 notes these off-balance-sheet liabilities). The choice of locked box or completion accounts (2.9) decides when that bridge is measured. In public deals, the IFA must tell independent shareholders whether the terms are fair and reasonable, and minorities challenging a squeeze-out argue about value. A lawyer who understands the vocabulary can draft the price mechanics properly without offering an opinion on the number itself."},
    {t:"Agree that it isn't relevant, and stop asking", correct:false,
     why:"The Intro does say valuation is not the lawyer's job, so agreeing may seem correct. But the lawyer still drafts how value becomes price. If the debt, cash and debt-like definitions are wrong, the client can pay millions more than intended for the same agreed value. Stopping would leave that risk undrafted."},
    {t:"Explain that the lawyer is responsible for confirming the valuation is correct", correct:false,
     why:"Lawyers often confirm many things in a deal, so this may sound plausible. But the Intro (2.1) puts valuation with the client and its financial advisers, who carry responsibility for it. The lawyer documents the agreed value accurately and allocates risk; it does not certify the number."},
    {t:"Say the questions are only relevant for tax", correct:false,
     why:"Debt and cash definitions do have tax angles, so this answer is partly familiar. But their main effect is on the price itself: equity value is enterprise value less net debt, so every item classed as debt reduces what the seller receives. That is a commercial and legal point, not only a tax one."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.1, 2.9; Gaughan Ch 15", takeaway:"Lawyers don't value; they turn a value into a price through definitions and mechanics."
},

{ id:"r0-14-01", lesson:"0.14", type:"mcq", dia:"valueBridge",
  prompt:"What is the difference between ENTERPRISE VALUE and EQUITY VALUE?",
  options:[
    {t:"EV is the whole business; equity is EV less net debt", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan Ch 15 defines enterprise value as 'the value of the entire business', reflecting the combined claims of equityholders and debtholders, and explains that equity value is reached by deducting the value of the liabilities, mainly interest-bearing debt, from enterprise value. In practice cash is usually netted against debt, so equity value equals enterprise value minus net debt. The distinction matters in every deal because multiples such as EV/EBITDA produce an enterprise value, while the buyer of shares pays for equity. Confusing the two is a classic error: an offer of '8x EBITDA' is not the share price. Gaughan also notes adjustments: liabilities that may not be on the balance sheet, such as unfunded pension and contingent liabilities, reduce equity value, and non-operating assets not reflected in cash flows, such as surplus real estate, add to it."},
    {t:"They are the same thing", correct:false,
     why:"The two terms are often used loosely in the press, which makes them sound interchangeable. But Gaughan Ch 15 defines enterprise value as the whole business to all capital providers, and equity value as what remains after deducting debt. Treating them as the same would overpay for the shares of an indebted company."},
    {t:"Equity value always exceeds enterprise value", correct:false,
     why:"For a cash-rich company equity value can exceed enterprise value, which may make this sound general. But most businesses carry net debt, and then equity value is below enterprise value. Only a business with more cash than debt has equity value above EV."},
    {t:"Enterprise value ignores the operating business", correct:false,
     why:"Enterprise value includes debt, which might suggest it is a financing figure rather than a business value. In fact Gaughan describes it as the value of the entire business, the operating assets that generate cash flows, regardless of how they are financed. Equity value is the shareholders' residual share of it."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15", takeaway:"EV = whole business; equity = EV − net debt (± adjustments)."
},

{ id:"ap0-14-01", lesson:"0.14", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A target is valued at an enterprise value of S$100m. It has S$30m of borrowings and S$5m of cash.",
  prompt:"What is the equity value, which is roughly what the shareholders receive?",
  options:[
    {t:"S$75m", correct:true,
     why:"Equity value is enterprise value minus net debt. Net debt is S$30m of borrowings less S$5m of cash, so S$25m, and equity value is S$100m − S$25m = S$75m. This is why SPAs are often priced 'on a cash-free, debt-free basis': the parties agree an enterprise value and then adjust for actual debt and cash, either as at the locked-box date or at completion through completion accounts (Intro 2.9). It is also why the definitions matter. If the SPA treats a S$3m unpaid tax bill or a S$2m customer deposit as debt, equity value falls accordingly. If it does not, the buyer effectively pays for those obligations twice."},
    {t:"S$100m", correct:false,
     why:"S$100m is the headline number, and sellers often quote it. But it is the value of the whole business to all capital providers. The shareholders receive equity value, which is S$100m less net debt of S$25m (S$30m borrowings less S$5m cash), giving S$75m."},
    {t:"S$70m (EV less debt only)", correct:false,
     why:"Deducting the S$30m of borrowings is right, but ignoring the S$5m of cash is not. Cash in the business belongs to the shareholders, so it reduces net debt. Net debt is S$25m, not S$30m, and equity value is S$75m. This is why SPAs define cash as carefully as debt."},
    {t:"S$135m (EV plus debt and cash)", correct:false,
     why:"Debt and EV both appear on the funding side, which might suggest adding them. But debt is a claim ranking ahead of the shareholders. It reduces what is left for equity, so it is deducted from enterprise value, not added to it."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15; Intro 2.9", takeaway:"Equity = EV − (debt − cash)."
},

{ id:"f0x-14-02", lesson:"0.14", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Peer companies trade at about 8x EV/EBITDA. The target has EBITDA of S$5m, interest-bearing debt of S$12m and no surplus cash. It also has an unfunded staff retirement liability of S$3m that is not on its balance sheet.",
  prompt:"Applying Gaughan's method, what is the indicated equity value?",
  options:[
    {t:"About S$25m: S$40m less S$15m of debt items", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan Ch 15 sets out the steps. Derive the EV/EBITDA multiple from comparable companies or transactions, apply it to the target's EBITDA to get enterprise value (8 × S$5m = S$40m), then 'back out the debt of the target from this value to get the value of its equity' (S$40m − S$12m = S$28m). Gaughan adds that liabilities which may not appear on the balance sheet, specifically unfunded pension and contingent liabilities, may need to be deducted too, giving about S$25m. For the lawyer, this is the reason diligence on such liabilities matters and why the SPA should say whether they count as debt-like items in the price adjustment. A buyer that ignores the S$3m will overpay by that amount."},
    {t:"S$40m: the multiple gives the price of the shares", correct:false,
     why:"A multiple applied to EBITDA produces a single headline number, which looks like a price. But EBITDA is earnings before interest, available to all capital providers, so an EV/EBITDA multiple gives enterprise value. Gaughan's next step is to back out the debt to reach equity value."},
    {t:"S$28m: only balance-sheet debt is deducted", correct:false,
     why:"Deducting only balance-sheet debt is the usual first step. But Gaughan Ch 15 warns that unfunded pension and contingent liabilities may not appear on the balance sheet and may need to be deducted too. Ignoring the S$3m retirement liability overstates equity value by that amount."},
    {t:"S$52m: debt is added to EV", correct:false,
     why:"Debt is part of the capital invested, which might suggest adding it. But in moving from enterprise value to equity value, debt is a claim ahead of shareholders and must be subtracted. S$52m would value the equity above the whole business, which is impossible for an indebted company."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15", takeaway:"Multiple × EBITDA = EV; then deduct debt and debt-like items."
},

{ id:"r0-14-02", lesson:"0.14", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Analysts value a target at '8x EBITDA'. What kind of valuation is this?",
  options:[
    {t:"A comparable-multiples approach", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan Ch 15 describes comparable multiples as 'a quick and easy method' with three basic steps: select comparable companies or transactions, compute their multiples (P/E, price-to-book, EV/EBITDA, price-to-revenue), and apply the average or adjusted multiple to the target's corresponding measure. EBITDA multiples are sometimes called cash-flow multiples because EBITDA is used as a proxy for cash flow. Gaughan stresses comparability: a troubled company should not get the multiple of healthy peers, outliers may need removing, and courts have rejected multiples without a reliable basis for comparability. He also distinguishes trailing multiples (historical earnings) from forward multiples (forecasts). A DCF, by contrast, builds value from projected free cash flows discounted at a cost of capital."},
    {t:"A discounted cash flow (DCF) valuation", correct:false,
     why:"EBITDA is sometimes used as a proxy for cash flow, which makes this answer tempting. But a DCF projects free cash flows year by year, adds a terminal value and discounts them at the cost of capital. '8x EBITDA' applies a market multiple to one year's earnings, which is the comparable-multiples method."},
    {t:"A net-asset (book value) valuation", correct:false,
     why:"Both methods produce a single value, so they can be confused. But net-asset valuation adds up assets less liabilities from the balance sheet, which suits property or investment companies. An EBITDA multiple values the business's earning power using benchmarks from comparable companies or deals."},
    {t:"A liquidation valuation", correct:false,
     why:"A multiple can be applied to any business, including a failing one, which may make this sound possible. But liquidation value estimates what the assets would fetch if broken up and sold. An EBITDA multiple assumes the business continues to trade and values its ongoing earnings."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15", takeaway:"'8x EBITDA' = comparable multiples."
},

{ id:"f0x-14-03", lesson:"0.14", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A target's net income last year was S$10m, including a one-off S$4m gain on selling a warehouse. Peers trade at 12x earnings. The seller's banker proposes a value of S$120m (12 × S$10m).",
  prompt:"What is the flaw?",
  options:[
    {t:"Exclude the one-off gain: about S$72m at 12x", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan Ch 15 notes that when using P/E multiples 'we need to make sure that they are applied to permanent income', meaning income that excludes non-recurring items. A one-off gain on selling a warehouse will not happen again, so capitalising it at 12x treats a single event as if it recurred every year. Stripping it out leaves S$6m of recurring earnings and an indicated value of about S$72m. For the lawyer, this is a reminder of why financial diligence focuses on the 'quality of earnings' (Intro 2.6) and why buyers ask for warranties on the accounts and on the absence of unusual items in the period used to price the deal."},
    {t:"There is no flaw: net income is net income", correct:false,
     why:"Net income is the reported figure, so using it can seem objective. But Gaughan Ch 15 says multiples should be applied to permanent income. A one-off gain will not recur, and multiplying it by 12 treats a single event as if it happened every year, overstating value by about S$48m."},
    {t:"The multiple should be applied to revenue instead", correct:false,
     why:"Revenue multiples are a legitimate alternative, so switching measure can look like a fix. But the problem is not the choice of measure; it is a non-recurring item distorting earnings. The correct step is to strip out the one-off gain and apply the P/E multiple to recurring earnings."},
    {t:"The gain should be multiplied by a higher multiple because it is cash", correct:false,
     why:"The sale did bring in cash, which may seem to justify a premium. But value from a one-off event should not be capitalised at any multiple. If the cash remains in the business, it is counted once in the net-cash adjustment between enterprise and equity value."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15; Intro 2.6", takeaway:"Apply multiples to recurring earnings, not one-offs."
},

{ id:"r0-14-04", lesson:"0.14", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A valuation uses recent M&A deals for similar companies as its benchmark. What is this method, and what must you remember about the prices it uses?",
  options:[
    {t:"Precedent transactions, which embed a control premium", correct:true,
     why:"Precedent-transaction analysis takes the multiples paid in actual acquisitions of similar targets (Gaughan Ch 15's OCI example derives an average EBITDA multiple from four past acquisitions). Because those were acquisitions of control, the prices include a control premium and the buyers' expected synergies. Trading comparables, by contrast, use the market prices of listed peers, which reflect minority, non-controlling trades; Gaughan notes that a stock price at a moment in time 'does not reflect a control premium that normally accompanies acquisition offers'. So precedent transactions are the right benchmark for pricing a control acquisition, and the wrong one for a minority stake. Mixing the two up either overpays for a minority interest or underprices a sale of control."},
    {t:"Discounted cash flow, which ignores any control premium", correct:false,
     why:"A DCF also produces a value for a deal, which may make it seem like the method described. But a DCF is built from the target's own projected cash flows, not from prices paid for other companies. Benchmarking against recent acquisitions is precedent-transaction analysis."},
    {t:"Trading comparables, which already include a control premium", correct:false,
     why:"Trading comparables also benchmark against similar companies, so they are easily confused with precedents. But they use undisturbed market prices for small, non-controlling trades. Gaughan notes that a share price at a moment in time does not reflect a control premium; precedent deal prices do."},
    {t:"Net asset value, based on the balance sheet", correct:false,
     why:"NAV is another common benchmark, but it is built from the target's balance sheet, not from other deals. Using prices paid in recent acquisitions of similar companies is precedent-transaction analysis, and those prices already include a control premium."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15", takeaway:"Precedent transactions embed a control premium; trading comps don't."
},

{ id:"ap0-14-03", lesson:"0.14", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A fund is buying a non-controlling 10% stake in a listed company and wants a benchmark for the price.",
  prompt:"Which comparables are more appropriate, and why?",
  options:[
    {t:"Trading comparables, without a control premium", correct:true,
     why:"Precedent-transaction multiples come from control acquisitions, so they include a control premium for the power to direct the business and often the value of expected synergies. A 10% holder gets neither: it cannot appoint the board, set strategy or capture synergies. Gaughan Ch 15 explains the control premium as the price of that 'additional control characteristic'. Paying a control multiple for a minority stake would transfer value to the seller. Trading comparables, the market prices of listed peers, reflect the value of non-controlling interests and are the better benchmark. The fund's lawyer would then focus on the minority protections the price does not include: information rights, board observer seats, anti-dilution, and exit rights."},
    {t:"Precedent transactions, because they are always higher", correct:false,
     why:"A seller of a minority stake will naturally prefer the higher benchmark. But precedent transactions are higher because they include a control premium and expected synergies. A 10% holder gets neither control nor synergies, so paying a control multiple would transfer value to the seller."},
    {t:"Neither: minority stakes can't be valued", correct:false,
     why:"Minority stakes lack control, which might suggest they cannot be valued sensibly. But they are valued every day on the stock market. The point is to match the benchmark to the position: trading comparables reflect minority prices and fit a 10% stake."},
    {t:"Liquidation value", correct:false,
     why:"Liquidation value is a floor in some analyses, which may make it seem a cautious choice. But it estimates break-up proceeds and suits a business that will stop trading. The company here is a going concern, and the fund is buying into its ongoing earnings."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15", takeaway:"Minority stake → trading comps, not control-deal multiples."
},

{ id:"f0x-14-04", lesson:"0.14", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A buyer is valuing two otherwise identical businesses: one listed on SGX, the other a closely held private company whose shares have no market.",
  prompt:"Which valuation adjustment does Gaughan say may be especially relevant to the private company?",
  options:[
    {t:"A marketability discount for the unlisted shares", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan Ch 15 notes that many techniques apply to both public and private companies, such as the choice of discount rate and comparable multiples, but that some, 'such as the marketability discount, may be more relevant to the valuation of closely held businesses'. The marketability of shares varies considerably: listed shares can be sold quickly at a known price, while shares in a private company may take time and cost to sell, if a buyer can be found at all. A valuer may therefore discount the value of private-company shares relative to listed peers. Note the interaction with control: a buyer of 100% of a private company gets control, which carries its own premium, while a minority stake in a private company may attract both a minority discount and a marketability discount. That is why minority holders in private companies negotiate exit rights in shareholders' agreements."},
    {t:"A control premium, because private companies are always controlled", correct:false,
     why:"Many private companies are owner-controlled, so a control premium can sound relevant. But whether a premium applies depends on what is bought: a controlling block or a minority stake. Gaughan Ch 15 singles out the marketability discount as especially relevant to closely held businesses, because their shares cannot be sold readily."},
    {t:"No adjustment: identical businesses have identical values", correct:false,
     why:"If the businesses are identical, it may seem their shares should be worth the same. But the shares differ: one can be sold quickly on SGX at a known price, the other may take time and cost to sell, if a buyer can be found. Gaughan notes that this difference in marketability affects value."},
    {t:"A liquidity premium for the private company", correct:false,
     why:"Investors sometimes speak of an illiquidity premium in expected returns, which may cause confusion. But in valuing a share, illiquidity reduces what a buyer will pay today. That is why Gaughan describes a marketability discount, not a premium, for closely held businesses."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15", takeaway:"Closely held shares may carry a marketability discount."
},

{ id:"d3-005", lesson:"0.14", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which valuation method values a target from its projected future free cash flows?",
  options:[
    {t:"Discounted cash flow (DCF)", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan Ch 15 describes DCF as computing enterprise value as the present value of projected cash flows: the individually projected free cash flows for the forecast years (often five) plus a capitalised terminal value for the years beyond. Free cash flow is broadly EBITDA less capital expenditure needed to maintain the business, changes in working capital and cash taxes, which is the cash available to all capital providers. Discounting uses the weighted average cost of capital. Gaughan stresses that the terminal value is often the largest part of the total, so the long-term growth assumption is crucial; flawed growth assumptions 'can result in disastrous overpayment'. He also cites Kaplan and Ruback's study finding that DCF estimates in leveraged transactions were, at the median, within 10% of actual transaction values."},
    {t:"Net asset value from the balance sheet", correct:false,
     why:"NAV is a valuation method and does use the balance sheet's figures for the future in some sense, but it values assets less liabilities as they stand. It does not project future cash flows. The method built on projected free cash flows and a terminal value is DCF."},
    {t:"Comparable companies", correct:false,
     why:"Comparable-companies analysis does look forward when it uses forward multiples, which can blur the line. But it borrows peers' market multiples rather than projecting the target's own cash flows. DCF is the intrinsic method that builds value from the target's projected free cash flows."},
    {t:"Accretion/dilution analysis", correct:false,
     why:"Accretion and dilution analysis uses projected earnings, which may make it seem like a valuation method. But it measures the effect of a deal on the acquirer's EPS, not the target's value. Gaughan shows that EPS can rise without any value being created."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15", takeaway:"DCF = PV of projected FCF + terminal value."
},

{ id:"f0x-14-05", lesson:"0.14", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A beverage brand has grown 40% a year for three years. The buyer's DCF assumes that growth continues for five more years and then levels off at 8% a year forever. The terminal value is 80% of the total valuation.",
  prompt:"What lesson does Gaughan draw from a case like Quaker Oats and Snapple?",
  options:[
    {t:"Extrapolated growth in the terminal value leads to overpaying", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan Ch 15 uses Quaker Oats' acquisition of Snapple as 'a classic example of overpaying'. Quaker paid US$1.7 billion in 1994 and sold Snapple for US$300 million in 1997. It had assumed Snapple's impressive past growth would continue, when much of the brand's distribution potential was already used up; it also mishandled the marketing and distribution after completion. Gaughan's point is that because the terminal value is so large a share of a DCF, the long-term growth assumption is critical, and where growth is slowing it must be reflected in a lower growth rate or a lower multiple. Here, 80% of the value sits in the terminal value, so the 8% perpetual growth assumption deserves hard questions. This is valuation work for the client and its bankers, but it is also the hubris and winner's-curse story from Lesson 1.1."},
    {t:"Past growth is the most reliable predictor, so the DCF is sound", correct:false,
     why:"Recent growth is concrete evidence, so extrapolating it feels data-driven. But Gaughan's Quaker-Snapple study shows the danger: Snapple's growth was slowing because its distribution was largely saturated. Past growth must be tested for sustainability, and slowing growth needs a lower rate or a lower multiple."},
    {t:"DCF should never include a terminal value", correct:false,
     why:"Because the terminal value dominates the result, removing it might seem to remove the risk. But a business is expected to keep earning beyond the forecast period, so a DCF needs a terminal value. The fault here is the 8% perpetual growth assumption, not the terminal value as such."},
    {t:"The lawyer should rewrite the DCF", correct:false,
     why:"A lawyer who spots an optimistic model may want to fix it. But the Intro (2.1) leaves valuation to the client and its advisers. The lawyer's contribution is risk allocation: for example, an earn-out tied to actual growth shifts the risk of the forecast back to the seller."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15 (Quaker–Snapple); Wan Ch 1 [1.38]–[1.41]", takeaway:"Terminal-value growth assumptions drive DCF; extrapolated growth leads to overpayment."
},

{ id:"f0-14-01", lesson:"0.14", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is a 'control premium'?",
  options:[
    {t:"The extra paid over market price for control", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan Ch 15 explains that there is 'a major difference' between the price of a single share on an exchange and the price of a block that gives effective control. The buyer of control can direct the business, appoint the board and deploy the assets as it chooses, and 'this additional control characteristic commands its own price'. That price is the control premium. In takeovers it is usually measured against the undisturbed share price before the bid. It links several parts of the course. The Code's mandatory-offer rules make a bidder share the premium with all shareholders. Precedent-transaction multiples embed it. And for the buyer's shareholders, the deal only creates value if the synergies exceed the premium. A minority stake, by contrast, may be priced at a discount for lack of control."},
    {t:"A discount for buying a minority stake", correct:false,
     why:"Control premiums and minority discounts are two sides of the same idea, so they are easy to swap. A minority discount reduces the value of a non-controlling stake. A control premium is the extra a buyer pays above the market price of single shares to gain control of the business."},
    {t:"The break fee paid if a deal fails", correct:false,
     why:"Break fees and control premiums both appear in takeover discussions, so they get confused. But a break fee is a deal-protection payment by the target if a deal fails, capped at about 1% under Code Rule 13. The control premium is part of the price the buyer pays for the shares."},
    {t:"The stamp duty on the transfer", correct:false,
     why:"Stamp duty is a cost of buying shares, so it can look like part of what the buyer pays over market. But it is a tax on the transfer, paid to IRAS, at 0.2% for shares. The control premium is value paid to the selling shareholders for the right to control the company."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15; Intro 2.2", takeaway:"Control premium = price of control above the market price."
},

{ id:"f0-14-02", lesson:"0.14", type:"mcq", dia:"valueBridge",
  prompt:"For an acquisition to create value for the BUYER's shareholders, what must be true?",
  options:[
    {t:"Net synergies must exceed the premium paid", correct:true,
     why:"The buyer pays the target's standalone value plus a premium. If the synergies it can actually achieve, net of integration costs, are worth less than that premium, the buyer's shareholders lose value even if the combined business is bigger, because the excess has been transferred to the target's shareholders. Gaughan Ch 4 notes that synergy is the most-cited justification for deals and, especially on the revenue side, the hardest to deliver. Wan Ch 1's event studies make the same point from the market's side: the bidder's share price falls on announcement when investors think the premium exceeds the likely synergies. The Intro (2.2) calls synergy 'the most often over-promised' justification. For the lawyer, it explains why boards need careful advice and why integration risk is priced rather than warranted."},
    {t:"The combined company must be larger than the buyer alone", correct:false,
     why:"Size and market share are common deal goals, and managers often present them as success. But Gaughan's test is value per shareholder. A buyer can grow and still lose value if it pays a premium larger than the synergies it can achieve. That is the agency-cost story from Lesson 1.1."},
    {t:"The deal must be paid entirely in cash", correct:false,
     why:"Cash deals avoid issuing shares, which might look safer for value. But the form of consideration does not decide whether value is created. A cash deal at too high a premium destroys value as surely as a share deal; the test is net synergies against the premium."},
    {t:"The deal must be accretive to EPS", correct:false,
     why:"Boards often announce that a deal is accretive as if that proved its merit. But accretion is an accounting effect on EPS. Gaughan's bootstrapping discussion shows EPS can rise with no synergies at all, so accretion does not show value creation."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 4, Ch 15; Wan Ch 1 [1.51]–[1.55]; Intro 2.2", takeaway:"Value created only if net synergies > premium."
},

{ id:"ap0-14-02", lesson:"0.14", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A target trades at a market value of S$200m. The buyer pays S$260m. It expects synergies worth S$50m in present-value terms, costing S$5m to achieve.",
  prompt:"Does the deal create value for the buyer's shareholders?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: S$45m net synergies < S$60m premium", correct:true,
     why:"The premium is S$260m − S$200m = S$60m. Net synergies are S$50m − S$5m = S$45m. The buyer is paying S$15m more for the synergies than they are worth, so its shareholders are worse off by roughly that amount, while the target's shareholders receive the full S$60m premium. This matches Wan's event-study framing in Lesson 1.1: the bidder's share price would be expected to fall on announcement, and the overall net gain (S$45m of synergies created) is positive, so the deal may still be a 'success' for the economy while being a poor one for the buyer's shareholders. Note also that if the synergies are mainly revenue synergies, Gaughan's warning that they are the harder kind to achieve makes the gap worse."},
    {t:"Yes: S$50m of synergies is a large amount", correct:false,
     why:"S$50m sounds substantial, and the deal does create synergies. But the buyer pays a S$60m premium for them, and they cost S$5m to achieve. Net synergies of S$45m are less than the premium, so the buyer's shareholders lose about S$15m while the target's shareholders gain S$60m."},
    {t:"Yes: any deal with synergies creates value", correct:false,
     why:"Synergies do add value to the combined business, so this sounds right at first. But the question is about the buyer's shareholders. If the premium paid exceeds the net synergies, the excess is transferred to the target's shareholders, leaving the buyer's shareholders worse off."},
    {t:"It can't be judged without the target's EPS", correct:false,
     why:"EPS figures are often quoted in deal announcements, which makes them seem decisive. But EPS effects depend on accounting and the relative P/E ratios, and can rise without value being created. Whether the deal creates value for the buyer depends on net synergies versus the premium."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 4, Ch 15; Wan Ch 1", takeaway:"Premium S$60m > net synergies S$45m → buyer loses ~S$15m."
},

{ id:"f0-14-03", lesson:"0.14", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What makes an all-share acquisition 'accretive' or 'dilutive', according to Gaughan?",
  options:[
    {t:"Whether EPS rises or falls, driven by the P/E gap", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan Ch 15 sets out two factors that drive the initial change in EPS in a share-for-share deal. First, the P/E differential: the higher the acquirer's P/E ratio relative to the target's, the greater the increase in the acquirer's EPS, because it issues relatively few highly valued shares for the target's earnings. Second, relative size: the larger the target's earnings relative to the acquirer's, the greater the increase. A deal that lifts EPS is 'accretive'; one that lowers it is 'dilutive'. Gaughan also notes that a buyer may accept initial dilution if it expects to break even within a period it considers acceptable. None of this says whether the deal creates value. That depends on whether the price is justified by the target's cash flows and achievable synergies."},
    {t:"Whether it reduces the acquirer's share count", correct:false,
     why:"Share count obviously affects EPS, so focusing on it is understandable. But an all-share deal always increases the share count. Whether EPS rises depends on how much earnings come in relative to the shares issued, which Gaughan ties to the P/E differential and the target's relative earnings."},
    {t:"Whether it is paid entirely in cash", correct:false,
     why:"Cash deals avoid issuing new shares, so they can seem the natural route to accretion. But accretion just means EPS goes up, and it can happen in cash or share deals. In a share deal it depends on the acquirer's P/E relative to the target's and the size of the target's earnings."},
    {t:"Whether it triggers a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"Both concepts arise in public acquisitions, which may cause the mix-up. But a mandatory offer is a Code obligation triggered by crossing 30% of voting rights. Accretion and dilution are financial measures of the effect on the acquirer's EPS and have nothing to do with the Code."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15", takeaway:"EPS change driven by P/E differential and relative earnings."
},

{ id:"ap0-14-04", lesson:"0.14", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A buyer trades at 25x earnings and pays for a target valued at 10x earnings entirely in new shares, with no expected synergies. Management announces that the deal is 'immediately accretive, proving it creates value', and predicts the share price will rise.",
  prompt:"What does Gaughan's discussion of 'bootstrapping' suggest?",
  options:[
    {t:"EPS rises mechanically; no value is created", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan Ch 15 calls this 'bootstrapping EPS': increasing EPS simply by buying companies with lower P/E ratios. It was common among 1960s conglomerates, when the market was not efficient in valuing them. Gaughan says two conditions are needed for it to lift the share price: the market must keep applying at least the acquirer's pre-merger P/E to the combined earnings, and the target must have a lower P/E. 'If the market is efficient, bootstrapping EPS is not possible': the post-merger P/E will be a weighted average of the two companies' P/Es, so the share price does not rise just because EPS did. With no synergies, the combined business is worth the sum of its parts. Management's claim confuses an accounting effect with value creation."},
    {t:"Agree: accretion always means value creation", correct:false,
     why:"Management's claim sounds intuitive: higher EPS should mean a higher share price. But Gaughan explains that in an efficient market the combined company's P/E becomes a weighted average of the two, so the price does not rise just because EPS did. With no synergies, no value is created."},
    {t:"The deal must be dilutive, because issuing shares always dilutes", correct:false,
     why:"Issuing new shares does dilute existing holders' percentage ownership, which makes this answer intuitive. But EPS can still rise when a high-P/E buyer issues relatively few shares for a low-P/E target's earnings. That mechanical rise is exactly what Gaughan calls bootstrapping."},
    {t:"It depends on the stamp duty", correct:false,
     why:"Stamp duty is a transaction cost and does slightly reduce value, but at 0.2% it is minor and has no bearing on the EPS arithmetic. The question is whether a mechanical EPS rise reflects real value; Gaughan's answer is that it does not in an efficient market."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15 (bootstrapping)", takeaway:"Bootstrapping: EPS up ≠ value up; efficient markets blend the P/E."
},

{ id:"f0-14-04", lesson:"0.14", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each valuation method to what it does.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Discounted cash flow (DCF)", r:"Values projected free cash flows (plus a terminal value) discounted to present value"},
    {l:"Comparable companies", r:"Applies listed peers' trading multiples to the target"},
    {l:"Precedent transactions", r:"Uses multiples paid in similar past acquisitions (control premium included)"},
    {l:"Net asset value", r:"Values the underlying assets less liabilities"}
  ],
  why:"Financial advisers usually triangulate value using several methods, and Gaughan Ch 15 covers the main ones. DCF is the intrinsic method: free cash flows for a forecast period plus a terminal value, discounted at the cost of capital; it is only as good as its growth and discount-rate assumptions. Comparable companies applies the trading multiples of similar listed businesses, which reflect minority, non-controlling prices. Precedent transactions uses multiples paid in actual acquisitions, which embed a control premium and expected synergies. Net asset value sums assets less liabilities, and suits asset-heavy businesses such as property or investment holding companies. In Singapore practice these are the figures an IFA uses when telling independent shareholders whether an offer is fair, and the figures a dissenting minority argues over when challenging a squeeze-out price.",
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15", takeaway:"DCF · trading comps · precedent deals · NAV."
},

{ id:"f0x-14-06", lesson:"0.14", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An IFA is advising ListCo's independent directors on a cash offer at S$1.50. ListCo's undisturbed share price was S$1.15. Peers trade at 9x EBITDA, and recent take-privates in the sector were done at 11x to 12x; the offer implies 11.5x. ListCo's assets are mainly property, with a revalued NAV of S$1.80 per share.",
  prompt:"Which point would most concern a minority shareholder asking whether the offer is fair?",
  options:[
    {t:"The offer is below revalued NAV per share", correct:true,
     why:"Each method tells a different story, which is why advisers triangulate. Against the undisturbed price, S$1.50 is a 30% premium. Against trading comparables (9x), 11.5x looks generous, but trading comps reflect minority prices, so a control offer should exceed them. Against precedent take-privates (11x–12x), which already embed a control premium, 11.5x is in the range. But for a property-heavy company, net asset value is often the most relevant measure, and the offer is S$0.30 below revalued NAV per share. A minority holder would ask why it should sell for less than the underlying assets are worth, and the IFA would need to address it, for example by explaining discounts at which comparable property companies trade, costs of realisation, or the illiquidity of the shares. This is the sort of valuation debate behind 'fair and reasonable' opinions."},
    {t:"Nothing: any premium to market makes an offer fair", correct:false,
     why:"A 30% premium to the undisturbed price is attractive, and IFAs do treat it as an important measure. But it is one measure among several. For a property-heavy company, NAV is often the most relevant comparison, and an offer below revalued NAV is the point a minority holder will press."},
    {t:"The offer exceeds trading multiples, so it must be too high", correct:false,
     why:"An offer above trading multiples can look generous or even excessive. But trading comparables reflect minority, non-controlling prices, and a control offer is expected to exceed them. The better comparison for a control price is precedent transactions, and the offer sits within that range."},
    {t:"Valuation is irrelevant to whether an offer is fair", correct:false,
     why:"Fairness can sound like a matter of process rather than numbers. But an IFA's 'fair and reasonable' opinion is fundamentally a valuation exercise, comparing the offer against market price, trading and transaction multiples, and NAV. Minority holders rely on that analysis when deciding whether to accept."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 15; Intro 2.7 (IFA)", takeaway:"Triangulate: market premium, trading comps, precedents and NAV can disagree."
}

]);

;
