/* DealCraft — Chapter 2 · Lesson 2.1 The Private SPA & Deal Anatomy (audited Oct 2026).
   Sources: Intro 2.4–2.11; Wan Ch 2, Ch 4; Stamp Duties Act (shares). Supersedes familyA/reinforceA/auditA/deep* for 2.1. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"fa-1-01", lesson:"A.1", type:"mcq", dia:"dealGap",
  prompt:"For a PRIVATE company acquisition, what is the master contract?",
  options:[
    {t:"The sale and purchase agreement (SPA)", correct:true,
     why:"In a private deal the buyer negotiates directly with a small number of identifiable owners, so the transaction is a private contract: the SPA. Everything that matters is in it or hangs off it: the price mechanism, the conditions to completion, the interim covenants, the warranties and their limits, the indemnities and the completion deliverables. Because there are no dispersed shareholders to protect, the parties have broad freedom to structure, condition and price the deal as they agree. The constraints come from general contract law, company law (the constitution, s 76, directors' duties) and any sector regulator, rather than from a takeover regime."},
    {t:"The scheme document and explanatory statement", correct:false,
     why:"A scheme document and explanatory statement belong to a court-sanctioned scheme of arrangement under s 210 of the Companies Act, which is the route used to take a listed company private (or to reorganise a company with many members). A private company with a handful of shareholders can simply sell its shares under a contract. You would only use a scheme for a private company in unusual cases, for example where a holdout minority must be bound and the majorities can be reached."},
    {t:"The offer document under the Take-Over Code", correct:false,
     why:"An offer document is the formal document a bidder sends to shareholders in a Code-regulated takeover. The Code is aimed at listed companies, listed REITs and business trusts, and unlisted public companies with more than 50 shareholders and net tangible assets of S$5m or more. A private company is outside that scope, so there is no offer document; the bargain is recorded in the SPA."},
    {t:"The implementation agreement", correct:false,
     why:"An implementation agreement is used in a recommended public deal, typically a scheme, to set out how the bidder and the target board will cooperate: timetable, conduct of business, deal protection, conditions. It is a public-deal document because in a public deal the bidder contracts with the target company, not with its many shareholders. In a private deal the buyer contracts directly with the shareholders, so the SPA does that job."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 4; Intro 2.4, 2.8", takeaway:"Private target = SPA (a risk-allocation contract); no Take-Over Code."
},

{ id:"d2-006", lesson:"A.1", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which part of the SPA is the primary home of the risk-shifting the parties fight over?",
  options:[
    {t:"Price, conditions, warranties and indemnities", correct:true,
     why:"Reading an SPA is reading a risk-allocation document. The middle clauses (consideration and price mechanism, conditions, interim covenants, warranties, limitations on liability and indemnities) decide who carries which risk of the business. The price mechanism decides who bears trading between the accounts date and completion; conditions decide who bears regulatory and consent risk in the gap; warranties and indemnities decide who bears hidden liabilities; the limitations decide how much of that protection is real. That is where most negotiating time is spent. Parties, recitals, completion mechanics and boilerplate still matter, but they rarely move value."},
    {t:"The notices and service clause", correct:false,
     why:"Notices boilerplate decides how and where formal communications must be sent. It can matter at the margin, for example if a warranty claim notice is served to the wrong address and misses the time limit, but it does not itself allocate any business risk. The commercial tug-of-war is over price, conditions, warranties, limitations and indemnities."},
    {t:"The recitals", correct:false,
     why:"Recitals describe the background (who the parties are, what is being sold). They are not operative provisions and generally create no rights or obligations of their own, although a court may use them to understand the purpose of the operative terms. No risk is shifted by a recital."},
    {t:"The counterparts and execution clause", correct:false,
     why:"A counterparts clause allows the parties to sign separate copies that together form one agreement, which is useful for remote signing. It is purely mechanical. It has nothing to do with who bears the risk of a hidden tax liability or a lost customer."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.8", takeaway:"SPA risk-shifting lives in price + conditions + warranties + limitations + indemnities."
},

{ id:"fa-1-02", lesson:"A.1", type:"order",
  prompt:"Order these private-deal stages by when they typically first happen.",
  steps:["NDA / confidentiality agreement","Letter of intent / heads of terms (mostly non-binding)","Confirmatory due diligence during exclusivity","SPA signed, with the disclosure letter delivered","Completion deliverables (transfers, resignations, board resolutions)"],
  why:"The NDA comes first because the seller will not open its books without it. Heads of terms then record the headline deal and usually grant binding exclusivity, which is what persuades the buyer to spend money on diligence. Diligence findings feed the SPA: they decide which warranties the buyer asks for, which risks need specific indemnities and what the price should be. The disclosure letter is delivered at signing, against the warranties, so it belongs with the SPA rather than after it. Completion is the final step, when the share transfer forms, resignations, board resolutions and funds change hands. If there are conditions (merger control, MAS approval, consents), there is a gap between signing and completion.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 4; Intro 2.5, 2.8", takeaway:"NDA → heads + exclusivity → diligence → SPA + disclosure letter → completion."
},

{ id:"fa-1-03", lesson:"A.1", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does a shareholders' agreement (SHA) do that an SPA does not?",
  options:[
    {t:"It governs the ongoing relationship after completion", correct:true,
     why:"An SPA governs a one-off event: the transfer of shares and the risk allocation around it. Once completion happens and the parties are co-owners (a JV, a minority stake, a management rollover), the SPA has done its job. The SHA, read with the constitution, governs what happens next. It sets reserved matters that need the investor's consent, board composition and quorum, how deadlock is broken, pre-emption and transfer restrictions, drag-along and tag-along, anti-dilution and the exit route. A buyer taking less than 100% should negotiate the SHA as carefully as the SPA, because the SHA is what protects the value of its stake for years afterwards."},
    {t:"It qualifies the seller's warranties", correct:false,
     why:"Qualifying the warranties is the job of the disclosure letter, which is delivered at signing and sets out the exceptions to the warranties in the SPA. The SHA looks forward to how the company will be run; it does not cut down the seller's liability for the state of the business at the time of sale."},
    {t:"It sets the purchase price and payment mechanics", correct:false,
     why:"Price, deferred consideration, earn-outs and the completion-accounts or locked-box mechanism all sit in the SPA. An SHA may contain valuation formulas for future transfers (for example a good leaver/bad leaver price), but it does not set the price for the acquisition itself."},
    {t:"It transfers the shares at completion", correct:false,
     why:"The SPA contains the obligation to sell and buy, and the transfer is effected by the completion deliverables: the instrument of transfer, stamping, and (for a private company) the update to the electronic register of members kept by ACRA. The SHA regulates the parties' relationship as shareholders once they are both on the register."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.8", takeaway:"SPA = the transfer; SHA = the ongoing co-ownership relationship."
},

{ id:"fa-1-04", lesson:"A.1", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Because the target is a private company, the parties can freely agree conditions, price mechanics and warranties without the Take-Over Code constraining them.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. A private company (no more than 50 members) is outside the Code, which protects dispersed shareholders of listed companies, listed REITs and business trusts, and must also be observed by unlisted public companies with more than 50 shareholders and NTA of S$5m or more. In a private deal there is no equality-of-treatment rule, no mandatory offer, no restriction on deal protection and no Code timetable. The parties remain bound by general contract law, company law (the constitution's pre-emption rights, s 76, directors' duties), competition law and any sector regulator. Within those limits, they can price, condition and allocate risk as they choose."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"It is true. The Code's reach is defined by the type of company. A private company is never within it. The point to watch is the edge case: an unlisted PUBLIC company with more than 50 shareholders and NTA of S$5m or more must observe the Code's letter and spirit where possible, so 'unlisted' does not always mean 'outside the Code'."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) Introduction; Wan Ch 3; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Private company = freedom of contract; the Code does not bite (but watch unlisted public cos >50 holders, NTA ≥ S$5m)."
},

{ id:"d5-005", lesson:"A.1", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the function of the disclosure letter in a private acquisition?",
  options:[
    {t:"It qualifies the warranties by disclosure", correct:true,
     why:"The warranties ask questions about the business; the disclosure letter gives the seller's honest answers. It usually contains general disclosures (public registers, the data room) and specific disclosures against particular warranties. Anything fairly disclosed cannot found a warranty claim, so the buyer must deal with it some other way: a price cut, a specific indemnity, a retention or a condition. Anything not disclosed stays at the seller's risk. Buyers therefore negotiate hard over the standard of disclosure ('fair', with enough detail to identify the nature and scope of the matter) and over whether the whole data room can be generally disclosed."},
    {t:"It sets the purchase price", correct:false,
     why:"The price and how it is adjusted sit in the SPA's consideration clause. A disclosure may lead the buyer to renegotiate the price, but the letter itself only qualifies the warranties."},
    {t:"It lists the conditions precedent", correct:false,
     why:"Conditions precedent are in the SPA's conditions clause. A disclosure may prompt the buyer to add a condition (for example, a key customer's consent), but the disclosure letter does not create one."},
    {t:"It is the seller's non-binding term sheet", correct:false,
     why:"The non-binding term sheet is the letter of intent or heads of terms, which comes much earlier. The disclosure letter is delivered at signing and is part of the binding bargain, because it fixes the scope of the warranties the seller has given."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.8, 2.10", takeaway:"Disclosure letter qualifies warranties: disclosed = carved out; undisclosed = seller's risk."
},

{ id:"rA-1-01", lesson:"A.1", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In a private SPA, what is the job of the CONDITIONS clause?",
  options:[
    {t:"It lists the gates to be met before completion", correct:true,
     why:"Signing creates a binding contract, but completion only happens when the conditions are satisfied or waived. The conditions clause therefore governs the gap between signing and completion. It lists the gates (merger control, MAS or other regulatory approval, third-party consents, shareholder approvals, sometimes 'warranties true at completion' or 'no MAC'), says who must work to satisfy them and to what standard (reasonable or best endeavours), who can waive each one, and the long-stop date after which either party may walk away. Making a risk a condition rather than a warranty is powerful for a buyer: it can refuse to complete instead of having to complete and sue."},
    {t:"It fixes the purchase price and payment mechanics", correct:false,
     why:"Price and payment sit in the consideration clause. Conditions are about whether completion happens at all, not how much is paid when it does."},
    {t:"It lists the seller's exceptions to its warranties", correct:false,
     why:"Exceptions to the warranties are in the disclosure letter. Conditions are not exceptions to anything; they are the prerequisites to the obligation to complete."},
    {t:"It sets the governing law and dispute forum", correct:false,
     why:"Governing law and jurisdiction or arbitration are boilerplate. They decide where and under which law disputes are resolved, not whether completion takes place."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2; Intro 2.5", takeaway:"Conditions clause = the gates to completion, the standard of effort, and the long-stop date."
},

{ id:"rA-1-02", lesson:"A.1", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the difference between WARRANTIES and COVENANTS in an SPA?",
  options:[
    {t:"Warranties state facts; covenants are promises", correct:true,
     why:"A warranty is a contractual statement about the past or present state of the business: the accounts are true and fair, there is no litigation, the material contracts are listed. If it is untrue, the buyer claims damages for the loss in value caused, subject to disclosure and the SPA's limitations. A covenant is a promise about future conduct: carry on the business in the ordinary course until completion, do not declare dividends, procure a consent, do not compete for two years. Breach of a covenant is a breach of promise; the remedy may include an injunction as well as damages. Mixing them up leads to the wrong remedy being pleaded."},
    {t:"They are two words for the same clause", correct:false,
     why:"They do different work. One allocates the risk that a fact is wrong; the other imposes an obligation to act. An SPA usually has a warranties schedule and separate covenant clauses for conduct of business and restrictive covenants."},
    {t:"Warranties bind the buyer; covenants bind the seller", correct:false,
     why:"Both sides give both. The seller gives most warranties, but the buyer warrants its capacity and authority and sometimes its financing. The buyer gives covenants too, for example to retain books and records or not to solicit employees. The distinction is fact versus promise, not who gives it."},
    {t:"Covenants only ever appear in public deals", correct:false,
     why:"Interim conduct-of-business covenants, restrictive covenants and further-assurance covenants are central to private SPAs. A buyer that agrees a locked-box price, for example, relies heavily on the seller's covenant not to leak value before completion."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10", takeaway:"Warranty = statement of fact; covenant = promise to act or refrain."
},

{ id:"rA-1-03", lesson:"A.1", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: heads of terms (a term sheet) for a private deal are usually intended to be legally binding as to the commercial terms.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Heads of terms are normally expressed to be 'subject to contract' and non-binding as to the commercial deal (price, structure, key terms). Only specific clauses are made binding: exclusivity, confidentiality, costs, governing law, and sometimes a no-poach. The reasons are practical. The buyer has not yet done diligence, so it cannot commit to a price; the seller does not want to be locked in before seeing the buyer's mark-up of the SPA. The heads set the direction and create a moral commitment, but the binding bargain is the SPA. Whether a document is binding is a question of construction, so the 'non-binding' wording must be clear."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The standard practice is the opposite. If commercial terms were binding at heads stage, the parties would be bound before diligence and before the detailed terms are agreed, and courts might struggle to enforce an incomplete bargain. Parties therefore state expressly which clauses bind and which do not."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Heads of terms: non-binding on the deal, binding on exclusivity, confidentiality and costs."
},

{ id:"rA-1-04", lesson:"A.1", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A seller grants the buyer a period of EXCLUSIVITY (a lock-out) early in a private deal. What does it achieve?",
  options:[
    {t:"It stops the seller talking to other buyers for a time", correct:true,
     why:"Diligence, lawyers, accountants and financing are expensive, and a buyer will not incur those costs if the seller can use its work to extract a better price from someone else. Exclusivity is a negative promise: for a fixed period the seller will not solicit, negotiate with or provide information to other bidders. It is one of the few binding parts of the heads of terms. It does not oblige the seller to sell. A well-drafted clause is time-limited, covers the seller's advisers and affiliates, and may require the seller to tell the buyer about unsolicited approaches."},
    {t:"It fixes the final purchase price", correct:false,
     why:"Exclusivity is about process, not price. The price in the heads remains non-binding and is usually adjusted after diligence. A seller can grant exclusivity and still refuse to sell at the buyer's eventual price."},
    {t:"It transfers ownership of the shares immediately", correct:false,
     why:"Ownership passes only on completion under the SPA, after any conditions are met. Exclusivity is a negative promise not to talk to rivals for a period."},
    {t:"It waives the buyer's need for warranties", correct:false,
     why:"Exclusivity has nothing to do with the contractual protection the buyer will negotiate. The buyer still needs warranties, indemnities and limitations in the SPA."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Exclusivity buys the buyer a safe window to spend on the deal; it does not oblige a sale."
},

{ id:"au-1-01", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A locked-box SPA fixes the price off 31 March accounts. On 15 May, before completion, the target pays its seller a S$2m special dividend. Permitted leakage covers only ordinary salaries.",
  prompt:"What is the buyer's cleanest remedy?",
  options:[
    {t:"A dollar-for-dollar leakage claim for the S$2m", correct:true,
     why:"A locked-box price is fixed by reference to a balance sheet at the locked-box date, and the buyer takes the economic benefit and risk of trading from that date. The price is only fair if no value leaves the 'box' to the seller between the locked-box date and completion. The seller therefore covenants that there will be no leakage except listed permitted leakage, and promises to repay any leakage dollar for dollar. A special dividend to the seller is classic leakage. The buyer simply claims the S$2m: it does not need to prove that the share value fell, and warranty limitations such as baskets do not normally apply to the leakage covenant."},
    {t:"A completion-accounts adjustment", correct:false,
     why:"A locked-box deal has no completion accounts. That is its defining feature: the price is fixed at signing off historic accounts, giving price certainty and avoiding post-completion disputes. Leakage protection replaces the completion-accounts true-up. If the parties had chosen completion accounts, the dividend would reduce net assets at completion and the price would fall automatically."},
    {t:"A warranty claim, proving the dividend reduced the value of the shares", correct:false,
     why:"A warranty route is the wrong tool. The buyer would have to prove its loss in value, and the claim would be subject to disclosure, de minimis, basket, cap and time limits. The no-leakage covenant exists precisely so that the buyer recovers the exact amount that left the box without those hurdles."},
    {t:"Nothing: a lawfully declared dividend cannot be challenged", correct:false,
     why:"Whether the dividend was lawful under company law (paid out of profits, s 403) is a separate question from whether the seller broke its contract. The seller promised that no value would leave the box other than permitted leakage. The dividend may be valid as a corporate act and still be a breach of the SPA."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.9 [practice]", takeaway:"Locked-box leakage → $-for-$ recovery under the no-leakage covenant."
},

{ id:"au-1-02", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Diligence uncovers a live IRAS dispute over the target's 2023 transfer pricing. The seller lists it in the disclosure letter against the tax warranty.",
  prompt:"How should the buyer protect itself?",
  options:[
    {t:"A specific indemnity, perhaps with escrow", correct:true,
     why:"Once a matter is fairly disclosed, the warranty is qualified and the buyer cannot sue on it. A known, identifiable risk therefore needs a different tool. A specific indemnity is a promise to pay the amount of a defined loss (here, any tax, interest and penalties from the 2023 transfer-pricing dispute) whether or not it was disclosed. It is usually outside the general warranty limitations, has its own cap and time limit, and gives the seller conduct-of-claims rights or obligations. Because the seller may have spent the sale proceeds by the time IRAS decides, the buyer often asks for an escrow or a retention from the price to make the indemnity collectable."},
    {t:"Rely on the tax warranty", correct:false,
     why:"The disclosure carves the dispute out of the warranty. Relying on it would leave the buyer with no remedy for the one tax risk it actually knows about. This is the most common trap for buyers who do not read the disclosure letter against the diligence report."},
    {t:"Make the dispute's resolution a condition precedent to completion", correct:false,
     why:"Transfer-pricing disputes can take years to resolve. A condition tied to the outcome would stall the deal well past any sensible long-stop date. Conditions suit gates that will be passed within months (clearances, consents). A known liability of uncertain size is better priced or indemnified."},
    {t:"Ask the seller to delete the disclosure", correct:false,
     why:"Deleting a true disclosure does not remove the risk. It also invites a later fight over whether the buyer can claim for something it knew about at signing, which depends on the SPA's knowledge clause and is uncertain. The clean answer is to allocate the risk expressly."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10; Wan Ch 4", takeaway:"Known + disclosed risk → specific indemnity (secured by escrow), not a warranty."
},

{ id:"au-1-03", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Signed heads of terms give the buyer 60 days' exclusivity; everything else is expressed to be non-binding. On day 20 the seller signs an SPA with a rival.",
  prompt:"What can the buyer do?",
  options:[
    {t:"Sue for breach of exclusivity, not force the sale", correct:true,
     why:"Only the clauses expressed to be binding are enforceable. The seller broke a valid negative promise, so the buyer has a contractual damages claim. In practice damages are usually measured by the costs the buyer wasted on diligence and advisers in reliance on exclusivity; a claim for the lost profit on the deal is hard because the seller was never bound to sell and the price was never agreed. Some heads provide expressly for a cost reimbursement on breach to avoid that argument. The commercial terms were never binding, so there is nothing to specifically enforce."},
    {t:"Obtain specific performance of the heads of terms", correct:false,
     why:"Specific performance requires a binding obligation to perform. The heads said expressly that the commercial terms were not binding, and the SPA terms were never agreed. A court has no contract of sale to enforce."},
    {t:"Nothing, because heads of terms are never enforceable", correct:false,
     why:"Heads are a mix. Parties routinely make particular clauses binding (exclusivity, confidentiality, costs, governing law), and courts enforce those clauses like any other contract. The seller breached one of them."},
    {t:"Have the rival SPA set aside automatically", correct:false,
     why:"The rival is not a party to the heads and owes the buyer nothing. Breach of exclusivity gives a claim against the seller; it does not void the seller's later contract with a third party. A claim against the rival would need something more, such as knowingly inducing the breach."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.5, 2.8", takeaway:"Heads: only the binding clauses bite; exclusivity breach → damages (usually wasted costs), not the deal."
},

{ id:"au-1-04", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Warranties are given at signing and repeated at completion. Between the two, the target loses its biggest customer, making a 'no material contracts terminated' warranty untrue at completion. The SPA makes 'warranties true in all material respects at completion' a condition for the buyer's benefit.",
  prompt:"What is the buyer's position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Refuse to complete, or waive, complete and claim", correct:true,
     why:"A condition that exists for the buyer's benefit gives the buyer a choice. It can rely on the failed condition and decline to complete; the SPA will then usually terminate at the long-stop date or on notice. Or it can waive the condition, complete, and sue, because the repetition of the warranties at completion means they were also given as at that date and were untrue. If it completes, it should reserve its rights expressly, since some SPAs say that waiving a condition also waives claims for the underlying breach. Losing the biggest customer is likely to be 'material' on any reading."},
    {t:"It must complete, because the warranty was true at signing", correct:false,
     why:"Truth at signing is not enough here. The SPA repeats the warranties at completion and makes their truth at completion a condition. Without repetition and the condition, the buyer would be in a much weaker position: it would be relying on a MAC clause or an interim covenant."},
    {t:"The deal terminates automatically", correct:false,
     why:"Failure of a protective condition does not end the contract by itself. The party entitled to the benefit decides whether to rely on it or waive it. Automatic termination usually happens only if the condition is still unsatisfied at the long-stop date and the SPA says so."},
    {t:"Only the seller can decide whether to waive the condition", correct:false,
     why:"The condition was negotiated to protect the buyer. Waiver belongs to the party for whose benefit the condition was included, which is why SPAs label each condition as waivable by the buyer, by the seller or by both."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.11", takeaway:"Failed protective condition → buyer chooses: walk, or waive + claim on the repeated warranty."
},

{ id:"rA-1-05", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"BuyCo is acquiring 70% of a private company. The founder will keep 30% and stay as CEO. BuyCo plans an exit by trade sale in five years and wants to be able to sell 100% then.",
  prompt:"Where must BuyCo secure the right to force the founder to sell alongside it at exit?",
  options:[
    {t:"In an SHA giving BuyCo a drag-along right", correct:true,
     why:"The SPA governs the purchase of the 70%; once it completes, its job is done. The rights BuyCo needs for the next five years belong in the SHA, ideally mirrored in the constitution so they bind any future holder of the founder's shares. A drag-along allows a majority that accepts a bona fide third-party offer to require the minority to sell on the same terms, which lets BuyCo deliver 100% to a trade buyer. The founder will usually ask in return for a tag-along (the right to join any sale by BuyCo), a floor price, and limits on the warranties a dragged seller must give."},
    {t:"In the SPA's warranty schedule", correct:false,
     why:"Warranties are statements about the state of the business at signing and completion. They do not give future rights over the founder's remaining shares."},
    {t:"In the disclosure letter", correct:false,
     why:"The disclosure letter qualifies the warranties. It cannot create an obligation on the founder to sell in five years."},
    {t:"Nowhere: a 70% holder can always compel the minority to sell", correct:false,
     why:"Under the Companies Act, compulsory acquisition under s 215 needs a takeover offer accepted by 90% of the shares, and a scheme needs court sanction. Neither works for a 70/30 private exit. Without a contractual drag right, the founder can refuse to sell and block a 100% trade sale."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2; Intro 2.7–2.8", takeaway:"Exit rights over a continuing minority (drag/tag) live in the SHA + constitution, not the SPA."
},

{ id:"p2x-A1-01", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An SPA has a warranty cap of 30% of price (price S$40m), a de minimis of S$50,000 per claim, and a 'tipping' basket of S$400,000 (once exceeded, the whole amount is recoverable). After completion the buyer finds three breaches worth S$30,000, S$180,000 and S$350,000.",
  prompt:"How much can the buyer recover?",
  options:[
    {t:"S$530,000", correct:true,
     why:"Work through the limitations in order. The de minimis excludes any single claim below S$50,000, so the S$30,000 claim drops out entirely. The two qualifying claims total S$180,000 + S$350,000 = S$530,000. That exceeds the S$400,000 basket. Because the basket is a 'tipping' (first-dollar) basket, once it is crossed the buyer recovers the whole qualifying amount, not just the excess. S$530,000 is far below the cap of S$12m (30% of S$40m). If the basket had been a 'deductible', the buyer would recover only S$130,000. This is why buyers fight hard over the type of basket."},
    {t:"S$560,000", correct:false,
     why:"This includes the S$30,000 claim. The de minimis removes any individual claim below S$50,000 from the calculation altogether; it also does not count towards the basket. Small claims are excluded precisely so the parties do not litigate over trivia."},
    {t:"S$130,000", correct:false,
     why:"This would be right for a deductible ('excess') basket, where only the amount above the threshold is recoverable. The SPA here uses a tipping basket, so once qualifying claims exceed S$400,000 the full S$530,000 is recoverable."},
    {t:"Nothing, because no single claim exceeds the S$400,000 basket", correct:false,
     why:"A basket is an aggregate threshold. Qualifying claims are added together to see whether the basket is crossed. It is the de minimis that applies claim by claim."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10 [practice]", takeaway:"De minimis filters single claims; basket is aggregate; tipping basket = recover from dollar one."
},

{ id:"p2x-A1-02", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A private SPA for 100% of TargetCo is priced at S$10m. TargetCo's net asset value per its latest accounts is S$12m. The SPA is signed in Singapore.",
  prompt:"What stamp duty is payable on the share transfer, and by whom?",
  options:[
    {t:"S$24,000, payable by the buyer", correct:true,
     why:"Stamp duty on a transfer of shares in a Singapore company is 0.2% of the higher of the consideration and the market value of the shares, and IRAS accepts net asset value as the measure of market value for unlisted shares. Here the NAV of S$12m exceeds the price of S$10m, so duty is 0.2% × S$12m = S$24,000. By statute the transferee (buyer) pays, although the SPA can reallocate the cost. A document executed in Singapore must be stamped within 14 days (30 days if executed abroad). Different and much higher rates apply if TargetCo is a property-holding entity owning Singapore residential property."},
    {t:"S$20,000 (0.2% of the price), payable by the buyer", correct:false,
     why:"Duty is charged on the higher of the consideration and market value. Using the price alone would understate the duty where the shares are sold below NAV, which is exactly the situation the 'higher of' rule addresses."},
    {t:"S$24,000, payable by the seller", correct:false,
     why:"The amount is right but the payer is wrong. Under the Stamp Duties Act the transferee is liable for duty on a share transfer. Parties can agree to split it, but the default rule puts it on the buyer."},
    {t:"Nothing, because private share transfers are exempt", correct:false,
     why:"There is no general exemption for private share transfers. Relief is available in limited cases, such as qualifying intra-group reorganisations or reconstructions, but an arm's-length sale to a third party is dutiable."}
  ],
  cite:"Stamp Duties Act (shares) [external]; Intro 2.13", takeaway:"Share stamp duty: 0.2% × higher of price or NAV, paid by buyer, within 14 days."
},

{ id:"p2x-A1-03", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A buyer agrees to buy 60% of a private company from its largest shareholder. The constitution gives every shareholder a pre-emption right over shares any member proposes to transfer. Two minority holders own the other 40%.",
  prompt:"What must the buyer's lawyers deal with before completion?",
  options:[
    {t:"Waivers of the minority's pre-emption rights, as a condition", correct:true,
     why:"Pre-emption rights in the constitution bind the company and its members. If the seller transfers in breach of them, the minority can challenge the registration of the transfer and claim the shares on the constitution's terms. The usual fix is for each minority holder to waive its rights in writing for this transfer, often in return for something (a tag-along, or a better seat in the post-deal SHA). If a holder refuses, the seller must run the pre-emption process and may lose some of the shares to the minority. The buyer should make the waivers (and directors' approval to register the transfer, if the constitution requires it) conditions to completion."},
    {t:"Nothing, because a sale of a majority stake overrides pre-emption rights", correct:false,
     why:"Pre-emption clauses usually apply to any transfer, whatever its size. A majority sale is the very situation they are designed for, because it changes who the minority's co-owner will be."},
    {t:"Obtain SIC consent", correct:false,
     why:"The Securities Industry Council administers the Take-Over Code. The Code does not apply to a private company, so SIC consent is irrelevant. The constraint here is the constitution, which is a statutory contract between the company and its members under s 39 of the Companies Act."},
    {t:"Make a mandatory offer for the remaining 40%", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer obligation arises under Rule 14 of the Code at the 30% threshold, but only for companies within the Code. A private company is outside it, so crossing 30% or 50% triggers no offer obligation."}
  ],
  cite:"Companies Act s 39; Wan Ch 2; Intro 2.8", takeaway:"Private share deal: clear constitutional pre-emption rights (waiver) before completion."
},

{ id:"p2x-A1-04", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Between signing and completion (awaiting a regulatory approval), the target's managing director awards himself a S$1.5m bonus and signs a new 5-year office lease. The SPA has standard conduct-of-business covenants and a completion-accounts mechanism.",
  prompt:"Which statement best describes the buyer's position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Both likely breach the interim covenants", correct:true,
     why:"Interim covenants usually require the seller to procure that the target carries on business in the ordinary course and does not, without the buyer's consent, change remuneration above a threshold or enter long-term or unusual commitments. Both acts are typical breaches. Under completion accounts, the bonus is a liability or cash outflow that reduces net assets or working capital at completion, so the price drops automatically. The lease does not show up as a fixed sum in the same way, so the buyer's remedy is a claim for breach of covenant, and possibly a right to refuse to complete if the SPA makes compliance with the covenants a condition or a termination right."},
    {t:"Nothing can be done because the buyer does not own the target until completion", correct:false,
     why:"That is exactly why interim covenants exist. The buyer is bound to buy but does not yet control the business, so it contracts with the seller for control over what management may do in the gap. The covenants are enforceable against the seller before completion."},
    {t:"The buyer's only remedy is a warranty claim after completion", correct:false,
     why:"A warranty claim may also be available if the warranties are repeated at completion, but the direct remedy is for breach of the covenant. That claim is usually not subject to the warranty limitations or to disclosure."},
    {t:"The buyer can immediately appoint its own directors to stop management", correct:false,
     why:"Until completion, the seller still owns the shares and controls the board. The buyer can seek an injunction to restrain a threatened breach of covenant, but it has no power to appoint directors before it owns the shares."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.5, 2.11; Wan Ch 4", takeaway:"Gap-period acts are policed by interim covenants; completion accounts catch cash leakage automatically."
},

{ id:"p2x-A1-05", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A seller wants price certainty and a clean break. The target's working capital swings sharply month to month, and its latest audited accounts are eight months old. The buyer is worried about trading between the accounts date and completion.",
  prompt:"Which price mechanism best fits the buyer's concern, and what will the seller ask for in return?",
  options:[
    {t:"Completion accounts with a net-debt adjustment", correct:true,
     why:"Completion accounts set the final price by reference to the balance sheet at completion, so the buyer pays for the business it actually receives. That suits a target with volatile working capital and stale accounts, because the buyer does not carry eight months of unknown trading. The cost is uncertainty and a post-completion process: accounts are drawn up, reviewed, and disputes go to an independent accountant. The seller will want a precise hierarchy of accounting policies, a fixed working-capital target derived from a normal level, short review periods and a binding expert determination to limit the scope for argument."},
    {t:"Locked box off the eight-month-old accounts", correct:false,
     why:"A locked box gives the seller the certainty it wants, but it puts eight months of trading risk on the buyer, protected only by the no-leakage covenant. Leakage protection catches value paid out to the seller; it does not catch ordinary trading losses or a working-capital collapse. With old accounts and volatile working capital, that is the opposite of what the buyer needs."},
    {t:"An earn-out over three years", correct:false,
     why:"An earn-out bridges a disagreement about future performance. It does not deal with the balance-sheet position at completion, and it delays the clean break the seller wants."},
    {t:"A fixed price with no adjustment and no leakage covenant", correct:false,
     why:"This leaves the buyer with no protection at all for the gap: not for trading, and not even for value extracted by the seller. No well-advised buyer would accept it on these facts."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.9", takeaway:"Volatile working capital + stale accounts → completion accounts; locked box shifts gap trading risk to buyer."
},

{ id:"p2x-A1-06", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The SPA has an entire-agreement clause and a clause in which the buyer confirms it did not rely on any statement outside the SPA. After completion the buyer discovers the seller's CFO overstated the order book in a management presentation.",
  prompt:"What is the main legal issue in a claim for misrepresentation?",
  options:[
    {t:"The non-reliance clause and the s 3 reasonableness test", correct:true,
     why:"An entire-agreement clause on its own usually stops pre-contract statements becoming terms, but does not necessarily exclude liability for misrepresentation. A non-reliance acknowledgement goes further: if effective, it may prevent the buyer from showing that it relied on the statement, which is an element of the claim. Courts treat clauses that exclude or restrict misrepresentation liability as subject to the reasonableness requirement in s 3 of the Misrepresentation Act, read with the Unfair Contract Terms Act. Between sophisticated parties with lawyers, such clauses are often upheld. Fraud cannot be excluded. The buyer's safer route is a warranty in the SPA covering the order book."},
    {t:"Nothing, because entire-agreement clauses are unenforceable in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"Entire-agreement clauses are routinely enforced. The question is what they achieve: they prevent the statement from being a contractual term, but the effect on misrepresentation claims depends on the wording and on s 3."},
    {t:"Whether the Take-Over Code's information standards were breached", correct:false,
     why:"The Code's standards of care for documents and statements apply in Code-regulated offers. This is a private deal, so the issue is contractual and under the general law of misrepresentation."},
    {t:"Whether the CFO personally gave a warranty", correct:false,
     why:"The CFO is not usually a party to the SPA and gives no warranties. The claim is against the seller for a misrepresentation made on its behalf, and the contractual clauses decide whether that claim survives."}
  ],
  cite:"Misrepresentation Act s 3; UCTA [external]; Intro 2.10", takeaway:"Entire agreement + non-reliance can shut out misrepresentation claims (subject to s 3 reasonableness; never fraud). Put key facts in warranties."
},

{ id:"p2x-A1-07", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A buyer takes warranty & indemnity (W&I) insurance on a private acquisition, and the seller's liability for warranty claims is capped at S$1. Diligence had flagged a pending employment claim.",
  prompt:"Which statement about the buyer's protection is correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"Unknown breaches covered; the known claim needs an indemnity", correct:true,
     why:"W&I insurance lets a seller take a clean exit (hence the S$1 cap) while the buyer claims against an insurer for breaches of warranty. Insurers underwrite only unknown risk. They exclude matters disclosed or known to the buyer's deal team, and commonly exclude forward-looking warranties, certain tax items, pension underfunding and anything flagged in diligence. The pending employment claim is known, so it will be excluded. The buyer must deal with it separately: a specific indemnity from the seller (perhaps with an escrow), a price reduction, or a special policy for that risk if one is available."},
    {t:"The policy covers everything, including matters flagged in diligence", correct:false,
     why:"No insurer covers known risks under a standard W&I policy. The underwriting process looks at the diligence reports precisely to identify and exclude what the buyer already knows."},
    {t:"The seller remains fully liable despite the S$1 cap", correct:false,
     why:"The S$1 cap is the point of the structure: the seller's warranty liability is nominal, and the insurer stands in its place. The seller remains liable for fraud and for any specific indemnities it gives outside the cap."},
    {t:"W&I insurance replaces the need for due diligence", correct:false,
     why:"The opposite. Insurers require a proper diligence exercise and will not cover areas the buyer did not investigate. Poor diligence leads to wider exclusions."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10 [practice]", takeaway:"W&I covers unknown warranty breaches; known issues need a specific indemnity or price cut."
},

{ id:"p2x-A1-08", lesson:"A.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An SPA conditional on MAS approval has a long-stop date of 30 June. MAS has not decided by then. The buyer wants to keep going; the seller has had a better offer.",
  prompt:"What happens on 1 July?",
  options:[
    {t:"Either party may usually terminate by notice", correct:true,
     why:"The long-stop date is the outer limit for satisfying conditions. A typical clause says that if any condition is not satisfied or waived by that date, either party may terminate by notice (sometimes it terminates automatically). Neither side is in breach merely because the regulator was slow, provided each complied with its effort obligations. A buyer that wants protection against regulatory delay must negotiate it at signing: an automatic extension if approval is pending, a reverse break fee, or a seller obligation not to terminate while the approval process is live. If the seller did not use the agreed efforts to obtain approval, it may not be able to rely on its own breach to terminate."},
    {t:"The SPA continues indefinitely until MAS decides", correct:false,
     why:"That would defeat the purpose of a long-stop date, which protects both sides from being bound indefinitely. Without an extension right, the contract does not wait for the regulator forever."},
    {t:"The buyer may complete without MAS approval", correct:false,
     why:"MAS approval is a legal requirement for certain acquisitions of control of regulated entities. Completing without it would breach the regulatory regime. Regulatory conditions of this kind cannot be waived, regardless of what the SPA says."},
    {t:"The seller must pay a break fee", correct:false,
     why:"A break fee is payable only if the SPA provides for one in the relevant circumstances. Nothing on these facts suggests the seller agreed to pay one for regulatory delay."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.5, 2.11", takeaway:"Long-stop passes with a CP unmet → either party can usually walk; negotiate extensions up front."
}

]);

;
/* ---- data/p2/A2.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Chapter 2 · Lesson 2.2 Leveraged Buyouts: Structure & Financing (audited Oct 2026).
   Sources: Wan Ch 15 §D–F; Gaughan Ch 8 (LBO economics); Intro 2.9, 2.14–2.15. Gaughan is US/finance vocabulary, never Singapore law. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"fa-2-01", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq", dia:"capitalStack",
  prompt:"What is a 'BidCo' in a leveraged buyout?",
  options:[
    {t:"A new shell funded by equity and debt", correct:true,
     why:"A financial sponsor rarely buys a target directly. It forms a new company (often a chain: TopCo, HoldCo, BidCo) and funds it with equity from its fund and with acquisition debt from lenders. BidCo signs the SPA, pays the price and becomes the target's shareholder. The structure ring-fences the deal: the fund's liability is limited to the equity it put in, the lenders lend to a single-purpose borrower whose only asset is the target, and different layers of debt can be placed at different levels. Using a fresh vehicle also means BidCo's only business is the deal, so its accounts are clean for the lenders."},
    {t:"The target company being acquired", correct:false,
     why:"The target is what is being bought; BidCo is the buyer. They are separate legal entities until and unless the sponsor later merges them, for example by amalgamating BidCo into the target to push the debt down."},
    {t:"The investment bank running the sale process", correct:false,
     why:"The sell-side bank advises the seller and runs the auction. Banks may also lend to BidCo, but they are lenders or advisers, never the acquisition vehicle itself."},
    {t:"The escrow agent holding the consideration", correct:false,
     why:"An escrow agent is a neutral party holding money (for example a retention) under an escrow agreement. BidCo is the principal buyer and owner of the target after completion."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §E; Gaughan Ch 8; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"BidCo = the SPV buyer (sponsor equity + acquisition debt)."
},

{ id:"fa-2-02", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The defining financial feature of an LBO is that the purchase is funded largely by…",
  options:[
    {t:"Debt secured on and serviced by the target", correct:true,
     why:"In an LBO the sponsor typically puts in a minority of the price as equity and borrows the rest. The lenders are not relying on the sponsor's balance sheet; they rely on the target's cash flows to pay interest and principal, and on the target's assets as security. That is why lenders want guarantees and security from the target group, and why the debt is often pushed down to the operating company after completion. In Singapore that push-down is where s 76 (financial assistance) becomes relevant, but only where the target is a public company or a subsidiary of a public company."},
    {t:"The sponsor's own cash reserves, with no borrowing", correct:false,
     why:"An all-equity purchase is not leveraged. Without debt there is no gearing effect on returns, which is the economic reason sponsors use the LBO model."},
    {t:"A share-for-share exchange with the target's shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Scrip-for-scrip is how a listed acquirer can pay with its own shares. Sellers in an LBO are normally paid cash, raised from the sponsor's equity and the acquisition debt."},
    {t:"A government grant", correct:false,
     why:"LBOs are financed privately, by bank loans, bonds, private credit funds and sponsor equity. Grants play no part in the model."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §E–F; Gaughan Ch 8; Intro 2.9, 2.15", takeaway:"LBO = debt-funded, serviced and secured by the target; flags s 76 if a public company is in the chain."
},

{ id:"d1-019", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq", dia:"capitalStack",
  prompt:"In an LBO, why does the sponsor want to 'push down' the acquisition debt onto the target?",
  options:[
    {t:"So the target's cash flows service the debt", correct:true,
     why:"Before push-down, the debt sits at BidCo, whose only asset is its shares in the target. BidCo's lenders are structurally subordinated: the target's own creditors are paid out of the target's assets first, and cash only reaches BidCo as dividends. Pushing the debt down (by target guarantees and security, or by merging BidCo into the target) gives the lenders a direct claim on the operating assets and cash. That makes the debt cheaper and allows more of it, which increases leverage and the sponsor's equity returns. It also aligns the interest cost with the entity that earns the profits. The legal cost is that the target is now helping to pay for its own acquisition, which engages s 76 where the target is public or a subsidiary of a public company."},
    {t:"To avoid paying any interest", correct:false,
     why:"Debt bears interest wherever it sits. Push-down changes who owes the debt and what secures it, not whether interest is payable."},
    {t:"Because the Take-Over Code requires it", correct:false,
     why:"The Code says nothing about where acquisition debt should sit. Its concern in financed offers is that the offeror has the resources to pay, which is why a cash offer must be supported by a confirmation of financial resources. Push-down is a financing choice."},
    {t:"To make the target a public company", correct:false,
     why:"Push-down has nothing to do with the target's status. In fact a take-private usually delists and may convert the target to a private company, partly so that s 76 no longer applies to later financing."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §E–F; Gaughan Ch 8; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Push-down cures structural subordination and supports more debt, but engages s 76 for public companies."
},

{ id:"fa-2-03", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A sponsor needs to sign a buyout knowing it can pay on the completion date. What financing feature addresses this?",
  options:[
    {t:"Certain funds", correct:true,
     why:"A seller will not sign with a buyer that might fail to pay. Certain funds means the lenders' conditions to drawdown are limited to matters within the buyer's control or fundamental to the lenders (corporate authorities, no insolvency, no major breach of the facility, the acquisition itself completing). Due diligence, credit committee approval and market flex outs must all be cleared before signing. On the equity side the fund gives an equity commitment letter to BidCo. Together they let the buyer sign an SPA without a financing condition, which is what sellers in competitive processes demand."},
    {t:"A material adverse change clause", correct:false,
     why:"A MAC clause lets a buyer walk if the target suffers a serious deterioration. It protects the buyer against changes in the business, not the seller against the buyer's lack of money. Lenders may want a MAC in the facility, which is exactly what certain funds tries to prevent."},
    {t:"A locked-box mechanism", correct:false,
     why:"A locked box fixes the price off a historic balance sheet. It decides how much is paid, not whether the buyer will have the money to pay it."},
    {t:"A break fee payable by the seller", correct:false,
     why:"A break fee (or a reverse break fee payable by the buyer) compensates the other side if the deal fails. A reverse break fee can reassure a seller about financing risk, but it is a remedy for failure, not evidence that the money is committed."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §D–E; Intro 2.9", takeaway:"Certain funds = committed financing with no diligence or credit outs, so the buyer can sign without a financing condition."
},

{ id:"au-2-02", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A sponsor will sign a private SPA with a fixed completion date and no financing condition. The bank's commitment letter says funding is 'subject to satisfactory confirmatory due diligence and credit committee approval'.",
  prompt:"What is the problem, and the fix?",
  options:[
    {t:"It isn't certain funds; sign facilities before signing", correct:true,
     why:"Without a financing condition in the SPA, the sponsor must complete on the agreed date whether or not the bank funds. A commitment letter that is subject to diligence and credit approval gives the bank two easy exits, so the sponsor is carrying the funding risk. If the bank walks, the sponsor will be in breach of the SPA and exposed to damages or specific performance. The fix is to finish the bank's diligence and approvals before signing and sign a full facility agreement (or a short-form interim facility) with certain-funds conditions. If that cannot be done in time, the sponsor must negotiate a financing condition, which most sellers will resist or price."},
    {t:"None, because the bank has issued a commitment letter", correct:false,
     why:"A commitment letter is only as good as its conditions. Diligence and credit-committee outs mean the bank has not yet decided to lend. The sponsor cannot rely on it for a fixed completion date."},
    {t:"Add a MAC clause in favour of the seller", correct:false,
     why:"A MAC clause concerns changes in the target's business. It does nothing about whether the sponsor's lenders will fund, and a seller would not need a MAC in its own favour."},
    {t:"Ask the target to guarantee the bank loan before signing", correct:false,
     why:"The target is still owned by the seller before completion, and its directors would have no corporate reason to guarantee a buyer's debt. If the target is public or a subsidiary of a public company, it also engages s 76. And it does not fix the bank's diligence and credit outs."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15; Intro 2.14", takeaway:"No financing condition → you need certain funds before signing."
},

{ id:"fa-2-04", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario", dia:"s76test",
  scenario:"To fund BidCo's acquisition of a public target, lenders want the TARGET to grant security over its assets and guarantee the acquisition loan.",
  prompt:"What is the immediate legal problem?",
  options:[
    {t:"s 76: a guarantee is assistance even without cash leaving", correct:true,
     why:"Section 76 prohibits a public company, or a company whose holding company or ultimate holding company is a public company, from giving financial assistance for the acquisition of its own shares or those of its holding company. A guarantee or security over the target's assets for BidCo's acquisition loan is one of the forms of assistance the section lists. It does not matter that no cash moves: the company takes on a contingent liability that may deplete its assets. The deal team must either fit within an exception or use a whitewash procedure (shareholder approval with directors' solvency statements and other conditions), or restructure, for example by delisting and converting the target to a private company before giving the security."},
    {t:"None, because a target can always secure its buyer's debt", correct:false,
     why:"This is the classic trap. Using the target's balance sheet to fund the purchase of its own shares is what s 76 targets where a public company is in the chain. The answer is different for a private company with no public parent, which has been outside the prohibition since 2015."},
    {t:"Stamp duty on the security documents", correct:false,
     why:"Stamp duty on security documents is not the headline issue; Singapore no longer charges duty on most mortgages and charges. The legal problem is financial assistance."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer is triggered", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer arises under Rule 14 of the Code when someone crosses 30% of a Code company. Granting security is not an acquisition of shares and triggers nothing under the Code."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F [15.62]; CA s 76; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Public target's security/guarantee for the buyout loan = financial assistance (s 76): fit an exception or whitewash."
},

{ id:"au-2-01", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq", dia:"capitalStack",
  prompt:"A sponsor buys a company for EV S$100m, funded S$70m debt and S$30m equity. Five years later it sells for EV S$130m, having repaid S$20m of the debt. Ignoring fees, what multiple of money does the equity make?",
  options:[
    {t:"About 2.7x (S$80m back on S$30m)", correct:true,
     why:"At exit, the equity is worth the enterprise value less the debt still outstanding. Debt outstanding is 70 − 20 = S$50m, so equity is 130 − 50 = S$80m, and S$80m ÷ S$30m ≈ 2.7x. The enterprise value rose only 30%, but the equity nearly tripled. Two things land on the thin equity layer: the whole S$30m rise in EV, and the S$20m of debt repaid from the target's cash flows. This is the leverage effect that makes LBOs attractive, and the reason sponsors care so much about cash generation and debt paydown, not just growth."},
    {t:"1.3x, the same as the rise in EV", correct:false,
     why:"That would be the result only for an all-equity purchase, where the equity is the whole EV. With debt in the structure, the lenders' claim is fixed, so all the gain in EV (and the debt paydown) accrues to the equity."},
    {t:"4.3x (S$130m on S$30m)", correct:false,
     why:"This treats the whole exit price as belonging to the equity. S$50m of debt is still outstanding and is repaid from the sale proceeds before the sponsor receives anything."},
    {t:"2.0x (S$60m on S$30m)", correct:false,
     why:"This deducts the original S$70m of debt rather than the S$50m still outstanding. The S$20m repaid during the hold period has already been paid out of the target's cash and increases the equity's share at exit."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 8 (LBO economics); Intro 2.9", takeaway:"Exit equity = EV − net debt outstanding; leverage magnifies the return."
},

{ id:"rA-2-01", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq", dia:"capitalStack",
  prompt:"Why does heavy leverage AMPLIFY a sponsor's equity returns in an LBO?",
  options:[
    {t:"A thin equity slice takes all the upside", correct:true,
     why:"Lenders' returns are capped at interest and repayment of principal. Everything above that belongs to the equity. If the equity is 30% of the capital structure, a 10% rise in EV is roughly a 33% rise in equity value, before counting debt paydown. Leverage also brings a tax advantage, because interest is generally deductible while dividends are not, subject to the applicable rules. Gaughan stresses that the same mechanics work in reverse: a modest fall in EV can wipe out the equity, which is why over-levered deals fail."},
    {t:"Because interest payments are refunded at exit", correct:false,
     why:"Interest is a cost that reduces the cash available to the equity. It is never refunded. The amplification comes from the fixed nature of the lenders' claim, not from any recovery of interest."},
    {t:"Because lenders share the equity upside equally", correct:false,
     why:"Senior lenders do not share in the upside. Mezzanine lenders sometimes take warrants, but even then the bulk of the upside sits with the sponsor's equity. The upside concentrates on the equity precisely because the lenders' return is capped."},
    {t:"Because leverage removes all downside risk", correct:false,
     why:"Leverage increases downside risk. With high debt, a fall in earnings can breach financial covenants and lead to the lenders enforcing, leaving the equity with nothing."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 8; Wan Ch 15", takeaway:"Thin equity + capped lender returns = magnified gains and losses."
},

{ id:"rA-2-02", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What security do LBO lenders typically take over the deal structure?",
  options:[
    {t:"Share charges, debentures and group guarantees", correct:true,
     why:"Lenders want two routes to recovery. A charge or mortgage over the shares lets them take control of the whole group by enforcing and selling the shares in one go, without dismantling the business. Debentures with fixed and floating charges over the group's assets, plus cross-guarantees, give them direct claims against the operating companies. The second layer is what raises s 76 where a public company is in the chain, and it also raises corporate-benefit questions for each guaranteeing subsidiary's directors, who must be satisfied that giving the guarantee is in that company's interests."},
    {t:"No security at all, because LBO debt is always unsecured", correct:false,
     why:"Senior LBO debt is heavily secured. Some high-yield bonds and holdco notes are unsecured or second-lien, but the senior facilities that fund most of the price take full security."},
    {t:"Only a personal guarantee from the sponsor's partners", correct:false,
     why:"Sponsors invest through limited-liability fund structures specifically so that no individual is exposed. Lenders rely on the deal structure (shares and assets), not on the sponsor's principals."},
    {t:"A mortgage over the lenders' own premises", correct:false,
     why:"Security is always taken by the creditor over the debtor's assets. A lender taking security over its own property makes no sense."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15", takeaway:"LBO security = share charge + debentures + guarantees over the target group."
},

{ id:"rA-2-03", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq", dia:"capitalStack",
  prompt:"Where does MEZZANINE debt sit in an LBO capital structure?",
  options:[
    {t:"Between senior debt and equity", correct:true,
     why:"Senior lenders will only lend up to a certain multiple of earnings. If the sponsor needs more money but does not want to put in more equity, mezzanine fills the gap. It is subordinated to the senior debt, by contract (an intercreditor agreement) or structurally (it is lent to a holding company above the senior borrower). Because it carries more risk, it is priced higher, often with payment-in-kind interest that accrues rather than being paid in cash, and sometimes with warrants for a slice of the equity upside. It ranks ahead of equity, which is the residual claim paid last."},
    {t:"Ahead of the senior secured lenders", correct:false,
     why:"Senior debt ranks first by definition. Mezzanine is subordinated, which is why it charges more."},
    {t:"Below the ordinary equity", correct:false,
     why:"Equity is always the last claim in a solvent or insolvent distribution. Mezzanine is debt and ranks above it."},
    {t:"It is another name for the sponsor's equity", correct:false,
     why:"Mezzanine is debt, often from specialist funds, with equity-like features. The sponsor's equity may include shareholder loans, but those are distinct from third-party mezzanine."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 8", takeaway:"Mezzanine = subordinated debt between senior and equity, priced for its risk."
},

{ id:"rA-2-04", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In many buyouts, target management 'rolls over' some of their proceeds into equity in the new structure. Why do sponsors want this?",
  options:[
    {t:"To align management", correct:true,
     why:"A sponsor buys a business it does not run. It depends on the managers to deliver the plan behind its returns. Requiring them to reinvest part of their sale proceeds (rollover), and giving them a separate incentive pool of 'sweet equity' that pays out only if returns are high, makes them co-investors. Leaver provisions in the SHA usually force a manager who leaves early to sell back at cost or a discount, which helps retention. Because managers are on both sides of the deal, the rollover also creates conflicts, which Lesson 2.4 deals with."},
    {t:"To avoid needing any senior debt", correct:false,
     why:"Rollover equity is small compared with the debt. It does not replace senior financing; its purpose is alignment and retention."},
    {t:"To trigger a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"Mandatory offers belong to the Take-Over Code. In a private buyout there is no offer obligation. In a public take-private, management's rollover may make them concert parties of the sponsor, which is a constraint, not a goal."},
    {t:"To make the target a public company", correct:false,
     why:"Rollover has nothing to do with listing. It aligns managers through shares in the private buyout structure."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15; Gaughan Ch 8", takeaway:"Management rollover aligns and retains the people who run the plan."
},

{ id:"rA-2-05", lesson:"A.2", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: because the acquisition debt is serviced out of the TARGET's cash flows, the quality and stability of those cash flows is central to whether an LBO is viable.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"An LBO only works if the target generates predictable cash to pay interest and repay principal. Gaughan's profile of an ideal LBO candidate follows from this: stable and predictable cash flows, low existing debt, low capital-expenditure needs, a strong market position, a capable management team and assets that can support security. Volatile or cyclical earnings make leverage dangerous, because a downturn can breach the leverage and interest-cover covenants before the business has time to recover."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"It is true. The lenders' model is built on the target's cash flows, and the sponsor's return depends on debt being repaid out of them. A business that burns cash cannot carry acquisition debt for long."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 8", takeaway:"LBO viability rides on stable target cash flows."
},

{ id:"au-2-03", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"After acquiring a listed target, BidCo keeps it listed with minorities. Later the cash-rich target declares a large dividend, which BidCo uses to repay its acquisition loan. The dividend is lawfully declared out of profits.",
  prompt:"Is this prohibited financial assistance?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: s 76(8)(a) excludes a distribution by way of a lawfully made dividend", correct:true,
     why:"Section 76 still applies, because the target is a public company and assistance given after an acquisition to discharge acquisition debt can be caught. But the section excludes certain transactions from its scope, including a distribution of the company's assets by way of a dividend lawfully made. Wan [15.72]–[15.73] explains that the 2014 amendments removed the old requirement that the dividend be paid in good faith and in the ordinary course of commercial dealing. So any dividend lawfully declared (out of profits under s 403, by the proper organ) is outside s 76, even if the shareholder uses it to repay acquisition debt. Every shareholder, including the minorities, receives the same dividend per share."},
    {t:"Yes, because the dividend is used to repay acquisition debt", correct:false,
     why:"Since the 2014 amendments the use to which the shareholder puts the dividend does not take it outside the exclusion. The test is whether the dividend was lawfully made."},
    {t:"Only if the dividend was paid in the ordinary course of business", correct:false,
     why:"That was the old wording. It was removed by the Companies (Amendment) Act 2014, so there is no longer an 'ordinary course' test for dividends."},
    {t:"s 76 cannot apply because the acquisition has already completed", correct:false,
     why:"Section 76 catches assistance given before, at the same time as, or after the acquisition. Assistance after completion, such as paying off the buyer's acquisition loan, is caught. This dividend escapes because of the dividend exclusion, not because of timing."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.72]–[15.73]; CA ss 76(8)(a), 403", takeaway:"Lawful dividend = s 76(8)(a) exclusion (no 'ordinary course' test since 2014)."
},

{ id:"p2x-A2-01", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario", dia:"capitalStack",
  scenario:"A buyout's senior facility has a maintenance covenant: total net debt must not exceed 6.0x EBITDA. Net debt is S$300m. EBITDA was S$60m at signing and falls to S$45m in a downturn.",
  prompt:"What is the position at the next test date?",
  options:[
    {t:"About 6.7x: a breach; seek a cure or waiver", correct:true,
     why:"At signing leverage was 300 ÷ 60 = 5.0x, inside the covenant. After the downturn it is 300 ÷ 45 ≈ 6.67x, above the 6.0x limit. A covenant breach is usually an event of default, which lets the lenders accelerate and, ultimately, enforce their security. Facilities often give the sponsor an equity cure: it can inject new equity, treated either as reducing net debt or as notional EBITDA, to bring the ratio back into compliance. To get back to 6.0x by paying down debt, net debt must fall to 6.0 × 45 = S$270m, so the cure is about S$30m. Otherwise the sponsor negotiates a waiver or reset, usually for a fee and a higher margin."},
    {t:"No breach, because the debt amount has not changed", correct:false,
     why:"A leverage covenant tests debt against earnings. Debt can stay constant and the covenant still be breached if earnings fall. That is how downturns cause covenant defaults."},
    {t:"Leverage is 5.0x, so there is headroom", correct:false,
     why:"5.0x was the position at signing. Covenants are tested on the latest figures, typically the last twelve months' EBITDA at each quarterly test date."},
    {t:"The lenders must immediately take the shares", correct:false,
     why:"A breach gives the lenders rights; it does not oblige them to do anything. Lenders almost always prefer a negotiated cure, waiver or restructuring to immediate enforcement, which tends to destroy value."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 8; Wan Ch 15", takeaway:"Leverage covenant = net debt ÷ EBITDA; falling EBITDA alone can breach it → equity cure or waiver."
},

{ id:"p2x-A2-02", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario", dia:"capitalStack",
  scenario:"HoldCo borrowed S$50m of payment-in-kind (PIK) notes and owns 100% of OpCo. OpCo has S$200m of senior bank debt and S$40m owed to trade creditors. OpCo becomes insolvent and its assets realise S$230m. HoldCo has no other assets.",
  prompt:"What do the HoldCo noteholders recover?",
  options:[
    {t:"Nothing", correct:true,
     why:"This is structural subordination. HoldCo's noteholders are creditors of HoldCo, not of OpCo. OpCo's own creditors, senior lenders and trade creditors alike, are paid out of OpCo's assets first. OpCo has S$240m of claims and only S$230m of assets, so there is no surplus for its shareholder. HoldCo's only asset is its shares in OpCo, which are worthless, so the noteholders recover nothing. That is why holdco debt prices well above OpCo senior debt, and why creditors high in the structure ask for guarantees from the operating companies (which brings s 76 back into play for public companies)."},
    {t:"S$30m, being S$230m less the S$200m of senior debt", correct:false,
     why:"This ignores OpCo's trade creditors, who are OpCo creditors and rank alongside the unsecured portion of OpCo's claims, ahead of OpCo's shareholder. Even after the bank is paid, the remaining S$30m goes to OpCo's creditors, not up to HoldCo."},
    {t:"S$50m in full, because PIK notes accrue interest", correct:false,
     why:"PIK describes how interest is paid (by adding to the principal rather than in cash). It says nothing about ranking. The notes are still claims against HoldCo alone."},
    {t:"A pro rata share alongside OpCo's trade creditors", correct:false,
     why:"HoldCo's creditors have no claim against OpCo at all unless OpCo guaranteed the notes. Without a guarantee, they cannot share in OpCo's assets."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 8; Wan Ch 15", takeaway:"Debt higher in the structure is structurally subordinated to every creditor of the operating company."
},

{ id:"p2x-A2-03", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A sponsor is choosing between three private targets: (1) a regional laundry services business with long-term hospital contracts and modest capex; (2) a fast-growing software start-up burning S$5m a month; (3) a commodity shipping company with highly cyclical earnings and an ageing fleet needing replacement.",
  prompt:"Which is the best LBO candidate?",
  options:[
    {t:"The laundry services business", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan's ideal LBO target has stable, predictable cash flows, low capital-expenditure needs, limited existing debt and a defensible market position. Long-term hospital contracts give visibility of revenue, and modest capex means most operating cash is free to service debt. That supports a high debt quantum at a reasonable price. Leverage needs cash to pay interest from day one, so the business that throws off steady cash is the natural fit."},
    {t:"The software start-up", correct:false,
     why:"A cash-burning business cannot service acquisition debt; there is no free cash flow to pay interest. Growth companies are normally funded with equity (venture or growth capital). Lenders who lend to them do so on very different terms and amounts."},
    {t:"The shipping company", correct:false,
     why:"Cyclical earnings are the enemy of leverage. A downturn can push the business into covenant breach just when it most needs flexibility, and the fleet replacement programme will absorb the cash that would otherwise repay debt."},
    {t:"All three equally, because the sponsor can always use more equity", correct:false,
     why:"An LBO is defined by leverage. If a business can only be bought with mostly equity, it is not an LBO candidate, and the sponsor's return model will not work in the same way."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 8", takeaway:"Ideal LBO target: stable cash flows, low capex, low existing debt, strong market position."
},

{ id:"p2x-A2-04", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two years after a buyout of a Singapore private company (no public company anywhere in the group), the sponsor has the target borrow S$80m and pay it all out as a special dividend to BidCo: a 'dividend recap'. The target has distributable profits of S$40m.",
  prompt:"What is the main legal obstacle?",
  options:[
    {t:"Dividends need profits; S$80m exceeds them", correct:true,
     why:"Section 403 of the Companies Act requires dividends to be paid only out of profits. With S$40m of distributable profits, the target cannot lawfully pay an S$80m dividend; it would need to return the excess some other way, such as a capital reduction under the solvency-statement procedure. Separately, the directors owe duties to act in the company's interests. Where a company is insolvent or near insolvency, those interests include creditors' interests, so loading the target with debt to pay shareholders can be a breach of duty and an unlawful distribution can be recovered. Financial assistance is not the issue here because there is no public company in the group."},
    {t:"Section 76 financial assistance", correct:false,
     why:"Since 2015, s 76 applies only to public companies and companies whose holding company or ultimate holding company is public. A wholly private group is outside it. Even for a public company, a lawful dividend would fall within the s 76(8)(a) exclusion."},
    {t:"The Take-Over Code's restriction on frustrating action", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 5 restrains an offeree board from frustrating a bona fide offer. It has nothing to do with a private company paying dividends to its sponsor owner."},
    {t:"There is no obstacle, because BidCo owns 100% and can approve anything", correct:false,
     why:"Unanimous shareholder approval cannot authorise a dividend out of capital or override duties owed for the benefit of creditors where solvency is in question. Capital maintenance rules protect creditors, not shareholders."}
  ],
  cite:"CA s 403; Wan Ch 15 §F; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Dividend recap: dividends only out of profits; directors must weigh creditors near insolvency; s 76 irrelevant to a wholly private group."
},

{ id:"p2x-A2-05", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"BidCo has bought 100% of a private target (no public parent). BidCo's lenders want direct access to the target's cash and assets and a single borrower.",
  prompt:"Which structuring step most directly achieves the push-down?",
  options:[
    {t:"Amalgamate BidCo and the target (ss 215A–215K)", correct:true,
     why:"An amalgamation under the Companies Act combines two or more companies into one. The amalgamated company succeeds by operation of law to all the property, rights and liabilities of the amalgamating companies. If BidCo and the target amalgamate, the acquisition debt and the operating business end up in the same company, so the lenders' claim is no longer structurally subordinated. Where one company wholly owns the other, the short-form procedure is available, needing board resolutions rather than a shareholder vote. Each board must make solvency statements, and the process gives creditors protection. Because there is no public company in the group, s 76 does not complicate the push-down."},
    {t:"Ask the target to transfer its business to BidCo for no consideration", correct:false,
     why:"A gratuitous transfer of the target's business would be a distribution of assets that is not out of profits and not by an authorised procedure, a breach of capital maintenance and of directors' duties. It would also require consents for every contract and asset being moved."},
    {t:"Convert the target into a public company", correct:false,
     why:"Converting the target to a public company would bring s 76 into play and make any push-down harder. It achieves nothing for the lenders."},
    {t:"List BidCo on SGX", correct:false,
     why:"Listing is an exit route, not a financing step. It would also bring BidCo within s 76 and the Code."}
  ],
  cite:"CA ss 215A–215K; Wan Ch 15; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Push-down by amalgamation: debt and business in one company; short-form if wholly owned; solvency statements."
},

{ id:"p2x-A2-06", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A subsidiary of a private Singapore group is asked to guarantee its parent's S$100m acquisition facility. The subsidiary's own net assets are S$20m and it receives nothing directly from the transaction.",
  prompt:"What must the subsidiary's directors consider before approving the guarantee?",
  options:[
    {t:"Corporate benefit to the subsidiary, and solvency", correct:true,
     why:"Each company in a group is a separate legal person and its directors owe duties to that company, not to the group. A guarantee of a parent's debt is a real liability for the subsidiary. The directors must honestly consider whether the guarantee is in the subsidiary's interests. Benefits to the group that flow back to the subsidiary (continued funding, shared services, the group's survival) can count. If the guarantee would leave the subsidiary insolvent or near insolvent, the directors must give weight to creditors' interests. Lenders usually ask for board minutes that record this analysis and a shareholder resolution approving the guarantee."},
    {t:"Only whether s 76 permits it", correct:false,
     why:"For a wholly private group s 76 does not apply. The relevant question is directors' duties. Even where s 76 is cleared, the directors' duty issue remains."},
    {t:"Nothing, because the parent owns 100% and can direct the board", correct:false,
     why:"A parent can appoint and remove directors, but it cannot relieve them of their duties. A 100% shareholder's approval can help where the company is solvent, but it cannot authorise a transaction that prejudices creditors of an insolvent company."},
    {t:"Whether the Take-Over Code requires a whitewash", correct:false,
     why:"A Code whitewash concerns waivers of the mandatory offer obligation. It has nothing to do with intra-group guarantees in a private group."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Upstream guarantees: each subsidiary's board must find corporate benefit; creditors' interests near insolvency."
},

{ id:"p2x-A2-07", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Five years into a buyout, a sponsor is choosing an exit. Equity markets are weak, a strategic buyer has offered a full price for 100%, and another PE fund has offered slightly less with a quick closing.",
  prompt:"Which statement best compares the options?",
  options:[
    {t:"A trade sale gives the cleanest full exit", correct:true,
     why:"Sponsors exit in three main ways. A trade sale to a strategic buyer often achieves the best price because the buyer can pay for synergies, and it gives a clean 100% cash exit, though merger control and diligence can lengthen the timetable. A secondary buyout sells to another sponsor; it can be quick and certain because sponsors are familiar with each other's structures. An IPO depends on market conditions. The sponsor usually sells only part of its stake at listing and is locked up for the rest, so it does not get a clean exit. In weak markets the IPO price would likely be discounted. Running a dual track keeps both live until the last moment."},
    {t:"An IPO is always the highest-value exit", correct:false,
     why:"IPO valuations depend on market conditions and are usually set at a discount to encourage aftermarket trading. A strategic buyer that can capture synergies may pay more. In weak markets an IPO is often the least attractive route."},
    {t:"A secondary buyout is unlawful because the target has already been leveraged once", correct:false,
     why:"Secondary buyouts are common. The new sponsor refinances the existing debt with its own acquisition financing."},
    {t:"The sponsor must offer the business to the management first", correct:false,
     why:"There is no legal right of first refusal for management unless the SHA grants one. Management may be invited to roll over again with the new owner."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 8; Intro 2.9", takeaway:"Exit menu: trade sale (price, clean), secondary buyout (speed), IPO (market-dependent, partial)."
},

{ id:"p2x-A2-08", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does acquisition debt generally lower the overall cost of financing a buyout compared with all-equity funding?",
  options:[
    {t:"Debt ranks first and gets a tax shield", correct:true,
     why:"Lenders take less risk than equity investors: they are paid first and, in a senior secured facility, have security over the business. They therefore require a lower return than the sponsor's equity. Interest is generally deductible against taxable income, subject to the applicable rules on deductibility, which reduces the after-tax cost of debt further, while dividends to equity are paid out of after-tax profits. Gaughan treats this tax shield as one source of LBO value. The trade-off is risk: more debt raises the chance of financial distress, which eventually raises the cost of both debt and equity."},
    {t:"Debt never has to be repaid", correct:false,
     why:"Debt has to be repaid or refinanced at maturity. Some instruments (PIK notes) defer cash interest, but principal and accrued interest are still due."},
    {t:"Lenders share the sponsor's equity upside", correct:false,
     why:"Senior lenders do not share in the upside; their return is capped. That is why debt is cheaper, not because lenders take equity."},
    {t:"Interest is paid by the seller", correct:false,
     why:"The seller is paid out at completion and has no role in servicing the buyer's debt. Interest is paid from the target's cash flows."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 8", takeaway:"Debt is cheaper: priority, capped return, and the interest tax shield, at the cost of distress risk."
}

]);

