/* DealCraft — Chapter 2 · Lesson 2.4 MBOs & Management Conflicts (audited Oct 2026).
   Sources: Wan Ch 15 §G [15.81]–[15.97]; Code (2026) rr 7.1 n 1, 9.2 & n 1, 10 n 4, 14, 17.2 n 4; SFA Pt 12 (insider trading). */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"fa-4-01", lesson:"A.4", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the DEFINING issue in a management buyout (MBO)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Management's conflict of interest", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.83]–[15.84] identifies two linked concerns. As buyers, the managers want the lowest possible price. As directors and senior officers, they owe fiduciary duties to act in the company's interests, which in a sale means seeking the best price for shareholders. Wan says it is difficult, if not impossible, to discharge that duty from a conflicted position. The conflict is sharpened by information asymmetry: the managers know the company's real value and prospects better than the shareholders and better than any rival bidder. Every special MBO rule (independent advice, wider information parity, limits on management's retained interest) responds to this conflict."},
    {t:"The stamp duty rate on the share transfer", correct:false,
     why:"Stamp duty applies to any share purchase in the same way. It is not what makes an MBO different from an ordinary buyout."},
    {t:"Whether the Take-Over Code applies", correct:false,
     why:"The Code applies only if the target is a Code company. The conflict exists in private and public MBOs alike; the Code is one of the tools used to manage it when it applies."},
    {t:"The choice between locked-box and completion accounts", correct:false,
     why:"Price mechanics matter in a private MBO, but they are the same choices as in any SPA. The issue that defines an MBO is that insiders are buying."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.83]–[15.84]; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"MBO's defining issue: managers on both sides, with better information."
},

{ id:"rA-4-01", lesson:"A.4", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Wan identifies information asymmetry as the first MBO concern. Between whom does it run?",
  options:[
    {t:"Management vs shareholders and rival bidders", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.83] says the managers know the current value and future prospects of the company in full, while the other shareholders do not. It adds that any competing bidder will also lack that information, which tilts the contest in management's favour to the detriment of shareholders and rivals alike. The Code addresses the second gap directly through the wider parity rule for MBOs in Rule 9.2 note 1. Wan [15.86] points out that it does not address the first gap specially: shareholders rely on the ordinary disclosure in the announcement and offer document."},
    {t:"Between management and the lending banks only", correct:false,
     why:"Lenders do their own diligence and usually receive management's model. They are not the parties disadvantaged by the asymmetry."},
    {t:"Between the SIC and SGX", correct:false,
     why:"Regulators are not parties to the information gap Wan describes, which is between insiders and outsiders in the transaction."},
    {t:"There is no asymmetry because all information is public", correct:false,
     why:"Internal forecasts, plans and current trading information are not public. That non-public knowledge is the source of the managers' advantage."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.83], [15.86]", takeaway:"MBO asymmetry: management vs shareholders and vs rival bidders; the Code only fixes the bidder gap specially."
},

{ id:"au-4-02", lesson:"A.4", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The CFO of a target joins forces with an outside CEO candidate and a PE fund to buy the company. What is this called?",
  options:[
    {t:"A BIMBO", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.81] fn 130 sets out the variants. An MBO is an acquisition by the incumbent management; an MBI (management buy-in) is an acquisition by a new management team from outside; a BIMBO combines incumbent managers with incoming ones. Because the CFO is an insider, the conflict and information-asymmetry analysis applies to the CFO, and the Code's MBO provisions (Rule 7.1 note 1, Rule 9.2 note 1, Rule 10 note 4) are engaged as a 'management buy-out or similar transaction'. The incoming CEO has no fiduciary conflict at the target but will be a concert party of the bidder."},
    {t:"An MBI", correct:false,
     why:"An MBI involves only outside managers. Here an incumbent, the CFO, is also a buyer, which is what makes it a BIMBO."},
    {t:"A pure MBO by the CFO", correct:false,
     why:"A pure MBO is led by incumbent management alone. The outside CEO makes this a hybrid."},
    {t:"A reverse takeover", correct:false,
     why:"An RTO is a listed company acquiring a larger unlisted business in exchange for its shares, so that the vendors end up controlling the listed company. It has nothing to do with management buyers."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.81] fn 130", takeaway:"MBO = insiders; MBI = outsiders; BIMBO = both."
},

{ id:"fa-4-02", lesson:"A.4", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which package best manages the conflict in a listed-company MBO?",
  options:[
    {t:"Early independent advice, recusal and abstention", correct:true,
     why:"The Code's principal tool, Wan [15.87] says, is the requirement for competent independent advice, which Rule 7.1 note 1 makes 'particularly important' in an MBO and requires to be appointed as soon as the board becomes aware an offer may be made. A committee of independent directors is not mandated by the Code, but Wan [15.89] notes it has become the practice in Singapore, as in the UK, and it helps directors show they have discharged their duties. The conflicted managers should take no part in the board's evaluation or recommendation. In a scheme, the bidder and its concert parties (including the managers) do not vote with independent shareholders, which effectively gives the independent holders a vote. In an offer, the safeguard is each holder's own decision to accept, informed by the IFA."},
    {t:"Simply disclosing the conflict in the annual report", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure after the event does nothing to protect shareholders during a live transaction. The safeguards have to operate on the decision itself."},
    {t:"Increasing the price by 10%", correct:false,
     why:"An arbitrary price uplift does not fix a process defect, and nobody independent would have tested whether the starting price was fair."},
    {t:"Obtaining a whitewash waiver", correct:false,
     why:"A whitewash is a waiver of the mandatory offer obligation on an issue of new shares. It has nothing to do with managing directors' conflicts in a buyout."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.86]–[15.90]; Code r 7.1 n 1", takeaway:"MBO safeguards: early IFA (Code), independent committee and recusal (practice), MBO parties out of any scheme vote."
},

{ id:"rA-4-02", lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A listed target sets up a three-person committee of 'independent directors' to evaluate an MBO. One member is a non-executive director who has agreed to reinvest his own 2% holding into the MBO vehicle.",
  prompt:"What is the problem?",
  options:[
    {t:"He is not independent: likely a concert party", correct:true,
     why:"The committee exists to negotiate with and evaluate the management bid on behalf of the shareholders who will exit. A director who has agreed to roll his shares into the bidder shares the bidder's interest in a low price and will probably be treated as acting in concert with it. He cannot evaluate the offer impartially, and his presence undermines the committee's work and the board's recommendation. He should be replaced by a genuinely disinterested director. In Singapore practice, directors in his position usually apply to the SIC to be exempted from making a recommendation on the offer (while still taking responsibility for the accuracy of facts in the circular), and his arrangement will need to be disclosed."},
    {t:"No problem: non-executive directors are always independent", correct:false,
     why:"Independence is about the person's interest in the transaction, not the label. A non-executive who is buying alongside management has a conflict."},
    {t:"No problem as long as he discloses his interest", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure is necessary but not enough. The point of the committee is that its members have no stake on the buyer's side."},
    {t:"The committee is unnecessary because the IFA decides", correct:false,
     why:"The IFA advises; it does not decide. The board, through its independent members, must form and publish its own view on the offer, informed by that advice."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.89]; Code r 7.1 n 1", takeaway:"A director rolling into the bidder is not 'independent' for the MBO committee."
},

{ id:"fa-4-04", lesson:"A.4", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Because the managers know the business best, an MBO can safely be negotiated as an ordinary arm's-length deal.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The managers' superior knowledge is the problem, not the solution. They are fiduciaries of the company and buyers of it at the same time, and they know more than the shareholders and any rival bidder. An arm's-length deal assumes two independent sides; in an MBO one side is on both sides. That is why the Code steps up the independent-advice requirement and the information-parity rule for MBOs, why practice uses an independent committee, and why management's retained interest must carry real risk. In a private MBO, the same logic explains why sellers resist giving warranties to the managers."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This is the classic error. An MBO is inherently conflicted, and treating it as arm's length leaves shareholders exposed to an insider buying cheaply."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.83]–[15.87]; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"An MBO is never arm's length; it is a conflicted deal needing safeguards."
},

{ id:"rA-4-03", lesson:"A.4", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"During an MBO, management holds internal projections showing upside not shared with the selling shareholders. What must happen?",
  options:[
    {t:"Share them with the independent side before pricing", correct:true,
     why:"Directors' fiduciary duties are owed to the company, not to individual shareholders (Percival v Wright; Peskin v Anderson), so the managers are not normally under a direct duty to disclose to each shareholder. But that does not let them exploit information obtained through their office. They must cooperate with the independent directors so that the board and the IFA evaluate the price with full information; the Code expects directors involved in the offer to help assemble information for the independent directors. Material projections relied on by the bidder may need to be disclosed in the offer documents, and Rule 9.2 note 1 entitles a rival bidder to them. On a listed target, buying shares personally while holding that information also risks breaching the SFA's insider-trading provisions."},
    {t:"Nothing: managers may keep their projections secret to get a lower price", correct:false,
     why:"That is the abuse the MBO safeguards exist to prevent. Using company information to buy the company cheaply exposes the managers to claims for breach of duty and, on a listed company, to insider-trading liability."},
    {t:"The projections must be destroyed", correct:false,
     why:"Destroying company records is improper and could itself be a breach of duty. The obligation is to put the information before those who make the decision."},
    {t:"The sellers must simply trust management", correct:false,
     why:"Trust is precisely what the conflict undermines. Independent evaluation with full information is the safeguard."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.83], [15.86], [15.93]; Code r 9.2 n 1", takeaway:"Conflicted managers can't buy cheap on inside info: put it before the independent side."
},

{ id:"au-4-04", lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A rival bidder appears against an MBO for a listed target and asks for the detailed operating model that management gave to the MBO's lending banks.",
  prompt:"Must the target board provide it?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes, including information given to financiers", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 9.2 requires information given to one offeror or potential offeror to be given, on request, equally and promptly to any other bona fide offeror or potential offeror. In an MBO, note 1 widens what must be shared: information generated by the target (including by management acting as such) that is passed to external providers or potential providers of finance to the MBO bidder, and any other information material to making an offer that the board knows management possesses. Without this, a rival would be bidding blind against insiders. Trade and business secrets are excluded from note 1, and the target should consult the SIC before refusing a request for secrets already given to one bidder. Wan [15.86] notes this fixes the bidder-versus-bidder gap but not the gap between management and shareholders."},
    {t:"No, because information given to lenders is not information given to a bidder", correct:false,
     why:"That is exactly the gap note 1 to Rule 9.2 closes for MBOs. In an MBO, the financiers' package is treated as information to be shared."},
    {t:"Only if the rival bid is higher", correct:false,
     why:"The test is whether the rival is a bona fide offeror or potential offeror, not whether its price is higher. A rival may need the information to decide whether to bid higher."},
    {t:"Yes, and it must also be published to all shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Parity under Rule 9.2 runs between bidders. Shareholders rely on the information in the offer documents and the board circular, and Rule 9.1 equality of information to shareholders."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.86]; Code r 9.2 n 1", takeaway:"MBO parity covers info given to the MBO's financiers and material info management holds."
},

{ id:"au-4-03", lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An MBO vehicle (BidCo) has an offer out for a listed target at S$1.00. BidCo holds 27%. During the offer period two managers buy another 4% on market at S$1.05.",
  prompt:"What are the Code consequences?",
  options:[
    {t:"Concert parties: a mandatory offer at S$1.05", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.94] says managers are likely to be treated as acting in concert with the MBO vehicle, so their purchases aggregate with BidCo's. BidCo's 27% plus the managers' 4% is 31%, which crosses 30% and triggers a mandatory offer under Rule 14. A mandatory offer must be in cash (or with a cash alternative) at not less than the highest price paid by the offeror or its concert parties during the offer period and the six months before, so the price must be at least S$1.05. Even without the trigger, purchases above the offer price during the offer require the offer to be raised. Wan fn 151 adds a further cost: a mandatory offer cannot carry an acceptance condition above 50%, which removes BidCo's ability to insist on reaching the 90% needed for compulsory acquisition."},
    {t:"None, because managers act in a personal capacity", correct:false,
     why:"That is the argument the concert-party analysis defeats. Managers investing in the bidder vehicle cooperate with it to obtain control and are treated as acting in concert."},
    {t:"Only an insider-trading issue, not a Code issue", correct:false,
     why:"Insider trading is a separate risk under the SFA (Wan [15.93]). The Code consequences are aggregation, a mandatory offer and the price floor."},
    {t:"The offer lapses automatically", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing lapses. The consequences add obligations: a mandatory offer on Rule 14 terms and a higher price."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.94] fn 151; Code r 14", takeaway:"Managers = concert parties: their buys aggregate, can trigger r 14, and set the price floor."
},

{ id:"au-4-05", lesson:"A.4", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In a listed-company MBO, all other shareholders exit for cash while managers keep an equity stake via BidCo. Is that a prohibited special deal?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not if they bear full equity risk; consult the SIC", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 10 prohibits arrangements with some shareholders on favourable terms not extended to all. Management keeping an interest in the business while everyone else is bought out looks like such an arrangement. Note 4 to Rule 10 requires the SIC to be consulted where management will remain financially interested after the offer, and the principle the Council safeguards is that the risks as well as the rewards of an equity shareholding must apply to management's retained interest. Wan [15.95] describes this as a limited exception. The note gives an example of what fails: an option arrangement guaranteeing the original offer price as a minimum, because that removes the downside."},
    {t:"Always prohibited, because other shareholders cannot roll over", correct:false,
     why:"The Code expressly accommodates management's continuing interest, provided it carries real equity risk and the SIC is consulted."},
    {t:"Always permitted, because managers are not ordinary shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"It is permitted only on conditions. A retained interest with a guaranteed floor or other risk-free sweetener would be a special deal."},
    {t:"The question does not arise because the Code excludes MBOs", correct:false,
     why:"The Code applies to MBOs of Code companies and contains specific MBO provisions in Rules 7.1, 9.2, 10 and 17.2."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.95]; Code r 10 n 4", takeaway:"Manager rollover OK only if they take full risk and reward (consult the SIC)."
},

{ id:"au-4-01", lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In a private MBO, the non-executive selling shareholders refuse to give business warranties: 'You run the company. You know more than we do.' Management, backed by a PE fund, insists on a full warranty package.",
  prompt:"What best describes the dynamic?",
  options:[
    {t:"Warranties as allocation, then warranties from management", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.96]–[15.97] explains that in a private MBO the usual information function of warranties is weak, because the buyers are the insiders who know the business. Selling shareholders reasonably resist warranting matters the managers know better. Management may still press for warranties as a matter of risk allocation: if something goes wrong that nobody knew about, who pays? The financial investors face the mirror image. They are investing alongside managers who ran the business, so they will want warranties from management about the target when they subscribe for BidCo shares, and Wan says it is hard for management to resist. In a Code offer the question does not arise, because selling shareholders give no warranties."},
    {t:"The sellers must give full warranties as in any SPA", correct:false,
     why:"There is no legal requirement for any particular warranty package. The market recognises MBO sellers' resistance, and the outcome is negotiated, often with limited warranties or W&I insurance."},
    {t:"Warranties are prohibited in an MBO", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing prohibits them. The debate is about who should give them and why."},
    {t:"The Take-Over Code resolves it", correct:false,
     why:"A private MBO is outside the Code. In a Code offer, shareholders accept the offer without giving warranties."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.96]–[15.97]", takeaway:"Private MBO: sellers resist warranties; investors take warranties from managers."
},

{ id:"fa-4-03", lesson:"A.4", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each management-incentive/rollover term to what it does.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Rollover", r:"Managers take part of their consideration as equity in BidCo rather than cash"},
    {l:"Ratchet", r:"Adjusts management's equity share based on performance"},
    {l:"Good leaver / bad leaver", r:"Determines the price a departing manager gets for their equity"},
    {l:"Vendor loan", r:"Deferred consideration the seller leaves in as debt"}
  ],
  why:"Buyouts rely on incentive engineering. A rollover keeps managers invested by converting part of their proceeds into BidCo equity. A ratchet increases (or decreases) management's share of the equity depending on the sponsor's returns or other targets. Leaver provisions in the SHA decide what a departing manager receives: a good leaver (death, illness, dismissal without cause) usually gets fair value; a bad leaver (resignation, dismissal for cause) gets the lower of cost and fair value. A vendor loan is part of the price that the seller lends back to the buyer, reducing the cash needed at completion. In a listed MBO, each must be checked against Rule 10 note 4 so that management's retained interest carries real equity risk.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §G; Intro 2.12, glossary", takeaway:"Rollover, ratchet, leaver terms and vendor loans align management incentives."
},

{ id:"rA-4-04", lesson:"A.4", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In an MBO/buyout, what is a management 'ratchet'?",
  options:[
    {t:"An equity share that rises with performance", correct:true,
     why:"A ratchet gives managers a larger share of the equity at exit if the sponsor's return (usually measured as an IRR or money multiple) passes agreed hurdles. Some ratchets also work downwards if targets are missed. The aim is to concentrate management's reward on the outcomes the sponsor values. Ratchets need careful drafting: the measurement point, how the return is calculated, how leaver events interact with the ratchet, and the tax treatment of the extra equity all matter."},
    {t:"A clause reducing the purchase price over time", correct:false,
     why:"A ratchet changes how the equity is split among investors after the deal. It does not change the price paid to the sellers."},
    {t:"A lender's right to seize the shares", correct:false,
     why:"That is enforcement of security. A ratchet is an incentive term between the equity investors."},
    {t:"A restriction stopping managers from ever selling", correct:false,
     why:"Transfer restrictions are separate SHA terms. A ratchet scales management's equity share to performance."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15; Gaughan Ch 8", takeaway:"Ratchet = more equity for management if return targets are hit."
},

{ id:"rA-4-05", lesson:"A.4", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: keeping an MBO approach confidential matters because a leak can destabilise the business and, for a listed target, may force an early announcement before a proper process is in place.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"An uncontrolled leak can unsettle staff, customers and lenders, and it exposes the conflict before the independent directors and adviser are in place. For a listed target, Rule 2 of the Code requires an announcement where there is rumour or speculation or an untoward price movement once an approach has been made or is contemplated, so a leak can force a premature announcement and start the offer period. Wan [15.93] notes that in an MBO the managers in practice approach the independent directors, who can then control the process, information flow and timing."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"It is true. Leaks destabilise the business and, for a listed target, can trigger the Code's announcement obligations before the parties are ready."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.93]; Code r 2; Intro 2.2", takeaway:"Manage MBO confidentiality; a leak can force a Code announcement."
},

{ id:"p2x-A4-01", lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In a listed MBO at S$2.00 per share, the PE fund offers the CEO a put option: for three years after completion he may sell his BidCo shares back to the fund at a price equivalent to S$2.00 per original target share.",
  prompt:"How will the SIC likely view this?",
  options:[
    {t:"Unacceptable: it removes the equity risk", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4 to Rule 10 permits management to remain financially interested after an offer only if the risks as well as the rewards of an equity holding apply to their retained interest. The note gives this exact example: the Council would not normally find acceptable an option arrangement that guaranteed the original offer price as a minimum. With the put, the CEO keeps all the upside and has none of the downside, so he is effectively getting a better deal than the shareholders who were bought out at S$2.00. That is a special deal under Rule 10. The parties should consult the SIC early and restructure, for example by dropping the floor."},
    {t:"Acceptable, because the CEO is management", correct:false,
     why:"Being management is what brings note 4 into play; it does not make any arrangement acceptable. The test is whether the retained interest carries risk as well as reward."},
    {t:"Acceptable, because the put is at the offer price, not above it", correct:false,
     why:"A floor at the offer price is precisely the example the note rules out. It removes the downside risk that ordinary equity carries."},
    {t:"Irrelevant to the Code, because it is a contract between the CEO and the fund", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 10 covers arrangements between the offeror (or its concert parties) and shareholders of the offeree. The fund is part of the offeror side and the CEO is an offeree shareholder."}
  ],
  cite:"Code r 10 n 4; Wan [15.95]", takeaway:"A put at the offer price guarantees a floor → special deal; retained interest must carry risk."
},

{ id:"p2x-A4-02", lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The board of a listed company learns from its CEO that she is talking to a PE fund about an MBO. The chairman suggests waiting to appoint an independent financial adviser until a firm offer is announced, to save costs.",
  prompt:"What does the Code expect?",
  options:[
    {t:"Appoint the IFA as early as possible", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 7.1 requires the offeree board to obtain competent independent advice on any offer and make its substance known to shareholders. Note 1 says the requirement is particularly important in a management buy-out or an offer by or with the cooperation of a controlling shareholder. Because the adviser's responsibility is considerable, the board should appoint it as soon as possible after it becomes aware that such an offer may be made. Early appointment lets the adviser help the independent directors negotiate the price, assess any information management holds and consider whether to test the market, rather than simply giving a fairness view at the end."},
    {t:"Wait until the firm offer is announced, as with any offer", correct:false,
     why:"For an MBO the Code specifically asks for early appointment. Waiting leaves the independent directors without expert support during the negotiations, when the conflict matters most."},
    {t:"The CEO's own financial adviser can serve as the independent adviser", correct:false,
     why:"The CEO is on the bidder side. Her adviser is not independent of the offer and cannot advise the offeree board."},
    {t:"No adviser is needed because it is a management offer", correct:false,
     why:"The reverse: independent advice is most important in a management offer."}
  ],
  cite:"Code r 7.1 n 1; Wan [15.87]", takeaway:"MBO: appoint the IFA as soon as the board knows an offer may be made."
},

{ id:"p2x-A4-03", amended:true, lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Under the 2026 Code, a bona fide rival asks an MBO target for 'everything you have given the management consortium'. The board replies that the rival must first list the specific questions it wants answered.",
  prompt:"Is the board right?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: on request it must give the same information", correct:true,
     why:"Before the 2026 amendments, Rule 9.2 said the competing offeror should specify the questions to which it required answers. That wording has been deleted. The amended rule says that on request the offeree board must provide the requesting offeror with all the information it has provided to another offeror or potential offeror, and all it provides in the seven days following the request. In an MBO, note 1 defines that information to include material passed to the consortium's financiers and other material information the board knows management holds. Trade and business secrets are still carved out of note 1, and the board should consult the SIC before refusing secrets already given to one bidder."},
    {t:"Yes: competing offerors must always specify their questions", correct:false,
     why:"That was the pre-2026 position. The requirement to specify questions was removed, making parity easier for rivals to obtain."},
    {t:"Yes, but only for MBOs; other offers get everything", correct:false,
     why:"The amendment applies to all offers, and note 1 for MBOs no longer refers to specified questions either."},
    {t:"No: the board must publish all information to the market", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 9.2 is about parity between offerors. It does not require publication; Rule 9.1 governs information to shareholders."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 9.2 & n 1; amendments digest p64", takeaway:"2026: no need to specify questions; rival gets everything given + next 7 days."
},

{ id:"p2x-A4-04", lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In a listed MBO, the three managers (holding 12% between them) will exchange their target shares for BidCo shares, while all other shareholders get cash. A 6% family shareholder, unconnected to management, asks for the same BidCo-share option and BidCo agrees.",
  prompt:"What is the Code consequence of agreeing to the family holder's request?",
  options:[
    {t:"BidCo shares must be offered to all holders", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 17.2 normally requires a securities offer to all shareholders where the offeror and its concert parties have acquired 10% or more of a class in exchange for securities during the offer period and the three months before it. Note 4 makes an exception for MBOs: if the only offeree shareholders who receive offeror securities are members of management, the SIC will not require securities to be offered to everyone, even if management exchanges more than 10%, provided Rule 10 note 4 is complied with. But note 4 also says that if offeror securities are made available to any non-management shareholder, regardless of the size of their holding, the Council will normally require them to be made available to all shareholders on the same terms. Agreeing to the family holder's request therefore converts the deal into one where every shareholder must be offered the BidCo-share alternative."},
    {t:"Nothing: the family holder is below 10%", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 says the size of the non-management holder's stake does not matter. Once any non-management holder gets offeror securities, the exception is lost."},
    {t:"Only the managers' exchange is affected", correct:false,
     why:"The managers' exchange was protected by the MBO exception. Extending securities to a non-management holder takes the whole transaction outside it."},
    {t:"The family holder becomes a concert party, so nothing changes", correct:false,
     why:"Being a concert party does not make someone 'management'. The exception is limited to members of the offeree's management."}
  ],
  cite:"Code r 17.2 n 4, r 10 n 4", takeaway:"MBO securities exception covers management only; offer securities to any outsider → offer to all."
},

{ id:"p2x-A4-05", lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An MBO bidder intends to reach 90% and then compulsorily acquire the rest under s 215. It sets a 90% acceptance condition. Before the offer closes, a manager's on-market purchases take the concert group above 30%.",
  prompt:"Why is this a problem for the bidder's plan?",
  options:[
    {t:"A mandatory offer can't keep a 90% condition", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.94] fn 151 makes exactly this point. Once the concert group crosses 30%, Rule 14 requires a mandatory offer, and a mandatory offer can only be conditional on the offeror receiving acceptances that bring it above 50% of the voting rights. The bidder can no longer insist on 90% as a condition. If acceptances exceed 50% but fall short of 90%, the offer becomes unconditional and the bidder must buy all accepted shares while being left with minorities it cannot squeeze out. That prejudices a buyout financed on the assumption of 100% ownership and debt push-down. The lesson: managers should not deal in target shares during an MBO without clearing it with the bidder's advisers."},
    {t:"No problem: the bidder can keep its 90% condition", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14 offers are subject to only one permitted condition, the more-than-50% acceptance condition. A 90% condition is not allowed."},
    {t:"The manager's purchases are void", correct:false,
     why:"The purchases are valid. Their effect is to change the nature of the offer, not to undo the trades."},
    {t:"s 215 no longer applies to the offer", correct:false,
     why:"Section 215 can still apply if the bidder in fact receives 90% acceptances. The problem is that the bidder can no longer make the offer conditional on reaching that level."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.94] fn 151; Code r 14; CA s 215", takeaway:"Managers' dealings can trigger r 14 → acceptance condition capped at 50% → s 215 plan at risk."
},

{ id:"p2x-A4-06", lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two weeks before an MBO is announced, the target's COO buys 200,000 target shares on SGX in his own name. He knows of the MBO and of internal forecasts not yet released.",
  prompt:"What is his main legal exposure?",
  options:[
    {t:"Insider trading under the SFA", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.93] notes that while the Code does not itself regulate management's dealings while in possession of unpublished information, managers must make sure they stay within insider-trading law. Under Part 12 of the SFA, a person connected to the company who possesses information that is not generally available and that a reasonable person would expect to have a material effect on the share price must not deal in the shares. Both the pending MBO and the unreleased forecasts are classic inside information. As a likely concert party of the bidder, his purchases also count for Code purposes, and they may be caught by Rule 11's dealing restrictions and Rule 12's disclosure rules once the offer period starts."},
    {t:"None, because the Code does not regulate management's dealings", correct:false,
     why:"Wan's point is the opposite: the absence of a Code rule does not mean managers are free to deal. Insider-trading law under the SFA applies with full force."},
    {t:"Only a breach of the company's internal dealing policy", correct:false,
     why:"An internal policy breach is the least of his problems. Dealing on inside information is a criminal offence and a civil penalty contravention."},
    {t:"None, because he bought on the market rather than from a shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"Insider-trading prohibitions apply to on-market trades. Most insider-trading cases involve exchange trades with anonymous counterparties."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.93], Ch 16; SFA Pt 12 [external]", takeaway:"Managers dealing pre-announcement on MBO/forecast knowledge = insider trading risk."
},

{ id:"p2x-A4-07", lesson:"A.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Activist shareholders in a listed target say the management buyout must be approved by a majority of the shareholders who are not connected to management, as in Delaware. The bidder is considering an offer or a scheme.",
  prompt:"Which statement is correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"No majority-of-minority rule; a scheme gives one in effect", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [15.90] notes that a vote of the selling shareholders is not provided for in the Code. In a takeover offer each shareholder simply decides whether to accept; there is no vote on whether the offer proceeds. A scheme is different. The scheme must be approved at a court meeting by a majority in number representing 75% in value of those voting, and the SIC's conditions for exempting a scheme from parts of the Code require the bidder and its concert parties (which in an MBO includes management) to abstain or vote separately. That gives the independent shareholders a decisive vote. Wan also notes that the SIC could recommend an auction in a suitable case, now reflected in Appendix 4."},
    {t:"The Code requires a majority-of-the-minority vote for every MBO", correct:false,
     why:"There is no such requirement. Wan discusses it as a possible reform, not current law."},
    {t:"Neither route gives independent shareholders any say", correct:false,
     why:"In an offer they decide individually whether to accept; in a scheme they vote, without the bidder's concert parties."},
    {t:"SGX interested-person rules automatically require an independent vote", correct:false,
     why:"Wan fn 146 says it is not clear that an MBO by way of offer falls within the SGX interested-person transaction rules at all."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [15.90]–[15.91] fn 146; Code (definition of offer, notes on schemes); App 4", takeaway:"No MOM vote in a Code offer; a scheme gives independent holders a vote (concert parties out)."
}

]);

;
