/* DealCraft — Chapter 2 · Lesson 2.6 Strategic & Regulated Share Deals (audited Oct 2026).
   Sources: Intro 2.3, 2.6, 2.10–2.14, 2.16; Wan Ch 3 [3.9], Ch 4 §C, Ch 5 §C; Competition Act ss 34, 54, 69; SGX Mainboard Rules Ch 10 [external]. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"fa-6-01", lesson:"A.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"On legal due diligence for a STRATEGIC share deal, which clause type is the classic hidden landmine?",
  options:[
    {t:"Change-of-control clauses", correct:true,
     why:"In a share deal the target's contracts stay with the target, so in principle nothing needs to be assigned and no consents are needed. Change-of-control clauses are the exception. They are drafted to react to a change in who owns or controls the contracting party, and give the counterparty a right to consent, terminate, renegotiate or exercise an option (for example to buy out a JV stake). For a strategic buyer the risk is acute: customers or suppliers may not want to deal with a competitor. Diligence must find every such clause, read the definition of 'control', and decide whether to seek waivers before signing or make the important ones conditions."},
    {t:"The governing-law and notices boilerplate", correct:false,
     why:"Boilerplate rarely allocates commercial risk. It does not react to a change of ownership in the way a change-of-control clause does."},
    {t:"The recitals", correct:false,
     why:"Recitals are background, not operative terms. They cannot give anyone a right to terminate on a share sale."},
    {t:"The counterparts clause", correct:false,
     why:"A counterparts clause concerns signing mechanics. It has no effect on the deal's risk profile."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 4 §C; Intro 2.6", takeaway:"Share-deal DD: hunt change-of-control clauses in material contracts."
},

{ id:"au-6-03", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A distribution agreement lets the supplier terminate on 'a change of control of the Distributor or any of its holding companies'. The buyer is acquiring the Distributor's ultimate parent, not the Distributor itself.",
  prompt:"Does the clause bite?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: it catches indirect changes; seek consent", correct:true,
     why:"Change-of-control clauses are often drafted to reach up the ownership chain precisely so that a counterparty cannot be bypassed by a sale at holding-company level. Buying the ultimate parent changes who ultimately controls the Distributor, which is what the clause is about. Diligence therefore has to read each clause's wording rather than assume that only a direct sale of the contracting entity matters, and has to map the group structure to see which entities' clauses are triggered. If the contract is material, the buyer should seek a waiver before signing or make it a condition; otherwise it should price the risk of losing the contract."},
    {t:"No: the Distributor's own shares are not being sold", correct:false,
     why:"The clause covers 'any of its holding companies'. An indirect change of control is exactly what it was drafted to catch."},
    {t:"No: change-of-control clauses are unenforceable in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"They are ordinary contractual terms and are enforced according to their wording."},
    {t:"Only if the Distributor is listed", correct:false,
     why:"Listing is irrelevant to a contractual trigger. The clause applies however the Distributor is owned."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10", takeaway:"Read CoC wording: 'holding companies' catches indirect deals."
},

{ id:"rA-6-02", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A key customer contract lets the customer terminate on a 'change of control' of the supplier, defined as any person acquiring more than 50% of the voting shares OR the right to appoint a majority of the board. A buyer will acquire 45% of the supplier, and the new shareholders' agreement gives it the right to appoint four of seven directors.",
  prompt:"Is the clause triggered?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: a board-majority right is one limb", correct:true,
     why:"The definition has two alternative limbs. The buyer stays below 50% of the votes, so the first limb is not met. But the SHA gives it the right to appoint four of seven directors, which is a majority of the board, so the second limb is. A deal team that looked only at the shareholding percentage would miss this. The lesson is to read the definition of 'control' in each material contract against the whole deal package, including the SHA and any options, and to seek a waiver or restructure (for example, three of seven seats with a casting vote held elsewhere) if the customer matters."},
    {t:"No: the buyer is below 50%", correct:false,
     why:"That deals with only one limb. The board-appointment limb is satisfied independently."},
    {t:"No: rights under a shareholders' agreement do not count", correct:false,
     why:"The definition refers to 'the right to appoint a majority of the board' without limiting how that right arises. A contractual right in an SHA is such a right."},
    {t:"Only if the customer can show it is harmed", correct:false,
     why:"A change-of-control termination right is usually triggered by the event itself. The customer does not need to show harm unless the clause says so."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.6, 2.10", takeaway:"Test CoC definitions against the whole package: board-appointment rights can trigger below 50%."
},

{ id:"rA-6-01", lesson:"A.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In a strategic SHARE deal, what is the buyer's primary protection against the liabilities it inherits with the entity?",
  options:[
    {t:"DD plus warranties and indemnities", correct:true,
     why:"A share buyer takes the company with all its history: past contracts, litigation, regulatory breaches and tax. It cannot leave liabilities behind as an asset buyer can. So its protection is investigation and contract. Diligence finds the issues. Warranties make the seller answerable for undisclosed problems and, through the disclosure process, force the seller to reveal what it knows. Specific indemnities cover known problems dollar for dollar. A tax deed or tax covenant gives the buyer an indemnity for pre-completion tax. Price adjustments, retentions and W&I insurance support all of this."},
    {t:"Defining the perimeter precisely", correct:false,
     why:"Perimeter definition is the asset-deal protection. In a share deal the whole company transfers, so there is no perimeter to draw inside it."},
    {t:"A transitional services agreement", correct:false,
     why:"A TSA bridges shared services after a carve-out. It does not protect against inherited liabilities."},
    {t:"A mandatory general offer", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer is a Code obligation on acquiring control of a Code company. It has nothing to do with the buyer's protection against the target's liabilities."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.3, 2.10", takeaway:"Share deal protection = DD + warranties + indemnities + tax deed."
},

{ id:"fa-6-04", lesson:"A.6", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: In a strategic share deal, the buyer inherits the target's historic tax position.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"A share buyer acquires the company as it stands, so any tax the company owes for past periods (underpaid income tax, GST errors, withholding tax failures, transfer-pricing adjustments) remains the company's liability after completion. The buyer bears it economically through the reduced value of its shares. That is why tax diligence is a standard workstream and why the SPA includes tax warranties (which flush out disclosure) and a tax deed or covenant (an indemnity for pre-completion tax, usually not subject to the general warranty limitations). In an asset deal, by contrast, the seller's tax history stays with the seller entity, although some taxes attach to particular assets."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"It is true. Nothing inside the company moves on a share sale, so its tax history moves with it."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2 §E; Intro 2.3, 2.13", takeaway:"Share deal inherits tax history → tax DD + tax deed."
},

{ id:"fa-6-02", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The target is a licensed payment-services firm. The buyer will acquire 100% of its shares.",
  prompt:"What is the key completion gate?",
  options:[
    {t:"MAS controller approval", correct:true,
     why:"Sector statutes for MAS-regulated firms (banks, insurers, capital-markets licensees, payment institutions) generally require a person to obtain MAS approval before becoming a substantial shareholder or controller. MAS assesses whether the incoming controller is fit and proper (honesty, integrity, reputation, competence, financial soundness) and whether the acquisition is in the public interest. Completing without approval is an offence, and MAS can order the shares disposed of. So the approval is a mandatory condition that the parties cannot waive. The SPA should allocate who prepares the application, the efforts standard, and what happens if MAS imposes conditions. The timetable must allow for MAS's review."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer under the Take-Over Code", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer arises only for Code companies when 30% is crossed. A private payment firm is outside the Code. The distinctive gate here is the regulator."},
    {t:"Stamp duty clearance from IRAS", correct:false,
     why:"Stamp duty is paid after signing; it is not a gate that must be cleared before completion."},
    {t:"SGX transaction classification", correct:false,
     why:"SGX classification applies when a listed company makes an acquisition that is large relative to its own size. It may apply if the buyer is listed, but it is not the regulated-firm gate."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 3; Intro 2.14 (Chapter 9)", takeaway:"Regulated target → MAS controller approval (fit-and-proper) as a mandatory CP."
},

{ id:"fa-6-03", lesson:"A.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A clearance the law requires before completion (e.g. MAS controller approval, or a merger filing in a jurisdiction where filing is compulsory and suspensory) is best characterised as which kind of condition?",
  options:[
    {t:"A mandatory, non-waivable condition", correct:true,
     why:"Conditions fall into two families. Mandatory conditions are imposed by law: the parties cannot lawfully complete without them, so neither side can waive them. Examples are MAS controller approval, a compulsory and suspensory foreign merger filing, and FDI approval where required. Protective conditions are negotiated for a party's benefit (no MAC, warranties true at completion, key consents) and can be waived by that party. Singapore's own CCCS regime is voluntary and non-suspensory, so a CCCS clearance condition is a protective condition the parties chose to include, not a mandatory one."},
    {t:"A protective condition the buyer may waive", correct:false,
     why:"Protective conditions are the buyer's own negotiated protections. A legal requirement cannot be waived by agreement between private parties."},
    {t:"A condition subsequent that operates after completion", correct:false,
     why:"A required approval must be obtained before completion. A condition subsequent (which unwinds a completed deal if not met) is rare and awkward, and would not satisfy a regulator who requires prior approval."},
    {t:"A warranty", correct:false,
     why:"A warranty is a statement of fact giving a damages claim. A required clearance is a gate to completion."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 5 §C; Intro 2.11", takeaway:"Legal approvals = mandatory (non-waivable) conditions; a CCCS condition is a protective choice."
},

{ id:"rA-6-03", lesson:"A.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A required MAS approval and a CCCS clearance the parties have agreed to seek will take months. How are they best handled in the deal?",
  options:[
    {t:"As CPs with efforts duties and a long-stop", correct:true,
     why:"Approvals that gate completion are conditions. Because they can take months, the SPA must also say who does what (preparing filings, responding to the regulator's questions), to what standard (reasonable endeavours, all reasonable endeavours, or an obligation to accept remedies), and by when. The long-stop date caps how long the parties stay bound. The gap also creates other risks the SPA must address: interim covenants to stop the target being run down, a MAC condition, and, for competitors, rules against exchanging sensitive information before completion."},
    {t:"As warranties that clearance already exists", correct:false,
     why:"A party cannot warrant a regulator's future decision. These are gates to completion."},
    {t:"Ignored until after completion", correct:false,
     why:"Completing before a legally required MAS approval is unlawful. Completing before an agreed CCCS clearance breaches the SPA and risks a later CCCS direction to unwind."},
    {t:"As boilerplate", correct:false,
     why:"Regulatory conditions are among the most negotiated provisions in a strategic deal."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.5, 2.16", takeaway:"Slow approvals → CPs + efforts standard + long-stop date."
},

{ id:"au-6-01", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two Singapore competitors agree a share sale that may raise s 54 concerns. The buyer's GC says: 'CCCS notification is voluntary, so we can skip it and complete next week.'",
  prompt:"What is the best advice?",
  options:[
    {t:"Voluntary, but CCCS can still intervene; weigh notifying", correct:true,
     why:"Singapore's merger regime is voluntary and non-suspensory: there is no legal duty to notify CCCS and no prohibition on completing before clearance. But s 54 still prohibits mergers that have resulted, or may be expected to result, in a substantial lessening of competition. CCCS can investigate an un-notified merger on its own initiative, including after completion, and can give directions such as requiring the merger to be unwound or assets divested, and impose financial penalties. Where the parties' overlap is significant (CCCS's guidelines flag market-share indicators), the prudent course is to notify and either make clearance a condition or allocate the risk expressly."},
    {t:"Correct: voluntary means there is no competition risk", correct:false,
     why:"Voluntary notification does not switch off s 54. It only means the parties choose whether to seek comfort in advance."},
    {t:"Wrong: completion before clearance is a criminal offence in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore's regime is non-suspensory. Completing without clearance is not an offence; the risk is CCCS enforcement under s 54."},
    {t:"Irrelevant, because merger control only applies to listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"Section 54 applies to mergers between any undertakings, private or listed."}
  ],
  cite:"Competition Act ss 54, 69; Chapter 8", takeaway:"CCCS: voluntary, non-suspensory, but s 54 still bites; clearance CP is a choice."
},

{ id:"rA-6-05", lesson:"A.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Buyer and target are competitors. Their SPA is conditional on CCCS clearance, which is pending. Why must they NOT integrate or coordinate commercially yet?",
  options:[
    {t:"They are still competitors: s 34 and gun-jumping risk", correct:true,
     why:"Signing an SPA does not merge two businesses. Until completion they remain separate undertakings, and s 34 of the Competition Act prohibits agreements and concerted practices that prevent, restrict or distort competition. Agreeing on prices, allocating customers or sharing current pricing and strategy data could be such a practice. The usual safeguards are clean-team arrangements for diligence and integration planning (limited people, aggregated or historic data), interim covenants that give the buyer consent rights only over matters that protect value rather than control day-to-day competition, and no joint marketing before completion. Where other jurisdictions with suspensory regimes are involved, gun-jumping attracts heavy fines."},
    {t:"Because s 76 forbids integration", correct:false,
     why:"Section 76 concerns financial assistance for share acquisitions. It has nothing to do with pre-completion coordination."},
    {t:"Because the Code requires a standstill", correct:false,
     why:"The Code governs offers for Code companies. The risk here comes from competition law."},
    {t:"There is no restriction; they can integrate now because Singapore notification is voluntary", correct:false,
     why:"A voluntary merger regime does not license competitors to coordinate before they become one undertaking. Section 34 still applies to them as separate firms."}
  ],
  cite:"Competition Act s 34; Chapter 8", takeaway:"Competitors stay separate until completion: s 34 risk; clean teams for sensitive data."
},

{ id:"rA-6-04", lesson:"A.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why do parties negotiate a MAC (material adverse change) condition when a strategic deal has a long clearance gap?",
  options:[
    {t:"So the buyer can walk on serious target harm", correct:true,
     why:"The longer the gap between signing and completion, the more chance there is that the target deteriorates while the buyer is bound. A MAC condition allocates that risk: if a material adverse change occurs, the buyer may refuse to complete. Courts in comparable jurisdictions interpret MACs strictly. The change must be significant and durable, judged over the long term rather than a short dip, and a buyer that invokes a MAC carries the burden of proof. Sellers negotiate carve-outs for general economic, market and industry conditions, changes in law, and the effects of announcing the deal. Buyers who want certainty sometimes prefer objective triggers (a fall in EBITDA below a number)."},
    {t:"To fix the purchase price permanently", correct:false,
     why:"A MAC is a walk-away right, not a price mechanism."},
    {t:"To transfer employees", correct:false,
     why:"A MAC has nothing to do with employees. In a share deal the employees stay with the company anyway."},
    {t:"To avoid competition clearance", correct:false,
     why:"A MAC does not affect whether clearance is needed. It addresses deterioration while clearance is pending."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.11", takeaway:"MAC = a strictly read walk-away for serious, durable gap-period deterioration."
},

{ id:"au-6-02", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A buyer will acquire 40% of an UNLISTED public company with 80 shareholders and net tangible assets of S$20m.",
  prompt:"Does the Take-Over Code matter?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes, for unlisted public companies of that size", correct:true,
     why:"The Code is drafted with listed companies, listed REITs and listed business trusts in mind, but its Introduction says that unlisted public companies and unlisted registered business trusts with more than 50 shareholders (or unitholders) and NTA of S$5m or more must also observe the letter and spirit of the General Principles and Rules wherever possible (Wan [3.9]). Such companies may apply to the SIC to waive the Code. Here the target has 80 shareholders and NTA of S$20m, so it is within the Code. A 40% acquisition crosses 30%, which would trigger a mandatory offer under Rule 14 unless the SIC grants a waiver or the Code is waived for the company."},
    {t:"No: the Code applies only to SGX-listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"This is the common trap. The Code's reach extends to qualifying unlisted public companies and unlisted registered business trusts."},
    {t:"Only if the buyer is itself listed", correct:false,
     why:"Whether the Code applies depends on the target, not the buyer."},
    {t:"Only for acquisitions above 50%", correct:false,
     why:"The mandatory-offer threshold is 30%, so a 40% acquisition is caught."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [3.9]; Code (2026) Introduction", takeaway:"Unlisted public co, >50 holders, NTA ≥ S$5m → Code applies (subject to SIC waiver)."
},

{ id:"p2x-A6-01", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An SGX Mainboard-listed buyer with a market capitalisation of S$1.2bn agrees to buy a private target for S$300m in cash. The target's profits are about 15% of the buyer's.",
  prompt:"What does the SGX listing regime require, and how should the SPA reflect it?",
  options:[
    {t:"Likely a major transaction: make approval a condition", correct:true,
     why:"Chapter 10 of the Mainboard Rules classifies acquisitions by comparing the transaction with the listed issuer on several relative figures, including consideration against the issuer's market capitalisation and the target's profits against the issuer's. Where any relative figure exceeds 20%, the acquisition is generally a major transaction requiring a circular and shareholder approval. Here S$300m ÷ S$1.2bn = 25%, above 20%, so approval is likely needed even though the profits test is lower. Because the buyer cannot complete without that approval, the SPA should make it a condition, oblige the buyer to convene the meeting promptly and its board to recommend, and allocate the risk of a 'no' vote (sometimes through a reverse break fee)."},
    {t:"Nothing: SGX rules apply only to the target, not the buyer", correct:false,
     why:"Chapter 10 regulates the listed issuer's own acquisitions and disposals. A listed buyer must classify its acquisition."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer for the buyer's own shares", correct:false,
     why:"The buyer is acquiring a private company for cash. Nobody is acquiring voting control of the buyer, so Rule 14 is not engaged."},
    {t:"Only an immediate announcement, because it is below 50%", correct:false,
     why:"The 20% figure, not 50%, is the usual major-transaction threshold. Announcement alone is the rule for smaller discloseable transactions."}
  ],
  cite:"SGX Mainboard Rules Ch 10 [external]; Intro 2.14", takeaway:"Listed buyer: any relative figure > 20% → major transaction → shareholder approval as a CP."
},

{ id:"p2x-A6-02", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"CCCS indicates it will clear a strategic acquisition only if the buyer divests one overlapping product line. The SPA obliges the buyer to use 'reasonable endeavours' to obtain clearance, with no express obligation to accept remedies. The buyer refuses to divest and the long-stop passes.",
  prompt:"What is the likely position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Probably walk: reasonable endeavours rarely forces a sale", correct:true,
     why:"Endeavours clauses are graded. A reasonable-endeavours obligation generally requires one reasonable course of action and allows a party to weigh its own commercial interests, so it is unlikely to oblige the buyer to sell part of its business. An 'all reasonable' or 'best' endeavours standard goes further but still may not require a significant divestment. A seller who wants regulatory risk on the buyer must negotiate an express remedies covenant (the buyer must accept any divestment up to a defined size, or a 'hell or high water' obligation to accept whatever is required) and often a reverse break fee if clearance fails. Without those, the seller bears much of the regulatory risk."},
    {t:"The buyer must divest, because reasonable endeavours means doing everything possible", correct:false,
     why:"That overstates the standard. Reasonable endeavours is the weakest of the usual formulations and generally does not require a party to act against its own commercial interests."},
    {t:"The seller can force completion without clearance", correct:false,
     why:"If clearance is a condition, the seller cannot force completion until it is satisfied or waived. And the buyer would not waive a condition it is relying on."},
    {t:"CCCS will order completion", correct:false,
     why:"CCCS assesses whether a merger lessens competition; it does not order parties to complete their contracts."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.11, 2.16; Chapter 8", takeaway:"Reasonable endeavours ≠ accept remedies; regulatory risk needs an express remedies covenant/reverse fee."
},

{ id:"p2x-A6-03", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Between signing and completion, a sector-wide downturn cuts the target's revenue by 25%. Its competitors suffer the same fall. The MAC definition excludes changes in general economic or industry conditions 'except to the extent they have a disproportionate effect on the Target compared with other participants in its industry'.",
  prompt:"Can the buyer refuse to complete on the MAC?",
  options:[
    {t:"Probably not: industry-wide downturns are carved out", correct:true,
     why:"Sellers routinely carve out general economic and industry conditions because those are risks the buyer would face anyway and can assess for itself. The 'disproportionate effect' exception brings back cases where the target suffers more than its peers. Here the competitors suffered the same 25% fall, so the target is not disproportionately affected and the carve-out applies. Even without the carve-out, a buyer must show the change is material and durable over the long term, and a cyclical dip often fails that test. A buyer worried about market conditions should negotiate objective conditions, such as a minimum EBITDA, rather than rely on a general MAC."},
    {t:"Yes: a 25% revenue fall is always a MAC", correct:false,
     why:"There is no fixed percentage. Whether a change is material depends on the drafting and its durability, and here the carve-out applies first."},
    {t:"Yes: MAC clauses protect buyers against all market risk", correct:false,
     why:"Most MAC clauses specifically exclude general market risk. The buyer assumed it when it signed."},
    {t:"No: MAC clauses are unenforceable", correct:false,
     why:"MAC clauses are enforceable, but they are interpreted strictly and are hard to invoke successfully."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.11", takeaway:"General market/industry carve-out applies unless the target is hit disproportionately."
},

{ id:"p2x-A6-04", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The target owns 50% of a joint venture. The JV agreement gives the other JV partner a call option to buy the target's 50% at fair value if the target undergoes a change of control. The buyer values the JV interest as the main reason for the deal.",
  prompt:"What should the buyer do?",
  options:[
    {t:"Get the JV partner's waiver, or make it a condition", correct:true,
     why:"A change-of-control call option in a JV agreement is one of the most dangerous provisions in strategic share deals. The buyer acquires the target's shares, which triggers the option, and the JV partner can then buy the JV stake the buyer was paying for. Fair-value pricing is no comfort if the buyer wanted the strategic asset, not cash. The buyer should approach the JV partner early (often with the seller), negotiate a waiver or amendment, and make it a condition if it cannot be obtained before signing. If the partner will not cooperate, the buyer may need to restructure the price, or include an adjustment if the option is exercised."},
    {t:"Nothing: JV agreements do not affect a share sale of a JV party's parent", correct:false,
     why:"This clause is triggered by a change of control of the target, which is exactly what the share sale does."},
    {t:"Rely on a warranty that the JV interest is owned by the target", correct:false,
     why:"The warranty will be true at completion. The problem is the option exercisable after completion, which a warranty does not address."},
    {t:"Ask the target to sell its JV stake before completion", correct:false,
     why:"That would remove the asset the buyer wants. The goal is to keep the JV stake by obtaining the partner's waiver."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 4 §C; Intro 2.6, 2.10", takeaway:"CoC call options in JV agreements can strip the key asset; get the waiver or make it a CP."
},

{ id:"p2x-A6-05", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A seller is worried that a strategic buyer's acquisition may be blocked by a foreign merger regulator. The buyer insists on a clearance condition. The seller has rejected another bidder that had no overlap.",
  prompt:"Which term best compensates the seller for taking this regulatory risk?",
  options:[
    {t:"A reverse break fee on regulatory failure", correct:true,
     why:"A seller that agrees a regulatory condition is taking a risk: the deal may fail months later, after the business has been held in limbo and the other bidder has gone. A reverse break fee shifts part of that cost back to the buyer, whose overlap creates the risk. It also incentivises the buyer to work hard for clearance. Sellers often combine it with a strong efforts covenant and a remedies commitment. In a public deal, a fee payable by the target is capped by the Code, but a fee payable by a buyer in a private deal is a matter of negotiation, subject to the penalty doctrine (the fee should protect a legitimate interest rather than punish)."},
    {t:"A warranty from the buyer that clearance will be obtained", correct:false,
     why:"No buyer can warrant a regulator's decision, and a damages claim would be hard to quantify."},
    {t:"A MAC clause in the seller's favour", correct:false,
     why:"A MAC protects against deterioration of the target, not against regulatory failure."},
    {t:"A completion-accounts mechanism", correct:false,
     why:"Completion accounts adjust price for the balance sheet. They do nothing for regulatory risk."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.11, 2.16", takeaway:"Regulatory risk from the buyer's overlap → reverse break fee + strong efforts covenant."
}

]);

;
