/* DealCraft — Chapter 2 · Lesson 2.8 Consortia, Concert Parties & Minorities (audited Oct 2026).
   Sources: Intro 2.4, 2.7–2.8; Wan Ch 2, Ch 7 §D, Ch 9 §D; Code (2026) Definitions (acting in concert, close relatives), r 10 n 6, r 14; CA ss 184, 216; IRDA s 125. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"fa-8-01", lesson:"A.8", type:"mcq", dia:"thresholdLadder",
  prompt:"Two private-equity funds team up to jointly acquire and control a target to which the Take-Over Code applies (a listed company, or an unlisted public company with more than 50 shareholders and NTA of S$5m+). For the Code's control thresholds, how are they treated?",
  options:[
    {t:"As concert parties, counted as one", correct:true,
     why:"The Code defines persons acting in concert as individuals or companies who, under an agreement or understanding (formal or informal), cooperate through the acquisition of shares to obtain or consolidate effective control. Two funds agreeing to buy and share control of the target fit that definition exactly. Their holdings are aggregated for Rule 14, so if together they cross 30% a mandatory offer is triggered, even if each holds only 15%. They will usually bid as joint offerors, and Rule 10 note 6 allows joint offerors to agree among themselves on the future membership, control and management of the target. Their relationship is governed by a consortium agreement."},
    {t:"As wholly independent investors measured separately", correct:false,
     why:"That is exactly what the concert-party rules prevent. Otherwise cooperating acquirers could split a stake to stay under the threshold."},
    {t:"As exempt from all thresholds because they are funds", correct:false,
     why:"Fund status gives no exemption. Fund managers are presumed to act in concert with the funds they manage on a discretionary basis, and cooperation between funds to obtain control is caught by the general definition."},
    {t:"As a single company by operation of law", correct:false,
     why:"They remain separate entities. Aggregation is a Code concept for thresholds and obligations, not a legal merger."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) Definitions (acting in concert); Wan Ch 9 §D; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Consortium = concert parties: holdings aggregated; governed by a consortium agreement."
},

{ id:"au-8-01", lesson:"A.8", type:"scenario", dia:"thresholdLadder",
  scenario:"Two PE funds form a 50:50 consortium to buy 100% of a PRIVATE company (not public, no public parent) from its founder.",
  prompt:"Which issues actually drive the legal work?",
  options:[
    {t:"Consortium SHA, SPA and merger control", correct:true,
     why:"Concert-party aggregation is a Code concept, and the Code does not reach a private target. The consortium's real issues are internal. Who appoints the board and chair? Which decisions need both funds? How is deadlock broken in a 50:50 structure? Who funds further capital, and what happens if one fund cannot? Are there transfer restrictions and a lock-up? How and when does each fund exit, and can one force the other to sell? Externally, the SPA is negotiated jointly, and merger control matters if either fund's portfolio companies overlap with the target. Section 76 is not engaged because no public company is in the chain."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer, because together they exceed 30%", correct:false,
     why:"The 30% trigger is a Code rule. A private company is outside the Code."},
    {t:"Nothing: consortia need no special documents", correct:false,
     why:"A 50:50 consortium without a deadlock mechanism and exit terms is a classic failure point."},
    {t:"A s 76 whitewash, because there are two buyers", correct:false,
     why:"The number of buyers is irrelevant to s 76. A private target with no public parent is outside it."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 §D; Intro 2.4, 2.7", takeaway:"Private-target consortium → SHA/deadlock/funding/exit + merger control, not Code concert rules."
},

{ id:"p2x-A8-01", amended:true, lesson:"A.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Under the 2026 Code, an individual holds 16% of an SGX-listed company. His long-term cohabiting partner (they are not married) buys a further 15% on market with her own savings. Neither has told the other about their purchases.",
  prompt:"What is the Code position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Presumed in concert: a cohabitant counts as a spouse", correct:true,
     why:"Class (h) of the concert-party presumptions covers an individual and his close relatives. The 2026 amendments expanded 'close relatives' (adding grandparents and grandchildren, the spouses of siblings and children, nephews and nieces, and in-laws) and added that a reference to a spouse includes a de facto spouse, cohabitant and civil partner. So the partner is presumed to act in concert with him, and their combined 31% crosses 30%. The presumption can be rebutted. Note 1 to the definition lists the factors the SIC considers for close relatives: the pattern, volume, timing and price of purchases; voting patterns; and whether the relative has independent financial means commensurate with the purchases. Using her own savings helps, but they should consult the SIC before relying on it."},
    {t:"No issue: they are not married, so they are not close relatives", correct:false,
     why:"That was arguable before 2026. The amended definition expressly treats a cohabitant as a spouse."},
    {t:"No issue: acting in concert requires a formal agreement", correct:false,
     why:"The presumption applies unless rebutted, and the general definition covers informal understandings too."},
    {t:"They must each make separate offers for the company", correct:false,
     why:"If they are concert parties, the obligation falls on the person whose acquisition crossed the threshold (and the principal members of the group), resulting in one offer, not two."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) Definitions 1 (acting in concert, class (h)), 5 (close relatives) & n 1; amendments digest p14", takeaway:"2026: 'spouse' includes cohabitant/civil partner → presumed concert parties; rebut with the n 1 factors."
},

{ id:"p2x-A8-02", lesson:"A.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A consortium is bidding for a listed company. An existing 25% shareholder will 'join' the consortium by rolling his shares into BidCo, contributing no cash, taking no board seat in BidCo, and receiving a put option to sell his BidCo shares to the PE sponsor after two years at the offer price plus 8% a year.",
  prompt:"What is the likely Code concern?",
  options:[
    {t:"He may get a special deal, not be a joint offeror", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 10 note 6 says that where two or more persons form a consortium on terms such that each can be considered a joint offeror, Rule 10 is not breached merely because one of them is already a shareholder, and joint offerors may agree on future control and management. The question is whether this shareholder is really a joint offeror. Indicators against: he contributes no cash, has no role in control of BidCo, and has a guaranteed exit at a premium. That looks less like a co-bidder taking equity risk and more like a shareholder being given a better deal than everyone else. The SIC would likely regard the put as a favourable arrangement, as it does with guaranteed floors for management under note 4. The parties should consult the SIC."},
    {t:"No concern: anyone who rolls into BidCo is a joint offeror", correct:false,
     why:"Joint-offeror status depends on substance: equity risk, contribution and role in control. A label does not decide it."},
    {t:"The only issue is stamp duty on the roll-over", correct:false,
     why:"Tax may matter, but the Code issue is equality of treatment."},
    {t:"He must be treated as management under Rule 10 note 4", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 concerns members of the offeree's management. A 25% shareholder who is not management is assessed under the general rule and note 6."}
  ],
  cite:"Code r 10 & nn 4, 6; Wan Ch 9 §D", takeaway:"Joint offeror = real equity risk and role in control; a guaranteed-exit 'joiner' is a special deal."
},

{ id:"au-8-02", lesson:"A.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Without any SHA, a 30% shareholder in a private company can block which of these by votes alone?",
  options:[
    {t:"A special resolution to amend the constitution", correct:true,
     why:"A special resolution requires at least 75% of the votes cast at the meeting (or, for a written resolution of a private company, 75% of the total voting rights). A holder with more than 25% of the votes that will be cast can therefore block it. Special resolutions are needed for the most fundamental changes: altering the constitution, reducing capital, changing the company's name, converting to public or private status, and a members' voluntary winding up. Ordinary resolutions, including appointing and removing directors, need only a simple majority, so a 30% holder cannot block them. Day-to-day and strategic business decisions are for the board, where a shareholder has no say unless it has board seats or reserved matters."},
    {t:"An ordinary resolution to appoint a director", correct:false,
     why:"Ordinary resolutions pass with more than 50% of the votes cast. A 30% holder is outvoted unless the constitution or an SHA gives it appointment rights."},
    {t:"A board decision to enter a major contract", correct:false,
     why:"Board decisions are for the directors. Without a board seat or a reserved-matters clause, a shareholder has no vote on them."},
    {t:"The majority's sale of its own shares", correct:false,
     why:"Without pre-emption or transfer restrictions in the constitution or an SHA, each shareholder can sell its own shares freely."}
  ],
  cite:"CA s 184 (special resolution 75%); Intro 2.7", takeaway:">25% blocks special resolutions only; everything else needs an SHA."
},

{ id:"fa-8-04", lesson:"A.8", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A 15% shareholder in a private company where all members vote has negative control because it can block a special resolution.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Negative control means being able to block special resolutions, which need 75% of the votes cast. With all members voting, a holder needs more than 25% to block one. A 15% holder falls well short. Its protection must come from contract: reserved matters, board representation, pre-emption and anti-dilution, tag-along and put options. Where turnout is low the arithmetic can change (15% of all shares could be more than 25% of the votes actually cast), but a minority cannot plan around other shareholders staying away."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Blocking a special resolution needs more than 25% of the votes cast. With full turnout, 15% cannot block one."}
  ],
  cite:"CA s 184; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Negative control needs >25% of votes cast; 15% blocks nothing at full turnout."
},

{ id:"p2x-A8-03", lesson:"A.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A private company's majority holder (72%) proposes a special resolution to amend the constitution. A 20% holder opposes. The remaining 8% holders do not attend or vote.",
  prompt:"Does the resolution pass?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the 20% opposing holder and the absent 8% together hold more than 25%", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders who do not vote are not counted at a meeting. Only votes actually cast are in the denominator, so the absent 8% cannot help the opposing holder block the resolution."},
    {t:"Yes: 72 of the 92 votes cast is about 78%, above the 75% needed", correct:true,
     why:"A special resolution at a meeting needs at least 75% of the votes cast by those entitled to vote and voting. Votes cast are 72 + 20 = 92, of which 72 are in favour: 78.3%. The resolution passes, even though the 20% holder opposed it, because the abstaining 8% are excluded from the denominator. Had all 28% of the minority voted against, the result would be 72 ÷ 100 = 72%, below 75%, and it would fail. A minority relying on blocking rights needs either more than 25% itself or an SHA reserved-matter veto that does not depend on turnout. A written special resolution of a private company is different: it needs 75% of the total voting rights of all members, so absent members count against it."},
    {t:"No: 72% is below 75% of the total shares", correct:false,
     why:"At a meeting, the 75% is measured against votes cast, not against all shares in issue. Non-voting holders are left out of the calculation."},
    {t:"No: the 20% holder has a veto as the largest minority", correct:false,
     why:"No shareholder has a statutory veto by being the largest minority. Blocking power depends on the arithmetic of votes cast, or on contract."}
  ],
  cite:"CA ss 184, 184A; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Special resolution = 75% of votes cast at a meeting (abstainers excluded); written = 75% of total."
},

{ id:"fa-8-02", lesson:"A.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A fund takes a 20% NON-controlling stake in a private company. What is its most important source of protection?",
  options:[
    {t:"An SHA with reserved matters and exit rights", correct:true,
     why:"A 20% holder cannot block special resolutions and is outvoted on ordinary ones. Its statutory fallback (an oppression claim under s 216) is slow, uncertain and remedial rather than preventive. So it protects itself in advance through an SHA, ideally with key terms mirrored in the constitution: reserved matters needing its consent (new issues, major acquisitions and disposals, borrowing, related-party deals, changes to the business), a right to appoint a director, pre-emption and anti-dilution on new issues, tag-along if the majority sells, information rights (monthly accounts, budgets), and an exit route such as a put option or IPO or sale rights after a period."},
    {t:"The Take-Over Code's mandatory-offer rule", correct:false,
     why:"The Code does not apply to a private company. Even in a Code company, the mandatory-offer rule protects minorities only when someone takes control."},
    {t:"A guarantee that the company will be sold within a year", correct:false,
     why:"No such right exists by default. Exit rights have to be negotiated."},
    {t:"Automatic board control", correct:false,
     why:"A 20% holder has no board control. Its influence comes from negotiated rights."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 7 §D; Intro 2.8", takeaway:"Minority stake → protect via SHA: reserved matters, board seat, tag, anti-dilution, info, exit."
},

{ id:"rA-8-01", lesson:"A.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A fund takes a 20% stake in a private company. Which SHA protection most directly guards against being outvoted on fundamental changes?",
  options:[
    {t:"Reserved matters", correct:true,
     why:"Reserved matters give a minority a contractual veto over defined decisions, regardless of the size of its shareholding. Typical lists cover changes to the constitution and share capital, new issues, major acquisitions and disposals, borrowing above a limit, related-party transactions, changes to the nature of the business, the annual budget, and appointing or removing senior executives. Lists are often split between shareholder-level and board-level matters. Drafting matters: the veto should be framed as an undertaking by the shareholders (and the company, where enforceable) not to proceed without consent, and the list should be specific enough to apply without argument."},
    {t:"A drag-along right over the majority", correct:false,
     why:"Drag-along lets a majority force the minority to sell. It protects the majority's exit, not the minority's say."},
    {t:"A responsibility statement", correct:false,
     why:"A responsibility statement is a Code document concept, unrelated to private-company governance."},
    {t:"A break fee", correct:false,
     why:"A break fee compensates for a failed deal. It is not a governance right."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Reserved matters = a minority veto over fundamental decisions."
},

{ id:"p2x-A8-04", lesson:"A.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An SHA, to which the company is also a party, says the company 'shall not amend its constitution without the Investor's consent'. The majority later passes a valid special resolution amending the constitution without that consent.",
  prompt:"What is the investor's strongest position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Enforce the shareholders' voting undertakings", correct:true,
     why:"In Russell v Northern Bank Development Corp, the House of Lords held that a company cannot validly contract out of its statutory power to alter its constitution by special resolution, so an undertaking by the company itself was unenforceable against it. But an agreement among the shareholders about how they will exercise their votes is enforceable between them, and the court can restrain a party from voting in breach. The practical lesson is to draft reserved matters as undertakings by the shareholders (to vote, or procure votes, only in accordance with the SHA), with the company's undertaking severable. Entrenching key rights in the constitution itself, where the Companies Act permits entrenchment, gives further protection."},
    {t:"The amendment is automatically void because the SHA forbids it", correct:false,
     why:"An SHA cannot invalidate a special resolution passed in accordance with the Companies Act. The investor's remedy is against the shareholders who broke their contract."},
    {t:"Nothing: SHAs are unenforceable in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"SHAs are ordinary contracts and are enforced between the parties. The limitation is narrower: a company cannot fetter its statutory powers."},
    {t:"Ask the SIC to set aside the resolution", correct:false,
     why:"The SIC administers the Take-Over Code. It has no role in a private company's constitutional amendments."}
  ],
  cite:"Russell v Northern Bank [1992] 1 WLR 588; Intro 2.7–2.8", takeaway:"Draft reserved matters as shareholder voting undertakings; company can't fetter statutory powers."
},

{ id:"au-8-04", lesson:"A.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A 20% investor in a private company learns the board plans to issue new shares to a strategic partner at a steep discount, which would dilute it to 12%.",
  prompt:"Which pre-agreed protections would let it stop or neutralise this?",
  options:[
    {t:"A veto plus pre-emption rights", correct:true,
     why:"Three tools work together. A reserved-matters veto lets the investor refuse consent to the issue altogether. Pre-emption rights require new shares to be offered first to existing holders in proportion to their holdings, so the investor can keep its 20% by subscribing (if it has the money). Anti-dilution protection adjusts the investor's economics if shares are issued below the price it paid, usually by issuing it additional shares or adjusting a conversion ratio. Separately, directors must issue shares for a proper purpose; an issue at a steep discount designed to dilute a minority could be challenged as an improper exercise of power or as oppression under s 216."},
    {t:"A tag-along right on any sale", correct:false,
     why:"Tag-along operates when a majority sells existing shares. It does nothing on a new issue."},
    {t:"A drag-along right on any sale", correct:false,
     why:"Drag-along lets a majority force a sale. It protects the majority, not a diluted minority."},
    {t:"A mandatory general offer", correct:false,
     why:"A Code concept that does not apply to a private company."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Dilution defence: reserved-matter veto + pre-emption + anti-dilution (and proper-purpose/s 216)."
},

{ id:"rA-8-03", lesson:"A.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"An SHA gives existing shareholders PRE-EMPTION rights. What do they do?",
  options:[
    {t:"First right to take up new or sold shares", correct:true,
     why:"There are two kinds. Pre-emption on issue requires new shares to be offered first to existing holders pro rata, so each can keep its percentage. Pre-emption on transfer (a right of first refusal or first offer) requires a holder who wants to sell to offer its shares to the other holders first, which lets the remaining holders control who joins the register. For a private company these are often in the constitution as well as the SHA, so that they bind the company and all members. A buyer of shares in a private company must check for them and obtain waivers, as Lesson 2.1 shows."},
    {t:"They force the company to pay dividends", correct:false,
     why:"Dividends are a separate matter. Pre-emption concerns first rights over new or transferred shares."},
    {t:"They let a holder appoint the auditor", correct:false,
     why:"Auditor appointment is unrelated."},
    {t:"They trigger a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"Pre-emption is a private-company contractual right, not a Code trigger."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Pre-emption = first rights on new and transferred shares."
},

{ id:"rA-8-02", lesson:"A.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the difference between a TAG-ALONG and a DRAG-ALONG right?",
  options:[
    {t:"Tag lets you join a sale; drag forces one", correct:true,
     why:"They are mirror images. A tag-along protects a minority from being left behind with a new controller it did not choose: if the majority sells, the minority can require the buyer to buy its shares on the same terms. A drag-along protects the majority's ability to deliver 100% to a buyer: if holders of a specified percentage accept a bona fide offer, they can compel the rest to sell on the same terms. Most SHAs have both. Minorities negotiate the drag-along threshold, a minimum price, and limits on what warranties a dragged holder must give."},
    {t:"They are identical rights", correct:false,
     why:"They point in opposite directions: one lets the minority join, the other lets the majority compel."},
    {t:"Tag-along forces a sale; drag-along blocks one", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses them. Neither blocks a sale."},
    {t:"Both apply only to public companies", correct:false,
     why:"Both are typical private-company SHA rights. In a Code company, exits run through the Code's offer rules instead."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Tag = minority joins; drag = majority compels."
},

{ id:"fa-8-03", lesson:"A.8", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each shareholders'-agreement right to what it achieves.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Drag-along", r:"A majority can force minorities to sell into a deal"},
    {l:"Tag-along", r:"Minorities can join a majority's sale on the same terms"},
    {l:"Reserved matters", r:"Key decisions need the investor's consent"},
    {l:"Anti-dilution", r:"Preserves an investor's economics if shares are issued below its price"}
  ],
  why:"An SHA allocates control and exit among co-owners. Drag-along lets a selling majority force minorities to sell, so a buyer can get 100%. Tag-along lets minorities join a majority's sale on equal terms, so they are not left behind with a new controller. Reserved matters give an investor a veto over defined decisions regardless of its voting power. Anti-dilution protects an investor's economics if new shares are issued at a lower price, usually through extra shares or a conversion adjustment.",
  cite:"Intro 2.8, glossary", takeaway:"Drag (force sale) · tag (join sale) · reserved matters (veto) · anti-dilution (protect economics)."
},

{ id:"d2-008", lesson:"A.8", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each minority-investor right to the problem it solves.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Information rights", r:"Not knowing how the company is performing"},
    {l:"Board seat", r:"No voice in board decisions"},
    {l:"Put option", r:"No market to sell into"},
    {l:"Right of first refusal", r:"Unwanted new co-owners"}
  ],
  why:"Each right addresses a weakness of a minority in a private company. Without information rights, a minority sees only the statutory accounts once a year; it negotiates monthly management accounts, budgets and access. Without a board seat, it has no voice where most decisions are made. Because private shares have no market, a put option (on a date, on deadlock, or on the majority's breach) gives it an exit at an agreed price or formula. A right of first refusal over other holders' shares lets it decide whether to buy before an outsider joins the register.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 7 §D; Intro 2.8", takeaway:"Minority kit: information · board seat · put option · ROFR."
},

{ id:"rA-8-04", lesson:"A.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A minority investor negotiates a board seat and detailed information rights. What is the main purpose?",
  options:[
    {t:"To monitor and influence", correct:true,
     why:"A minority cannot control outcomes by votes, so it secures visibility and a voice. The board seat lets it hear proposals early and argue against them. Information rights give it the data to judge performance and spot problems. Both work alongside reserved matters. One caution: a nominee director owes duties to the company, not to the investor that appointed him, so he must act in the company's interests and handle confidential board information carefully. The SHA usually allows him to share information with the appointing investor, which needs to be squared with that duty."},
    {t:"To take over day-to-day management", correct:false,
     why:"A board seat gives oversight and influence, not operational control."},
    {t:"To avoid stamp duty on its shares", correct:false,
     why:"Governance rights have nothing to do with duty."},
    {t:"To force an immediate sale", correct:false,
     why:"Exit is handled by tag, drag and put/call terms. Information rights are for monitoring."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Board seat + info rights = oversight; the nominee director's duty runs to the company."
},

{ id:"rA-8-05", lesson:"A.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"An SHA contains PUT and CALL options over the minority's shares. What is their typical role?",
  options:[
    {t:"Agreed exit and entry routes", correct:true,
     why:"A private company has no market for its shares, so the SHA creates one. A put lets the minority require the majority (or the company, within capital-maintenance limits) to buy its shares on a trigger: a date, a deadlock, a change of control, or the majority's material breach. A call lets the majority require the minority to sell, often on the minority's default or a manager's departure. The price may be fixed, formula-based or fair value set by an expert. Triggers and pricing need care: a call at a discount on 'default' is sometimes challenged as a penalty if it is not linked to a legitimate interest."},
    {t:"To set the company's dividend policy", correct:false,
     why:"Dividends are separate from put and call options."},
    {t:"To appoint the auditors", correct:false,
     why:"Unrelated. Put and call options are exit mechanisms."},
    {t:"To register the company on SGX", correct:false,
     why:"They are private liquidity mechanisms, not a listing route."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Put/call = engineered exit routes in a private company."
},

{ id:"au-8-03", lesson:"A.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A 50:50 JV between two groups keeps deadlocking on the annual budget. The SHA has a 'Russian roulette' clause.",
  prompt:"How does it break the deadlock?",
  options:[
    {t:"A shotgun: name a price to buy or sell at", correct:true,
     why:"A Russian roulette (or shotgun) clause ends the relationship rather than resolving the disputed issue. After a deadlock and usually a cooling-off and escalation process, one party serves a notice naming a price. The recipient chooses whether to buy the server's shares or sell its own at that price. Because the server does not know which way the recipient will choose, it is pushed to name a fair price. The weakness is financial asymmetry: a party that cannot raise the money to buy may be forced to sell cheaply. Alternatives include a Texas shoot-out (sealed bids), a casting vote, expert determination of the specific issue, or a put/call at fair value."},
    {t:"The chairman gets a casting vote", correct:false,
     why:"A casting vote is a different deadlock tool, and in effect gives one party control."},
    {t:"The company is wound up automatically", correct:false,
     why:"Winding up is a last resort, sometimes reached through the court on the just and equitable ground. It is not how a Russian roulette works."},
    {t:"An independent expert sets the budget", correct:false,
     why:"Expert determination resolves the disputed issue and keeps the JV alive. Russian roulette ends the relationship through a buy or sell."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.7 [practice]", takeaway:"Russian roulette: name a price; the other chooses buy or sell."
},

{ id:"p2x-A8-05", lesson:"A.8", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Three friends set up a private company 10 years ago on the understanding that all three would be directors and share in management. Two of them, holding 70%, remove the third (30%) as director, stop paying her any return, and refuse to buy her shares. There is no SHA.",
  prompt:"What is her most promising remedy?",
  options:[
    {t:"A s 216 oppression claim seeking a buy-out", correct:true,
     why:"Section 216 of the Companies Act allows a member to apply to court where the company's affairs are conducted, or directors' powers exercised, in a manner oppressive to, or in disregard of, her interests as a member. In a company formed on the basis of mutual trust and participation in management (a 'quasi-partnership'), excluding a founder from management without offering her a fair exit is a classic ground. The court has wide remedial powers, and the most common order is that the majority buy the minority's shares at a fair value, often without a minority discount in a quasi-partnership. A just and equitable winding up under the IRDA is a more drastic alternative. An SHA with exit rights would have avoided the litigation."},
    {t:"Block the removal, because she holds more than 25%", correct:false,
     why:"Directors can be removed by ordinary resolution. A 30% holder can block special resolutions, not her own removal."},
    {t:"A derivative action under s 216A for her personal loss", correct:false,
     why:"A derivative action is brought on behalf of the company for wrongs done to the company. Her complaint is about her own exclusion as a member, which is the territory of s 216."},
    {t:"Nothing: majority rule always prevails", correct:false,
     why:"Majority rule is the starting point, but s 216 exists to protect minorities from oppressive or unfairly prejudicial conduct."}
  ],
  cite:"CA ss 216, 216A; IRDA s 125; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Excluded quasi-partner without SHA → s 216 oppression, usually a buy-out at fair value."
}

]);

