/* DealCraft — Chapter 3 · Lesson 3.5 Dealings, Disclosure & Restrictions (audited Oct 2026).
   Sources: Code (2026) Definitions (associate (a)–(j)); rr 11.1–11.3 & notes; r 12 & nn 1–11; Wan Ch 7 [7.75]–[7.85], Ch 16; SFA (substantial shareholdings, insider trading). */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"fb-5-02", lesson:"B.5", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"During an offer period, what must the offeror, the offeree and their associates do about dealings in relevant securities for their own accounts?",
  options:[
    {t:"Disclose publicly by noon the next dealing day", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 12 starts from the premise that it is undesirable to fetter the market, so parties to an offer (other than a partial offer) and their associates are free to deal, but subject to disclosure. Rule 12.1 requires dealings for their own accounts or for discretionary clients to be disclosed publicly. Note 4 sets the deadline at 12 noon on the dealing day after the transaction; note 5 says disclosure goes to the Securities Exchange, the SIC and the press. Note 6 lists the contents: the number of securities, the prices, full details of any options or derivatives, the identity of the person dealing and the ultimate beneficial owner, and the resulting total holding and percentage. Dealings for non-discretionary clients are disclosed privately to the SIC under Rule 12.2, and pure brokerage dealings need not be disclosed (Rule 12.3)."},
    {t:"Keep all dealings strictly confidential until the offer closes", correct:false,
     why:"The opposite is required. Secret accumulation by the parties would distort the market and could set the offer price without shareholders knowing."},
    {t:"Stop dealing entirely for the whole offer period", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 12 says parties and associates are free to deal, subject to disclosure. Specific restrictions do apply, such as Rule 11.2 on offeror sales and Rule 11.3 on dealing in offeror shares in a securities offer, but there is no blanket ban."},
    {t:"Only disclose dealings after completion", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure must be made by noon the next dealing day, so shareholders can react while the offer is live."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 12.1–12.3, nn 4–6; Wan Ch 7 [7.76]–[7.85]", takeaway:"Offer-period dealings: disclose publicly by 12 noon next dealing day (SGX, SIC, press)."
},

{ id:"rB-5-01", lesson:"B.5", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why must the offeror, offeree and their associates DISCLOSE their dealings during an offer period?",
  options:[
    {t:"To keep the market fair and informed", correct:true,
     why:"Dealings during an offer have consequences for everyone. A bidder's purchases above the offer price raise the price it must offer all shareholders (Rule 21.1). Purchases by an offeree's associates may support the price or signal views on the offer. Concert parties' purchases count toward the offeror's thresholds. If these dealings were hidden, shareholders deciding whether to accept would be acting on a false picture, contrary to GP 12 (no false market) and GP 10 (sufficient information). Disclosure lets the market and the SIC see what is happening and lets the SIC enforce the pricing rules."},
    {t:"To calculate stamp duty", correct:false,
     why:"Dealing disclosure has nothing to do with stamp duty. It is about market integrity and price-setting."},
    {t:"Because the CCCS requires it", correct:false,
     why:"This is a Code requirement policed by the SIC. CCCS deals with competition law."},
    {t:"It is not required; dealings are private", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 12 requires disclosure by the parties and their associates during the offer period."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 12, GP 10, GP 12; Wan Ch 7", takeaway:"Disclose dealings in an offer period → fair, informed market; enforces pricing rules."
},

{ id:"ab-5-01", amended:true, lesson:"B.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During an offer period, the proprietary trading desk of the investment bank acting as financial adviser to the bidder buys target shares on Tuesday.",
  prompt:"What does r 12 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Disclose publicly by noon on Wednesday", correct:true,
     why:"Class (c) of the associate definition covers stockbrokers, financial and other professional advisers to the offeror, the offeree, or anyone appointed in connection with the transaction, together with persons controlling, controlled by or under the same control as them. The bank's group therefore includes its proprietary desk. Its own-account dealings fall under Rule 12.1 and must be publicly disclosed by noon the next dealing day. Note that the 2026 amendments deleted 'banks' as a separate category from class (c), so a bank that is merely a lender (and not an adviser) is no longer automatically an associate on that ground; but a bank acting as financial adviser plainly still is. Large global groups can sometimes get an exemption for discretionary funds behind effective Chinese walls (note 11)."},
    {t:"Nothing: only the bidder itself discloses", correct:false,
     why:"The associate category exists because advisers' and group companies' dealings can affect the offer."},
    {t:"Disclosure only to the target company under the SFA", correct:false,
     why:"Code disclosure is public and goes to SGX, the SIC and the press. SFA disclosure duties may apply separately."},
    {t:"Disclosure within 30 days", correct:false,
     why:"The deadline is 12 noon on the next dealing day."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) Definition 2(c) (associate), r 12.1, nn 4, 11; Wan Ch 7 [7.77]–[7.84]", takeaway:"Advisers (and their groups) are associates: disclose dealings by 12 noon next day. 2026: 'banks' as such removed."
},

{ id:"p2x-B5-01", lesson:"B.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A pension fund that has no connection with either party holds 6% of an offeree company. During the offer period it buys another 0.5%.",
  prompt:"Does it have to disclose under the Code?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: a 5% holder is an associate", correct:true,
     why:"Class (f) of the associate definition includes a holder of 5% or more of the equity share capital of the offeror or offeree, including a holder who acquires shares that take it through 5%. Persons acting as a syndicate to acquire or hold shares are treated as a single holder. The rationale is that a large holder's dealings can influence the outcome and the market's view of the offer. The fund is therefore an associate even though it is unconnected to the parties, and its 0.5% purchase must be disclosed by noon the next dealing day. Its SFA substantial-shareholding notification obligations apply separately."},
    {t:"No: it is not connected to either party", correct:false,
     why:"Class (f) does not require any connection. Size alone (5% or more) makes it an associate."},
    {t:"Only under the SFA, not the Code", correct:false,
     why:"Both apply. The Code disclosure is faster and public; the SFA notice has its own timing and recipients."},
    {t:"Only if it buys more than 1%", correct:false,
     why:"There is no minimum size of dealing for Rule 12 disclosure by an associate."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) Definition 2(f) (associate), r 12.1", takeaway:"≥5% holders of offeror/offeree are associates → disclose every offer-period dealing."
},

{ id:"rB-5-04", lesson:"B.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bidder's London adviser prepares an 'opening position disclosure' for a Singapore target, as it would in a UK bid, and plans nothing further.",
  prompt:"What does the Singapore Code actually require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Rule 12 dealing disclosure by next-day noon", correct:true,
     why:"Opening position disclosures, where persons with 1% or more disclose their positions at the start of an offer period, are a UK City Code requirement. Wan [7.75]–[7.85] notes the Singapore Code has no equivalent. Singapore instead relies on dealing-by-dealing disclosure under Rule 12 by the parties and their associates (including 5% holders and advisers), together with SFA substantial-shareholding notices. The offer document itself must also disclose the offeror's and concert parties' holdings and their dealings in the preceding period under Rule 23.3. The London adviser's opening disclosure does no harm, but on its own it does not satisfy Rule 12."},
    {t:"An opening position disclosure, exactly as in the UK", correct:false,
     why:"That is the UK rule. Singapore has no opening position disclosure requirement."},
    {t:"Nothing: only SFA substantial-shareholder notices apply", correct:false,
     why:"SFA notices apply, but the Code adds its own offer-period dealing disclosures, which are faster and go to SGX, the SIC and the press."},
    {t:"A single disclosure when the offer closes", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure is dealing by dealing during the offer period, not a closing summary."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 7 [7.75]–[7.85]; Code (2026) rr 12, 23.3", takeaway:"SG = r 12 dealing disclosure; opening position disclosure is UK-only."
},

{ id:"p2x-B5-02", lesson:"B.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During an offer period, a fund manager connected with the offeree company (and therefore an associate) buys offeree shares for a non-discretionary client, on that client's specific instructions.",
  prompt:"How is the dealing disclosed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Privately to the SIC under Rule 12.2", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 12 distinguishes three situations. Dealings by a party or associate for its own account or for discretionary clients are disclosed publicly under Rule 12.1, because the associate controls the decision. Dealings for non-discretionary clients (who make their own investment decisions) are disclosed privately to the SIC under Rule 12.2, giving the identity of the associate, the number of securities and the prices; the SIC reserves the right to publish them. Dealings purely as brokerage agent and not as principal need not be disclosed (Rule 12.3). Where a group has several investment management operations, their holdings are normally aggregated as one person."},
    {t:"Publicly by 12 noon the next day, like any associate dealing", correct:false,
     why:"Public disclosure applies to own-account and discretionary dealings. Non-discretionary client dealings go to the SIC privately."},
    {t:"Not at all, because the client made the decision", correct:false,
     why:"Private disclosure is still required. Only pure brokerage dealings are exempt."},
    {t:"Only to the offeree company", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 12.2 private disclosure is made in writing to the SIC."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) rr 12.1–12.3 & notes 5–6", takeaway:"Own/discretionary → public; non-discretionary → private to SIC; pure broking → none."
},

{ id:"p2x-B5-03", lesson:"B.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During an offer period, an associate of the offeror holding 3% of the offeree's shares enters a cash-settled equity swap giving it long exposure to a further 1% of offeree shares.",
  prompt:"Must the swap be disclosed under Rule 12?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: only 5%+ holders with long exposure", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 treats options and derivatives over relevant securities as relevant securities, and entering into, closing out or varying a derivative is a dealing. But note 8 limits disclosure of dealings in convertibles, warrants, options and derivatives to persons who own or control 5% or more of the class that is the subject of the instruments, and to instruments that give long economic exposure. The associate holds 3%, so the swap need not be disclosed under Rule 12. Any dealing in the shares themselves would still need to be disclosed. Separately, if the swap is part of an arrangement with the offeror that might induce the associate to deal or refrain from dealing, note 7 requires the arrangement to be disclosed whether or not any dealing takes place, and Rule 14 note 16 may count the exposure towards the offeror's thresholds."},
    {t:"Yes: all derivative dealings by associates must be disclosed", correct:false,
     why:"Note 8 restricts derivative disclosure to holders of 5% or more and to long exposure."},
    {t:"Yes, because swaps carry voting rights", correct:false,
     why:"Cash-settled swaps carry no votes. They matter because of economic exposure."},
    {t:"No, because derivatives are never relevant securities", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3 expressly includes options and derivatives. The exemption here comes from the 5% threshold in note 8."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 12 nn 3, 7, 8; r 14.1 n 16", takeaway:"Derivative dealings disclosed only if ≥5% of the class and long exposure (but arrangements always disclosed)."
},

{ id:"p2x-B5-04", lesson:"B.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Before any announcement, the target's general counsel learns her company is in talks with a bidder. Her husband, who has not been told anything, buys target shares in his own name.",
  prompt:"What is the position under Rule 11.1?",
  options:[
    {t:"He is treated as an insider; dealing is prohibited", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 11.1 prohibits any person (other than the offeror) who has confidential price-sensitive information about an actual or contemplated offer from dealing in offeree securities between the time there is reason to suppose an offer is contemplated and the announcement. The SIC regards a director or employee of a company concerned, who actually received the information, as having such information. The Rule then says that close relatives, related trusts and companies controlled by such a person are deemed to be in the same position. So for Code purposes the husband's dealing is caught. Under the SFA, liability for insider trading depends on whether he possessed inside information, so the facts about what he knew will matter there. In practice, the general counsel should ensure family members do not deal."},
    {t:"No problem: he received no information himself", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 11.1 expressly deems close relatives to be in the same position as the insider for the Code's dealing restriction."},
    {t:"No problem: Rule 11.1 only restricts the offeror", correct:false,
     why:"The reverse. Rule 11.1 restricts persons other than the offeror who hold price-sensitive information."},
    {t:"He may deal if he discloses by 12 noon the next day", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 12 disclosure applies to permitted dealings during an offer period. Dealing in breach of Rule 11.1 is prohibited outright."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 11.1; SFA Pt 12", takeaway:"r 11.1: insiders (and their close relatives) must not deal pre-announcement."
},

{ id:"fb-5-03", lesson:"B.5", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A person who learns of an impending offer in confidence may freely trade the target's shares before it is announced.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Two regimes prohibit it. Under the Code, Rule 11.1 bars any person (other than the offeror) who has confidential price-sensitive information about an actual or contemplated offer from dealing in offeree securities until the approach, offer or termination of discussions is announced. Under the SFA, information about an unannounced offer is classic inside information, and dealing on it, or tipping others, is an offence and a civil-penalty contravention. After discussions end following an announcement that talks were taking place, persons privy to that information still may not deal until the position is announced (note 1 on Rules 11.1–11.2)."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"It is false. Dealing on confidential knowledge of an impending offer breaches Rule 11.1 and is insider trading under the SFA."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 11.1 & n 1; Wan Ch 16, Ch 7", takeaway:"Trading on confidential offer information breaches r 11.1 and is insider dealing."
},

{ id:"rB-5-03", lesson:"B.5", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A person learns of an impending, not-yet-announced takeover offer and buys the target's shares. What is the main legal exposure?",
  options:[
    {t:"Insider trading under the SFA", correct:true,
     why:"Knowledge of a pending offer is price-sensitive and not generally available, so it is inside information. Under Part 12 of the SFA, a person who possesses inside information and knows (or ought reasonably to know) that it is not generally available and is price-sensitive must not deal in the securities or tip others. Contravention can lead to criminal prosecution or a civil penalty action by MAS, and to civil claims by those who dealt at the same time. The Code adds Rule 11.1, enforced by the SIC through its own sanctions. The SIC refers suspected crimes to the appropriate authority."},
    {t:"Nothing: anyone may trade on an impending offer", correct:false,
     why:"Trading on material non-public information is insider dealing, a serious SFA offence."},
    {t:"Only a Code cold-shouldering", correct:false,
     why:"Cold-shouldering is a possible Code sanction, but insider trading is also a separate criminal and civil-penalty matter under the SFA."},
    {t:"A stamp-duty surcharge", correct:false,
     why:"The exposure is criminal and civil liability, not a tax consequence."}
  ],
  cite:"SFA Pt 12; Code (2026) r 11.1; Wan Ch 16", takeaway:"Trading on an unannounced offer = insider dealing (SFA) + r 11.1 breach."
},

{ id:"rB-5-05", lesson:"B.5", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Before sounding out a big shareholder about a possible bid, advisers 'wall-cross' them. What does that mean?",
  options:[
    {t:"Making them insiders under confidentiality", correct:true,
     why:"A bidder often wants to know before announcing whether major holders will give irrevocable undertakings. To ask, it must tell them about the proposed offer, which is inside information. Wall-crossing is the controlled process for doing this: the adviser gets the shareholder's agreement in advance to receive confidential information, keep it confidential and not deal until it is announced or ceases to be inside information. Once wall-crossed, the shareholder is caught by Rule 11.1 and the SFA insider-trading rules. Rule 2 also limits approaches before announcement to a small number of people, so wall-crossing must be kept tight, and the number of people approached is relevant to when an announcement becomes necessary."},
    {t:"They publicly announce the deal", correct:false,
     why:"Wall-crossing is the opposite of announcement: it is a confidential, controlled briefing of a specific person."},
    {t:"They pay the shareholder a fee to sell", correct:false,
     why:"Paying a fee to a selected shareholder would raise special-deal issues under Rule 10. Wall-crossing is about sharing information under restrictions."},
    {t:"They waive the Code", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing is waived. Wall-crossing operates within the Code and the insider-trading rules."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) rr 2, 11.1; SFA Pt 12; Wan Ch 4", takeaway:"Wall-crossing = confidential inside briefing + no-dealing undertaking."
},

{ id:"p2x-B5-05", lesson:"B.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During its cash offer, before going unconditional as to acceptances, the bidder wants to sell 2% of the target shares it bought earlier, at a price above the offer price, to raise funds.",
  prompt:"What does Rule 11.2 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"SIC consent, 24 hours' notice, offer-price floor", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 11.2 restricts sales by the offeror and its concert parties during the offer period because sales can undermine the offer and mislead the market about the bidder's intentions. Before the offer becomes unconditional as to acceptances, a sale requires (i) the SIC's prior consent, (ii) 24 hours' advance public announcement, and (iii) a price not below the offer price. Once the intention to sell is announced, the offeror and concert parties cannot make further purchases, and only in exceptional circumstances will the SIC allow the offer price to be revised. For a mandatory offer, the SIC will not consent unless circumstances warrant the sale. After the offer becomes unconditional, sales need disclosure of the intention in the offer document and 24 hours' notice."},
    {t:"Nothing: bidders may sell freely if they disclose by noon the next day", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure under Rule 12 is needed, but Rule 11.2 imposes additional restrictions on offeror sales."},
    {t:"Sales are prohibited in all circumstances", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 11.2 permits sales on conditions. It is not a total ban."},
    {t:"Only target board consent is needed", correct:false,
     why:"The SIC's consent is required, not the target board's."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 11.2", takeaway:"Offeror sales mid-offer: SIC consent + 24h notice + ≥ offer price; then no more buying."
},

{ id:"p2x-B5-06", lesson:"B.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A listed bidder offers its own shares as consideration for a target. During the offer period, its board wants to run a share buy-back to support its share price.",
  prompt:"What does the Code say?",
  options:[
    {t:"It cannot deal in its own securities", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 11.3 says that where the consideration includes securities of the offeror (or any other company), neither the offeror nor its concert parties may deal in any such securities during the offer period, whether through share repurchases or otherwise. The value of a securities offer depends on the price of the consideration shares. If the bidder could buy its own shares during the offer, it could prop up that price and make the offer look more valuable than it is, which would create a false market. In a securities exchange offer, dealings by parties and associates in relevant offeror securities must also be disclosed under Rule 12 (note 2)."},
    {t:"It can, provided it discloses under Rule 12", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 11.3 prohibits the dealing outright. Disclosure does not cure it."},
    {t:"It can, with shareholder approval of the buy-back mandate", correct:false,
     why:"A buy-back mandate under the Companies Act does not override Rule 11.3."},
    {t:"Rule 11.3 applies only to cash offers", correct:false,
     why:"It applies to offers whose consideration includes securities. Cash offers do not raise the same concern."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 11.3, r 12 n 2", takeaway:"Securities offer → offeror can't deal (incl. buy-backs) in its consideration shares."
},

{ id:"p2x-B5-07", lesson:"B.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A target director who owns shares has signed the board circular recommending shareholders REJECT an offer. A week later he wants to accept the offer for his own shares.",
  prompt:"What must he do first?",
  options:[
    {t:"Give 24 hours' public notice first", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2 on Rules 11.1 and 11.2 says directors and financial advisers who own securities must not deal contrary to any advice they have given shareholders, or advice with which they can reasonably be assumed to be associated, without giving at least 24 hours' advance notice of their intentions with an appropriate explanation. Shareholders who relied on the board's 'reject' recommendation are entitled to know if a director is now doing the opposite. The director will also need to disclose the dealing under Rule 12 as an associate, and the board will need to consider whether its recommendation remains appropriate."},
    {t:"Nothing: directors may deal freely in their own shares", correct:false,
     why:"Directors are subject to the note on dealing contrary to published advice, as well as Rule 12 disclosure and insider-trading law."},
    {t:"He is prohibited from accepting until the offer closes", correct:false,
     why:"He may accept, but only after 24 hours' advance notice and explanation."},
    {t:"He needs the bidder's consent", correct:false,
     why:"The bidder's consent is irrelevant. The protection is for the shareholders who received his advice."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) notes on rr 11.1–11.2, n 2; r 12", takeaway:"Dealing against your own published advice → 24h notice + explanation first."
},

{ id:"fb-5-01", lesson:"B.5", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under the Securities and Futures Act, a person becomes a 'substantial shareholder' of a listed company (with notification duties) at what level?",
  options:[
    {t:"5% or more", correct:true,
     why:"A person with an interest in 5% or more of the voting shares of a listed company is a substantial shareholder and must notify the company (and the market is informed) within the statutory period, and again on each change in the percentage level of its interest and when it ceases to be a substantial shareholder. Interests are widely defined to include shares held through nominees, controlled companies and certain agreements. In a takeover, these notices run alongside the Code's Rule 12 dealing disclosures; the Code also treats a 5% holder of the offeror or offeree as an associate."},
    {t:"1% or more", correct:false,
     why:"1% is the creeper increment under Rule 14.1(b) for holders in the 30–50% band. It is not the SFA disclosure threshold."},
    {t:"30% or more", correct:false,
     why:"30% is the Code's mandatory-offer trigger, not the substantial-shareholder level."},
    {t:"0.5% or more", correct:false,
     why:"There is no 0.5% threshold in the SFA regime. The level is 5%."}
  ],
  cite:"SFA (substantial shareholdings); Wan Ch 7 §D", takeaway:"Substantial shareholder = 5%+ (SFA notification); also a Code associate."
},

{ id:"rB-5-06", lesson:"B.5", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the market need to see CHANGES in substantial shareholdings, not just the initial 5% crossing?",
  options:[
    {t:"So stake-building is visible", correct:true,
     why:"A single notice at 5% tells the market little about whether someone is moving towards 30%. Requiring notification of each change in the percentage level of a substantial holding lets the company, its shareholders and the SIC see accumulation as it happens. That supports the Code's mandatory-offer regime (a creeping build-up towards 30% is visible), helps identify possible concert parties buying in parallel, and allows the target board to consider whether an offer may be imminent, which itself has consequences under Rule 5 and Rule 2."},
    {t:"To recalculate dividends", correct:false,
     why:"Dividends are paid per share and do not depend on substantial-shareholding notices."},
    {t:"To set the audit fee", correct:false,
     why:"Irrelevant. The notices serve market transparency around control."},
    {t:"Because the Code bans stake-building", correct:false,
     why:"Stake-building below the thresholds is lawful. It must be disclosed, not avoided."}
  ],
  cite:"SFA; Wan Ch 7", takeaway:"Disclose changes too → watch accumulation toward control."
},

{ id:"rB-5-07", lesson:"B.5", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: dealing transparency during an offer matters partly because the highest price a bidder pays in the market can raise the price it must offer everyone.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Under Rule 21.1, if the offeror or its concert parties buy shares during the offer period at above the offer price, the offer must be raised to the highest price paid, and Rule 20.4 extends the higher price to those who have already accepted. For mandatory offers, Rule 14.3 sets the floor at the highest price in the offer period and the prior six months, and Rule 17 can require cash where 10% or more was bought for cash. None of this can be enforced unless the purchases are visible. Rule 12 disclosure is therefore what lets shareholders and the SIC check that the pricing rules are respected."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"It is true. Because market purchases can lift the required offer price, dealing disclosure is central to equal treatment."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) rr 12, 14.3, 17, 20.4, 21.1", takeaway:"Bidder's market buys can raise the offer floor → disclose them."
}

]);

;
