/* DealCraft — Chapter 3 · Lesson 3.6 Directors' Duties, Protections & Defences (audited Oct 2026).
   Sources: Code (2026) r 5 & nn 1–10, r 7.1, r 9.2, r 13 & nn 1–6; GP 7; Wan Ch 1 [1.60]–[1.64], Ch 8 §C–E [8.57]–[8.80], Ch 13 [13.9]–[13.12]; Gaughan Ch 5–7 (comparative). */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"fb-6-01", lesson:"B.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"During a bona fide offer, what does the Code require of the TARGET board regarding defensive ('frustrating') action?",
  options:[
    {t:"No frustrating action without shareholder approval", correct:true,
     why:"GP 7 and Rule 5 reserve the decision on an offer to the shareholders. The restriction applies during the offer and even before announcement once the board has reason to believe a bona fide offer is imminent. Unless it is acting under a contract entered into earlier, the board must not, without shareholder approval, take action that could frustrate the offer. Rule 5 lists examples: issuing shares or options, issuing convertibles, buying or selling assets of a material amount, entering contracts outside the ordinary course (including service contracts), and share buy-backs or financial assistance. This is a shareholder-centred model, very different from US law, where boards have wide latitude to adopt defences. The board remains free to give its opinion, seek a better offer and argue its case."},
    {t:"The board may deploy any defensive measure it considers to be in the company's interests", correct:false,
     why:"That is closer to the US position. In Singapore, frustrating action needs shareholder approval, whatever the board thinks of the offer."},
    {t:"The board must always recommend acceptance of the first offer received", correct:false,
     why:"The board can recommend rejection, provided it gives reasons and publishes the IFA's advice. What it cannot do is take action that takes the decision away from shareholders."},
    {t:"The board may adopt a poison pill as in Delaware", correct:false,
     why:"Poison pills are a US device. Issuing rights designed to dilute a bidder would be frustrating action requiring shareholder approval under Rule 5."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) GP 7, r 5; Wan Ch 8 §C, §E; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"No frustrating action without shareholder approval; the decision is shareholders'."
},

{ id:"d2-009", amended:true, lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Facing a hostile bid, a target board wants to issue a large block of new shares to a friendly party to dilute the bidder.",
  prompt:"Can the board do this under the Code?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: that is frustrating action needing approval", correct:true,
     why:"Issuing authorised but unissued shares is the first example in Rule 5's list. A dilutive issue to a friendly holder would put a block of votes in the hands of someone who will not accept, making it harder or impossible for the bidder to succeed, and shareholders would lose the chance to decide on the offer. The board needs shareholder approval at a general meeting. Under the 2026 note 9, it must then get competent independent advice on whether the financial terms are fair and reasonable, consult the SIC on the meeting date, and send a circular with full details, the board's opinion and reasons, and the status of the offer. Under Companies Act principles, an issue made for the purpose of defeating a bid may also be an improper exercise of directors' powers."},
    {t:"Yes: the board may deploy any defence it thinks is in the company's interests", correct:false,
     why:"The Singapore Code does not leave defence to board discretion. Frustrating action needs shareholder approval."},
    {t:"Yes: poison pills are standard in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"They are not. This kind of dilutive issue is exactly what Rule 5 restricts."},
    {t:"Only if the bidder consents", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 on Rule 5 says the SIC would normally waive the meeting if the offeror has no objection, but a hostile bidder will object. The default test is shareholder approval."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 5 & nn 1, 9; Wan Ch 8 §C, §E", takeaway:"Dilutive defensive issue = frustrating action → shareholder approval (with IFA + circular)."
},

{ id:"rB-6-01", lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During a hostile offer, a target proposes to sell a subsidiary. The subsidiary's net assets are 8% of the group's, but it owns the patents behind the group's flagship product.",
  prompt:"Is the sale of 'a material amount' requiring shareholder approval under Rule 5?",
  options:[
    {t:"Probably: core patents can be material below 10%", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2 on Rule 5 explains how the SIC judges 'material amount'. It compares the value of the assets with the offeree's assets (fixed plus current assets less current liabilities), the consideration with the offeree's assets, and the profits attributable to the assets with the offeree's profits. Relative values of 10% or more are normally material, but values below 10% may be material if the asset is of particular significance. A subsidiary holding the patents behind the flagship product is a classic 'crown jewel', and selling it could make the target far less attractive to the bidder. The SIC also aggregates a series of smaller transactions. The board should consult the SIC in advance."},
    {t:"No: anything below 10% is automatically immaterial", correct:false,
     why:"10% is a guide, not a safe harbour. The note expressly says lower values may be material if the asset is particularly significant."},
    {t:"No: Rule 5 applies only to share issues", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 5 lists asset disposals and acquisitions of a material amount as frustrating actions."},
    {t:"Yes, but only if the bidder objects in writing", correct:false,
     why:"The test is whether the action could frustrate the offer. The bidder's lack of objection may lead the SIC to waive the meeting (note 1), but materiality does not depend on objection."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 5(d) & n 2", takeaway:"'Material amount': ≥10% relative value normally, lower if the asset is especially significant; aggregate small deals."
},

{ id:"p2x-B6-01", amended:true, lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Under the 2026 Code, during a hostile offer, the target board negotiates a sale of a non-core division that it believes is in shareholders' interests. To avoid a shareholder meeting, it makes completion conditional on the hostile offer lapsing or being withdrawn.",
  prompt:"How will the SIC normally treat this?",
  options:[
    {t:"It will normally waive approval, with full disclosure", correct:true,
     why:"New note 10 on Rule 5 says that for a proposed action within Rule 5 that would require shareholder approval, the SIC would normally waive the requirement if taking the action is conditional on the offer being withdrawn or lapsing. The logic is that such an action cannot frustrate the offer: if shareholders accept, the action never happens. In return, the board must publish an announcement with the details in note 9(c) (full details of the action, the board's opinion and reasons, the current status of the offer, and anything else shareholders need). The SIC may also require a document to be sent and a paid press notice to ensure wide dissemination. Shareholders can then weigh the offer against the board's alternative plan."},
    {t:"It will treat it as frustrating action requiring a meeting regardless", correct:false,
     why:"Note 10 provides the opposite default where the action is conditional on the offer failing."},
    {t:"It will prohibit any asset sale during an offer", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 5 requires approval for material actions, not a ban. Note 10 allows conditional actions with disclosure."},
    {t:"It will allow it without any announcement", correct:false,
     why:"The waiver comes with an obligation to announce details so shareholders can compare the alternative with the offer."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 5 nn 9–10; amendments digest p42", takeaway:"2026 n 10: action conditional on the offer failing → approval normally waived, but full announcement."
},

{ id:"p2x-B6-02", lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During an offer period, the target board wants to (a) pay a special dividend of twice the usual amount and (b) give the CEO a 40% pay rise and a new five-year contract.",
  prompt:"What does Rule 5 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Consult the SIC; the pay terms need approval", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 on Rule 5 says dividends other than in the normal course and usual quantum during an offer period may frustrate an offer, for example by changing the value of what the bidder is buying or creating cash obligations, so the offeree and its advisers must consult the SIC in advance. Scheduled dividends declared before the offer period (or before the board had reason to believe an offer was imminent) are outside the Rule. Note 4 says any change to a director's service contract that significantly improves his terms is treated as a contract 'other than in the ordinary course of business'. A 40% rise and a five-year contract are significant improvements and would also act as a poison pill on control. A genuine promotion or new appointment is not prevented, but the SIC must be consulted in advance."},
    {t:"Both are ordinary business decisions for the board", correct:false,
     why:"Notes 3 and 4 specifically bring abnormal dividends and improved directors' contracts within Rule 5."},
    {t:"Only the dividend matters, because pay is for the remuneration committee", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 expressly treats significant improvements to directors' terms as outside the ordinary course."},
    {t:"Neither matters if announced to SGX", correct:false,
     why:"Announcement does not satisfy Rule 5. Shareholder approval or SIC clearance is needed."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 5 nn 3–4", takeaway:"Abnormal dividends → consult SIC; big director pay improvements = outside ordinary course."
},

{ id:"p2x-B6-03", lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A target board facing an unsolicited offer it considers too low hires a bank to run a sale process and contacts possible white knights.",
  prompt:"Is soliciting a competing offer frustrating action?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not normally: seeking rivals isn't frustrating", correct:true,
     why:"Note 8 on Rule 5 says an offeree board may consider soliciting a competing offer or running a sale process, and the SIC will not normally treat that as frustrating the original offer. A better or alternative offer is generally in shareholders' interests, and seeking one neither hinders the first offer nor deprives shareholders of the chance to decide on it. The board must still treat bidders equally under Rule 9.2: information given to a white knight must be given to the first bidder on request. It must not give the white knight deal protections that exceed Rule 13, and it should consult the SIC in cases of doubt."},
    {t:"Yes: any step that might defeat the first offer needs shareholder approval", correct:false,
     why:"Note 8 specifically excludes soliciting competing offers, because competition benefits shareholders."},
    {t:"Yes, unless the first bidder consents", correct:false,
     why:"The first bidder's consent is not needed to look for a better offer."},
    {t:"Only if the white knight offers less", correct:false,
     why:"The point of a white knight is usually to offer more. Seeking a competing offer is permitted regardless."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 5 n 8, r 9.2", takeaway:"Seeking a white knight / sale process ≠ frustrating action (but keep information parity)."
},

{ id:"fb-6-02", amended:true, lesson:"B.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under Rule 13, a break fee payable by the offeree company normally cannot exceed…",
  options:[
    {t:"1% of the target's value", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 13 says a break fee must be minimal, normally no more than 1% of the offeree's value calculated by reference to the offer price. Note 4 allows the calculation on a fully diluted basis (in-the-money options at see-through value, convertibles at offer price times conversion ratio) and requires non-recoverable taxes to be included. The offeree board and its financial adviser must give the SIC written confirmations: that the fee was agreed in normal commercial negotiations, the basis and circumstances of payment, information about possible competitors, that all related arrangements are disclosed, and, since 2026, that they believe the fee is in shareholders' best interests with an explanation why. The fee must be disclosed in the Rule 3 announcement and offer document. Separately, a break fee may raise a s 76 financial-assistance issue (Wan [8.71])."},
    {t:"10% of the target's value", correct:false,
     why:"10% is far above the Code's limit and would be a serious deterrent to competing bidders."},
    {t:"Any amount the parties agree", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 13 caps break fees. The SIC must be consulted at the earliest opportunity whenever one is proposed."},
    {t:"5% of the offer premium", correct:false,
     why:"The metric is the value of the offeree at the offer price, not a share of the premium."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 13 & n 4; Wan Ch 8 §D [8.57]–[8.58], [8.71]", takeaway:"Break fee ≤ 1% of offeree value (at offer price) + written confirmations; check s 76."
},

{ id:"ab-6-02", amended:true, lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bidder offering S$500m for a target asks for a S$7.5m break fee, payable if the board withdraws its recommendation.",
  prompt:"What is the Code position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Too high: the cap is 1% (S$5m)", correct:true,
     why:"1% of S$500m is S$5m, so S$7.5m (1.5%) exceeds the normal cap. The SIC would not normally accept it. Even at S$5m, the target board and its financial adviser must confirm in writing that the fee resulted from normal commercial negotiations, explain its basis and when it becomes payable, give information on possible competing offerors, confirm full disclosure of related arrangements, and (since the 2026 amendments) confirm and explain why they believe it is in shareholders' best interests. Rule 13 also applies to non-cash arrangements with similar economic effect, such as options over assets, so the bidder cannot evade the cap by restructuring the fee."},
    {t:"Fine if shareholders approve it", correct:false,
     why:"The cap is a Code limit, and shareholder approval does not lift it."},
    {t:"Fine: fees up to 2% are allowed for recommended deals", correct:false,
     why:"The cap is normally 1%, whether or not the deal is recommended."},
    {t:"Break fees are banned in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"They are permitted within the 1% cap and the safeguards."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 13 & n 1; Wan Ch 8 [8.57]–[8.58]", takeaway:"Break fee ≤1% (at offer price) + written confirmations (2026: explain best interests)."
},

{ id:"p2x-B6-04", amended:true, lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Under the 2026 Code, a target agreed a 1% break fee with Bidder A. Bidder B then emerges and asks for its own 1% break fee before it will make a higher competing offer.",
  prompt:"What is the limit?",
  options:[
    {t:"All break fees together: 1% of value", correct:true,
     why:"New note 5 on Rule 13 addresses multiple bidders. If the offeree agrees to more than one break fee, the aggregate value of all fees that may be payable must be minimal, normally no more than 1% of the offeree's value calculated by reference to the price of the first competing offer at the time of its Rule 3.5 firm-intention announcement. Any break fee can only become payable if an offer becomes or is declared unconditional. So the board cannot give B a further full 1% on top of A's 1%; it must keep the total within the cap. The amendment prevents a target stacking fees in a contest, which would transfer value from shareholders to losing bidders."},
    {t:"Each bidder can have its own 1% fee", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5 caps the aggregate, not each fee separately."},
    {t:"No second fee is ever allowed", correct:false,
     why:"A second fee is possible, provided the total stays within 1%."},
    {t:"The cap rises to 2% in a competitive situation", correct:false,
     why:"The cap stays at 1% in aggregate. Competition is a reason to limit fees, not increase them."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 13 n 5; amendments digest p82", takeaway:"2026: total break fees ≤ 1% (first competing offer price); payable only if an offer goes unconditional."
},

{ id:"fb-6-03", amended:true, lesson:"B.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why must a no-shop or exclusivity covenant in an implementation agreement contain a 'fiduciary-out'?",
  options:[
    {t:"So the board can follow its duties", correct:true,
     why:"Directors owe duties to act in the company's interests, and they cannot contract out of them; a covenant that forces them to breach those duties is likely unenforceable (Wan Ch 8 §D, discussing Dawson v Coats Paton). New note 6(a) on Rule 13 now says expressly that exclusivity arrangements limiting the offeree board's ability to engage with competing offerors must be subject to a proviso relieving the directors of those obligations if necessary to discharge their fiduciary duties, including their obligations under Rule 5 and Rule 9.2. A no-shop can still stop the board actively soliciting rivals, but it cannot stop the board responding to an unsolicited superior proposal or giving a rival the information Rule 9.2 requires."},
    {t:"So the bidder can walk away if financing fails", correct:false,
     why:"Financing risk is dealt with through certain funds, conditions or reverse break fees. A fiduciary-out protects the target board, not the bidder."},
    {t:"To give the directors a personal tax indemnity", correct:false,
     why:"A fiduciary-out has nothing to do with indemnities. It preserves the board's ability to act in shareholders' interests."},
    {t:"To exempt the deal from the mandatory-offer rule", correct:false,
     why:"That would be a whitewash or SIC dispensation. A fiduciary-out concerns deal protection."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 13 n 6(a); Wan Ch 8 §D (Dawson v Coats Paton); Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Exclusivity/no-shop must carry a fiduciary-out (2026 r 13 n 6(a), incl. r 5 and r 9.2)."
},

{ id:"p2x-B6-05", amended:true, lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Under the 2026 Code, an implementation agreement gives the bidder a 14-calendar-day matching right if a superior proposal emerges, and requires the target to pass the bidder full details of any rival approach, including the rival's identity and price.",
  prompt:"How are these terms likely to be viewed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Both likely deter competitors", correct:true,
     why:"New note 6 on Rule 13 asks offeree directors to consider whether commitments in an implementation agreement would deter competing offerors. Under note 6(c), a matching right cannot last so long that it removes any practical likelihood of a competitor making a proposal, and a duration exceeding 7 calendar days would normally be regarded as having that effect. Under note 6(b), an obligation to tell the original offeror about a potential competing offer may reduce the likelihood of rivals approaching; notifying only the fact of an approach has limited anti-competitive effect compared with passing on detailed information. The board should negotiate a matching right of 7 days or less and limit notification to the fact of an approach, or risk the SIC regarding the terms as unacceptable."},
    {t:"Both are standard and acceptable", correct:false,
     why:"The 2026 notes specifically flag long matching rights and detailed notification as deterrents to competition."},
    {t:"Only the matching right matters", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6(b) also addresses notification obligations, distinguishing the fact of an approach from its details."},
    {t:"Matching rights are banned entirely", correct:false,
     why:"They are permitted, but the duration must leave rivals a practical chance. Seven days is the normal benchmark."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 13 n 6(b)–(c); amendments digest p83", takeaway:"2026: matching rights ≤ 7 calendar days; notify the fact of an approach, not its details."
},

{ id:"ab-6-01", amended:true, lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A scheme implementation agreement bars the target from convening a meeting on any rival proposal until eight weeks after the first bidder's scheme meeting. A higher all-cash rival bid arrives.",
  prompt:"How did the SIC treat such a restriction in the SPH contest?",
  options:[
    {t:"It ruled the restriction unenforceable", correct:true,
     why:"In 2021, Keppel's scheme for SPH contained a restriction of this kind. When Cuscaden made a higher all-cash offer, the SIC ruled that the restriction could not be enforced because it would have prevented shareholders from considering a competing offer. SPH shareholders ultimately chose Cuscaden. Wan [1.60]–[1.64], [8.80] and [13.12] discuss the case as showing that deal protections which effectively deny shareholders a choice between bids will not stand. The 2026 notes on Rule 13 (fiduciary-outs, matching rights of no more than 7 days, limits on notification) build on the same principle."},
    {t:"It upheld the restriction as ordinary contract", correct:false,
     why:"The SIC refused to let a deal protection deny shareholders a choice between bids."},
    {t:"It required Cuscaden to wait until the Keppel vote", correct:false,
     why:"That was the effect the SIC's ruling removed."},
    {t:"It ordered SPH to pay Cuscaden a break fee", correct:false,
     why:"No such order was made. Break fees are agreed, not ordered."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 1 [1.60]–[1.64]; Ch 8 [8.80]; Ch 13 [13.12]; Code (2026) r 13 n 6", takeaway:"SPH: protections that block shareholders from a rival bid won't stand."
},

{ id:"rB-6-04", amended:true, lesson:"B.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A rival bidder emerges and asks the target board for the diligence information already given to the first bidder. What does the Code expect?",
  options:[
    {t:"Give the rival the same information", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 9.2 requires information given to one offeror or potential offeror to be given equally and promptly, on request, to any other bona fide offeror or potential offeror. The 2026 amendments removed the old requirement that the rival specify the questions it wanted answered, and added that the board must provide all the information it has provided, and provides in the seven days after the request, to the other offeror. The target can set up a data room and attach reasonable conditions (confidentiality, non-solicitation). Where trade secrets were given to one bidder, the target must consult the SIC before refusing the same information to another. Equal information keeps the contest fair and maximises value."},
    {t:"The board must refuse all information to the second bidder", correct:false,
     why:"Refusing a genuine rival the same information would breach Rule 9.2 and deter competition."},
    {t:"The board may charge the second bidder a fee for it", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 9.2 requires equal treatment. A fee designed to disadvantage one bidder would undermine it."},
    {t:"Only the SIC can release the information", correct:false,
     why:"The board provides the information. The SIC is consulted only in specific cases, such as trade secrets."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 9.2 & notes 3, 5", takeaway:"r 9.2 (2026): rival gets everything given + next 7 days; no need to specify questions."
},

{ id:"ab-6-03", lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The CEO of a listed target also sits on the board of the bidder in a scheme take-private.",
  prompt:"What is expected of the CEO?",
  options:[
    {t:"Abstain; independent directors recommend", correct:true,
     why:"One of the conditions for the SIC's usual exemption of a scheme from the offer rules is that directors of a merging company who are also directors of the other merging company, or who are acting in concert with the offeror, abstain from making a recommendation (condition (c) in the Note on the Definition of Offer). The CEO is on both sides, so his view cannot be independent. The independent directors make the recommendation, advised by the IFA the offeree must appoint (condition (d)). The CEO still takes responsibility for the accuracy of factual information about the target in the scheme document, and should be excluded from the target's deliberations on the scheme terms (Wan [2.83], [13.9])."},
    {t:"He should lead the recommendation because he knows both companies best", correct:false,
     why:"His conflict is precisely why he must abstain."},
    {t:"He must resign from the target board", correct:false,
     why:"Abstention from the recommendation is required, not resignation."},
    {t:"Nothing, as long as he discloses the conflict", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure alone is not enough; abstention is a condition of the SIC's exemption."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) Note on Definition of Offer (c)–(d); Wan Ch 2 [2.83]; Ch 13 [13.9]", takeaway:"Common directors abstain from the scheme recommendation."
},

{ id:"rB-6-02", amended:true, lesson:"B.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why must the target board obtain and publish advice from an INDEPENDENT financial adviser (IFA) on an offer?",
  options:[
    {t:"For an objective fairness view", correct:true,
     why:"GP 8 and Rule 7.1 require the offeree board to obtain competent independent advice on any offer and to make its substance known to shareholders. Directors may have conflicts: they may be rolling over, staying on, or close to the bidder or a controlling shareholder. The IFA gives an outside view on fairness (usually price against value) and reasonableness (the wider context, such as alternatives and liquidity). The board then forms and publishes its own recommendation. Where the board and the IFA disagree, or the board cannot give a firm recommendation, Rule 7.1 note 3 requires the divergence to be explained. Since 2026, Rule 5 note 9 also requires IFA advice on any frustrating action put to shareholders."},
    {t:"To set the offer price", correct:false,
     why:"The IFA does not set the price. It assesses the bidder's price."},
    {t:"To act for the bidder", correct:false,
     why:"The IFA advises the offeree board and its shareholders. It must be independent of the bidder."},
    {t:"To replace the board's recommendation", correct:false,
     why:"The board must still give its own recommendation, informed by the IFA's advice."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) GP 8, r 7.1 & n 3, r 5 n 9", takeaway:"IFA gives shareholders an independent fair/reasonable opinion; board still recommends."
},

{ id:"rB-6-03", lesson:"B.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A US-style 'poison pill' (shareholder rights plan) is a classic takeover defence. How does it fare in Singapore?",
  options:[
    {t:"Effectively unavailable: it frustrates", correct:true,
     why:"A poison pill gives existing shareholders (other than the bidder) rights to buy new shares cheaply if someone crosses a threshold, diluting the bidder. In the US, Delaware courts allow boards to adopt pills subject to fiduciary review. In Singapore, issuing options or rights over unissued shares and creating convertible securities are listed examples of frustrating action in Rule 5, so a board cannot deploy one against a bona fide offer without shareholder approval. Gaughan's US defences are useful vocabulary for comparison, but the Code's shareholder-centred model leaves little room for board-led defences."},
    {t:"It is mandatory in every listed company", correct:false,
     why:"Poison pills are not required anywhere in Singapore law."},
    {t:"It is imposed by the SIC", correct:false,
     why:"The SIC does not impose defences. It enforces the restriction on frustrating action."},
    {t:"It is required by the SFA", correct:false,
     why:"The SFA governs market conduct and disclosure. It does not require takeover defences."}
  ],
  cite:"Code (2026) r 5(b)–(c); Gaughan Ch 5 (comparative)", takeaway:"Poison pills = frustrating action under r 5 → need shareholder approval."
},

{ id:"fb-6-04", lesson:"B.6", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each US takeover defence/tactic (Gaughan, comparative; not Singapore law) to what it does.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Poison pill", r:"Dilutes a hostile bidder by letting others buy cheap shares"},
    {l:"White knight", r:"A friendlier rival bidder invited to outbid the hostile one"},
    {l:"Greenmail", r:"Buying back a raider's stake at a premium to make it go away"},
    {l:"Staggered board", r:"Directors elected in classes, slowing a board takeover"}
  ],
  why:"Gaughan's US material supplies the vocabulary of takeover tactics. A poison pill dilutes a hostile bidder once it crosses a threshold. A white knight is a friendlier rival brought in to outbid the hostile party. Greenmail is a targeted buy-back of a raider's stake at a premium. A staggered board elects directors in classes so a bidder cannot replace the board in one vote. In Singapore, a white knight is the one that fits comfortably with the Code (soliciting a competing offer is not frustrating action under Rule 5 note 8). A pill would be a share or option issue needing shareholder approval; greenmail would be a buy-back caught by Rule 5(f) and an unequal special deal; staggered boards matter less because directors can be removed by ordinary resolution.",
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 5–7 (comparative); Code (2026) r 5 & n 8; Wan Ch 8 §E", takeaway:"US defences: pill, white knight, greenmail, staggered board; only white knights sit easily with the SG Code."
}

]);

;
