/* ================= 2.2 — Leveraged Buyouts: Structure & Financing ================= */
{
  id:"fa-2-01", dia:"capitalStack", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is a 'BidCo' in a leveraged buyout?",
  options:[
    {t:"A newly-formed shell company used to make the acquisition, funded by sponsor equity plus acquisition debt", correct:true,
     why:"In a leveraged deal the buyer is typically a fresh shell — BidCo — into which a financial sponsor injects equity and lenders advance acquisition debt. BidCo acquires the target, and the intention is usually to service and repay that debt from the target's own cash flows and assets, which is what raises the financial-assistance question."},
    {t:"The target company being acquired", correct:false,
     why:"The target is the company being bought, not the acquisition vehicle. BidCo is the newly-formed shell that does the buying."},
    {t:"The investment bank running the sale process", correct:false,
     why:"The bank is a financial adviser. BidCo is the special-purpose acquisition vehicle funded with equity and debt."},
    {t:"The escrow agent holding the consideration", correct:false,
     why:"An escrow agent is a neutral stakeholder for held-back price. BidCo is the buyer entity itself."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §E; Gaughan Ch 8; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"BidCo = the SPV buyer (sponsor equity + acquisition debt)."
},
{
  id:"fa-2-02", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The defining financial feature of an LBO is that the purchase is funded largely by…",
  options:[
    {t:"Debt, which is intended to be serviced and secured against the target's own assets and cash flows", correct:true,
     why:"An LBO uses a high proportion of debt, with the expectation that the target's own assets secure the borrowing and its cash flows repay it. That 'push-down' of debt onto the target is exactly what engages financial assistance (s 76) where a public company sits in the chain, because the target is helping fund the purchase of its own shares."},
    {t:"The sponsor's own cash reserves, with no borrowing", correct:false,
     why:"An all-equity purchase is not 'leveraged'. The whole point of an LBO is the use of substantial debt to amplify equity returns."},
    {t:"A share-for-share exchange with the target's shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Scrip-for-scrip is a securities-consideration technique, not the LBO model. An LBO is characterised by acquisition debt, usually paid to sellers in cash."},
    {t:"A government grant", correct:false,
     why:"LBOs are financed by private debt and equity, not grants. The leverage — and its push-down onto the target — is the defining feature."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §E–F; Gaughan Ch 8; Intro 2.9, 2.15", takeaway:"LBO = debt-funded, secured on the target — which flags s 76."
},
{
  id:"fa-2-03", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A sponsor needs to sign a buyout knowing it can pay on the completion date. What financing feature addresses this?",
  options:[
    {t:"'Certain funds' — committed, near-unconditional financing", correct:true,
     why:"Certain funds means the debt and equity commitments are firm and near-unconditional, so the buyer can sign confident it can pay on a fixed completion day. A lender's soft 'we'll confirm in 72 hours' can collide badly with a deal that must complete on a set date, so certainty of funding is negotiated up front (and in public deals is effectively required before an offer is announced)."},
    {t:"A material adverse change clause", correct:false,
     why:"A MAC lets a buyer walk on a serious deterioration in the target; it is about exiting, not about proving the buyer has money. Funding certainty is 'certain funds'."},
    {t:"A locked-box mechanism", correct:false,
     why:"Locked-box is a pricing mechanism (fixing value to a historic balance sheet). It does not address whether the buyer's financing is committed — that is certain funds."},
    {t:"A break fee", correct:false,
     why:"A break fee compensates a party if the deal falls through. It does not ensure the buyer actually has committed money to complete, which is the certain-funds concept."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §D–E; Intro 2.9", takeaway:"Certain funds = committed financing so the buyer can sign and pay for sure."
},
{
  id:"fa-2-04", dia:"s76test", lesson:"A.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"To fund BidCo's acquisition of a public target, lenders want the TARGET to grant security over its assets and guarantee the acquisition loan.",
  prompt:"What is the immediate legal problem?",
  options:[
    {t:"Financial assistance (s 76): the target would be assisting the purchase of its own shares, and a guarantee/security is a contingent depletion of its assets", correct:true,
     why:"Where a public company is in the chain, s 76 prohibits the company from assisting an acquisition of its own shares. A target guarantee or security over its assets for the buyer's acquisition loan is 'financial' assistance because it contingently depletes the company's assets (PP v Lew Syn Pau). The security/guarantee must wait for a whitewash or exception, usually sequenced as a condition."},
    {t:"None — a target can always secure its buyer's debt", correct:false,
     why:"This is exactly the trap. Using the target's balance sheet to fund the purchase of its own shares is the classic financial-assistance problem where a public company is in the chain."},
    {t:"Stamp duty on the security documents only", correct:false,
     why:"Duty may arise on some documents, but the headline legal problem is financial assistance under s 76, not stamp duty."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer is triggered", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer is a Code trigger on crossing 30% of a listed target. The problem here is the target funding its own acquisition — financial assistance."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F [15.62]; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Target security/guarantee for the buyout loan = financial assistance (s 76)."
},

/* ================= 2.3 — Financial Assistance in Buyouts (s 76) ================= */
{
  id:"fa-3-01", dia:"s76test", lesson:"A.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the FIRST question to ask in any LBO financial-assistance analysis?",
  options:[
    {t:"Is there a public company anywhere in the acquisition chain?", correct:true,
     why:"Post-2014, s 76 catches only a public company, or a company whose holding/ultimate holding company is public. So the threshold question is whether any public company sits in the chain: if the target is private with no public parent, s 76 does not apply at all and the buyout can push debt down freely. Only if a public company is present do you run the rest of the analysis."},
    {t:"What is the stamp duty rate on the share transfer?", correct:false,
     why:"Stamp duty is a routine overlay, not the gating s 76 question. The first move is to check for a public company in the chain, because that determines whether s 76 applies at all."},
    {t:"Has a mandatory offer been triggered?", correct:false,
     why:"Mandatory offers arise under the Code for listed targets. The s 76 analysis begins with whether a public company is in the acquisition chain, not with Code thresholds."},
    {t:"Is the consideration cash or scrip?", correct:false,
     why:"The consideration form matters for other purposes (s 161 authority for scrip), but the s 76 gate is whether a public company is in the chain."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"s 76 gate: is a public company anywhere in the chain? If not, s 76 is out."
},
{
  id:"fa-3-02", lesson:"A.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A buyout target is now a private company with a handful of shareholders, but its ultimate holding company is PUBLIC, so s 76 still applies. Which whitewash route is the practical choice?",
  options:[
    {t:"The shareholder-approved route (s 76(9B)): a solvency statement plus unanimous shareholder approval, with no cap", correct:true,
     why:"s 76(9B) needs a solvency statement and unanimous shareholder approval but imposes no cap, making it workable at buy-out scale once holders are few (unanimity is achievable). The director-approved route (s 76(9A)) is capped at 10% of capital and reserves — useless for a buyout — and the traditional route (special resolution + court + newspaper notice) is cumbersome."},
    {t:"The director-approved route (s 76(9A)), because it has no cap", correct:false,
     why:"s 76(9A) IS capped (10% of capital and reserves), which makes it useless at buyout scale. It is s 76(9B) that is uncapped — the cap is on the wrong route here."},
    {t:"No whitewash is needed for a private company", correct:false,
     why:"If the private company has a public parent it can still fall within s 76, so a whitewash or exception may be required. Where s 76 applies, s 76(9B) is the practical cure."},
    {t:"The traditional court route, because it is the fastest", correct:false,
     why:"The traditional route (special resolution + court + newspaper notice) is the slow, cumbersome one. For a private target with few holders, s 76(9B) is faster and simpler."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Within s 76 with few holders → s 76(9B): solvency + unanimous, uncapped. (No public company in the chain → s 76 doesn't apply at all.)"
},
{
  id:"fa-3-03", lesson:"A.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In Brady v Brady (adopted in Wu Yang), what distinction is central to the 'for the purpose of' limb of financial assistance?",
  options:[
    {t:"The reason for a transaction is not the same as its purpose — a genuine commercial benefit to the target does not sanctify assistance given for the acquisition", correct:true,
     why:"Brady v Brady draws a line between a transaction's larger commercial reason and its immediate purpose. Even if the assistance produces a real commercial benefit to the target, that benefit is only the reason; if the purpose is to facilitate the acquisition of shares, the assistance still offends s 76. Wu Yang adopted this reasoning, so 'it was good for the company' is not a defence."},
    {t:"Financial assistance is permitted whenever the board acts in good faith", correct:false,
     why:"Good faith alone does not cure financial assistance. Brady v Brady's point is precisely that a commercial benefit (even a genuine one) does not sanctify assistance whose purpose is to fund the share acquisition."},
    {t:"Only cash payments count as financial assistance", correct:false,
     why:"Assistance can be a guarantee or security (a contingent depletion of assets), not just cash. Brady v Brady concerns the purpose limb, not the form of assistance."},
    {t:"Private companies are always exempt", correct:false,
     why:"That is a scope point (post-2014, private companies with no public parent are outside s 76), not the Brady v Brady purpose/reason distinction."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Brady v Brady: commercial benefit is the 'reason', not the 'purpose' — no cure."
},
{
  id:"fa-3-04", lesson:"A.3", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Structuring the deal as an asset/business purchase (rather than a share purchase) removes the financial-assistance problem.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. s 76 is triggered by assistance to acquire SHARES. An asset/business deal involves no share acquisition, so there is nothing for the company to 'assist' — one of the recognised escape routes, alongside structuring as a private company with no public parent, relying on an exception, or running a whitewash."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"It is true. Because the prohibition bites on acquiring shares, moving to an asset purchase sidesteps s 76 entirely. The trade-off is the asset deal's own frictions (consents, licences, transfer mechanics)."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"No share acquisition, no s 76 — the asset route is an escape."
},
{
  id:"fa-3-05", lesson:"A.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the consequence if unlawful financial assistance is given in breach of s 76?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company may avoid the transaction (s 76A(2)) and officers in default face criminal sanction", correct:true,
     why:"Breach of s 76 has real teeth: the assisted transaction is voidable at the company's instance (s 76A(2)), and officers in default can be criminally liable. That is why practitioners sequence the whitewash or exception correctly against signing and completion and make it a condition precedent — you cannot fix it after the fact."},
    {t:"The assistance is automatically valid once completion occurs", correct:false,
     why:"Completion does not cleanse a breach. The transaction may be avoided and officers face sanction, which is why the cure must be in place before the assistance is given."},
    {t:"Only a small administrative fine applies", correct:false,
     why:"The consequences are more serious than an administrative fine — the company may avoid the transaction and officers face criminal liability."},
    {t:"The buyer automatically acquires good title regardless", correct:false,
     why:"A breach can unravel the assisted transaction, so title is not immune. The correct approach is to cure s 76 in advance via whitewash or exception."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §F; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Breach of s 76 = voidable transaction + officer criminal liability; cure it first."
},

/* ================= 2.4 — MBOs & Management Conflicts ================= */
{
  id:"fa-4-01", lesson:"A.4", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the DEFINING issue in a management buyout (MBO)?",
  options:[
    {t:"The conflict of interest — the managers are on both sides, buying the very company they run and know best", correct:true,
     why:"In an MBO the managers are simultaneously fiduciaries of the target and the buyers of it, creating an acute conflict: they know more than the sellers, and they benefit from a low price. The whole archetype is organised around managing that conflict, which is why the cure is an independent process — recusal of the conflicted managers, an independent committee, and independent advice."},
    {t:"The stamp duty rate on the share transfer", correct:false,
     why:"Stamp duty is a routine overlay. The defining feature of an MBO is the managers' conflict of interest, not tax."},
    {t:"Whether the Take-Over Code applies", correct:false,
     why:"The Code applies only if the target is listed. The archetype-defining issue of any MBO — listed or private — is the management conflict."},
    {t:"The choice between locked-box and completion accounts", correct:false,
     why:"Pricing mechanics matter but are not the defining MBO issue. The conflict of insiders buying their own company is."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §G; Intro 2.15, 2.7", takeaway:"MBO's defining issue: managers on both sides — a conflict of interest."
},
{
  id:"fa-4-02", lesson:"A.4", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the standard 'cure' for the conflict in an MBO or a controller freeze-out?",
  options:[
    {t:"An independent committee, recusal of the conflicted insiders, independent (IFA) advice, and abstention by the conflicted party at the vote", correct:true,
     why:"Where insiders sit on both sides, the cure is a robust independent process: the conflicted managers/controllers recuse themselves, an independent committee of disinterested directors runs the deal, an independent financial adviser opines on fairness, and the conflicted party abstains from voting. The recurring error is treating a conflicted deal as if it were arm's length."},
    {t:"Simply disclosing the conflict in the annual report", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure alone does not neutralise a live conflict in a transaction. The deal needs an independent committee, recusal, independent advice and abstention — active procedural safeguards, not just after-the-fact disclosure."},
    {t:"Increasing the price by 10%", correct:false,
     why:"A price bump does not address the process defect. Fairness comes from an independent committee, IFA opinion and abstention — a fair process, not an arbitrary uplift."},
    {t:"Obtaining a mandatory offer waiver", correct:false,
     why:"A whitewash of a mandatory offer is a separate Code mechanism. The conflict cure is the independent-process package."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 8, Ch 15 §G; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"Conflict cure: independent committee + recusal + IFA + abstention."
},
{
  id:"fa-4-03", lesson:"A.4", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each management-incentive/rollover term to what it does.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Rollover", r:"Managers take part of their consideration as equity in BidCo rather than cash"},
    {l:"Ratchet", r:"Adjusts management's equity share based on performance"},
    {l:"Good leaver / bad leaver", r:"Determines what a departing manager keeps of rolled-over equity"},
    {l:"Vendor loan", r:"Deferred consideration the seller leaves in as debt"}
  ],
  why:"MBOs and sponsor buyouts rely on incentive engineering. A rollover keeps managers invested by converting some of their proceeds into BidCo equity; a ratchet flexes their equity up or down with performance; good-leaver/bad-leaver terms decide what a departing manager keeps of that equity; and a vendor loan is deferred consideration the seller leaves in the structure as debt. Together they align managers with the sponsor and post-deal performance.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §G; Intro 2.12, glossary", takeaway:"Rollover, ratchet, leaver terms and vendor loans align management incentives."
},
{
  id:"fa-4-04", lesson:"A.4", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Because the managers know the business best, an MBO can safely be negotiated as an ordinary arm's-length deal.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. The managers' superior knowledge is precisely the problem: they are conflicted fiduciaries buying from the people they advise. Treating an MBO as arm's length is the recurring error; the deal needs an independent committee, recusal, an IFA opinion and abstention to protect the sellers/minorities."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This is the classic trap. Managers' inside knowledge and their position on both sides make the deal conflicted, not arm's length — hence the mandatory independent-process safeguards."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §G; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"An MBO is never arm's length — it is a conflicted deal needing safeguards."
},

/* ================= 2.5 — Asset Carve-outs & Transfer Mechanics ================= */
{
  id:"fa-5-01", dia:"carveOut", lesson:"A.5", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In an asset/business carve-out, what is the lawyer's central task?",
  options:[
    {t:"Defining the perimeter precisely and building the transfer mechanics — novations/consents, fresh licences, employee transfer, and a TSA for shared services", correct:true,
     why:"A carve-out is boundary-drawing plus plumbing: deciding exactly which assets, contracts, people and liabilities are in versus out, then moving each one. Contracts are novated/assigned (usually needing consent), non-transferable licences are re-applied for, employees may transfer under s 18A, and a transitional services agreement bridges shared IT/HR/finance until the business stands alone."},
    {t:"Maximising the warranty cap in the SPA", correct:false,
     why:"Warranties still feature, but the defining carve-out task is the perimeter and the transfer mechanics, not the size of the cap."},
    {t:"Triggering a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"Mandatory offers are a Code matter for listed targets. A private carve-out turns on perimeter and transfer mechanics, not Code thresholds."},
    {t:"Choosing between a scheme and an offer", correct:false,
     why:"Scheme-versus-offer is a public-company route choice. A carve-out's central task is defining what transfers and how."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2 §C; Intro 2.3, 2.15", takeaway:"Carve-out = perimeter + transfer mechanics (consents, licences, s 18A, TSA)."
},
{
  id:"fa-5-02", lesson:"A.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A carve-out includes an operator licence that, by statute, is entity-specific and cannot be transferred to the buyer.",
  prompt:"How should this be handled in the deal?",
  options:[
    {t:"As a condition precedent — the buyer must obtain a fresh licence before completion, because the business cannot lawfully operate without it", correct:true,
     why:"A non-transferable licence is a gating item: the acquired business cannot operate lawfully without it, so obtaining a fresh licence is made a condition precedent to completion. Classifying it correctly ([CP], not merely a warranty) is the useful advice — a warranty gives a damages claim but does not put an operating licence in the buyer's hands."},
    {t:"As a warranty that the licence is valid", correct:false,
     why:"A warranty gives a damages claim if a statement proves untrue; it does not solve a licence that legally cannot transfer. The buyer needs the licence actually obtained — a condition precedent."},
    {t:"As an indemnity for any licence-related loss", correct:false,
     why:"An indemnity reimburses a quantifiable loss but does not deliver the operating licence needed to run the business. The correct tool is a CP requiring a fresh licence."},
    {t:"Ignore it — licences always transfer with a business", correct:false,
     why:"In asset deals licences typically do NOT transfer; they are usually entity-specific. Re-licensing is often the critical path to completion, so it cannot be ignored."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2 §C; Intro 2.3, 2.16", takeaway:"Non-transferable licence = a [CP] (fresh application), not a warranty."
},
{
  id:"fa-5-03", lesson:"A.5", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the purpose of a transitional services agreement (TSA) in a carve-out?",
  options:[
    {t:"The seller temporarily provides shared services (IT/HR/finance) to the separated business until it can stand alone", correct:true,
     why:"A carved-out business often relied on the parent's shared back-office. A TSA has the seller continue providing those services (IT, HR, finance, procurement) for a transitional period at an agreed cost, bridging the gap until the buyer can run them independently. It is a signature carve-out document precisely because the separated business is not self-sufficient on day one."},
    {t:"It transfers the target's employees by operation of law", correct:false,
     why:"Automatic employee transfer on a going-concern sale is s 18A, not a TSA. A TSA is about temporary shared-service provision post-completion."},
    {t:"It caps the seller's warranty liability", correct:false,
     why:"Warranty caps sit in the SPA's limitations clause. A TSA governs transitional service provision, not liability limits."},
    {t:"It waives the need for third-party consents", correct:false,
     why:"Consents to novate contracts still have to be obtained; a TSA does not waive them. It only bridges shared operational services during separation."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.3, 2.8", takeaway:"TSA = seller keeps the lights on (shared services) until the carve-out stands alone."
},
{
  id:"fa-5-04", lesson:"A.5", type:"order",
  prompt:"Order these steps in executing an asset/business carve-out.",
  steps:[
    "Define the perimeter (assets, contracts, people, liabilities in vs out)",
    "Sign the business transfer agreement",
    "Obtain third-party consents and fresh licences (conditions)",
    "Consult and transfer employees (s 18A)",
    "Complete the transfer",
    "Run the transitional services agreement"
  ],
  why:"A carve-out follows a logical sequence: first draw the boundary (perimeter), then sign the business transfer agreement, then satisfy the gating items — consents to novate contracts and fresh licence applications — and consult/transfer the employees under s 18A. Completion transfers the perimeter, and the TSA then bridges shared services until the business is self-sufficient.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 2 §C; Intro 2.3", takeaway:"Perimeter → BTA → consents/licences → s 18A → complete → TSA."
},

/* ================= 2.6 — Strategic & Regulated Share Deals ================= */
{
  id:"fa-6-01", lesson:"A.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"On legal due diligence for a STRATEGIC share deal, which clause type is the classic hidden landmine?",
  options:[
    {t:"Change-of-control clauses in material contracts that can be triggered by the share sale", correct:true,
     why:"In a share deal the contracts stay in place, so consents are usually fewer — but a change-of-control clause can treat the share sale as a trigger, giving the counterparty a consent, termination or step-in right. Because the value being bought may sit in exactly those contracts, hunting for change-of-control clauses is a diligence priority; the fix is a consent/waiver, often made a condition precedent."},
    {t:"The governing-law and notices boilerplate", correct:false,
     why:"Boilerplate rarely allocates deal risk. The diligence priority is substantive triggers like change-of-control, not the standard closing clauses."},
    {t:"The recitals", correct:false,
     why:"Recitals set out background and are not operative risk provisions. The landmine is the change-of-control clause."},
    {t:"The counterparts clause", correct:false,
     why:"A counterparts clause merely allows signing in separate copies. It is not a risk trigger; change-of-control clauses are."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 4 §C; Intro 2.6", takeaway:"Share-deal DD: hunt change-of-control clauses in material contracts."
},
{
  id:"fa-6-02", lesson:"A.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The target is a licensed payment-services firm. The buyer will acquire 100% of its shares.",
  prompt:"What is the key completion gate?",
  options:[
    {t:"MAS controller approval on a fit-and-proper basis — a change of controller of a regulated firm needs the regulator's prior approval", correct:true,
     why:"Acquiring control of an MAS-regulated firm (bank, insurer, capital-markets/fund manager, payment institution) requires the incoming controller to be approved on a fit-and-proper basis (integrity, competence, financial soundness). This approval is a mandatory, non-waivable condition to completion — the regulated business cannot change hands without it — so it usually sets the deal's critical path."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer under the Take-Over Code", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer arises for listed targets crossing 30%. The distinctive gate for a regulated firm — listed or not — is MAS controller approval, not the Code."},
    {t:"Stamp duty clearance from IRAS", correct:false,
     why:"Stamp duty is a routine payment, not the completion gate. The gating approval for a regulated firm's change of control is MAS controller approval."},
    {t:"SGX transaction classification", correct:false,
     why:"SGX transaction classification applies when a listed buyer's acquisition is large relative to its size. The regulated-firm gate is MAS controller approval."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 3; Intro 2.14 (Chapter 9)", takeaway:"Regulated target → MAS controller approval (fit-and-proper) as a mandatory CP."
},
{
  id:"fa-6-03", lesson:"A.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A clearance the law requires before completion (e.g. MAS controller approval, or a merger filing in a jurisdiction where filing is compulsory) is best characterised as which kind of condition?",
  options:[
    {t:"A mandatory condition — required by law or a regulator and cannot be waived by the parties", correct:true,
     why:"Mandatory conditions are imposed by law or a regulator (sector-regulator approval, a compulsory merger filing, FDI sign-off) and the parties cannot waive them away. Note that Singapore's own CCCS regime is voluntary: a CCCS clearance is a condition only because the parties agree to make it one. They contrast with protective conditions, which the buyer negotiates for its own benefit (no MAC, warranties true at completion, financing available) and can usually choose to waive."},
    {t:"A protective condition the buyer may waive", correct:false,
     why:"Protective conditions are the buyer's own (waivable) protections. A regulator's approval is a mandatory condition — the parties cannot simply waive a legal requirement."},
    {t:"A condition subsequent that operates after completion", correct:false,
     why:"A regulatory clearance must generally be satisfied BEFORE completion (a condition precedent), not after. A condition subsequent is a different, less common device."},
    {t:"A warranty", correct:false,
     why:"A warranty is a statement of fact giving a damages claim. A required clearance is a condition to completion, not a warranty."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 5 §C; Intro 2.11", takeaway:"Regulator approvals = mandatory (non-waivable) conditions precedent."
},
{
  id:"fa-6-04", lesson:"A.6", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: In a strategic share deal, the buyer inherits the target's historic tax position.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. A share buyer takes the company as it stands, including its tax history and any latent tax exposures. That is why tax due diligence and a tax deed/covenant (a specific indemnity for pre-completion tax) are standard protections in a share deal — the buyer needs cover for liabilities it is inheriting with the entity."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"It is true. Tax history is inherited in a share deal because nothing inside the company moves. (In an asset deal, by contrast, the tax history stays behind with the seller.)"}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2 §E; Intro 2.3, 2.13", takeaway:"Share deal inherits tax history → tax DD + tax deed."
},

/* ================= 2.7 — Multi-Seller, Family & Earn-out Deals ================= */
{
  id:"fa-7-01", lesson:"A.7", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"An earn-out defers part of the price against future performance. Which cluster of issues are its notorious flashpoints?",
  options:[
    {t:"Who controls the business during the period, how the metric is defined and measured, what happens to a departing founder, and whether the deferred pool is secured", correct:true,
     why:"Earn-outs bridge a valuation gap but are dispute-prone: the buyer now owns and controls the business while the seller's payout depends on its performance; the metric can be manipulated unless objective and audited; a founder may leave mid-period; and the seller needs the deferred pool secured (e.g. escrow) to be sure of payment. Drafting must pin down control covenants, the metric, leaver treatment and security."},
    {t:"Stamp duty and GST on each instalment", correct:false,
     why:"Tax is a routine overlay, not the earn-out's core flashpoints. The dispute-drivers are control, metric definition, leaver treatment and security of the deferred pool."},
    {t:"Whether the Take-Over Code applies", correct:false,
     why:"This is a private deal, so the Code does not apply. The earn-out issues are commercial-legal drafting points, not Code points."},
    {t:"The class composition for a scheme meeting", correct:false,
     why:"Scheme classes arise in listed take-privates. An earn-out in a private deal raises control, metric, leaver and security issues instead."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 5; Intro 2.9, 2.17", takeaway:"Earn-out flashpoints: control · metric · leaver · security."
},
{
  id:"fa-7-02", lesson:"A.7", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Due diligence reveals that the target's flagship product content was created by the two founders personally, not by the company. The buyer is paying largely for that content.",
  prompt:"What is the best-practice response package?",
  options:[
    {t:"An IP-ownership warranty to flush out the position, a specific indemnity if a gap is confirmed, and — best of all — a pre-completion assignment of the IP into the company made a condition of closing", correct:true,
     why:"One finding, three instruments. The warranty forces the sellers to state (and thereby disclose) the true ownership position; a specific indemnity covers a confirmed gap pound-for-pound; and the cleanest fix is to require the founders to assign the IP into the company before completion, made a condition of closing so the buyer actually owns what it is paying for. This is the Project Sprouts pattern."},
    {t:"Simply raise the price to reflect the valuable IP", correct:false,
     why:"Paying more for an asset the company may not even own makes the problem worse. The response is to secure ownership (assignment as a CP) backed by warranty and indemnity, not to pay a premium."},
    {t:"Do nothing — IP ownership is a commercial matter", correct:false,
     why:"If the company may not own the core asset, that is a central legal risk, not a mere commercial detail. Doing nothing leaves the value free to walk out with the founders."},
    {t:"Rely on a non-compete alone", correct:false,
     why:"A non-compete stops the founders competing; it does not make the company the owner of the IP. You still need the warranty, indemnity and pre-completion assignment."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 4 §C; Intro 2.6, 2.17", takeaway:"Founder-owned IP → warranty + indemnity + assignment-as-a-CP."
},
{
  id:"fa-7-03", lesson:"A.7", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"With many individual sellers (e.g. a founding family and staff shareholders), which mechanism keeps the deal manageable for the buyer?",
  options:[
    {t:"Joint and several liability plus a sellers' representative (and powers of attorney) to bind the group and give the buyer a single point of recourse", correct:true,
     why:"Multi-seller deals risk fragmenting warranty recourse and signing logistics. The buyer typically seeks joint and several liability so it can claim the full amount from any seller, and the sellers appoint a representative (with powers of attorney) to negotiate, sign and receive notices on their behalf — giving the buyer a single counterparty rather than dozens."},
    {t:"A mandatory general offer to all sellers", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer is a Code mechanism for listed targets. In a private multi-seller deal the tools are joint and several liability and a sellers' representative, not a Code offer."},
    {t:"A scheme of arrangement to bind dissenting sellers", correct:false,
     why:"A scheme is a court process usually for companies with dispersed shareholders; a handful of identifiable private sellers are handled by contract — joint and several liability plus a sellers' representative."},
    {t:"Leaving each seller to negotiate a separate SPA", correct:false,
     why:"Dozens of separate SPAs would be unworkable and inconsistent. A single SPA with joint and several liability and a sellers' representative is the standard solution."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.8, 2.17", takeaway:"Multi-seller: joint & several liability + a sellers' representative."
},
{
  id:"fa-7-04", dia:"consideration", lesson:"A.7", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each consideration-protection tool to its function.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Escrow / retention", r:"Price held back as security for warranty/indemnity claims"},
    {l:"Earn-out", r:"Part of the price contingent on future performance"},
    {l:"Deferred consideration", r:"Part of the price paid later on a date or milestone"},
    {l:"Completion accounts", r:"Price trued up after completion against actual figures"}
  ],
  why:"The consideration toolkit both pays and protects. Escrow/retention holds back money as security for claims; an earn-out makes part of the price contingent on post-completion performance to bridge a valuation gap; deferred consideration simply pays part later; and completion accounts true the price up against actual closing figures. Choosing among them allocates performance and warranty risk between buyer and seller.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 5, Ch 15 §B–D; Intro 2.9", takeaway:"Escrow secures claims; earn-out/deferred split timing & risk; completion accounts true up."
},

/* ================= 2.8 — Consortia, Concert Parties & Minority Stakes ================= */
{
  id:"fa-8-01", dia:"thresholdLadder", lesson:"A.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Two private-equity funds team up to jointly acquire and control a target to which the Take-Over Code applies (a listed company, or an unlisted public company with more than 50 shareholders and NTA of S$5m+). For the Code's control thresholds, how are they treated?",
  options:[
    {t:"As concert parties — persons cooperating to acquire or consolidate control are counted as one, aggregating their holdings", correct:true,
     why:"The concert-party rules aggregate the holdings of those cooperating to obtain or consolidate control, so a consortium cannot slip under a threshold by splitting the stake. This is exactly what catches consortium buyers and bidder-plus-financier groups; the consortium must also regulate its internal relationship (governance, exit, deadlock) by a shareholders' agreement."},
    {t:"As wholly independent investors measured separately", correct:false,
     why:"Treating cooperating acquirers separately is what the concert-party rules exist to prevent. A consortium acting together is counted as one for the thresholds."},
    {t:"As exempt from all thresholds because they are funds", correct:false,
     why:"Fund status confers no exemption from concert-party aggregation. Cooperation to acquire or consolidate control is what matters."},
    {t:"As a single company by operation of law", correct:false,
     why:"They are not fused into one company; rather, their holdings are aggregated for threshold purposes, and their relationship is governed by a consortium shareholders' agreement."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 §D; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Consortium = concert parties: holdings aggregated; govern by an SHA."
},
{
  id:"fa-8-02", lesson:"A.8", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A fund takes a 20% NON-controlling stake in a private company. What is its most important source of protection?",
  options:[
    {t:"A shareholders' agreement conferring reserved matters, a board seat, tag-along, anti-dilution and information rights", correct:true,
     why:"A minority investor cannot rely on its votes alone, so it protects itself contractually through an SHA: reserved matters requiring its consent for key decisions, a board seat, tag-along rights (to sell alongside a majority exit), anti-dilution protection, and information rights. These give a minority influence and an exit it would not have from its shareholding alone."},
    {t:"The Take-Over Code's mandatory-offer rule", correct:false,
     why:"The Code protects public shareholders and is not the source of a private minority's rights. A private minority relies on the SHA it negotiates."},
    {t:"A guarantee that the company will be sold within a year", correct:false,
     why:"No such guarantee exists as a default protection. Exit is addressed through negotiated SHA mechanisms (tag-along, drag, put/call, exit provisions), not an automatic sale."},
    {t:"Automatic board control", correct:false,
     why:"A 20% holder does not have board control. Its influence comes from negotiated SHA rights (reserved matters, a board seat), not control."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 7 §D; Intro 2.8, glossary", takeaway:"Minority stake → protect via SHA: reserved matters, board seat, tag, anti-dilution, info."
},
{
  id:"fa-8-03", lesson:"A.8", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each shareholders'-agreement right to what it achieves.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Drag-along", r:"A majority can force minorities to sell into a deal"},
    {l:"Tag-along", r:"Minorities can join a majority's sale on the same terms"},
    {l:"Reserved matters", r:"Key decisions need the investor's consent"},
    {l:"Anti-dilution", r:"Preserves an investor's percentage/economics on new issues"}
  ],
  why:"An SHA allocates control and exit among co-owners. Drag-along lets a selling majority force minorities to sell (so a buyer can get 100%); tag-along lets minorities join a majority's sale on equal terms (so they are not left behind); reserved matters give an investor a veto over key decisions; and anti-dilution preserves its percentage or economics if new shares are later issued. These are the recurring levers in JV and minority-stake deals.",
  cite:"Intro 2.8, glossary", takeaway:"Drag (force sale) · tag (join sale) · reserved matters (veto) · anti-dilution (protect %)."
},
{
  id:"fa-8-04", lesson:"A.8", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A 15% shareholder has negative control because it can block a special resolution.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Correct. Negative control — the ability to block a special resolution — requires more than 25% of the votes, because special resolutions pass at 75%. A 15% holder falls short of that blocking stake, so it has neither positive nor negative control and must rely on contractual (SHA) protections instead."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Incorrect. Blocking a special resolution needs more than 25% (special resolutions require 75%). A 15% stake cannot block one, so it does not confer negative control."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 14; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Negative control needs >25% — 15% blocks nothing."
}

