window.DC_BANK.gaps2 = (window.DC_BANK.gaps2||[]).concat([

/* ===================== 14K — Fixed vs floating charges ===================== */
{ id:"c14t-K01", lesson:"14K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Romer LJ's three probanda in Re Yorkshire Woolcombers described a floating charge. Which have later cases treated as neither necessary nor sufficient?",
  options:[
    {t:"The first two: a charge over present and future assets of a class that changes in the ordinary course of business", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.073]–[14.074]. The probanda were: (a) a charge on a class of assets present and future; (b) a class that would change from time to time in the ordinary course of business; and (c) until the chargee takes some step, the company may carry on business in the ordinary way. Later decisions show (a) and (b) are 'neither sufficient nor necessary'. A fixed charge over a shifting fund of book debts is possible 'so long as sufficient control is maintained' and the company isn't free to deal with them in the ordinary course. A floating charge also need not cover the whole undertaking. Freedom to deal, the third probandum, is the real marker."},
    {t:"Only the third: freedom to carry on business in the ordinary way", correct:false,
     why:"That is the one that still matters. Freedom to deal is inconsistent with a fixed charge."},
    {t:"All three, so the probanda no longer have any role", correct:false,
     why:"The third, read properly, remains central. It's the first two that are neither necessary nor sufficient."},
    {t:"None: the probanda remain an exhaustive test", correct:false,
     why:"Tjio says the criteria 'are not exhaustive', and later cases have qualified them."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.073]–[14.074]", takeaway:"Probanda (a) and (b) aren't decisive; control and freedom to deal are." },

{ id:"c14t-K02", lesson:"14K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A debenture calls itself a 'fixed charge' over Lark Pte Ltd's trading stock. Lark may sell stock to customers in the ordinary course without asking the lender, but has promised not to grant any other security over it.",
  prompt:"How is the charge likely to be characterised?",
  options:[
    {t:"Floating: the negative pledge restricts Lark, but Lark can still deal with the stock free of the charge, so the lender lacks control", correct:true,
     why:"In Re Cosslett (Contractors) Ltd, Millett LJ said that while unfettered freedom to deal is inconsistent with a fixed charge, 'the opposite did not hold true': a floating charge can, and usually does, carry restrictions such as negative pledges (Tjio [14.075]). His test: 'The essence of a fixed charge is that the charge is on a particular asset or class of assets which the chargor cannot deal with free from the charge without the consent of the chargee ... whether the chargee is in control of the charged assets.' Lark can sell stock without consent, so the lender isn't in control. Tjio adds that the floating charge is the residual category, usually a failed fixed charge, and labels don't decide it."},
    {t:"Fixed, because the parties called it fixed", correct:false,
     why:"Characterisation is a matter of law. Labels are irrelevant where the substance shows freedom to deal (Tjio [14.075], [14.078])."},
    {t:"Fixed, because the negative pledge means Lark can't deal with the stock in the usual way", correct:false,
     why:"This is the 'logical fallacy' Tjio identifies in reading the third probandum: some contractual restrictions don't stop the chargor largely dealing with its assets."},
    {t:"Not a charge at all, just a contractual promise", correct:false,
     why:"There is a security interest. The question is only whether it is fixed or floating."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.075]; Re Cosslett (Contractors) Ltd", takeaway:"Restrictions ≠ control. Can deal without consent → floating." },

{ id:"c14t-K03", lesson:"14K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A shipowner's charterparty gives it a lien on sub-hires and sub-freights due to the charterer. The lien is never registered. The charterer goes into liquidation.",
  prompt:"What did the Court of Appeal decide in Diablo on similar facts?",
  options:[
    {t:"The lien created a floating charge from the point of agreement, so it was void against the liquidator for non-registration under s 131(3)(g)", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.081]: the Court of Appeal in Diablo (CA), with Chong JA giving the judgment of a full court, held that the lien 'was not just an unregistrable contractual device but a floating charge from the point of agreement', void against the liquidator for non-registration under s 131(3)(g). Tjio draws the lesson that 'the default position is also that a floating charge is created': parties can contract as they like, but the legal consequences are those the law recognises, and security must fit a recognised form that may need registration to protect third parties."},
    {t:"It was a fixed charge over identified receivables, so it didn't need registration", correct:false,
     why:"The court held the opposite: it was floating, and all floating charges are registrable under s 131(3)(g)."},
    {t:"It was a purely contractual right, enforceable against the liquidator without registration", correct:false,
     why:"That was the shipowner's argument, and it failed."},
    {t:"It was valid because liens on sub-freights are widely known in shipping", correct:false,
     why:"The court accepted that such liens are widely known, but that didn't excuse registration of what was in law a floating charge."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.081]; Diablo (CA)", takeaway:"Diablo: sub-freight lien = floating charge; unregistered → void vs liquidator." },

{ id:"c14t-K04", lesson:"14K", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Tjio says the reasoning in Spectrum, that you can't have a fixed charge over book debts and a floating charge over their proceeds, applies equally to shares and their dividends.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.077]: after Spectrum, with Re New Bullas Trading overruled, 'One cannot separate the fruits from the trees as a metaphysical contractual matter when the two are inextricably linked in reality. That would be the case with a book debt and its proceeds, but not shares and its dividends.' A book debt's only value is its collection, so control of the proceeds is control of the debt. A share keeps its value after a dividend is paid, so the two can be treated separately."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Tjio expressly distinguishes shares and dividends from book debts and their proceeds."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.077]", takeaway:"Can't split book debts from proceeds; shares from dividends is different." },

/* ===================== 14L — Conceptual basis for the floating charge ===================== */
{ id:"c14t-L01", lesson:"14L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which legal development made the floating charge possible?",
  options:[
    {t:"Equity's recognition that future property could be assigned now, without a fresh act of assignment when the property came into existence", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.082]: 'It was the recognition that future property could be assigned presently without a fresh act of assignment when the property came into existence that assisted the development of the floating charge.' That let a single instrument cover a fluctuating body of assets, such as stock in trade, so a trading company could give security while continuing to trade, which Tjio says 'facilitated corporate growth'."},
    {t:"The Companies Act's registration system", correct:false,
     why:"Registration came to regulate floating charges. It didn't create them. The floating charge is 'a remarkable invention of equity'."},
    {t:"The common law pledge, which requires delivery of possession", correct:false,
     why:"A pledge needs possession, which is the opposite of letting the debtor keep trading with the assets."},
    {t:"Statutory receivership under the IRDA", correct:false,
     why:"Receivership is an enforcement tool. The floating charge's origin lies in equity's treatment of future property."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.082]", takeaway:"Floating charge rests on equity's present assignment of future property." },

{ id:"c14t-L02", lesson:"14L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In Diablo (CA), what did Chong JA say about the nature of the floating charge before crystallisation?",
  options:[
    {t:"It confers an immediate security interest, but a proprietary interest in the charged assets arises only on crystallisation", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.083] quotes Chong JA: 'while a floating charge confers an immediate security interest, the charge enjoys a proprietary interest in the charged assets only after the event of crystallization.' Tjio notes that Singapore courts take 'a pragmatic view' that the floating charge creates an equity similar to a fixed charge, with a licence to the chargor to keep using the assets in the ordinary course, and that this equity, while a security interest, 'may not be proprietary'."},
    {t:"It is a fixed charge over all present assets from the start", correct:false,
     why:"That is one academic theory (a defeasible fixed charge that may be overreached). It isn't how Chong JA put it."},
    {t:"It is a mere contractual promise with no security until crystallisation", correct:false,
     why:"Chong JA said the floating charge confers an immediate security interest."},
    {t:"It is a charge over a notional fund that is a separate legal person", correct:false,
     why:"The notional fund theory treats the fund as having separate existence, but not as a legal person, and it isn't what Diablo adopted."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.083]; Diablo (CA)", takeaway:"Diablo: immediate security, proprietary only on crystallisation." },

{ id:"c14t-L03", lesson:"14L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Wren Pte Ltd, which has given a bank a floating charge over all its assets, sells its main factory machinery to a buyer in a one-off deal well outside its ordinary course of business. The company keeps trading.",
  prompt:"What is the effect on the floating charge?",
  options:[
    {t:"The sale doesn't crystallise the charge as a matter of law; the buyer takes subject to the charge only if it knew the sale was outside the ordinary course", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.084]: crystallisation by law happens when the company stops operating as a going concern, 'and not necessarily with dispositions of charged assets outside the ordinary course of business'. Instead, 'property rules of priority come into play so that the third party takes subject to the prior floating charge if the third party buyer takes the charged property with notice of the fact that it was disposed outside of the ordinary course of business.' The chargeholder can also seek an injunction to stop disposals outside the ordinary course."},
    {t:"The charge crystallises automatically over all of Wren's assets", correct:false,
     why:"Wren is still trading. A disposal outside the ordinary course doesn't crystallise the charge as a matter of law."},
    {t:"The buyer always takes free of the charge, because the charge is floating", correct:false,
     why:"The licence to deal covers only the ordinary course. A buyer with notice that the sale was outside it takes subject to the charge."},
    {t:"The sale is void because a floating charge forbids any disposal", correct:false,
     why:"A floating charge lets the company deal with its assets in the ordinary course. Even outside it, the sale isn't void; priority rules decide the buyer's position."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.084]", takeaway:"Sale outside ordinary course ≠ crystallisation; buyer with notice takes subject." }
]);

;
