/* DealCraft — Deep-dive batch #1
   Tests the most important concepts again from new angles, with longer
   explanations and diagrams (dia field). Concats onto existing family arrays;
   buildIndex dedupes by id. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};

/* ================= FAMILY 0 (core, multi-angle) ================= */
window.DC_BANK.familyA = (window.DC_BANK.familyA||[]).concat([
{ id:"d1-019", lesson:"A.2", type:"mcq", dia:"capitalStack",
  prompt:"In an LBO, why does the sponsor want to 'push down' the acquisition debt onto the target?",
  options:[
    {t:"So the target's own cash flows and assets service and secure the debt, amplifying the sponsor's equity returns", correct:true,
     why:"The economic engine of a leveraged buyout is using a high proportion of debt so that a small slice of sponsor equity controls a large asset — and, if the business performs, equity returns are amplified. To make the debt cheap and available, lenders want it serviced by the target's cash flows and secured on the target's assets, so the sponsor 'pushes the debt down' into the target/BidCo structure. That push-down is exactly what raises financial assistance (s 76) where a public company sits in the chain, because the target is helping fund the purchase of its own shares. The tension between the financing structure lenders want and the s 76 prohibition is the defining legal problem of the leveraged deal."},
    {t:"To avoid paying any interest", correct:false,
     why:"Debt still bears interest wherever it sits; push-down doesn't eliminate interest. It aligns the debt with the cash flows and assets that service and secure it, amplifying equity returns — and triggers the s 76 question."},
    {t:"Because the Take-Over Code requires it", correct:false,
     why:"The Code does not require debt push-down; it is a financing/structuring choice. Its legal significance is the financial-assistance issue, not a Code obligation."},
    {t:"To make the target a public company", correct:false,
     why:"Push-down has nothing to do with making the target public. It concerns where the acquisition debt sits and what secures it."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §E-F; Gaughan Ch 8; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"LBO pushes debt onto the target to amplify returns — which triggers s 76." },

{ id:"d1-020", lesson:"A.5", type:"scenario", dia:"carveOut",
  scenario:"A buyer is carving out one division from a large group: it wants specific contracts, the division's staff, an operating licence, and shared IT currently run by the parent.",
  prompt:"Which set of mechanisms does this require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Novate/assign the contracts (with consents), transfer staff under s 18A, re-apply for the (non-transferable) licence, and put a TSA in place for the shared IT", correct:true,
     why:"A carve-out is boundary-drawing plus plumbing. Each contract in the perimeter must be novated or assigned, which usually needs the counterparty's consent; the division's employees may transfer automatically under s 18A on a going-concern basis, triggering a duty to consult; the operating licence is typically entity-specific and non-transferable, so a fresh application is needed (often the critical path, made a condition precedent); and because the division relied on the parent's shared IT, a transitional services agreement bridges those services until the business can stand alone. Defining the exact perimeter — what's in and out — and sequencing these mechanisms is the core carve-out task."},
    {t:"A single share transfer moves everything automatically", correct:false,
     why:"A share transfer would buy a whole company, not carve one division out of a group — and it wouldn't isolate just the wanted contracts, staff and licence. A carve-out needs the asset-transfer mechanics: novation, s 18A, re-licensing and a TSA."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer and a whitewash", correct:false,
     why:"Those are public-takeover/financial-assistance concepts, irrelevant to a private carve-out. The mechanisms here are novation/consents, s 18A, re-licensing and a TSA."},
    {t:"Only a disclosure letter", correct:false,
     why:"A disclosure letter qualifies warranties; it does not transfer contracts, staff or licences. The carve-out requires the transfer mechanics and a TSA."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 2 §C; Intro 2.3, 2.15", takeaway:"Carve-out mechanics: novate + consent · s 18A · re-license · TSA." },

{ id:"d1-021", lesson:"A.4", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: In an MBO, the managers' inside knowledge of the company is a reason to give them fewer, not more, procedural safeguards.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The managers' superior, non-public knowledge is precisely why MORE safeguards are needed, not fewer. In an MBO the managers sit on both sides — they are fiduciaries of the company yet the buyers of it, and they know more than the sellers while benefiting from a low price. That acute conflict is cured by an independent process: the conflicted managers recuse themselves, an independent committee of disinterested directors runs the sale, an IFA opines on fairness, and the managers abstain from any vote. Treating an MBO as an arm's-length deal because 'management knows best' is the recurring error the safeguards exist to prevent."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Incorrect. Inside knowledge deepens the conflict, so it calls for stronger safeguards — an independent committee, recusal, an IFA opinion and abstention — not weaker ones."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 15 §G; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"MBO inside knowledge deepens the conflict → more safeguards, not fewer." }
]);

/* ================= FAMILY B ================= */
window.DC_BANK.familyB = (window.DC_BANK.familyB||[]).concat([
{ id:"d1-022", lesson:"B.2", type:"scenario", dia:"schemeVsOffer",
  scenario:"An acquirer wants 100% of a listed target or nothing, values timetable certainty, and has the target board's cooperation.",
  prompt:"Which route fits best, and what is the trade-off?",
  options:[
    {t:"A scheme of arrangement — all-or-nothing certainty and apathy helps, but it needs the board's cooperation (friendly only) and court time that makes a price bump awkward", correct:true,
     why:"A scheme delivers exactly what this acquirer wants: it is all-or-nothing (either it passes and binds 100%, or it fails), the outcome is known at the meeting, and apathetic non-voters don't count against the thresholds. The price is that a scheme is company-driven — the board must apply to court — so it only works friendly, and the court timetable makes revising the price mid-process awkward. If the acquirer needed to work hostile or wanted flexibility to bump the price by a quick announcement, an offer would be better (but then apathy hurts, and it can leave a stranded minority below 90%). Matching the route to what the client values — certainty vs flexibility, friendly vs hostile — is the route-choice skill."},
    {t:"A hostile takeover offer, because schemes cannot deliver 100%", correct:false,
     why:"This is backwards: a scheme is the route that delivers 100%-or-nothing, whereas an offer can leave a stranded minority if it falls short of the 90% squeeze-out level. And a scheme suits the friendly, cooperative situation described."},
    {t:"A partial offer, to acquire most of the shares", correct:false,
     why:"A partial offer deliberately seeks less than 100% and needs SIC consent and safeguards — the opposite of this acquirer's 100%-or-nothing objective. A scheme fits."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer under Rule 14", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer is triggered by crossing 30%, not a chosen route to 100%. This friendly, certainty-seeking acquirer wants a scheme."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 13; Intro 2.4, 2.17", takeaway:"Want 100%+certainty & board is friendly → scheme (but harder to bump price)." },

{ id:"d1-023", lesson:"B.3", type:"mcq", dia:"thresholdLadder",
  prompt:"A bidder crosses 30% of a listed target by buying a block on-market. What are the terms it must now offer everyone?",
  options:[
    {t:"A general offer to all shareholders, in cash or with a cash alternative, at the highest price it (or its concert parties) paid in the preceding 6 months, conditional only on securing >50%", correct:true,
     why:"Crossing 30% triggers a mandatory general offer under Rule 14, designed to give every shareholder an exit on a change of control. The terms are rigid and protective: the offer must go to all shareholders of the class; it must be in cash or accompanied by a cash alternative (minorities can't be forced to take paper only); it must be priced at least at the highest price the bidder or its concert parties paid in the look-back period (six months for a mandatory offer); and it can be conditional only on the bidder securing more than 50% — no financing, due-diligence or MAC conditions dressing it up. This rigidity is the price of buying past 30%."},
    {t:"A share-for-share offer at any price the bidder chooses", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer must be in cash or with a cash alternative and priced at least at the highest price paid — the bidder cannot force paper-only at a price of its choosing. The terms are rigid, not discretionary."},
    {t:"An offer only to shareholders holding more than 1%", correct:false,
     why:"Equality of treatment requires the offer go to ALL shareholders of the class. The 1% figure relates to the creep rule, not to who receives the mandatory offer."},
    {t:"Nothing — crossing 30% has no consequences", correct:false,
     why:"Crossing 30% is the effective-control line that triggers a mandatory general offer. It is one of the most consequential thresholds under the Code."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 9 §C, §E; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Cross 30% → mandatory offer: cash/cash-alt, highest 6-mth price, only >50% condition." },

{ id:"d1-024", lesson:"B.7", type:"scenario",
  prompt:"In a scheme, the bidder gives a group of shareholders preferential consideration not offered to the rest. What follows for class composition?",
  scenario:"The preferred group's rights under the scheme differ materially from ordinary holders'.",
  options:[
    {t:"They likely form a separate class, because their rights under the scheme are so dissimilar they cannot consult with the others on a common interest (Re TT International)", correct:true,
     why:"Class composition turns on rights, not merely interests. Where a group receives materially different consideration or treatment under the scheme, their rights differ so much from ordinary holders that they cannot sensibly consult together with a view to a common interest — the Re TT International test — so they must be placed in a separate class and vote separately. Getting this wrong is fatal to the court's jurisdiction to sanction the scheme. (By contrast, holders with the same rights but different motives — e.g. those who gave irrevocable undertakings — stay in the same class; and a bidder-related holder may need its own class, per Re Hellenic.)"},
    {t:"They stay in the same class as everyone else, since class depends on shareholding size", correct:false,
     why:"Class does not depend on size of holding; it depends on dissimilarity of rights. Materially different treatment under the scheme means a separate class, not the same one."},
    {t:"No classes are ever needed in a scheme", correct:false,
     why:"Schemes routinely require careful class construction — separate meetings for members whose rights differ enough that they can't consult together. Ignoring classes risks the court refusing to sanction."},
    {t:"The whole scheme automatically fails", correct:false,
     why:"Preferential treatment doesn't automatically fail the scheme; it requires the preferred group to vote as a separate class. Failure only follows if classes are mis-constituted or a class rejects the scheme."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 13; Re TT International; Intro 2.17", takeaway:"Materially different scheme rights → a separate class (Re TT International)." },

{ id:"d1-025", lesson:"B.8", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Under a takeover offer, a bidder that acquires not less than ____% of the shares to which the offer relates may compulsorily acquire the remaining minority under CA s 215.",
  accept:["90","90%","ninety"], answer:"90%",
  why:"Once a bidder holds not less than 90% of the shares to which the offer relates (excluding shares it already held when the offer was made), it may compulsorily acquire the outstanding minority under s 215 — the squeeze-out. This is why untendered shares hurt a bidder pursuing an offer: every share not accepted eats into the 90% needed. Dissenting minorities can apply to court to challenge the terms, and there is a reciprocal right for a minority to require the bidder to buy them out. Where the bidder cannot reach 90%, it may be left with a stranded minority, which is one reason a scheme (all-or-nothing) is sometimes preferred.",
  cite:"CA s 215; Wan Ch 14 §D", takeaway:"90% of the offer shares unlocks the s 215 squeeze-out." }
]);

/* ================= FAMILIES C–H (targeted multi-angle) ================= */
window.DC_BANK.familyC = (window.DC_BANK.familyC||[]).concat([
{ id:"d1-026", lesson:"C.1", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why might parties prefer a statutory amalgamation over an asset transfer to combine two businesses?",
  options:[
    {t:"Amalgamation vests all property, rights and liabilities in the surviving company by operation of law — no need to novate each contract or re-apply for licences", correct:true,
     why:"The great advantage of a statutory amalgamation is universal succession: once the Registrar issues the notice of amalgamation, the amalgamated company automatically takes all the property, rights and liabilities of each constituent company by operation of law. That avoids the asset-deal grind of conveying each asset, novating each contract (with consents) and re-applying for licences. Approval is by special resolution of each company plus directors' solvency statements, and — unlike a scheme — no court sanction is required, so timing is more certain. The trade-offs are that the surviving company inherits ALL liabilities (there is no cherry-picking a clean perimeter) and creditors have safeguards (solvency statements and challenge rights). Where the goal is a full combination rather than a selective purchase, amalgamation is often cleaner than an asset transfer."},
    {t:"Because amalgamation lets the buyer leave unwanted liabilities behind", correct:false,
     why:"That is the asset deal's feature, not amalgamation's. Amalgamation succeeds to ALL liabilities by operation of law — you cannot cherry-pick a clean perimeter. Its advantage is automatic universal succession, not liability-shedding."},
    {t:"Because amalgamation requires no shareholder approval", correct:false,
     why:"A standard amalgamation needs a special resolution (75%) of each amalgamating company plus solvency statements. Its advantage over a scheme is avoiding court sanction, not avoiding the shareholder vote."},
    {t:"Because it triggers a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"Triggering a mandatory offer would be a burden, not a reason to choose amalgamation. Amalgamation is chosen for automatic succession without court sanction."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 13 §D-F; CA ss 215A-215K; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Amalgamation = automatic universal succession, no court sanction (but inherits all liabilities)." }
]);

window.DC_BANK.familyE = (window.DC_BANK.familyE||[]).concat([
{ id:"d1-027", lesson:"E.2", type:"mcq", dia:"capitalStack",
  prompt:"A viable but over-leveraged company needs breathing space and new funding to restructure. Which IRDA tools fit, in what roles?",
  options:[
    {t:"A moratorium (s 64) stays enforcement to buy time; rescue financing (s 67) gives new money super-priority; a cross-class cram-down (s 70) can bind a dissenting class to the plan", correct:true,
     why:"Singapore's IRDA rescue framework supplies complementary tools. A moratorium (s 64) freezes creditor enforcement and proceedings, giving the company a court-supervised breathing space to negotiate a scheme. Rescue financing (s 67) makes new funding available by granting it super-priority (even over existing secured debt in some cases), because no one lends into distress without priority. And a cross-class cram-down (s 70) lets the court approve a plan over a dissenting class if at least one class approved, creditors as a whole meet the majority-in-number and 75%-in-value test, and the plan is fair and equitable to the dissenters, so a single out-of-the-money class cannot hold the rescue hostage. These are external statutory tools — cite the IRDA, not Wan. Together they let a viable business restructure its balance sheet while continuing to trade."},
    {t:"Only liquidation is available for an over-leveraged company", correct:false,
     why:"Liquidation is for unviable businesses; a viable but over-leveraged company uses the IRDA rescue tools — moratorium, rescue financing and cram-down — to restructure and keep trading, not to be wound up."},
    {t:"A Take-Over Code offer to creditors", correct:false,
     why:"The Code governs acquisitions of listed-company control, not creditor restructurings. Creditors are dealt with through schemes and the IRDA rescue tools, not a Code offer."},
    {t:"A mandatory buy-back of all debt at par", correct:false,
     why:"There is no mechanism forcing repayment of all debt at par in distress — that would defeat the restructuring. The tools are a moratorium, rescue financing and cram-down under the IRDA."}
  ],
  cite:"IRDA ss 64,67,70 (external); Intro 2.14", takeaway:"IRDA rescue: moratorium (time) + rescue financing (money) + cram-down (bind dissenters)." }
]);

window.DC_BANK.familyG = (window.DC_BANK.familyG||[]).concat([
{ id:"d1-028", lesson:"G.1", type:"scenario", dia:"mergerControl",
  scenario:"Two of the three main suppliers in a Singapore market plan to merge. Their internal papers tout 'pricing power' from reduced competition.",
  prompt:"What is the competition-law analysis and the document risk?",
  options:[
    {t:"The merger risks a substantial lessening of competition (s 54), so CCCS clearance should be considered; and the 'pricing power' papers are dangerous 'hot documents' that evidence the harm", correct:true,
     why:"Reducing three suppliers to two in a concentrated market is a textbook substantial-lessening-of-competition (SLC) concern under s 54 — the CCCS would look at market shares, closeness of competition, entry barriers and any efficiencies. Although notification in Singapore is voluntary, the parties should assess the SLC risk and consider notifying, because an un-notified anticompetitive merger can be investigated and unwound with penalties. Worse, the internal papers boasting of 'pricing power' are exactly the 'hot documents' a regulator seizes on — they evidence an intent to lessen competition and can sink the deal or shape remedies. This is why deal rationale must be documented with care, and why a clean-team protocol governs any exchange of sensitive competitor data during diligence."},
    {t:"There is no issue because notification is voluntary", correct:false,
     why:"Voluntary notification does not mean no substantive risk: an anticompetitive merger can still be investigated and unwound even if not notified. The SLC risk here is real, and the 'pricing power' documents make it worse."},
    {t:"Only the Take-Over Code applies", correct:false,
     why:"The Code governs control of listed companies; competition harm is governed by the Competition Act and CCCS. A 3-to-2 merger with pricing-power rationale is a merger-control problem, not a Code one."},
    {t:"The papers help the deal by showing commercial logic", correct:false,
     why:"Papers touting reduced competition and pricing power hurt, not help — they are precisely the evidence a competition regulator uses to establish an SLC. Rationale documents must be written with competition risk in mind."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [9.93]-[9.94]; Competition Act s 54; Intro 2.2, 2.6", takeaway:"3-to-2 merger = SLC risk; 'pricing power' papers are hot documents." }
]);

window.DC_BANK.familyH = (window.DC_BANK.familyH||[]).concat([
{ id:"d1-029", lesson:"H.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A foreign group wants to acquire 25% of a Singapore-incorporated bank.",
  prompt:"Why can this NOT be treated like an ordinary 25% stake in a normal company?",
  options:[
    {t:"Bank thresholds bite far below the Code's 30%: prior approval is needed to become a 5% substantial shareholder, a 12% or 20% controller, so a 25% stake needs fit-and-proper approval before completion", correct:true,
     why:"For financial institutions, the change-of-control gate is prudential, not just corporate. For a Singapore-incorporated bank, Banking Act ss 15A–15B require prior approval (by the Minister, administered by MAS) before a person becomes a substantial shareholder (5%), a 12% controller, a 20% controller or an indirect controller. A 25% stake crosses all three shareholding gates, well under the Take-Over Code's 30% line. So the acquirer must be approved on a fit-and-proper basis — assessed for integrity, competence and financial soundness — as a mandatory, non-waivable condition to completion. Thinking only in Code thresholds (and assuming 25% is 'just a blocking stake') would miss the MAS approval entirely, which is often the deal's critical path. This is why Chapter 9 layers the regulatory spine on top of the ordinary Chapter 2 share-deal analysis."},
    {t:"Because 25% triggers a mandatory offer under the Code", correct:false,
     why:"The Code's mandatory-offer trigger is 30%, not 25%. The reason a 25% bank stake is special is the Banking Act controller regime (approval at 5%, 12% and 20%), not the Code."},
    {t:"Because banks cannot be acquired by anyone", correct:false,
     why:"Banks can be acquired, but a change of control requires MAS approval of a fit-and-proper controller. The point is the approval gate, not an outright prohibition."},
    {t:"Because 25% is negative control, which is banned", correct:false,
     why:"Negative control (blocking special resolutions above 25%) is a general company-law concept and is not 'banned'. The FI-specific point is that approval is needed at 5%, 12% and 20% for a bank."}
  ],
  cite:"Banking Act ss 15A–15B [external]; Wan Ch 3; Intro 2.14", takeaway:"Bank: approval at 5% / 12% / 20% (+ indirect) — far below the Code's 30%." }
]);

;
/* ---- data/deep2.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Deep-dive batch #2. Multi-angle re-tests + diagrams across all
   families. Concats onto existing arrays; buildIndex dedupes by id. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};

window.DC_BANK.familyA = (window.DC_BANK.familyA||[]).concat([
{ id:"d2-006", lesson:"A.1", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which clause of the SPA is the primary home of the risk-shifting the parties fight over?",
  options:[
    {t:"The consideration/price, conditions, warranties, limitations and indemnities clauses (roughly clauses 3–8)", correct:true,
     why:"Reading an SPA is reading a risk-allocation document, and clauses 3–8 of the skeleton — consideration and price mechanism, conditions, interim covenants, warranties, limitations on liability, and indemnities — are almost entirely about who carries which risk. That is where the hardest negotiation happens. Parties and recitals, restrictive covenants, completion mechanics and boilerplate matter, but the core tug-of-war is in the price-and-protection middle."},
    {t:"The notices boilerplate", correct:false,
     why:"Boilerplate (notices, governing law) rarely allocates deal risk. The contested risk-shifting lives in consideration, conditions, warranties, limitations and indemnities."},
    {t:"The recitals", correct:false,
     why:"Recitals set background and are not operative risk provisions. The negotiation centres on price and the warranty/indemnity/limitations package."},
    {t:"The counterparts clause", correct:false,
     why:"A counterparts clause merely allows signing in separate copies. It's not where risk is allocated; clauses 3–8 are."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.8", takeaway:"SPA risk-shifting lives in price + conditions + warranties + limitations + indemnities." },
{ id:"d2-007", lesson:"A.6", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A required MAS controller approval and a compulsory overseas merger-control filing are best classified as which kind of condition?",
  options:[
    {t:"Mandatory conditions — imposed by law/a regulator and not waivable by the parties", correct:true,
     why:"Mandatory conditions are those the law or a regulator requires — a sector-regulator approval (MAS), a compulsory merger filing, or an FDI sign-off — and the parties cannot simply waive them away. They contrast with protective conditions (no MAC, warranties true at completion, financing available), which the buyer negotiates for itself and can usually choose to waive. Because mandatory conditions gate completion and take time, they often set the deal's critical path. (Singapore's CCCS notification is voluntary, so a CCCS clearance CP exists by agreement rather than by law.)"},
    {t:"Protective conditions the buyer may waive", correct:false,
     why:"Regulator approvals cannot be waived by the parties. Protective conditions are the buyer's own (waivable) protections; regulatory clearances are mandatory."},
    {t:"Warranties", correct:false,
     why:"A warranty is a statement of fact giving a damages claim. A required clearance is a condition to completion, not a warranty."},
    {t:"Boilerplate", correct:false,
     why:"Regulatory approvals are substantive mandatory conditions, not standard closing boilerplate."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 5 §C; Intro 2.11", takeaway:"Regulator approvals = mandatory (non-waivable) conditions precedent." },
{ id:"d2-008", lesson:"A.8", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each shareholders'-agreement right to what it protects for a minority investor.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Reserved matters", r:"A veto over key decisions"},
    {l:"Tag-along", r:"The right to sell alongside a majority exit"},
    {l:"Anti-dilution", r:"Preservation of percentage/economics on new issues"},
    {l:"Information rights", r:"Access to accounts and management information"}
  ],
  why:"A minority can't rely on its votes, so it protects itself contractually via an SHA. Reserved matters give a veto over key decisions; tag-along lets it sell on the same terms when the majority exits; anti-dilution preserves its stake/economics if new shares are issued; and information rights secure access to accounts and management information. These converted into an SHA are what make a minority stake safe to hold.",
  cite:"Wan Ch 7 §D; Intro 2.8", takeaway:"Minority SHA kit: reserved matters · tag-along · anti-dilution · information rights." }
]);

window.DC_BANK.familyB = (window.DC_BANK.familyB||[]).concat([
{ id:"d2-009", lesson:"B.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Facing a hostile bid, a target board wants to issue a large block of new shares to a friendly party to dilute the bidder.",
  prompt:"Can the board do this under the Code?",
  options:[
    {t:"No — that is frustrating action; the board generally cannot take steps to defeat a bona fide offer without shareholder approval", correct:true,
     why:"The Code reserves the decision on an offer to shareholders, so once a bona fide offer is imminent or made, the board cannot take frustrating action — such as issuing shares to dilute a bidder or disposing of key assets — without shareholder approval. This is why US-style unilateral defences (poison pills) are effectively unavailable to Singapore companies. The board's proper role is to give a reasoned recommendation (aided by the IFA), not to block shareholders from deciding."},
    {t:"Yes — the board may deploy any defence it thinks is in the company's interests", correct:false,
     why:"Unlike the US, the Singapore Code does not leave defence to board discretion. Frustrating action needs shareholder approval; the board can't unilaterally dilute a bidder."},
    {t:"Yes — poison pills are standard in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"Poison pills are a US (Delaware) device, effectively out of bounds in Singapore because of the no-frustrating-action principle. This defence is not permitted without shareholder approval."},
    {t:"Only if the bidder consents", correct:false,
     why:"Bidder consent is not the test; shareholder approval is. Frustrating action requires the target's shareholders to approve, not the bidder."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 8 §C, §E; Intro 2.15", takeaway:"No frustrating action without shareholder approval — the choice is shareholders'." },
{ id:"d2-010", lesson:"B.5", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"During an offer period, why must offer parties disclose their dealings in the target's shares?",
  options:[
    {t:"To keep the market informed and prevent a false market — supporting equality of treatment and no-false-market principles", correct:true,
     why:"The Code requires parties to an offer and their concert parties to make opening position disclosures and disclose their dealings, so the market can see stake-building and price-relevant activity as the contest unfolds. This transparency supports the 'no false market' and 'equality of treatment' principles and deters secret accumulation. It sits alongside the SFA's substantial-shareholder (5%) disclosure regime and the prohibition on insider dealing."},
    {t:"Because dealing is banned during offers", correct:false,
     why:"Parties may generally still deal (subject to restrictions and insider-trading law) — but they must disclose it. The rule is transparency, not a blanket dealing ban."},
    {t:"To calculate stamp duty", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure is about market transparency, not tax. It prevents a false market and supports equality of treatment."},
    {t:"Only after the offer closes", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure must be contemporaneous during the offer (daily), not deferred, so shareholders can react on an informed basis."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 7", takeaway:"Offer-period dealings must be disclosed → no false market, equality." },
{ id:"d2-011", lesson:"B.11", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A listed target's share price spikes on heavy volume amid rumours that a takeover is being discussed, though nothing is agreed.",
  prompt:"What is required?",
  options:[
    {t:"A holding announcement — telling the market talks are under way, to prevent a false market, without yet committing to a firm offer", correct:true,
     why:"Untoward price/volume movement or a leak signals that information may have escaped, risking a false market in the shares. The response is a holding announcement: the market is told that discussions are taking place, without committing to a firm offer, preserving market integrity while the parties continue. If and when a firm intention to make an offer crystallises, a formal offer announcement follows with the required terms."},
    {t:"Nothing until the deal completes", correct:false,
     why:"Waiting until completion would leave a false market running for months. Unusual price/volume movement or a leak triggers a holding announcement now."},
    {t:"An immediate mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer is triggered by crossing 30%, not by rumours. The response to a price spike/leak is a holding announcement."},
    {t:"Suspension of the directors", correct:false,
     why:"Directors aren't suspended for a price spike. The Code response is a holding announcement to prevent a false market."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 4 §E; Ch 16", takeaway:"Price/volume spike or leak → holding announcement (prevent a false market)." },
{ id:"d2-012", lesson:"B.13", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which enforcement tool is distinctive to the SIC's administration of the Code?",
  options:[
    {t:"'Cold-shouldering' — directing that market professionals not act for a person who won't comply with the Code", correct:true,
     why:"Because the Code is administered rather than litigated, the SIC's sanctions are reputational and market-based: public reprimand and, most distinctively, 'cold-shouldering', where market professionals are directed not to act for an offender for a period. That effectively locks a non-complier out of the market. Criminal market-misconduct offences (like insider dealing) are separate, prosecuted under the SFA by the authorities — not imposed by the SIC itself."},
    {t:"Imposing prison sentences directly", correct:false,
     why:"The SIC does not impose criminal penalties; those are prosecuted under the SFA. The SIC's distinctive tool is cold-shouldering (plus reprimand and rulings)."},
    {t:"Setting the offer price", correct:false,
     why:"The SIC does not set prices. It enforces Code compliance via rulings and sanctions like cold-shouldering."},
    {t:"Winding up the company", correct:false,
     why:"Winding up is an insolvency remedy, not an SIC enforcement tool. The SIC uses reprimand and cold-shouldering."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 16 §B", takeaway:"SIC's signature sanction: cold-shouldering (market lock-out)." }
]);

window.DC_BANK.familyC = (window.DC_BANK.familyC||[]).concat([
{ id:"d2-013", lesson:"C.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A small listed company will acquire a private business many times its own size, and the target's owners will end up controlling the enlarged listed entity.",
  prompt:"What is this and what follows?",
  options:[
    {t:"A reverse takeover (RTO) — treated almost like a new listing, requiring shareholder approval and re-compliance with admission standards", correct:true,
     why:"When a listed issuer acquires a business so large (relative to its size) that the target effectively reverses into the listed shell — with the target's owners ending up in control — SGX transaction classification treats it as a reverse takeover. An RTO is handled almost like a fresh listing: it needs shareholder approval and the enlarged group must meet listing admission requirements. This protects the listed buyer's shareholders and the market from a back-door listing."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer under Rule 14", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14 concerns a bidder crossing 30% of a target. Here the listed company is the acquirer; the issue is SGX transaction classification (an RTO), not a mandatory offer."},
    {t:"A whitewash", correct:false,
     why:"A whitewash waives a mandatory offer or cures financial assistance. It is unrelated to an oversized acquisition by a listed buyer, which is an RTO."},
    {t:"A capital reduction", correct:false,
     why:"A capital reduction adjusts share capital. An acquisition large enough to reverse into the shell is an RTO, treated like a new listing."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 6 §D; Intro 2.4", takeaway:"Target reverses into a listed shell → RTO (≈ new listing)." },
{ id:"d2-014", lesson:"C.4", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why is a PIPE commonly used in a de-SPAC?",
  options:[
    {t:"To top up funding and cover redemptions, since SPAC shareholders can redeem their shares and reduce the cash available", correct:true,
     why:"SPAC shareholders typically have redemption rights, so when a business combination is announced, some redeem their shares for escrowed cash, shrinking the funds the combined company will receive. A PIPE — private investment in public equity raised from third parties alongside the de-SPAC — tops up the capital and covers those redemptions, giving certainty that enough money reaches the target. It is a standard feature of de-SPAC financing."},
    {t:"To pay the SPAC's IPO underwriters", correct:false,
     why:"Underwriting fees are a cost of the earlier IPO, not the purpose of a PIPE. A PIPE funds the de-SPAC and covers redemptions."},
    {t:"To trigger a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"A PIPE is a funding mechanism, not a Code trigger. Its role is topping up capital against redemptions in the de-SPAC."},
    {t:"To liquidate the SPAC", correct:false,
     why:"Liquidation occurs only if no combination completes. A PIPE supports a successful de-SPAC, the opposite of liquidation."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 17 §F; Intro glossary", takeaway:"PIPE tops up de-SPAC funding and covers redemptions." }
]);

window.DC_BANK.familyD = (window.DC_BANK.familyD||[]).concat([
{ id:"d2-015", lesson:"D.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A REIT's sponsor proposes to sell one of its buildings to the REIT it manages, at a price the sponsor-controlled manager has approved.",
  prompt:"What safeguards must apply?",
  options:[
    {t:"The interested-person-transaction safeguards — independent valuation, an IFA opinion, and approval by independent unitholders with the interested party abstaining", correct:true,
     why:"An asset injection from the sponsor into the REIT is a classic interested-person transaction: the sponsor is effectively on both sides, and the sponsor-controlled manager's approval cannot cure that conflict. The IPT framework requires independent valuation of the asset, an IFA opinion that the deal is on normal commercial terms and not prejudicial to unitholders, and approval by independent unitholders with the interested party abstaining. These protect unitholders from the sponsor selling assets in at inflated prices."},
    {t:"None — the manager's approval is sufficient", correct:false,
     why:"The manager is sponsor-controlled, so its approval cannot neutralise the conflict. Independent valuation, IFA opinion and independent-unitholder approval are required."},
    {t:"Only SIC consent", correct:false,
     why:"The SIC administers the Code; an asset injection is governed by the IPT framework (SGX rules), not the Code. IPT safeguards apply."},
    {t:"An automatic prohibition — sponsors can never sell to their REIT", correct:false,
     why:"Such sales are permitted but regulated as IPTs with safeguards. They are not banned outright; they must clear valuation, IFA and unitholder approval."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 6 §D, Ch 12; Intro glossary (IPT)", takeaway:"Sponsor→REIT asset sale = IPT: valuation + IFA + independent unitholder approval." },
{ id:"d2-016", lesson:"D.1", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: In a business trust, the manager and trustee are two separate entities, exactly as in a REIT.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"In a business trust the two roles are fused into a single trustee-manager that both holds the assets and runs the business, whereas a REIT keeps a separate manager and trustee. Business trusts are governed by the Business Trusts Act; REITs are collective investment schemes under the SFA. Knowing which vehicle you're in matters for who owes duties and how a deal is approved."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Incorrect. A business trust has a single trustee-manager (the roles are fused); it is the REIT that separates manager and trustee."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 8 §G; Intro 2.7", takeaway:"Business trust = one trustee-manager; REIT = separate manager + trustee." }
]);

window.DC_BANK.familyE = (window.DC_BANK.familyE||[]).concat([
{ id:"d2-017", lesson:"E.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Weeks before a company slid into insolvency, it transferred a valuable property to a related party for well below market value.",
  prompt:"What is the risk to that transaction?",
  options:[
    {t:"It may be clawed back as a transaction at an undervalue — a liquidator/JM can apply to court to unwind it", correct:true,
     why:"A transfer for significantly less than the value given, made within the statutory look-back period before insolvency and while the company was insolvent, is a transaction at an undervalue that a liquidator or judicial manager can ask the court to reverse. This protects the general body of creditors from assets being spirited out cheaply on the eve of collapse. It is exactly why a buyer of assets from a financially shaky seller must weigh clawback risk, not just warranties. [external — IRDA transaction-avoidance provisions.]"},
    {t:"Nothing — completed transfers are always safe", correct:false,
     why:"Completed transfers are not immune: undervalue transactions and unfair preferences within the look-back period can be unwound by the court. Clawback risk is real in distress."},
    {t:"It triggers a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer is a Code concept for listed-company control, unrelated to insolvency clawback. The risk here is transaction-avoidance under the IRDA."},
    {t:"It is protected because it was to a related party", correct:false,
     why:"Being related-party makes it more suspect, not less — related-party undervalues are prime clawback targets. It is squarely at risk of being unwound."}
  ],
  cite:"IRDA (external); Intro glossary (clawback)", takeaway:"Pre-insolvency undervalue transfer → clawback risk (court can unwind)." },
{ id:"d2-018", lesson:"E.1", type:"mcq", dia:"capitalStack",
  prompt:"On a restructuring, which claimants typically convert into the new equity?",
  options:[
    {t:"The holders of the 'fulcrum' — the layer where enterprise value runs out", correct:true,
     why:"Value 'breaks' at the point in the capital stack where enterprise value is exhausted. Claims senior to that point are paid; claims junior get little or nothing and are usually wiped; and the fulcrum holders — just in the money — convert their debt into the post-restructuring equity. That is why identifying the fulcrum tells you who really controls the restructuring, and why distressed investors pursue 'loan-to-own' by buying the fulcrum debt cheaply."},
    {t:"The most senior secured lenders, who are already fully paid", correct:false,
     why:"Senior secured lenders above the fulcrum are typically paid in full and don't need to convert to equity. It's the fulcrum layer — where value runs out — that converts."},
    {t:"The existing ordinary shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Existing equity is usually the first to be wiped in a restructuring, not the recipient of new equity. The fulcrum debt holders take the equity."},
    {t:"Trade creditors, always", correct:false,
     why:"Trade creditors are unsecured and their fate depends on where they sit relative to the fulcrum. The fulcrum layer, wherever it falls, is what converts to equity."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11-12; Intro glossary", takeaway:"Fulcrum holders convert to the new equity on a restructuring." }
]);

window.DC_BANK.familyF = (window.DC_BANK.familyF||[]).concat([
{ id:"d2-019", lesson:"F.1", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A parent wants to separate a division but raise NO cash and give the business to its existing shareholders. Which route?",
  options:[
    {t:"A spin-off — distribute the subsidiary's shares pro-rata to existing shareholders, creating two independent companies", correct:true,
     why:"A spin-off distributes the subsidiary's shares directly to the parent's existing shareholders, so the group splits into two separately-owned companies and no cash is raised. The rationale is usually focus — letting each business be valued and managed on its own. It contrasts with a divestiture (sell for cash) and an equity carve-out (IPO a minority stake for cash while keeping control)."},
    {t:"A divestiture (trade sale)", correct:false,
     why:"A divestiture sells the business for cash — the opposite of the 'no cash, give to shareholders' objective. A spin-off distributes shares."},
    {t:"An equity carve-out IPO", correct:false,
     why:"A carve-out IPOs a minority stake to raise cash and keep control. It doesn't give the business to existing shareholders for no cash; a spin-off does."},
    {t:"A mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory offer is a Code control-acquisition trigger, not a separation route. The no-cash-to-shareholders route is a spin-off."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11; Intro glossary", takeaway:"No cash, shares to existing holders → spin-off." },
{ id:"d2-020", lesson:"F.5", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does a seller running a competitive auction generally resist granting early exclusivity to one bidder?",
  options:[
    {t:"Exclusivity removes the competitive tension that drives price; the seller keeps bidders competing as long as possible", correct:true,
     why:"A seller's leverage in an auction comes from keeping several credible bidders in play, competing on price and terms. Granting exclusivity stops the seller shopping the deal and hands the chosen bidder the upper hand, so sellers resist it — granting it, if at all, only late to a preferred bidder to finalise. Bidders push for the opposite (early exclusivity) precisely to escape the auction dynamic."},
    {t:"Exclusivity is illegal under the Take-Over Code", correct:false,
     why:"Exclusivity is a commercial term, not generally illegal. The seller resists it to preserve price tension, not because of a Code prohibition."},
    {t:"Because exclusivity increases stamp duty", correct:false,
     why:"Exclusivity has nothing to do with tax. Sellers resist it to keep bidders competing on price."},
    {t:"Because bidders never want exclusivity", correct:false,
     why:"Bidders frequently want exclusivity to avoid competition. The seller resists precisely because granting it kills the auction tension."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan Ch 4 §G; Intro 2.5", takeaway:"Sellers resist early exclusivity — it kills auction price tension." }
]);

window.DC_BANK.familyG = (window.DC_BANK.familyG||[]).concat([
{ id:"d2-021", lesson:"G.2", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Merging parties who compete want to share detailed pricing data in due diligence. What is the safe approach?",
  options:[
    {t:"Use a clean team — ring-fenced outside advisers review the sensitive data without passing it to the principals", correct:true,
     why:"Direct exchange of granular pricing/customer data between competitors risks a Competition Act s 34 information-exchange breach and 'gun-jumping' (acting like a merged entity before clearance). A clean team of outside advisers reviews the data and reports only aggregated or permissible conclusions to the principals, allowing diligence to proceed while insulating decision-makers from the sensitive information. An NDA alone does not solve the competition-law risk."},
    {t:"Share everything freely once an NDA is signed", correct:false,
     why:"An NDA addresses confidentiality, not competition law. Direct competitor data exchange can still breach s 34 / be gun-jumping despite an NDA — hence clean teams."},
    {t:"Publish the data publicly", correct:false,
     why:"Publishing sensitive data would harm the business and worsen the problem. The safe route is ring-fencing via a clean team."},
    {t:"Wait until after completion", correct:false,
     why:"Diligence needs access before signing; the safe route now is a clean team, not deferring diligence until after closing (when it's moot)."}
  ],
  cite:"Competition Act s 34; Intro 2.6", takeaway:"Competitor data in DD → clean team, not principal-to-principal." },
{ id:"d2-022", lesson:"G.3", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A regulator will clear a merger only if the parties address the overlap. Which is a STRUCTURAL remedy?",
  options:[
    {t:"Divesting the overlapping business or assets to a suitable purchaser", correct:true,
     why:"Structural remedies change the market structure permanently — most commonly by divesting an overlapping business or assets to a suitable buyer, removing the competition concern at source. Regulators usually prefer them because they are clean and need little ongoing monitoring. Behavioural remedies, by contrast, constrain conduct over time (e.g. supply/access commitments, price caps) and require supervision. Which is acceptable shapes whether and how a problematic merger can clear."},
    {t:"A promise to keep prices low for five years", correct:false,
     why:"A price commitment is a behavioural remedy (constraining conduct), not a structural one. Structural remedies alter market structure via divestiture."},
    {t:"Agreeing to supply rivals on fair terms", correct:false,
     why:"A supply/access commitment is behavioural, not structural. The structural remedy is divesting the overlapping business."},
    {t:"Publishing an annual compliance report", correct:false,
     why:"Reporting is a monitoring/behavioural element, not a structural fix. Structural = divestiture."}
  ],
  cite:"CCCS guidelines; Intro (Chapter 8)", takeaway:"Structural remedy = divestiture; behavioural = conduct commitments." }
]);

window.DC_BANK.familyH = (window.DC_BANK.familyH||[]).concat([
{ id:"d2-023", lesson:"H.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A buyer is acquiring a bank's retail-deposit business with millions of customer accounts.",
  prompt:"How are those customer contracts moved efficiently?",
  options:[
    {t:"By a statutory transfer-of-business scheme under the Banking Act, which moves assets, liabilities and customer contracts by operation of law on court/MAS approval", correct:true,
     why:"Novating millions of customer contracts individually is impossible, so the Banking Act provides a statutory transfer scheme: on court and MAS approval, the regulated business — assets, liabilities and customer contracts — moves by operation of law. This is the practical mechanism for a bank carve-out or business transfer, mirrored by the Insurance Act for insurance portfolios. It is an [external] regime — cite the statute, not Wan."},
    {t:"By asking each customer to sign a novation", correct:false,
     why:"Individually novating millions of accounts is impractical — precisely what the statutory transfer scheme avoids by moving the business by operation of law."},
    {t:"By a Take-Over Code offer", correct:false,
     why:"A Code offer acquires shares of a listed company; it doesn't transfer a banking business with its customer contracts. A statutory transfer scheme does."},
    {t:"By a scheme of arrangement between shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"A members' scheme concerns shareholders, not the transfer of a customer book. The Banking Act statutory transfer scheme is the mechanism."}
  ],
  cite:"Banking Act (external); Intro 2.15, 2.17", takeaway:"Bank customer book moves via a Banking Act statutory transfer scheme." },
{ id:"d2-024", lesson:"H.3", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A bank target can freely hand over full, identifiable customer files to the buyer during due diligence.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Banking secrecy (Banking Act s 47) protects customer information, so a bank generally cannot disclose identifiable customer files in DD. Diligence must instead rely on aggregated or anonymised data, tightly-controlled sampling, clean-team review, or specific statutory gateways. This is a real constraint on assessing asset quality that must be planned around and reflected in warranties, indemnities and price. [external]"},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Incorrect. Banking secrecy squarely restricts disclosure of customer information; handing over full customer files would breach s 47. Aggregated data and clean teams are used instead."}
  ],
  cite:"Banking Act s 47 (external); Wan Ch 16", takeaway:"Banking secrecy blocks raw customer files in DD — use aggregated data / clean team." }
]);

