{ id:"d4-018", lesson:"G.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A foreign buyer targets a company in a sector subject to a statutory foreign-ownership cap.",
  prompt:"What must the structuring achieve?",
  options:[
    {t:"Compliance with the cap — e.g. a capped stake or a qualifying local partner keeps foreign ownership within the limit", correct:true,
     why:"Sector-specific foreign-ownership caps (and requirements like bumiputera equity in some jurisdictions) limit how much a foreign acquirer may hold. The deal must be structured to stay within the cap — taking a permitted minority stake, partnering with a qualifying local holder, or using structures that respect the ownership limits. Breaching the cap can invalidate the acquisition or its licences. These are [external] sector rules, separate from merger control and SIRA."},
    {t:"Nothing — such caps are never enforced", correct:false,
     why:"Foreign-ownership caps are real, enforced sector limits. Structuring must comply; breach can void the deal or licences."},
    {t:"A Take-Over Code whitewash", correct:false,
     why:"A whitewash waives a mandatory offer — unrelated to ownership caps. Compliance is achieved by structuring within the cap."},
    {t:"An IRDA moratorium", correct:false,
     why:"A moratorium is a distressed-company tool. Foreign-ownership caps are complied with by structuring ownership, not insolvency mechanisms."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.14 (external, Chapter 8)", takeaway:"Foreign-equity cap → structure within the limit (capped stake / local partner)." }
