/* ---------- 6.7 — US Comparative ---------- */
{
  id:"ae-7-01", lesson:"E.7", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A scheme crams down a dissenting class of unsecured creditors, paying them 30% of their claims, while existing shareholders keep 20% of the equity with no new contribution.",
  prompt:"What problem does this raise, in US terms and under s 70?",
  options:[
    {t:"It breaches the absolute priority principle: a junior interest (existing equity) keeps value while the senior dissenting class is not paid in full, which s 70(4) also restricts for unsecured dissenters", correct:true,
     why:"Chapter 11 cramdown requires that no junior class receive or retain anything on account of its junior interest unless the dissenting senior class is paid in full. Section 70(4) adopts a version of this for unsecured dissenting classes. Shareholders keeping equity for nothing while unsecured creditors take 30% fails that test. [external]"},
    {t:"No problem: shareholders always keep some equity", correct:false,
     why:"Under absolute priority, equity is last in line."},
    {t:"It breaches the automatic stay", correct:false,
     why:"The stay concerns enforcement, not plan distributions."},
    {t:"It is a 363 sale issue", correct:false,
     why:"A 363 sale is an asset sale. This is about plan priority."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (comparative); IRDA s 70(4) (external)", takeaway:"Equity can't keep value over an unpaid dissenting senior class."
}

