/* ---------- 7.1 — The Separation Taxonomy ---------- */
{
  id:"af-1-01", dia:"separationTypes", lesson:"F.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"ParentCo offers its shareholders S$108 of SubCo shares for every S$100 of ParentCo shares they hand in. Shareholders who do nothing keep their ParentCo shares. The tendered ParentCo shares are cancelled.",
  prompt:"What is this, and what happens to ParentCo's share count?",
  options:[
    {t:"A split-off (exchange offer); ParentCo's share count falls by the shares tendered", correct:true,
     why:"Holders must give up parent shares to get subsidiary shares, and only those who choose to take part. That is Gaughan's split-off. The premium (here S$8 per S$100) is the incentive to exchange, as in Pfizer's 2013 Zoetis exchange. Because tendered parent shares are retired, the parent's share count shrinks, which can offset the lost subsidiary earnings on a per-share basis."},
    {t:"A spin-off; ParentCo's share count is unchanged", correct:false,
     why:"In a spin-off every holder receives subsidiary shares pro rata without giving anything up. Here holders surrender parent shares, and only if they elect to."},
    {t:"An equity carve-out; ParentCo issues new shares", correct:false,
     why:"A carve-out sells subsidiary shares to outside investors for cash. No outside investors or cash are involved here."},
    {t:"A split-up; ParentCo ceases to exist", correct:false,
     why:"In a split-up the whole parent is broken into several spun-off companies and disappears. Here ParentCo continues with fewer shares."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11", takeaway:"Swap parent shares for sub shares, by election = split-off; parent share count falls."
},
{
  id:"af-1-02", dia:"separationTypes", lesson:"F.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A conglomerate with property, logistics and healthcare arms separates all three into independent listed companies. Once the transaction completes, the original holding company no longer exists.",
  prompt:"Which label fits?",
  options:[
    {t:"A split-up", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan defines a split-up as breaking the entire firm into a series of spin-offs, so that the parent no longer exists and only the new companies remain. A spin-off separates one unit while the parent carries on."},
    {t:"A single spin-off", correct:false,
     why:"A spin-off separates one business and leaves the parent in place. Here every business leaves and the parent disappears."},
    {t:"A voluntary liquidation", correct:false,
     why:"A liquidation sells the assets in parcels and distributes proceeds. Here the businesses carry on as going concerns under new listed companies."},
    {t:"An equity carve-out", correct:false,
     why:"A carve-out floats part of one subsidiary to new investors for cash. Nothing is sold to outsiders here."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11", takeaway:"Whole group broken into spin-offs, parent gone = split-up."
},
{
  id:"af-1-03", dia:"separationTypes", lesson:"F.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A board wants the market to price its fast-growing digital division on its own. It does not want to create a separate legal entity or give up legal control of the division's assets.",
  prompt:"Which instrument meets both constraints?",
  options:[
    {t:"A tracking stock: shares tied to the division's earnings, with the division staying inside the company", correct:true,
     why:"A tracking (targeted, letter) stock gives holders an interest in a division's earnings, but the division remains part of the company, which keeps legal control of the assets. Holders do not own those assets. Any sell-off (spin-off, carve-out, divestiture) creates a separate legal entity, which the board has ruled out."},
    {t:"An equity carve-out of the division", correct:false,
     why:"A carve-out requires a separate subsidiary whose shares are sold, which the board has ruled out."},
    {t:"A spin-off of the division", correct:false,
     why:"A spin-off creates a separate independent company and gives up control, failing both constraints."},
    {t:"A split-off of the division", correct:false,
     why:"A split-off also separates the division into its own entity, owned by the holders who exchange."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (Tracking Stocks)", takeaway:"Separate pricing without a separate entity = tracking stock."
},
{
  id:"af-1-04", dia:"separationTypes", lesson:"F.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A parent sells 17% of a wholly owned subsidiary in an IPO. Three months later it distributes its remaining shares in the subsidiary to its own shareholders.",
  prompt:"What is the best description, and why sequence it this way?",
  options:[
    {t:"A carve-out followed by a spin-off; the IPO establishes a market price for the unit before full separation", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan describes carve-outs as often being the first step of a two-step exit: selling a stake 'tests the waters' and sets a market value, then the rest is spun off. GM did this with Delphi in 1999, a carve-out of just over 17% followed three months later by a spin-off of the remainder."},
    {t:"A dual-track exit", correct:false,
     why:"A dual-track runs an IPO and a trade sale in parallel and completes one. Here both steps are completed, in sequence, and there is no trade buyer."},
    {t:"A split-off", correct:false,
     why:"No holder surrenders parent shares. The second step is a pro-rata distribution, a spin-off."},
    {t:"A tracking stock issue", correct:false,
     why:"The subsidiary is a separate legal entity whose shares are sold and distributed. A tracking stock keeps the division inside the parent."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (Equity Carve-Outs; GM/Delphi)", takeaway:"Carve-out then spin-off: price the unit first, separate fully later."
},
{
  id:"af-1-05", lesson:"F.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"SPH moved its media business into a new wholly owned subsidiary, Media HoldCo. It then transferred Media HoldCo for a nominal sum to a company limited by guarantee and contributed about S$351.3m of cash and assets to it. Shareholders approved the transaction in general meeting.",
  prompt:"How should this separation be classified?",
  options:[
    {t:"A hive-down followed by a divestiture to a non-profit vehicle; shareholders received no shares, and the parent put value in rather than taking cash out", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [1.61]–[1.62]: the media business was isolated in a subsidiary, then the subsidiary was transferred out of the group for a nominal sum. SPH shareholders received nothing, so it was not a spin-off. There was no IPO, so it was not a carve-out. The company limited by guarantee let the media business raise funds without pressure for returns to shareholders, and let SPH refocus. Because it disposed of a core business, shareholder approval was needed."},
    {t:"A spin-off, because the media business became independent", correct:false,
     why:"In a spin-off the parent's shareholders receive the subsidiary's shares. Here the shares went to a company limited by guarantee, not to SPH shareholders."},
    {t:"An equity carve-out, because a new entity was created", correct:false,
     why:"A carve-out sells shares to new investors for cash. No shares were offered to the public, and SPH received only a nominal sum."},
    {t:"A takeover of SPH", correct:false,
     why:"The later Keppel and Cuscaden offers were for the rest of SPH. The media restructuring itself was a separation by SPH."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [1.61]–[1.64]", takeaway:"SPH media: hive-down, then transfer to a CLG; not a spin-off or carve-out."
},
{
  id:"af-1-06", lesson:"F.1", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A parent with bonds outstanding announces a spin-off of its most cash-generative subsidiary. Its share price rises on the announcement.",
  prompt:"What does the evidence Gaughan reports suggest for the parent's bondholders?",
  options:[
    {t:"They tend to lose: the spun-off assets no longer support their claims, and rating downgrades become more likely", correct:true,
     why:"Maxwell and Rao (80 spin-offs, 1976–1997) found bondholders suffered a negative abnormal return of about 88 basis points in the announcement month, with downgrades more likely, while shareholders gained about 2.9%. The loss of collateral and cash flow behind the bonds explains much of it. Lenders protect themselves through covenants restricting distributions and disposals."},
    {t:"They gain in line with shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The research points the other way: shareholder gains came partly at bondholders' expense."},
    {t:"They are unaffected because debt is never reallocated", correct:false,
     why:"Group debt is allocated between parent and spun-off entity, and the parent loses assets and cash flow. Bondholders are affected."},
    {t:"They automatically receive shares in the spun-off company", correct:false,
     why:"A spin-off distributes shares to shareholders, not creditors."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (Spinoffs and Bondholder Wealth)", takeaway:"Spin-offs can shift value away from bondholders."
},

/* ---------- 7.2 — Choosing the Tool ---------- */
{
  id:"af-2-01", lesson:"F.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A US parent owns 100% of a subsidiary. It wants to raise some cash now through a carve-out IPO and keep the option of a tax-free spin-off of the rest later.",
  prompt:"On Gaughan's account of US tax rules, what is the most it should sell in the carve-out?",
  options:[
    {t:"20%: it must keep at least 80% to preserve a later tax-free spin-off", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan: a tax-free spin-off under IRC ss 354–355 requires the parent to own at least 80% of the unit, so keeping 80% or more in the carve-out preserves that option. Keeping at least 50% preserves consolidation. This is US tax law, not Singapore law, but it explains why carve-outs are so often minority floats."},
    {t:"49%: it only needs to keep a majority", correct:false,
     why:"A majority keeps control and consolidation, but the tax-free spin-off test Gaughan describes needs 80%."},
    {t:"Any amount; tax does not depend on the parent's stake", correct:false,
     why:"The 80% ownership condition is one of the central requirements for a tax-free spin-off."},
    {t:"Nothing; a carve-out always blocks a later tax-free spin-off", correct:false,
     why:"GM–Delphi shows the two-step route works if enough is retained."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (Tax Treatment of Spinoffs; Equity Carve-Outs) — US, not SG law", takeaway:"US: keep ≥80% in the carve-out to preserve a tax-free spin-off."
},
{
  id:"af-2-02", lesson:"F.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A US parent acquired control of a business three years ago. The fit has not worked and the board wants a tax-free spin-off now. Its only stated reason is avoiding the capital-gains tax a sale would trigger.",
  prompt:"On Gaughan's summary of the US rules, what are the problems?",
  options:[
    {t:"Two: control was acquired less than five years ago, and tax avoidance alone does not satisfy the business-purpose test", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan lists, among the requirements, that the parent must not have acquired control of the unit less than five years earlier, and that the spin-off must have a business purpose beyond avoiding tax. The business purpose need not be the only purpose, so a genuine focus rationale would help. The five-year point cannot be fixed by drafting."},
    {t:"None; any spin-off is tax-free", correct:false,
     why:"Tax-free treatment depends on meeting the Code requirements. Gaughan stresses these rules are complex."},
    {t:"Only that the parent must first IPO the unit", correct:false,
     why:"An IPO is not a precondition. The problems are the holding period and the business purpose."},
    {t:"Only that shareholders must approve", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholder approval is usually not needed for a spin-off unless it covers substantially all of the parent's assets, and it does not decide tax treatment."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (Tax Treatment of Spinoffs) — US, not SG law", takeaway:"US tax-free spin: ≥80%, 5-year holding, real business purpose."
},
{
  id:"af-2-03", lesson:"F.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Division X is highly leveraged, low-growth and has weak margins. Division Y is large, profitable and lightly geared. The parent wants to separate both.",
  prompt:"Which pairing matches the evidence on carve-out versus spin-off choice?",
  options:[
    {t:"Spin off X; carve out Y", correct:true,
     why:"Michaely and Shaw found that riskier, more leveraged firms went the spin-off route, while bigger, less leveraged, more profitable firms chose carve-outs. The choice turns on access to capital markets: investors will buy a float of Y, but X may not attract public buyers."},
    {t:"Carve out X; spin off Y", correct:false,
     why:"That reverses the finding. Weaker businesses struggle to attract IPO investors, so they are usually spun off."},
    {t:"Carve out both; carve-outs are always cheaper", correct:false,
     why:"Carve-outs are more expensive than spin-offs (one study put it at about four times), and a weak unit may not float at all."},
    {t:"Neither; weak divisions cannot be separated", correct:false,
     why:"A spin-off needs no outside investors, which is why it suits a weaker unit."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (Equity Carve-Outs versus Spinoffs)", takeaway:"Strong unit → carve-out; weak, leveraged unit → spin-off."
},
{
  id:"af-2-04", lesson:"F.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two parents announce equity carve-outs on the same day. Parent A says it will use the proceeds to repay debt. Parent B says it will keep them for future investment.",
  prompt:"Based on the research Gaughan reports, which announcement is the market likely to reward?",
  options:[
    {t:"Parent A's", correct:true,
     why:"Allen and McConnell found an average excess return of +6.63% where carve-out proceeds were used to pay down debt, against −0.01% where they were retained for investment. The market's reaction depended on what the company did with the money."},
    {t:"Parent B's", correct:false,
     why:"Retaining the cash for investment was associated with essentially no gain (−0.01%)."},
    {t:"Neither; the use of proceeds is irrelevant", correct:false,
     why:"The study found a large difference based on use of proceeds."},
    {t:"Both equally, since carve-outs always return about 1.8%", correct:false,
     why:"1.8% is an average across studies. Within it, use of proceeds made a big difference."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (Disposition of Carve-Out Proceeds)", takeaway:"Carve-out proceeds used to repay debt → stronger market reaction."
},
{
  id:"af-2-05", lesson:"F.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A board wants to separate a loosely integrated division within six months, as cheaply as possible, and does not need any cash.",
  prompt:"Which route fits, and why?",
  options:[
    {t:"A spin-off: it is cheaper, quicker and carries lighter securities disclosure than a carve-out", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan: spin-offs are usually easier and less expensive to implement than carve-outs (one study found carve-outs about four times as costly) and much less time-consuming. Carve-outs also carry heavier securities-law disclosure. A division that was never well integrated is easier still to separate. With no need for cash, the carve-out's main advantage is irrelevant."},
    {t:"An equity carve-out, because it raises cash", correct:false,
     why:"Cash is not needed, and a carve-out is slower and costlier."},
    {t:"A dual-track process", correct:false,
     why:"Running an IPO and a sale in parallel is the most expensive option and is aimed at a cash exit."},
    {t:"A competitive auction", correct:false,
     why:"An auction is a sale process for cash. The board wants separation, not a sale."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (Spinoffs)", takeaway:"No cash needed, fast and cheap → spin-off."
},
{
  id:"af-2-06", lesson:"F.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A Mainboard-listed group agrees to sell a division. Relative figures under Rule 1006: net asset value of the division is 35% of group NAV; its net profits are 15% of group net profits; the consideration is 18% of market capitalisation.",
  prompt:"How is the disposal classified?",
  options:[
    {t:"A major transaction: one relative figure exceeds 20%, so it needs shareholder approval and a circular", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 1014 classifies a transaction as major where ANY relative figure under Rule 1006 exceeds 20%. The NAV figure (35%) does, even though the other two do not. The disposal must be made conditional on shareholder approval, with a circular to shareholders. Wan [2.25] notes this Listing Manual layer sits on top of the Companies Act."},
    {t:"Not major: the average of the figures is below 20%", correct:false,
     why:"The figures are not averaged. Any single figure above 20% makes it major."},
    {t:"Not major: the consideration figure is below 20%, and that is the only one that counts", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 1006 has several bases (NAV disposed, net profits, consideration and others). Any one can trigger."},
    {t:"A very substantial acquisition", correct:false,
     why:"That label is for acquisitions at 100% or more (Rule 1015). This is a disposal."}
  ],
  cite:"SGX Mainboard Rules 1006, 1014 [external]; Wan [2.25]", takeaway:"Any Rule 1006 figure >20% → major transaction → shareholder vote."
},
{
  id:"af-2-07", lesson:"F.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The same group sells a smaller business instead. Relative figures: NAV 12%, net profits 9%, consideration 14% of market capitalisation. The business is not part of the group's core operations.",
  prompt:"What does Chapter 10 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Announce the transaction with the prescribed details; no Rule 1014 shareholder approval is needed", correct:true,
     why:"Above 5% the issuer must announce the transaction and its terms (including the consideration and how it was arrived at). Shareholder approval under Rule 1014 applies only above 20%. The Exchange can still impose extra requirements in appropriate cases, and a disposal of core business would be looked at differently."},
    {t:"Shareholder approval, because any disposal above 5% is major", correct:false,
     why:"5% triggers disclosure. The major-transaction threshold is 20%."},
    {t:"Nothing at all", correct:false,
     why:"Relative figures above 5% require an announcement."},
    {t:"An independent valuation", correct:false,
     why:"A mandatory independent valuer applies to disposals where a relative figure exceeds 75% (Rule 1014(5))."}
  ],
  cite:"SGX Mainboard Ch 10; Practice Note 10.1 [external]", takeaway:">5% announce; >20% vote; >75% disposal also needs a valuer."
},
{
  id:"af-2-08", lesson:"F.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A listed company agrees to sell its main operating business. The NAV relative figure is 80%.",
  prompt:"Which set of requirements applies?",
  options:[
    {t:"Shareholder approval with a circular, an independent valuation of the assets, and very likely s 160 approval as well", correct:true,
     why:"At 80% it is a major transaction (above 20%), so Rule 1014 approval and a circular are needed. Because a disposal figure exceeds 75%, Rule 1014(5) also requires a competent, independent valuer. Selling the main operating business is also likely to be 'substantially the whole' of the undertaking under CA s 160, which needs approval in general meeting (Wan [2.21]–[2.22])."},
    {t:"Only an announcement", correct:false,
     why:"At 80% this is far above the 20% approval threshold."},
    {t:"SIC approval under the Take-Over Code", correct:false,
     why:"The Code governs offers for the company's shares, not the company's sale of its assets."},
    {t:"Court sanction", correct:false,
     why:"A sale of a business by contract needs shareholder approval, not court sanction. Court involvement comes with schemes and court-route capital reductions."}
  ],
  cite:"SGX Mainboard Rule 1014(5) [external]; CA s 160; Wan [2.21]–[2.25]", takeaway:"Disposal >75%: vote + independent valuer; check s 160 too."
},
{
  id:"af-2-09", lesson:"F.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An unlisted company's directors sell its only operating business without holding a general meeting. A minority shareholder finds out after completion. The buyer paid full value and had no actual notice of any irregularity.",
  prompt:"What can the shareholder achieve under s 160?",
  options:[
    {t:"Little against the buyer: s 160 lets members restrain a disposal beforehand, but a transaction with a buyer who gave value without actual notice cannot be called into question", correct:true,
     why:"s 160(1) requires approval in general meeting for a disposal of the whole or substantially the whole of the undertaking. s 160(2) lets a member apply to restrain the directors, which only helps before the deal. s 160(3) protects a buyer who paid valuable consideration without actual notice of the contravention. That is how the buyer was protected in K J Kim v Buck (Wan [2.23]). The shareholder's remaining options lie against the directors."},
    {t:"Have the sale set aside automatically", correct:false,
     why:"s 160(3) protects the innocent buyer. There is no automatic unwinding."},
    {t:"Nothing, because s 160 applies only to listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"s 160 is a Companies Act provision for companies generally. SGX rules add a separate layer for listed issuers."},
    {t:"Force a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"Mandatory offers are a Code concept triggered by acquiring control of a company's shares. They do not apply to a company selling its business."}
  ],
  cite:"CA s 160(1)–(3); Wan [2.21]–[2.23]", takeaway:"s 160: restrain before; innocent buyer for value is protected after."
},
{
  id:"af-2-10", lesson:"F.2", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company sells a business that is only 15% of its total assets. That business produces all of the company's revenue; what remains are passive landholdings.",
  prompt:"Could this still be a disposal of 'substantially the whole' of the undertaking?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: the test is qualitative as well as quantitative, and losing the only revenue-generating business goes to the heart of the company's existence", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [2.22] draws on US case law (Gimbel, Hollinger): quantitative factors (revenues, profits, assets) and qualitative factors (whether what remains is qualitatively different and whether the sale 'strikes at the heart of the corporate existence') both count. A sale that stops the company carrying on its business is the mischief s 160 targets (K J Kim, obiter). A low asset percentage does not settle the question."},
    {t:"No: anything under 50% of assets is outside s 160", correct:false,
     why:"There is no bright-line asset test. Qualitative factors can decide it."},
    {t:"No: s 160 applies only to sales of land", correct:false,
     why:"s 160 covers the company's undertaking or property generally."},
    {t:"Only if the buyer is a related party", correct:false,
     why:"Buyer identity is an interested-person issue. s 160 turns on the scale and nature of what is sold."}
  ],
  cite:"CA s 160; Wan [2.21]–[2.22]", takeaway:"'Substantially the whole' = quantitative + qualitative (heart of the business)."
},
{
  id:"af-2-11", lesson:"F.2", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each separation step to the approval it typically needs.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Sale of substantially the whole undertaking (CA s 160)", r:"Approval in general meeting (ordinary resolution)"},
    {l:"Listed issuer's disposal with a relative figure above 20%", r:"Shareholder approval + circular (SGX Rule 1014)"},
    {l:"Capital reduction without court (CA ss 78B–78C)", r:"Special resolution + directors' solvency statement"},
    {l:"Members' scheme of arrangement (CA s 210)", r:"Majority in number and 75% in value voting, then court sanction"}
  ],
  why:"Each tool has its own gate. s 160 needs the company's approval in general meeting, a simple majority of those present and voting (Wan [2.21], [2.26]). SGX Rule 1014 adds a listed-company vote for major transactions. The out-of-court capital reduction needs a special resolution and a solvency statement, with creditors able to object to the court (Wan [14.67]). A scheme needs the statutory majorities at the meeting and then the court's sanction (Wan Ch 13).",
  cite:"CA ss 78B–78C, 160, 210; SGX Rule 1014; Wan [2.21]–[2.26], [14.67], Ch 13", takeaway:"Know the gate: s 160 OR; SGX >20% vote; s 78C SR + solvency; s 210 majorities + court."
},

/* ---------- 7.3 — The Competitive Auction ---------- */
{
  id:"af-3-01", dia:"auctionProcess", lesson:"F.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The seller's adviser receives nine indicative bids after circulating the information memorandum.",
  prompt:"What does Wan describe as the usual next step?",
  options:[
    {t:"Shortlist a small group (usually no more than three) and only then release the more sensitive information", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [4.68]: the information memorandum starts with less sensitive information. After initial sifting and shortlisting (usually not more than three), more information goes to the shortlisted bidders. Keeping the circle small limits the risk of leaks and misuse, which is hard to prove under a confidentiality agreement if the deal fails."},
    {t:"Open the full data room to all nine", correct:false,
     why:"That spreads sensitive information widely. Wan notes breaches are hard to prove afterwards, so disclosure widens only as the field narrows."},
    {t:"Grant exclusivity to the highest indicative bidder", correct:false,
     why:"Indicative bids are non-binding. Exclusivity this early gives up the competitive tension the auction exists to create."},
    {t:"Sign an SPA with the highest bidder immediately", correct:false,
     why:"Binding terms come after diligence, in the final round."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [4.68]", takeaway:"Shortlist (≈3), then release sensitive information."
},
{
  id:"af-3-02", lesson:"F.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"You advise one of four bidders in a sealed-bid auction for a business whose future cash flows are highly uncertain. Your client's model gives the highest valuation of the four.",
  prompt:"What risk should you raise first?",
  options:[
    {t:"The winner's curse: in a sealed-bid auction under uncertainty, the winner tends to be whoever most overestimates value", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [1.40]: the winner's curse is accentuated in auctions. Holding the highest model valuation makes your client the likeliest to win, and also the likeliest to be wrong. The discipline is a walk-away price set before the final round, stress-tested assumptions, and contract protection for the key uncertainties."},
    {t:"That the seller may refuse to use a data room", correct:false,
     why:"Data rooms are standard. The distinctive risk of a high model valuation in an auction is overpaying."},
    {t:"That the Take-Over Code sets the price", correct:false,
     why:"This is a private auction, and the Code does not set prices in any case."},
    {t:"That the other bidders will be told its bid", correct:false,
     why:"Sealed bids are confidential. The risk lies in the client's own valuation."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [1.39]–[1.40]", takeaway:"Highest model valuation in an auction → beware the winner's curse."
},
{
  id:"af-3-03", lesson:"F.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Final bids arrive. Bidder A offers S$500m, conditional on financing and CCCS clearance, and marks up the SPA heavily. Bidder B offers S$480m, unconditional, on a locked box with the seller's SPA largely accepted.",
  prompt:"Must the seller take Bidder A's higher price?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the process letter reserves the seller's discretion, and a lower bid with more certainty can be worth more", correct:true,
     why:"The process letter reserves the seller's right to choose, change or end the process, and bids are not binding contracts until an SPA is signed. Value means price adjusted for risk: A's financing and regulatory conditions and SPA changes lower the chance of completing on those terms. B's S$20m discount buys certainty and a clean exit. Many sellers would choose B, or use B to press A to drop its conditions."},
    {t:"Yes: auction rules require the highest headline price", correct:false,
     why:"No such rule applies to a private auction. The seller keeps discretion under the process letter."},
    {t:"Yes, because Bidder A has a contract once it submits", correct:false,
     why:"A bid is an offer on the seller's draft terms. Nothing binds until the SPA is signed."},
    {t:"No, because conditional bids are unlawful", correct:false,
     why:"Conditions are lawful. They reduce the bid's value to the seller."}
  ],
  cite:"Chapter 7; Wan Ch 4 §G; Intro 2.9", takeaway:"Compare bids on price and certainty; the process letter keeps the choice with the seller."
},
{
  id:"af-3-04", dia:"auctionProcess", lesson:"F.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The data room for a carve-out contains a spreadsheet listing every employee by name with salary, medical claims and performance ratings.",
  prompt:"What should the seller do before bidders get access?",
  options:[
    {t:"Anonymise or aggregate it and release named data only if needed, late and under confidentiality: the seller has personal-data obligations", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [4.69]: where personal data of individuals is shared under a confidentiality agreement, the provider must keep its obligations under the Personal Data Protection Act in mind. Bidders need workforce cost and structure, not names and medical claims. Aggregated or anonymised data, with named details held back for the final stage or a clean team, meets the diligence need with less exposure."},
    {t:"Release it; the NDA covers everything", correct:false,
     why:"An NDA binds the bidder but does not discharge the seller's own data-protection obligations."},
    {t:"Delete all employee information from the data room", correct:false,
     why:"Workforce costs and terms matter to value and to s 18A analysis. The answer is to minimise and anonymise, not to withhold everything."},
    {t:"Send it only to the highest bidder", correct:false,
     why:"Who receives it does not solve the privacy issue. What is shared and in what form does."}
  ],
  cite:"Wan [4.69]; PDPA [external]", takeaway:"Personal data in the data room: minimise, anonymise, stage."
},
{
  id:"af-3-05", lesson:"F.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The seller's accountants prepared a vendor due-diligence report. The winning bidder wants to rely on it when setting its price.",
  prompt:"What does the bidder usually need to rely on the report?",
  options:[
    {t:"A reliance letter from the report provider, plus its own confirmatory diligence on the key points", correct:true,
     why:"A VDD report is written for the seller. To have any claim against the provider if it is wrong, the buyer needs a reliance letter, usually subject to a liability cap. Buyers still run confirmatory diligence, and W&I insurers will expect a proper diligence process. [practice]"},
    {t:"Nothing: anyone who reads a VDD report can sue on it", correct:false,
     why:"Without a reliance arrangement the provider owes duties to its client, the seller, not to bidders."},
    {t:"A warranty from the seller that the report is correct", correct:false,
     why:"Sellers usually refuse to warrant the VDD report as a whole. Reliance runs through the provider, with warranties covering specific facts."},
    {t:"SIC approval", correct:false,
     why:"This is a private auction. The SIC has no role."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro glossary (VDD) [practice]", takeaway:"Relying on VDD → reliance letter from the provider + confirmatory DD."
},
{
  id:"af-3-06", lesson:"F.3", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two rival offerors are bidding for a LISTED Singapore company. Late in the offer period neither has declared its offer final, and they have not agreed any alternative procedure.",
  prompt:"How is the contest resolved?",
  options:[
    {t:"Under the Code's default auction procedure (Appendix 4): if the competition persists at 5 pm on Day 46, up to five daily bidding rounds follow", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [5.149]: after the F&N contest, the 2016 Code amendments introduced a default auction procedure for competitive situations that continue late in the offer period with no agreed alternative. Rounds run one per day over up to five auction days, and a bidder may revise only if the other revised the previous day (Day 5 excepted). The SIC announces the end. This is different from a seller-run private auction."},
    {t:"The target board picks the winner at its discretion", correct:false,
     why:"In a public contest shareholders decide by accepting. The Code supplies the auction mechanism to end the contest."},
    {t:"The higher bidder wins automatically on Day 46", correct:false,
     why:"The default procedure lets both revise in rounds. Nothing is decided automatically on Day 46."},
    {t:"The seller's process letter governs", correct:false,
     why:"There is no seller running a sale here. Offers go to shareholders under the Code."}
  ],
  cite:"Take-Over Code App 4; Wan [5.145]–[5.150]", takeaway:"Public contest stalemate → Code App 4 default auction (post-Day 46)."
},

/* ---------- 7.4 — Dual-Track ---------- */
{
  id:"af-4-01", dia:"dualTrack", lesson:"F.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A PE fund runs a dual-track. IPO bookbuilding points to an equity value of about S$1.1bn, but markets are volatile, and at listing the fund could sell only part of its stake. A trade buyer bids S$1.05bn for 100%, binding, unconditional, on a locked box.",
  prompt:"Which is the stronger exit, and why?",
  options:[
    {t:"The trade sale: a certain, full cash exit now beats a higher headline value that would be realised only partly, and later", correct:true,
     why:"At IPO the seller usually sells only part of its holding, and SGX requires promoters to accept a moratorium on the rest. The retained stake stays exposed to market risk, and pricing may slip in a volatile market. The trade bid is 100%, certain and immediate. The S$50m headline gap is small against that. Late choice between tracks is the point of a dual-track."},
    {t:"The IPO, because its headline value is higher", correct:false,
     why:"Headline value ignores that the IPO is a partial exit with a lock-up and market risk on the rest."},
    {t:"Neither; the fund must complete both", correct:false,
     why:"A dual-track completes one route. Selling 100% to the buyer rules out listing the same shares."},
    {t:"The IPO, because trade sales always need shareholder approval", correct:false,
     why:"A private seller's sale of its shares needs no such approval. That is not the deciding factor."}
  ],
  cite:"Chapter 7; SGX Mainboard Ch 2 (promoter moratorium) [external]; Intro 2.9", takeaway:"Dual-track decision: full certain exit vs partial, locked-up, market-exposed IPO."
},
{
  id:"af-4-02", dia:"dualTrack", lesson:"F.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A seller's main goal is a clean break with as little residual liability as possible after exit.",
  prompt:"How do the two tracks compare on liability?",
  options:[
    {t:"The trade sale can be ring-fenced by caps, time limits and W&I insurance; the IPO brings statutory prospectus liability and a retained stake", correct:true,
     why:"In a trade sale, liability is contractual and negotiable: caps, short claim periods and W&I cover can reduce the seller's exposure to very little. An IPO prospectus carries statutory liability under the SFA for false or misleading statements [external], which cannot be capped away by contract, and the seller keeps a locked-up stake. For a clean break, the trade sale usually wins."},
    {t:"The IPO, because public investors cannot sue anyone", correct:false,
     why:"Prospectus liability is a statutory remedy for investors."},
    {t:"They are identical", correct:false,
     why:"One is contractual and negotiable. The other is statutory and comes with a retained stake."},
    {t:"The trade sale, because warranties are banned in trade sales", correct:false,
     why:"Warranties are standard in trade sales. The point is that their scope can be negotiated."}
  ],
  cite:"Chapter 7; SFA (prospectus liability) [external]; Intro 2.10", takeaway:"Clean break: trade sale (capped, insured) beats IPO (statutory liability + stake)."
},
{
  id:"af-4-03", dia:"dualTrack", lesson:"F.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Midway through a dual-track, the IPO window closes after a market sell-off. Trade bidders hear about it.",
  prompt:"What is the main effect on the seller's position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Its leverage drops: without a credible IPO alternative, bidders know the sale track is the only way out", correct:true,
     why:"The IPO track's value lies in the pressure it puts on bidders. Once it is no longer credible, bidders can press on price and terms. The seller's responses are to keep several bidders in play, avoid granting exclusivity, or pause if the timetable allows. This is why the tracks are kept alive until late and IPO readiness has to be real."},
    {t:"None; dual-track leverage does not depend on the IPO", correct:false,
     why:"The leverage comes precisely from each track being a credible alternative to the other."},
    {t:"The seller must now list", correct:false,
     why:"The window has closed. Listing is the route that has been lost."},
    {t:"The seller must grant exclusivity to the highest bidder", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing requires that, and granting it would weaken the seller further."}
  ],
  cite:"Chapter 7; Gaughan Ch 11", takeaway:"Lose a credible track → lose leverage."
},
{
  id:"af-4-04", dia:"dualTrack", lesson:"F.4", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A Singapore-listed parent plans to list a subsidiary on the Mainboard as the IPO track of a dual-track. The subsidiary's assets and operations are substantially the same as the parent's.",
  prompt:"What listing problem does this raise?",
  options:[
    {t:"SGX will not normally consider such a subsidiary suitable for listing, so the IPO track may not be credible", correct:true,
     why:"The Mainboard admission rules say a subsidiary or parent of an existing listed issuer will not normally be considered suitable for listing if its assets and operations are substantially the same as the existing issuer's. The Exchange looks at the commercial reasons. If the IPO track cannot pass this, it gives no real leverage in the sale track. Check listing suitability before relying on the IPO."},
    {t:"None; any subsidiary can list", correct:false,
     why:"The Mainboard rules address this overlap directly."},
    {t:"It requires a mandatory offer", correct:false,
     why:"Listing a subsidiary is not an acquisition of control. The problem is listing suitability."},
    {t:"The parent must delist first", correct:false,
     why:"No such requirement exists. The test is whether the subsidiary is sufficiently distinct."}
  ],
  cite:"SGX Mainboard Rules Ch 2 (admission) [external]", takeaway:"A listed parent's look-alike subsidiary will not normally be suitable for listing."
},

/* ---------- 7.5 — Sell-side Protections & Process ---------- */
{
  id:"af-5-01", lesson:"F.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Buyer diligence in an auction finds a S$6m tax exposure. The buyer has W&I insurance and assumes the policy will cover it.",
  prompt:"Is that right, and what should the buyer do?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: W&I policies exclude matters the buyer knew about before signing, so the buyer needs a specific indemnity, an escrow or a price reduction", correct:true,
     why:"W&I cover is for unknown breaches. Known issues, including those found in diligence or disclosed, are excluded as standard. Known risks are handled by a specific indemnity from the seller (possibly backed by escrow), by a price reduction, or occasionally by separate contingent-risk cover. [practice]"},
    {t:"Yes: W&I covers everything in the SPA", correct:false,
     why:"Known matters, and usually some categories such as fines and forward-looking statements, are excluded."},
    {t:"Yes, as long as the seller disclosed it", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure qualifies the warranty and makes the matter known, so it moves the risk outside cover."},
    {t:"No, but nothing can be done", correct:false,
     why:"Known risks are routinely priced or covered by a specific indemnity."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10, glossary (W&I) [practice]", takeaway:"Known issue → not W&I; use a specific indemnity, escrow or price."
},
{
  id:"af-5-02", lesson:"F.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Three sellers hold 50%, 30% and 20% of the target. Warranty liability is several, pro rata to holdings. A valid S$10m claim arises. The 20% seller has since become insolvent.",
  prompt:"How much can the buyer recover, and from whom?",
  options:[
    {t:"S$5m from the 50% seller and S$3m from the 30% seller; the S$2m share of the insolvent seller is the buyer's loss unless an escrow or W&I covers it", correct:true,
     why:"Several liability means each seller answers only for its own share. The solvent sellers cannot be made to pay the insolvent seller's portion. That credit risk is why buyers ask for escrows, retentions or W&I insurance in multi-seller deals."},
    {t:"S$10m from the 50% seller", correct:false,
     why:"That would be joint and several liability. Under several liability the 50% seller's exposure is S$5m."},
    {t:"S$8m split equally between the two solvent sellers", correct:false,
     why:"Several pro-rata liability does not reallocate a defaulting seller's share."},
    {t:"Nothing, because one seller is insolvent", correct:false,
     why:"The solvent sellers remain liable for their own shares."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10; Chapter 7", takeaway:"Several liability: each pays its share; the buyer bears a defaulter's share without escrow/W&I."
},
{
  id:"af-5-03", lesson:"F.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An SPA sets a de minimis of S$50k per claim and a basket of S$1m. After completion the buyer has ten valid claims of S$40k each and one valid claim of S$1.5m.",
  prompt:"What does the buyer recover if the basket is a TIPPING basket?",
  options:[
    {t:"S$1.5m", correct:true,
     why:"The S$40k claims are each below the S$50k de minimis, so they are ignored and do not count toward the basket. The S$1.5m claim exceeds the S$1m basket. With a tipping basket, once the threshold is passed the buyer recovers from the first dollar: S$1.5m. With a deductible basket it would recover only the excess, S$0.5m. [practice]"},
    {t:"S$0.5m", correct:false,
     why:"That is the result under a deductible basket. A tipping basket pays from the first dollar once the threshold is crossed."},
    {t:"S$1.9m", correct:false,
     why:"That counts the S$40k claims, which the de minimis excludes."},
    {t:"Nothing: total qualifying claims must exceed S$1.9m", correct:false,
     why:"The basket is S$1m, and the S$1.5m qualifying claim clears it."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10 [practice]", takeaway:"De minimis filters claims; tipping basket = from dollar one; deductible = excess only."
},
{
  id:"af-5-04", lesson:"F.5", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In an auction SPA the seller proposes that everything in the data room and the VDD reports is 'deemed disclosed' against all warranties.",
  prompt:"Why does the buyer resist, and what is the usual compromise?",
  options:[
    {t:"General disclosure moves every buried issue onto the buyer; the usual compromise is specific disclosure, or data-room disclosure limited to matters fairly disclosed with enough detail", correct:true,
     why:"If the whole data room counts as disclosed, any problem hidden somewhere in thousands of documents qualifies the warranties, and the buyer loses its claim. Buyers push for specific disclosure against named warranties, or accept data-room disclosure only where a matter is fairly disclosed in enough detail to identify its nature and scope. The seller's strength in an auction decides how far it gets. [practice]"},
    {t:"The buyer should accept; it makes no difference", correct:false,
     why:"It shifts undisclosed risk to the buyer, and that is the key risk-allocation lever."},
    {t:"The buyer resists because deemed disclosure is unlawful", correct:false,
     why:"It is lawful and common. The question is how far it reaches."},
    {t:"Deemed disclosure only matters in public takeovers", correct:false,
     why:"Code offers carry no warranties. This is an SPA issue in private sales."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.10 (warranties & disclosure) [practice]", takeaway:"Data-room deemed disclosure favours the seller; the buyer pushes for fair, specific disclosure."
},

/* ---------- 7.6 — Separation Mechanics ---------- */
{
  id:"af-6-01", dia:"carveOut", lesson:"F.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A solvent Singapore public company wants to distribute its shares in SubCo to its own shareholders by way of a capital reduction, and would rather avoid going to court.",
  prompt:"What does the out-of-court route require?",
  options:[
    {t:"A special resolution and a directors' solvency statement; creditors may still object to the court, which can then cancel the reduction", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [14.67]: since 2005 the Companies Act has allowed a capital reduction without court approval, on a special resolution (ss 78B–78C) backed by a directors' solvency statement (s 78C(3)). The solvency requirement is the creditors' main protection. Creditors can apply to the court to object (s 78D), and the court then either dismisses the objection or cancels the reduction (s 78F)."},
    {t:"An ordinary resolution only", correct:false,
     why:"A capital reduction needs a special resolution and, on this route, a solvency statement."},
    {t:"Court sanction in every case", correct:false,
     why:"The court route (ss 78G–78I) is an alternative. The solvency-statement route avoids court unless creditors object."},
    {t:"SIC consent", correct:false,
     why:"A capital reduction is a Companies Act process. The Code is not the gate."}
  ],
  cite:"CA ss 78B–78F; Wan [14.66]–[14.67]", takeaway:"Out-of-court reduction: SR + solvency statement; creditors can object (s 78D)."
},
{
  id:"af-6-02", dia:"carveOut", lesson:"F.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In the same demerger, the directors are not comfortable giving a solvency statement, and a major creditor has signalled it will object.",
  prompt:"Which route fits better?",
  options:[
    {t:"The court-sanctioned capital reduction: special resolution, then a petition to court, which settles a list of creditors and can impose safeguards", correct:true,
     why:"Wan [14.68]: the traditional court route (ss 78G–78I) also starts with a special resolution, then the company petitions the court. Where creditors' interests are in play the court settles a list of creditors (s 78H) and can require safeguards. It can also check fairness to shareholders. That fits when directors will not give a solvency statement or objection is certain."},
    {t:"Go ahead without a solvency statement on the out-of-court route", correct:false,
     why:"The solvency statement is required on that route. Without it, the out-of-court route is unavailable."},
    {t:"Use a whitewash instead", correct:false,
     why:"A whitewash deals with financial assistance or mandatory-offer waivers, not a reduction of capital."},
    {t:"Ignore the creditor; it has no standing", correct:false,
     why:"Creditor protection is central to capital reduction. On either route creditors can be heard."}
  ],
  cite:"CA ss 78G–78I; Wan [14.68]", takeaway:"No solvency statement or likely objection → court route (s 78H creditor list)."
},
{
  id:"af-6-03", dia:"carveOut", lesson:"F.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The division being sold sits inside the parent's main operating company alongside retained businesses. The buyer wants to buy shares, not assets.",
  prompt:"How is this usually done, and where does s 18A come in?",
  options:[
    {t:"Hive the division's assets, contracts and staff down into a new subsidiary, then sell its shares; s 18A bites on the hive-down, not the share sale", correct:true,
     why:"A share deal needs a company that holds only the division, so the business is first transferred into a NewCo (contracts novated or assigned, licences re-applied for, a TSA arranged). That internal transfer of a going concern is where s 18A carries the employees across on existing terms, with consultation. The later share sale does not change the employer, so there is no second transfer event (Intro 2.12)."},
    {t:"Sell the parent's shares", correct:false,
     why:"That would sell the retained businesses too. The division must first be isolated."},
    {t:"Sell shares in the operating company and ask the buyer to return the retained businesses", correct:false,
     why:"That is back to front and exposes the buyer to all the retained liabilities."},
    {t:"s 18A applies on the share sale", correct:false,
     why:"In a share sale the employer company is unchanged, so s 18A has nothing to transfer."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.3, 2.12; Wan [1.61] (SPH hive-down); Employment Act s 18A", takeaway:"Hive-down then share sale: s 18A applies at the hive-down."
},
{
  id:"af-6-04", dia:"carveOut", lesson:"F.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A parent with convertible bonds outstanding spins off a large subsidiary. After the distribution, the parent's share price falls to reflect the value that has left.",
  prompt:"What should the separation plan address for the convertible holders?",
  options:[
    {t:"An adjustment to the conversion ratio, since they get no spin-off shares yet the shares they convert into are now worth less", correct:true,
     why:"Gaughan: shareholders gain by keeping parent shares and receiving the spun-off shares, but warrant and convertible holders may not share in that gain without adjustment. The conversion ratio may need to change as the parent's price falls. Gaughan adds that group debt is usually allocated between parent and spun-off entity according to their post-transaction sizes."},
    {t:"Nothing; convertible holders receive spin-off shares automatically", correct:false,
     why:"The distribution goes to shareholders. Convertible holders are not yet shareholders."},
    {t:"Redeem the bonds at par in every case", correct:false,
     why:"Early redemption depends on the bond terms. The standard fix is an adjustment."},
    {t:"Put the whole group debt on the spun-off company", correct:false,
     why:"Debt is usually allocated in proportion to post-transaction size. Loading all of it onto one side is not the norm."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (Spinoffs)", takeaway:"Spin-off: adjust convertibles/warrants; allocate debt by size."
},
{
  id:"af-6-05", dia:"carveOut", lesson:"F.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Years after a spin-off, the separated company fails. Its employees and pensioners pursue the former parent, citing labour agreements and guarantees the parent had given.",
  prompt:"What should the separation documents have dealt with?",
  options:[
    {t:"Legacy liabilities: releasing or replacing parent guarantees, and allocating employee and pension obligations with indemnities", correct:true,
     why:"GM believed its 1999 carve-out and spin-off of Delphi took it out of auto parts, but its labour agreements left it partly responsible for Delphi workers' wages and benefits. That became a problem when Delphi filed for bankruptcy in 2005 (Gaughan Ch 11). Parent guarantees and shared obligations survive a separation unless they are released, replaced or indemnified."},
    {t:"Nothing; a spin-off automatically ends all parent liability", correct:false,
     why:"Contractual guarantees and agreements remain in force until released. GM/Delphi shows the risk."},
    {t:"Only the TSA's service levels", correct:false,
     why:"The TSA covers transitional services. Legacy liabilities need separate allocation and releases."},
    {t:"Only the exchange ratio", correct:false,
     why:"A spin-off has a distribution ratio, but that does not deal with liabilities."}
  ],
  cite:"Gaughan Ch 11 (GM/Delphi)", takeaway:"Separation ≠ release: deal with guarantees, pensions and legacy liabilities."
},
{
  id:"af-6-06", dia:"carveOut", lesson:"F.6", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The carved-out business is a subsidiary whose shares are being sold. Its biggest customer contract, in the subsidiary's own name, lets the customer terminate if the subsidiary leaves the parent's group.",
  prompt:"What does the buyer need?",
  options:[
    {t:"The customer's consent or waiver before completion, ideally as a condition precedent, because the change-of-control clause is triggered even though nothing is novated", correct:true,
     why:"In a share sale, contracts stay with the subsidiary, so no novation is needed. That is the main advantage over an asset deal. A change-of-control clause is still triggered by the change of ownership. For a key contract the buyer makes consent (or waiver) a condition, or adjusts price and protections."},
    {t:"A novation of the contract to the buyer", correct:false,
     why:"The contracting party (the subsidiary) does not change. The problem is the change-of-control trigger, not assignment."},
    {t:"Nothing; share sales never affect contracts", correct:false,
     why:"Change-of-control clauses exist precisely to catch share sales."},
    {t:"A s 18A notice", correct:false,
     why:"s 18A concerns employees on a business transfer, not customer contracts in a share sale."}
  ],
  cite:"Intro 2.3 (share vs asset deal); Lesson 2.5", takeaway:"Share sale: no novation, but change-of-control clauses still bite."
},
{
  id:"af-6-07", dia:"carveOut", lesson:"F.6", type:"order",
  prompt:"Order the typical steps of a carve-out sale of a division embedded in a group.",
  steps:[
    "Define the perimeter: which assets, contracts, people and liabilities go",
    "Reorganise internally (hive-down into a NewCo) and plan consents",
    "Sign the SPA with the TSA and separation agreements",
    "Complete with day-1 readiness (payroll, systems access, key contracts)",
    "Migrate off the TSA and exit it by its end date"
  ],
  why:"Everything follows from the perimeter: you cannot hive down, draft warranties or price a TSA until you know what is separating. The internal reorganisation creates the entity to be sold. The SPA is signed with the TSA and separation terms. Completion must leave the business able to operate from day 1. The TSA is a bridge with a set end, so migrating off it is the last workstream.",
  cite:"Intro 2.3, 2.8; Gaughan Ch 11 (Divestiture and Spinoff Process)", takeaway:"Perimeter → reorganise → sign (SPA + TSA) → day 1 → exit TSA."
}

